export const WEEK_INDEX = {"BUDDHISM":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"instant karma?","word":"karma","hook":"the vending machine tips over, everyone says karma: what the Buddha actually meant","length":"3:00","carry":"I am the owner of my actions","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Instant karma. A man kicks a vending machine and it tips over onto him. Someone cuts the line and their coffee comes out wrong. Everyone in the comments reaches for the same word, and they mean payback: the universe as a referee, settling scores where you can watch. It's satisfying. It's also not what the Buddha meant, and his version points the other way: back at the one acting. First, a guess.","screen":["`INSTANT KARMA?` → `THE UNIVERSE AS REFEREE.` → `NOT QUITE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Karma, or kamma in Pali, means action. A deed. Something done. Then the Buddha sharpened it. In one discourse he says it plainly: intention is what I call karma. Before the hand moves or the words come out, there's a leaning, a choice. That's where it starts. And then it grows. The old texts use a farming picture: deeds are seeds, and their results ripen like fruit. Not because a judge is keeping score. Because that's what seeds do. Two surprises. First, it's mostly about the one acting. Every choice shapes the chooser: patience, practiced, makes a patient person. Second, not everything is karma. Asked whether everything a person feels comes from past deeds, the Buddha said no: illness, weather, accidents and other people's choices play their part. So 'they deserved it' was never the point. In many temples and monasteries, people chant a reflection on it: I am the owner of my actions, heir to my actions.","screen":["`KARMA — ACTION` · `INTENTION IS KARMA` · `SEEDS, THEN FRUIT` · `NOT EVERYTHING IS KARMA` · then, large: `I AM THE OWNER OF MY ACTIONS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Bring to mind the next thing you'll do: a message, a meeting, a meal. Breathe in, and notice the intention underneath it. Breathe out, and let it lean toward kindness, if you like. That leaning is where karma starts.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Karma. Action, and the intention inside it: what you mean, and what it grows. First word on your strand.","screen":["`KARMA — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: I am the owner of my actions. Not a weight to carry. A handle you can hold. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY BOW THREE TIMES?`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"why bow three times?","word":"the three jewels","hook":"palms together, forehead to the floor, three times: who the bows are for, and why the incense","length":"3:00","carry":"bow to what wakes you up","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: karma, intention and what it grows. Today, a question a lot of people have at the altar: what am I doing when I bow?","screen":["`KARMA ✓` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Shoes off at the door. Palms together. Then down: knees, hands, forehead to the floor. Up. Again. And a third time. If you grew up going to temple, your body may know this before your mind ever asked why. Seen from outside, in a film or a yoga studio, it can look like worshiping a statue. It isn't quite that. The clue is the number three.","screen":["`PALMS TOGETHER. FOREHEAD DOWN.` → `ONCE, TWICE, THREE TIMES.` → `WHY THREE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Each bow has a name, and together Buddhists call them the three jewels. The first is the Buddha: not a god, but a person who woke up. Many teachers say the bow is to that possibility of waking up, in anyone. The second is the Dharma, Dhamma in Pali: the teaching, and the way things actually are. The third is the Sangha: the community that practices it, monks and nuns, and in many places everyone walking the path together. Before chanting, many Buddhists say they go for refuge to these three, in Pali: Buddham saranam gacchami, I go to the Buddha for refuge, and so on for the other two. So the statue is a reminder, a bit like a photo of someone you love. And the incense? At a Buddhist altar it's offered as respect, not as something a god needs. One old verse says the scent of flowers only travels with the wind, but the goodness of a person travels even against it.","screen":["`THE BUDDHA — ONE WHO WOKE UP` · `THE DHARMA — THE TEACHING` · `THE SANGHA — THE COMMUNITY` · `INCENSE — RESPECT, NOT FOOD FOR A GOD` · then, large: `THREE BOWS, THREE JEWELS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Bring your palms together at your chest, if you like. Breathe in. As you breathe out, tip your head forward, just slightly: a small bow to whatever in you is trying to wake up.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The three jewels. The Buddha, the Dharma and the Sangha: the one who woke up, the teaching, and the people who walk it. Second word.","screen":["`THE THREE JEWELS — your 2nd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: bow to what wakes you up. Three bows, and none of them is to a statue. Day two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: IS MEDITATION BUDDHIST?`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"is meditation Buddhist?","word":"sati","hook":"mindfulness before it was a corporate wellness word: the old word underneath the apps","length":"3:00","carry":"remember to notice","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the three jewels, three bows to what wakes you up. Today, the question a lot of people with a meditation app quietly wonder.","screen":["`THE THREE JEWELS ✓` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Mindfulness is on water bottles, on coloring books, in the employee handbook somewhere between the dental plan and the parking policy. Meditation apps teach it, and some give old Buddhist practices new names along the way: insight meditation, calming meditation. So, is meditation Buddhist? Much of what the apps teach grew out of Buddhist practice. The longer answer starts with the old word underneath, and it turns out to be about remembering.","screen":["`MINDFULNESS: ON A WATER BOTTLE.` → `NEW NAMES, OLD PRACTICES.` → `IS IT BUDDHIST?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"The old word is sati, in Pali. A Victorian scholar picked mindfulness to translate it, and the English stuck. Sati is related to a Sanskrit word for memory, so it isn't only paying attention. It's remembering to pay attention, and not letting it slip. The old texts give it a job. A fortress on the frontier has a wise gatekeeper who keeps out strangers and lets in the people he knows. He can only do that if he stays at his post and looks at every face. That gatekeeper, the text says, is sati. Here's what the apps often leave out. In the Buddha's teaching, mindfulness is one part of an eight-part path, sitting beside how you speak, how you act and how you earn a living. It isn't a productivity tool. It's meant to notice everything, even whether what you're doing is kind. So if you already sit with an app, you're practicing something with deep roots. The tradition just sets it in a bigger room.","screen":["`SATI — REMEMBERING TO NOTICE` · `THE GATEKEEPER AT THE GATE` · `ONE PART OF AN EIGHT-PART PATH` · `DEEP ROOTS, A BIGGER ROOM` · then, large: `REMEMBER TO NOTICE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Rest your attention on the breath. When a thought walks up to the gate, and one will, don't chase it and don't slam the gate. Just notice it: thought. Then remember the breath. Each time you remember, that's sati.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Sati. Mindfulness, older and fuller: remembering to notice, the gatekeeper at the gate. Third word on your strand.","screen":["`SATI — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: remember to notice. Forgetting isn't failing; remembering again is the practice. Day three.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: FOUR TRUTHS, NO JARGON.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"four truths, no jargon","word":"the four noble truths","hook":"not a gloomy poster: a doctor's visit in four steps","length":"3:00","carry":"a diagnosis, not a verdict","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: sati, remembering to notice, one part of an eight-part path. Today, the teaching that path belongs to.","screen":["`SATI ✓` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Buddhism has a reputation for being gloomy. Life is suffering, says the poster. But the first thing the Buddha is remembered teaching after he woke up sounds less like a poster and more like a visit to a good doctor. Here's what's wrong. Here's why. It can get better. Here's how. Four steps, in plain words.","screen":["`LIFE IS SUFFERING?` → `MORE LIKE A DOCTOR'S VISIT.` → `FOUR STEPS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"After he woke up, the story says, the Buddha walked to a deer park near Varanasi to find five old friends. What he told them came in four parts, called the four noble truths. One: there is dukkha. It's usually translated suffering, but it's wider: the ache of loss, of change, of things not quite fitting. Aging, sickness, death, being apart from what you love, not getting what you want. You could write the list yourself. Two: it has a cause, and the cause is craving, a thirst for things to be other than they are. Craving isn't love; it's the clutch inside wanting. Three: it can end. When the thirst lets go, the ache goes with it. The name for that ending is nirvana. Four: there's a path, with eight parts, from how you see things to how you speak, act, work and pay attention. Later teachers compared the four to a doctor: diagnosis, cause, cure, treatment. And the first one isn't a verdict. It doesn't say life is bad, or that you're doing it wrong. It says: this is hard, for everyone. Then it gets to work.","screen":["`THERE IS DUKKHA` · `IT HAS A CAUSE: CRAVING` · `IT CAN END` · `THERE'S A PATH` · then, large: `A DIAGNOSIS, NOT A VERDICT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, let something that's been hard lately come to mind, whatever comes. On the second, picture how many other people are carrying something like it today. On the third, let that land: not as a problem solved, but as company.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The four noble truths. There is dukkha; it has a cause; it can end; there's a path. Fourth word.","screen":["`THE FOUR NOBLE TRUTHS — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: a diagnosis, not a verdict. It's not just you, and it's not the end of the story. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY MONKS DON'T BEG.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"why monks don't beg","word":"dana","hook":"a shaved head, a simple robe, a bowl at dawn: what the alms round really is","length":"3:00","carry":"give first","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the four noble truths, a diagnosis, not a verdict. Today, the people who give their whole lives to the fourth one.","screen":["`THE FOUR NOBLE TRUTHS ✓` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Dawn in a town in Thailand or Laos. A line of monks in saffron robes walks barefoot down the street, heads shaved, each carrying a covered bowl. People kneel on mats by the curb with pots of rice. The monks don't ask for anything. And the people giving are often the ones who bow. To an outsider it can look like begging. It isn't, and the difference is today's word.","screen":["`DAWN. A LINE OF MONKS.` → `THEY DON'T ASK.` → `THE GIVERS BOW.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Dana means giving. Start with the monks. The shaved head and the simple robe go back to the Buddha's own leaving home: the story says he cut off his hair with his sword. Shaving it lets go of vanity, a way of saying: I'm not dressing for anyone. Traditionally a monk owns almost nothing: robes, a bowl, a razor, a needle, a few small things. In the Theravada tradition, monks don't eat after midday. That's a training rule for monastics, not a fast everyone keeps. And their food comes from giving. So the alms round isn't begging; it's an exchange. The monk gets a meal. The giver practices dana, which many Buddhists understand as making merit: good karma, and a lighter heart. When the Buddha met people who'd never heard him teach, the texts say he often started with giving, before anything else. In many East Asian monasteries, monks and nuns cook and farm for themselves, and lay people give in other ways. The open hand is the same.","screen":["`THE SHAVED HEAD, THE SIMPLE ROBE` · `A BOWL, A RAZOR, A NEEDLE` · `NOTHING AFTER MIDDAY` · `NOT BEGGING: GIVING` · then, large: `GIVE FIRST.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Rest your hands on your knees, palms up. On the first breath, notice what an open hand feels like. On the second, think of one thing you could give that isn't money: time, attention, a kind word. On the third, picture someone receiving it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Dana. Giving: what fills the monk's bowl, and the first practice the Buddha taught. Fifth word.","screen":["`DANA — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: give first. In the old order of teaching, the open hand came before everything else. That's day five.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: ONE FULL MOON, THREE STORIES.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"one full moon, three stories","word":"Vesak","hook":"lanterns, a baby statue bathed in water, temples at dawn: the Buddha's birthday, and more","length":"3:00","carry":"he was a person who woke up","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: dana, giving, the open hand that fills the monk's bowl. Today, one full moon that means three things.","screen":["`DANA ✓` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Lotus lanterns strung across a street in Seoul. A small statue of a baby, one hand pointing up, standing in a bowl of flowers while people line up to pour water over it. Families dressed in white at a temple in Sri Lanka before sunrise. Many of them will tell you it's the Buddha's birthday. Some will tell you it's more than that. Same festival, told differently. First, a guess.","screen":["`LANTERNS. A BABY STATUE. WHITE CLOTHES.` → `THE BUDDHA'S BIRTHDAY?` → `AND MORE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Vesak takes its name from a spring month in the old Indian calendar. In many Theravada countries, like Sri Lanka, Thailand and Myanmar, its full moon marks three things at once: the Buddha's birth, his awakening and his death. Tradition says all three fell on that same full moon. In Japan, the birthday has its own flower festival in April, and across much of East Asia his awakening and death are remembered on other days of the year. Different calendars, the same life. The water poured over the baby statue recalls a story: when he was born, streams of water fell from the sky to bathe him. And who is everyone celebrating? Buddha isn't a name. It's a title, from a word meaning to wake up. In one old story, a man follows his footprints and asks: are you a god? No. A spirit? No. A human being? No again. Then what are you? Remember me, he says, as awake. Not a god, by his own account: a person who woke up.","screen":["`VESAK — A SPRING FULL MOON` · `BIRTH, AWAKENING, DEATH` · `DIFFERENT DAYS, SAME LIFE` · `BUDDHA — ONE WHO WOKE UP` · then, large: `HE WAS A PERSON WHO WOKE UP.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, picture a single lantern being lit. On the second, bring to mind one person whose life lit something in yours. On the third, let yourself be glad they were here.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Vesak. One spring full moon for his birth, his awakening and his death, told differently in different places. Sixth word.","screen":["`VESAK — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: he was a person who woke up. That's who the lanterns are for. Day six, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHAT CARRIES ON?`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"what carries on?","word":"rebirth","hook":"if there's no soul packed up and moved, what goes on? one flame lighting the next","length":"3:00","carry":"one flame lights the next","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Vesak, and a person who woke up. Six words so far: karma, the three jewels, sati, the four noble truths, dana, Vesak. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`KARMA · THE THREE JEWELS · SATI · THE FOUR NOBLE TRUTHS · DANA · VESAK`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"35 sec","voice":"When a grandparent dies, many Buddhist families do more than hold a funeral. Monks chant. Food is offered, and the good of it is dedicated to the one who died. In many East Asian and Tibetan communities, the prayers go on for forty-nine days, a time, those traditions say, between one life and the next. Behind it is the question day one left hanging: when a life ends, what happens to everything it set growing? And there's a twist. In the Buddha's teaching, no fixed soul packs up and moves.","screen":["`A GRANDPARENT DIES.` → `FORTY-NINE DAYS OF PRAYERS.` → `WHAT GOES ON?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Many Buddhist traditions teach rebirth: a life, when it ends, is followed by another, shaped by karma, round and round, until someone wakes up. Teachers often prefer the word rebirth to reincarnation, because nothing solid moves across. An old book of questions gives the best-known picture. A king asks a monk: is the one who's reborn the same person, or someone else? Neither, says the monk. Light one lamp from another. Did the flame move over? No. Is the new flame unrelated? No again: it was lit from the first. So what passes on is momentum, not a soul. Traditions describe the details very differently: how, where, how soon. Some teachers also point to a version anyone can watch: each moment lit from the last. And for anyone unsure about all of it, there's an old answer too. The Buddha once spoke to villagers who didn't know which teachers to believe. A kind, clear life is good either way, he told them: if there's another life, it goes well; if there isn't, this one already does.","screen":["`REBIRTH, NOT A SOUL MOVING HOUSE` · `SAME PERSON? NEITHER, NOR ANOTHER` · `ONE LAMP LIGHTS ANOTHER` · `GOOD EITHER WAY` · then, large: `ONE FLAME LIGHTS THE NEXT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, notice this breath arriving. On the second, notice that it isn't the one before, and that it came from the one before. On the third, let it go, and let the next one light itself.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Rebirth. Not a soul moving house: one flame lighting the next, carried by karma. Seventh word, and a week's worth.","screen":["`REBIRTH — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one flame lights the next. That's week one: seven words, from a meme to a lamp. Tomorrow, the word behind the first truth.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT \"SUFFERING\".`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"not \"suffering\"","word":"dukkha","hook":"not \"suffering\" — the wobble in the wheel; the itch under everything","length":"6:15","carry":"this too","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: rebirth, one flame lighting the next. Your line was one flame lights the next. Week two opens the four truths one at a time, starting with the first one's word. Day eight.","screen":["`ONE FLAME LIGHTS THE NEXT.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"You finally get the thing. The new phone, the weekend off, the apartment with the good light. And for a day or two it's wonderful. Then, very quietly, something starts to itch. The phone has a scratch. The weekend is already half over on Saturday night. The light is great, but the neighbors are loud. Nothing is wrong, exactly. It just doesn't quite sit right. There's a word for that. It's usually translated as suffering, which is why so many people think Buddhism is gloomy. But the word is smaller and stranger than suffering. It's about a wheel.","screen":["`YOU FINALLY GET THE THING.` → `THEN, QUIETLY, AN ITCH.` → `A WORD ABOUT A WHEEL.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Dukkha. It's the first big word of the Buddha's teaching, and it's almost impossible to translate. Suffering is the usual choice. Some translators say stress, or unsatisfactoriness, which is accurate and nobody can say it twice. So teachers often reach for a picture instead. A popular explanation splits the word in two. Du, bad. Kha, a hole, and in particular the hole in the middle of a wheel, where the axle goes. A cart with a badly fitted wheel still rolls. You still get where you're going. But every turn has a little jolt in it. That's dukkha. Its opposite is sukha, a good axle hole, which came to mean happiness: a smooth ride. Scholars aren't sure that's where the word really comes from. But the picture has stuck because it fits. Buddhist teachers describe three layers of it. The first is plain pain: a toothache, a breakup, a funeral. Nobody needs that explained. The second is the pain of change: the good thing that's already ending while you're enjoying it, the Sunday evening feeling. The third is the subtlest: the low hum of everything being conditioned, dependent, never quite solid enough to rest on. The itch under everything. Seeing the wobble clearly is not the same as being crushed by it. Often it's a relief. Oh. It's not just me. It's the wheel.","screen":["`DUKKHA — HARD TO TRANSLATE.` · `DU, BAD. KHA, THE AXLE HOLE.` · `PAIN, CHANGE, THE HUM UNDERNEATH.` · `IT'S NOT JUST ME.` · then, large: `THE WOBBLE IN THE WHEEL.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Today the bell gives you a little longer: about forty-five seconds. Notice one small wobble right now. A tight shoulder. A worry about later. A message you haven't answered. Don't fix it. Just name it: this too. Then notice that the wobble and the breath are both here, and the breath is still coming in and going out. Stay with both until the bell.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Dukkha. Not only suffering: the wobble in the wheel, the itch under things, the ride that's never perfectly smooth. Eighth word.","screen":["`DUKKHA — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: this too. Two small words for the small wobbles, and the big ones. Day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: CRAVING.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"craving","word":"tanha","hook":"craving — wanting things to be other than they are","length":"6:15","carry":"the wanting is the wobble","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: dukkha, the wobble in the wheel. Your line was this too. Today, the second truth: where the wobble comes from. Day nine.","screen":["`DUKKHA — THIS TOO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"You're in a slow line at the grocery store. The person ahead is paying in coins. Counting them. Out loud. For a lot of people, something happens in the body. The jaw tightens. The weight shifts. A little voice starts narrating: why is this line always the slow one. Now here's the odd thing. The line is just a line. It isn't hurting anyone. What hurts, looked at closely, is the gap between the line that is and the line that's wanted. That gap has a name. The Buddha said it's where the wobble comes from.","screen":["`THE SLOW LINE.` → `THE JAW TIGHTENS.` → `THE GAP BETWEEN IS AND WANT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The second noble truth is about where dukkha comes from. In Pali it's samudaya, arising, origin. And the Buddha's answer is one word: tanha. It literally means thirst. Not a polite preference. Thirst. The dry-mouthed, leaning-forward kind that makes you reach before you think. In the first sermon he names three thirsts. The thirst for pleasant things: the next bite, the next scroll, the next thing to buy. The thirst to be something: to be seen, to be right, to last. And the thirst not to be: to make something go away, to disappear from a feeling, to wish this moment weren't happening. Some teachers read that third thirst widely: wanting something gone is a thirst too. On that reading, the grocery line is thirst of the third kind. Here's a careful point. Buddhism doesn't say every wish is a problem. Teachers use a different Pali word, chanda, for a wish or will that can be wholesome: the wish to be kind, the intention to practice, the will to get up and help someone. What the second truth names is the grasping kind. The kind that insists: this should be different, right now, or I can't be okay. So letting go of craving doesn't mean loving people less. The Buddha taught families how to care for each other. The trouble is the grip, not the care. And here's why it's hopeful, not harsh. If the wobble came from the world, you'd be stuck. You can't make every line fast. But if it comes from the thirst, there's something you can actually work with. The thirst is closer to home.","screen":["`SAMUDAYA — WHERE IT COMES FROM.` · `TANHA — LITERALLY, THIRST.` · `WANTING MORE, TO BE, NOT TO BE.` · `NOT EVERY WISH IS CRAVING.` · then, large: `THE WANTING IS THE WOBBLE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"The bell gives you about forty-five seconds. Find something you want right now. Food, a message, for this lesson to end. Anything. Feel where the wanting lives in your body: the chest, the hands, the jaw. Don't get rid of it, and don't follow it. Just notice that it's a feeling, and that it's thirsty, and keep breathing beside it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Tanha. Thirst: the second truth's answer to where dukkha comes from, wanting things to be other than they are. Ninth word.","screen":["`TANHA — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: the wanting is the wobble. The line was only ever a line. Day nine, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: IT CAN STOP.`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"it can stop","word":"nirvana","hook":"it can stop; not a heaven you go to, a fire with nothing left to burn","length":"6:15","carry":"there is an end","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: tanha, thirst, the second truth. Your line was the wanting is the wobble. A diagnosis, then a cause. Today, the third truth. Day ten.","screen":["`TANHA — THE WANTING IS THE WOBBLE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A headache that's sat behind the eyes all afternoon finally lifts. Nobody marked the minute it arrived. Sometimes the leaving is the only part anyone notices. If the Buddha's teaching stopped at the first two truths, a diagnosis and a cause, it would be a heavy teaching. It doesn't stop there. The third truth says the wobble can end, and the word for that ending is one you've likely heard: as the name of a band, a kind of heaven, a perfect afternoon. It's none of those.","screen":["`THE HEADACHE LIFTS.` → `A DIAGNOSIS. A CAUSE.` → `AND THEN: IT CAN END.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The third noble truth is nirodha. Cessation. Stopping. In the first sermon it's put simply: when the thirst fades away completely, when it's let go and released, dukkha ends. Remove the cause, and what it causes stops. The name for that ending is nirvana, in Sanskrit, or nibbana, in Pali. Teachers link the word to a flame going out, the way a lamp goes out when there's no more fuel. That's the image. Not a place you go. The fire of craving, with nothing left to burn. People sometimes hear that and think it sounds like nothingness. The texts don't describe it that way. The Dhammapada lists the best things there are: \"Health is the greatest of gifts, contentment the best riches; trust is the best of relationships, Nirvana the highest happiness.\" Happiness. Not blankness. And there's an exchange in the discourses that uses the same fire. A wanderer asks the Buddha where a fully awakened person goes after death. The Buddha asks him: when a fire goes out in front of you, which direction did it go? North? South? The man admits the question doesn't fit. The fire just went out. The Buddha says this question is like that one. Buddhist traditions describe nirvana in different words, and the texts often say what it isn't. But the third truth itself is simple. The wobble is real. And it isn't permanent.","screen":["`NIRODHA — STOPPING.` · `NIRVANA — A FLAME GOING OUT.` · `THE HIGHEST HAPPINESS.` · `WHICH WAY DID THE FIRE GO?` · then, large: `THERE IS AN END.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"The bell gives you about forty-five seconds. Notice any small wanting that's here. Then any small ache. Then look for a moment, even a flicker, where neither is pulling at you. A gap. A small quiet between two thoughts. It might be tiny. When it closes, wait for the next one. That tiny thing is what the third truth is pointing at.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Nirvana. The third truth's word: when the thirst ends, dukkha ends. The flame with nothing left to burn. Tenth word.","screen":["`NIRVANA — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: there is an end. Not a place to get to; a fire that's gone out. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: A PATH.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"a path","word":"the eightfold path","hook":"a path — eight parts, a life","length":"6:45","carry":"walk","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: nirvana, the flame going out. Your line was there is an end. Today, the fourth truth: how to get there. Day eleven.","screen":["`NIRVANA — THERE IS AN END.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A mechanic tells you the car has a problem, and walks off. A friend says you seem stressed, and changes the subject. Being told what's wrong and then left there is almost worse than not knowing. The first three truths could have been like that. There is dukkha. It comes from thirst. It can end. Great. How? The Buddha didn't leave it there. The fourth truth isn't an idea at all. It's directions. And they aren't a one-time fix, like a pill. They're something you walk, every day, for a life.","screen":["`THE PROBLEM, THEN SILENCE.` → `GREAT. HOW?` → `THE FOURTH TRUTH IS DIRECTIONS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The fourth noble truth is magga, the path. The way that leads to the end of dukkha. It has eight parts, so it's called the noble eightfold path. Here they are, quickly. Right view. Right intention. Right speech. Right action. Right livelihood. Right effort. Right mindfulness, the sati from day three. Right concentration. The word translated right is samma, and it's gentler than it sounds in English. It means whole, complete, well-tuned, the way a chord is right. Not right as opposed to wrong answers on a test. Right the way a thing fits. In one of the old discourses, a nun named Dhammadinna sorts the eight into three bundles. Wisdom: seeing clearly and meaning well. Conduct: how you speak, act and earn a living. And meditation: effort, mindfulness, concentration. Notice what that means. Most of the path isn't sitting on a cushion. It's how you talk to your sister and how you make your money. And here's what people often get wrong. The eight aren't steps you finish one at a time, like levels. They're more like the eight spokes of a wheel, and many Buddhists read the eight-spoked wheel that way. They turn together. A little honesty helps meditation. A little meditation helps honesty. One old verse says it plainly: \"The best of ways is the eightfold; the best of truths the four words.\" The four words are the four truths you now know. The eightfold way is how you walk them.","screen":["`MAGGA — THE PATH.` · `EIGHT PARTS, EACH ONE SAMMA.` · `WISDOM, CONDUCT, MEDITATION.` · `SPOKES OF A WHEEL, NOT STEPS.` · then, large: `THE BEST OF WAYS IS THE EIGHTFOLD.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"The bell gives you about forty-five seconds. Sit comfortably. Pick just one spoke of the wheel. Maybe speech: one thing you'd like to say more kindly. Maybe action: one small harm you'd like to skip. Maybe livelihood: one moment at work you'd like to do honestly. Hold that one spoke in mind, and breathe with it. Nobody walks all eight at once. One step is still walking.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The eightfold path. The fourth noble truth: eight parts, wisdom, conduct and meditation, turning together like a wheel. Eleventh word.","screen":["`THE EIGHTFOLD PATH — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: walk. The truths are something you walk, not something you believe. Day eleven.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: FIVE, AND THEY READ LIKE KINDNESS.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"five, and they read like kindness","word":"the precepts","hook":"five, and they read like kindness","length":"6:45","carry":"do less harm","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the eightfold path, eight spokes turning together. Your line was walk. Today, the conduct spokes, in five short lines. Day twelve.","screen":["`THE EIGHTFOLD PATH — WALK.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"When people hear a religion has rules, they brace. They picture a long list of thou-shalt-nots, somebody stern at the top, and a lot of guilt. Buddhism has a short list too. Five lines. Buddhists say them at temples and at home, often every week, some every day, in a temple in Sri Lanka or a living room in Los Angeles. But read them closely and they don't sound like a list of rules. They sound like a promise to be easy to live next to.","screen":["`A RELIGION HAS RULES.` → `FIVE LINES, SAID EVERY WEEK.` → `EASY TO LIVE NEXT TO.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The precepts. In Pali, sila: conduct, virtue, the way you live. There are five for lay Buddhists. Not to kill or harm living beings. Not to take what isn't given. Not to cause harm through sex. Not to speak falsely. And not to take intoxicants that cloud the mind. That's the list. Now listen to how they're actually said. Each one is phrased the same way: I undertake the training rule to refrain from harming living beings. I undertake the training rule to refrain from taking what is not given. And so on. Notice the words. I undertake. Nobody is ordering anyone. You take them on yourself. And training rule. Not a law, not a commandment. Something you practice, fail at sometimes, and pick up again, the way you'd train for a race. There's a teaching in the discourses that turns the whole thing around. It calls the five precepts five great gifts. When you keep them, the Buddha says, you give freedom from fear to countless beings. Everyone near you can relax. They don't have to worry you'll hurt them, rob them, betray them, lie to them, or lose control. And because you give them safety, you get to share in that safety too. That's why they read like kindness. They aren't about keeping yourself pure. They're about everyone else being able to breathe around you. Buddhists hold them in different ways. Some take all five for life. Some take them fresh on holy days. Monks and nuns take many more.","screen":["`SILA — HOW YOU LIVE.` · `FIVE LINES FOR LAY BUDDHISTS.` · `I UNDERTAKE THE TRAINING RULE.` · `FIVE GREAT GIFTS: NO FEAR.` · then, large: `EASY TO LIVE NEXT TO.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"The bell gives you about forty-five seconds. Think about the five: not harming, not taking, not betraying, not lying, not clouding the mind. Don't grade yourself. Just ask which one would make the biggest difference to the people around you this week. Picture someone near you feeling a little safer because of it, and rest there.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The precepts. Five training rules, taken on freely, that give everyone around you freedom from fear. Twelfth word.","screen":["`THE PRECEPTS — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: do less harm. It's a training rule, not a test you pass or fail. That's day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: IMPERMANENCE.`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"impermanence","word":"anicca","hook":"impermanence; the leaf, the mood, the year","length":"6:45","carry":"this passes","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the precepts, five training rules that give others freedom from fear. Your line was do less harm. Today, the first of three marks. Day thirteen.","screen":["`THE PRECEPTS — DO LESS HARM.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A leaf in October. Last spring it was a tight green bud. In summer it was so ordinary nobody noticed it. Now it's red and gold and loose on the branch, and next week it'll be on the sidewalk. Set this morning's mood beside this one. Look at last year: a worry that kept someone up in March can be hard to even recall by fall. The leaf, the mood, the year. They're all doing the same thing, at different speeds. They're passing. The Buddha asked people to look at that, closely, until they saw it.","screen":["`THE LEAF.` → `THE MOOD.` → `THE YEAR.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Anicca. In Pali, nicca means permanent, lasting, constant. Put an a in front and you get the opposite: not lasting. Impermanence. It's the first of what Buddhists call the three marks of existence, the three things true of everything that's made of parts and conditions. Anicca, impermanence. Dukkha, which you already know. And anatta, which is tomorrow. The Dhammapada puts the first one in four words: \"All created things perish.\" Now, that sounds obvious. Everyone knows things change. But here's the catch. It's easy to know it the way we know the sun is a star: true, and no help at all on a Tuesday. A good mood gets held as if it'll stay. A bad week feels like forever. An ending still startles. So the Buddha didn't teach anicca as a fact to agree with. He taught it as something to see, again and again, in small things, until it sinks in. Watch a breath: it comes, and goes. Watch a thought: it arrives, and leaves. Watch a sound fade away. And there's another side to it. Impermanence isn't only sad news. It's what makes change possible at all. If things were permanent, grief would never soften. A bad habit would never break. The seed would never grow. The same fact that takes the leaf in October brings the bud in April.","screen":["`ANICCA — NOT LASTING.` · `THE FIRST OF THREE MARKS.` · `ALL CREATED THINGS PERISH.` · `WHY GRIEF CAN SOFTEN.` · then, large: `THIS PASSES.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"The bell gives you about forty-five seconds. Watch something pass. Start with the breath: notice that each one begins, and ends, and is never quite the same as the last. Then notice sounds around you, arriving and leaving. If a feeling comes up, watch that too. You're not trying to hold anything or push anything away. Just watching things come and go, like leaves.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Anicca. Impermanence, the first of the three marks. Everything made of parts is passing, the hard things and the good ones. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`ANICCA — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: this passes. The leaf in October, and the bud in April. That's thirteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT-SELF.`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"not-self","word":"anatta","hook":"not-self — who's actually in there? (the question, not the answer)","length":"6:45","carry":"look","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: anicca, impermanence, the first mark. Your line was this passes. Today, the third mark, and the question under day seven's flame. Day fourteen.","screen":["`ANICCA — THIS PASSES.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Try this. Say the word I, and then try to point to it. A finger usually goes to the chest. But is that it? The body at six is not the body now. Opinions change. Taste in music changes. A mood can turn twice before lunch. So who's the I that stayed the same through all of that? It's an old question. The Buddha asked it too, and his answer is well known and often misunderstood. Today we're taking the question, not the answer.","screen":["`SAY \"I\", THEN POINT TO IT.` → `BODY, OPINIONS, MOODS, ALL CHANGING.` → `WHO'S ACTUALLY IN THERE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Anatta. Atta means self, the unchanging core, the owner. An-atta: not-self. It's the third mark of existence, after anicca and dukkha. Here's how the Buddha is remembered teaching it, to the same five companions from the deer park; tradition counts it as his second discourse. He goes through what a person is made of. The texts count five heaps: the body, feelings, perceptions, mental formations, and consciousness. And he asks about each one. Is the body permanent or impermanent? Impermanent. Is what's impermanent something you can rest on, or does it wobble? It wobbles. Then is it right to say of it: this is mine, this is what I am, this is my self? No. And he goes through all five the same way. It's not a lecture. It's a series of questions. Look, and see. Another discourse keeps the question open in a different way. A wanderer asks the Buddha straight out: is there a self? The Buddha is silent. Is there no self? Silent again. Later he explains to his attendant Ananda that either answer would have led the man into a wrong view. Some teachers say anatta is less a doctrine to hold than a way of looking: don't cling to anything as me or mine, and see what happens. Buddhist schools talk about this in different ways. Theravada teachers stress the five heaps and the practice of seeing them as not-self. Mahayana teachers speak of emptiness, and some schools of a buddha-nature in every being. Who's in there? Keep the question open.","screen":["`ANATTA — NOT-SELF.` · `FIVE HEAPS, EACH ONE QUESTIONED.` · `IS THERE A SELF? SILENCE.` · `A WAY OF LOOKING.` · then, large: `WHO'S IN THERE?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"The bell gives you about forty-five seconds. Sit, and look. Notice a sensation: is it you, or something happening? Notice a thought passing: did you choose it, or did it just arrive? Notice a feeling. Don't answer the question. Don't decide anything. Just keep looking, gently, at whatever shows up, and let the question stay open.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Anatta. Not-self, the third mark: nothing in the five heaps can be held as me, mine, or my self. A question to keep looking into. Fourteenth word.","screen":["`ANATTA — your 14th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: look. Two weeks, fourteen words. Next week turns to the heart: friendliness, compassion, good company. Day fourteen, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: A WORD FOR WISHING WELL ON PEOPLE YOU DON'T LIKE.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"a word for wishing well on people you don't like","word":"metta","hook":"a word for wishing well on people you don't like","length":"7:00","carry":"may you be at ease","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: anatta, not-self, a question kept open. Your line was look. Week three turns to the heart, starting with a word for friendliness. Day fifteen.","screen":["`ANATTA — LOOK.` → `WEEK THREE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"The coworker who replies all. The neighbor with the leaf blower at seven on a Saturday. The relative with opinions about your life at every holiday. Not villains. Just people who are hard to like. Maybe a face comes to mind already. Now here's an old Buddhist practice: people sit quietly and wish that person well. On purpose. Not because they've forgiven anything, and not because the person has earned it. Just as training, the way you'd train a muscle. It sounds almost backwards. Buddhists trace it to the Buddha's own teaching.","screen":["`THE ONE WHO REPLIES ALL.` → `NOW WISH THEM WELL.` → `ON PURPOSE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Metta. It's a word in Pali, the old language the Buddha's teachings were kept in. It comes from mitta, a friend. So metta is friendliness. A wish for someone's good, the plain warm kind you'd feel for a friend, pointed at anyone at all. You may know its Sanskrit cousin, maitri, or the name Maitreya, the friendly one, the Buddha that tradition says is still to come. There's a short teaching on it, the Metta Sutta, still chanted in Buddhist homes and monasteries. It pictures a mother protecting her only child with her own life, and says to hold a heart like that toward every living being. Seen and unseen. Near and far. Born and not yet born. Every one. And the Buddha's own verses put the reason bluntly: \"hatred does not cease by hatred at any time: hatred ceases by love—this is an old rule.\" An old rule. Not a new idea even then. How do people actually do it? Later teachers laid out a gentle order. Start with yourself, because it's hard to give what you've never felt. Then someone you love. Then someone neutral, the person who sells you coffee. Then, last and slowest, someone difficult. You don't pretend to like them. You just wish them what you'd want: to be safe, to be well, to be at ease. Some who practice it describe an odd side effect: it changes them more than anyone else. The difficult person is still difficult. They just carry less of that person around.","screen":["`METTA — FROM MITTA, A FRIEND.` · `LIKE A MOTHER WITH HER ONLY CHILD.` · `HATRED CEASES BY LOVE.` · `YOU, A FRIEND, A STRANGER, THE HARD ONE.` · then, large: `MAY YOU BE AT EASE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"The bell gives you about seventy-five seconds. Start where the old order starts: with yourself. Breathe in, and on the way out let a few words go: may I be at ease. Then someone you love: may you be at ease. Then a stranger, the person who sells you coffee. Then, if you're ready, that face from a minute ago. You don't have to feel warm. Just let the words go out, one breath at a time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Metta. Friendliness. A wish for anyone's good, even the people who make it hard. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`METTA — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: may you be at ease. You don't have to like someone to wish them well. Day fifteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: COMPASSION.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"compassion","word":"karuna","hook":"compassion — metta when it meets pain","length":"7:00","carry":"stay","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: metta, friendliness, from the word for a friend. Your line was may you be at ease. Day sixteen.","screen":["`METTA — MAY YOU BE AT EASE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A friend calls, and you can hear right away that something's wrong. The first instinct might be to fix it. Give advice. Find the silver lining. Change the subject to something lighter. Or the instinct might be to back away a little, because their pain is heavy and there's your own day to get through. Both are normal. Both are ways of leaving. There's a third thing a person can do, and it's much harder than fixing or leaving. You can stay. Today, what metta becomes when it meets pain.","screen":["`A FRIEND CALLS, SOMETHING'S WRONG.` → `FIX IT, OR BACK AWAY.` → `OR: STAY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Karuna. Compassion. It's the second of four qualities Buddhists call the brahmaviharas, the divine abodes, places a heart can live. Metta, friendliness. Karuna, compassion. Mudita, gladness at others' good fortune. And upekkha, a steady calm, which you'll meet in a few days. Metta wishes someone well. Karuna is what happens when that wish runs into someone's pain. An old commentary, Buddhaghosa's, explains the word this way: compassion is what makes a good heart tremble when others suffer. It doesn't turn away. It quivers, and stays. The same commentary warns about two traps. Compassion's far enemy is cruelty, the plain opposite. Its near enemy is sneakier: a worldly grief that looks like compassion but is really sorrow for oneself. Some modern teachers put the near enemy as pity. Pity looks down: oh, you poor thing, glad that isn't me. Compassion stands beside. There's a story in the monastic rules about this. The Buddha and his attendant Ananda find a monk lying sick in his own filth, and nobody is caring for him. The Buddha washes him himself, with Ananda's help. Then he gathers the monks and says, in effect: you have no mother or father here to care for you. If you don't care for each other, who will? Whoever would care for me should care for the sick. In Mahayana Buddhism, compassion is embodied by Avalokiteshvara, the bodhisattva who hears the cries of the world, known as Guanyin in China and Chenrezig in Tibet. But it can start very simply. Someone is hurting. You don't look away.","screen":["`KARUNA — METTA MEETS PAIN.` · `THE HEART TREMBLES, AND STAYS.` · `PITY LOOKS DOWN. COMPASSION BESIDE.` · `THE BUDDHA WASHES THE SICK MONK.` · then, large: `STAY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"The bell gives you about seventy-five seconds. Bring to mind someone who's hurting right now. Someone you know, or someone you saw on the news. Let yourself feel a little of it. Not all of it, just enough that your heart moves. Notice any urge to fix it, or to pull away. Don't act on either. Just stay with them in your mind, breathing. If you like, let a wish go toward them: may you be free from this pain.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Karuna. Compassion: metta when it meets pain, the heart that trembles and stays beside, not above. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`KARUNA — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: stay. Not fixing, not leaving: staying is its own kind of help. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE COMMUNITY.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"the community","word":"the sangha","hook":"the community; why the Buddha said it's the whole of the path","length":"7:00","carry":"you need people","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: karuna, compassion, metta meeting pain. Your line was stay. Day seventeen.","screen":["`KARUNA — STAY.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"New habits are hard to keep alone. The gym, eating better, an earlier bedtime, meditating every day. A week goes great, and then, quietly, it doesn't. The habits that last often have someone else in them. A running partner. A class. A friend who texts, you coming? Plenty of things are easier with company. The Buddha seems to have thought so too. When his attendant Ananda suggested friends were half the path, the Buddha corrected him.","screen":["`A HABIT TRIED ALONE.` → `A HABIT THAT LASTED.` → `SOMEONE ELSE WAS IN IT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Sangha. The word means an assembly, a gathering, a community. It's the third of the three jewels you bowed to on day two. The Buddha, the teacher. The Dharma, the teaching. And the Sangha, the community that practices it. In its narrowest sense, the sangha means the monks and nuns. The Buddha is remembered describing a fourfold assembly: monks, nuns, laymen and laywomen. Today some Buddhists, especially in Western groups, use sangha for any group that practices together: a temple congregation, a Tuesday-night sitting group, the people you meditate with online. Here's the story from the discourses. Ananda, the Buddha's attendant, and his cousin by tradition, says to him one day: it seems to me that good friendship is half of the holy life. And the Buddha answers: don't say that, Ananda. Good friendship, good companionship, is the whole of the holy life. Not half. The whole thing. He explains that a person with good friends can be expected to develop the whole path. The friend doesn't replace the practice. The friend is how the practice keeps going. When an old English translator met the word sangha, he reached for a very English word: the Church. It's a clumsy fit in some ways, but it catches something true. A sangha isn't a club you join because you already agree. It's the people who keep walking beside you on the days you'd rather quit.","screen":["`SANGHA — THE COMMUNITY.` · `THE THIRD OF THREE JEWELS.` · `MONKS, NUNS, LAYMEN, LAYWOMEN.` · `NOT HALF. THE WHOLE.` · then, large: `YOU NEED PEOPLE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"The bell gives you about seventy-five seconds. Bring to mind the people who walk with you. Not the whole world, just a few faces. The friend who checks in. The teacher who believed in you. Maybe the stranger who sat near you once, in a quiet room. Hold each face for a breath or two. Notice that you didn't get here alone. If you like, let a little metta go to each of them.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The sangha. The community, the third jewel; the people who practice together and keep each other walking. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`THE SANGHA — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you need people. Not half the path, the Buddha said: the whole of it. Day seventeen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE BUDDHA'S PAST LIVES AS ANIMALS.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"the Buddha's past lives as animals","word":"the Jataka","hook":"the Buddha's past lives as animals; the monkey king","length":"7:00","carry":"the bridge he made of his body","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the sangha, the community, the third jewel. Your line was you need people. Today, a story. Day eighteen.","screen":["`THE SANGHA — YOU NEED PEOPLE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A huge mango tree grows on the bank of a river. In it lives a troop of monkeys, eighty thousand of them, the story says, and their king. One night a human king's archers surround the tree. They mean to shoot every monkey at dawn. There's no way down. The monkeys are trapped between the arrows and the river. And the monkey king looks across the water at the far bank, and makes a decision. This is one of hundreds of stories Buddhists have told children, and grown-ups, for many centuries. The monkey king, they say, was the Buddha.","screen":["`A MANGO TREE BY A RIVER.` → `ARCHERS WAITING FOR DAWN.` → `THE MONKEY KING DECIDES.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The Jataka. The word means birth stories. They're tales of the Buddha's earlier lives, before he became the Buddha, when he was still on the long way. Buddhist tradition says he lived countless lives, as kings and merchants and poor men, and very often as animals: a hare, a deer, an elephant, a quail, a monkey. The Pali collection has around five hundred and fifty of them, and Jataka scenes are carved in stone on some of the oldest Buddhist monuments in India. Here's the monkey king's story, the way it's told. He leaps across the river to the far bank and ties one end of a long cane to a tree. He ties the other end around his own waist and leaps back. But the cane is too short. He can reach the mango tree only with his hands. So he grips a branch and stretches his body out across the gap. And he tells his monkeys: cross. One by one, all of them run across the cane, and over his back, to safety. His body is torn by it. The human king has watched the whole thing from below. He's so moved that he has the monkey king gently brought down and cared for. And before he dies, the monkey king teaches him what a ruler is for: a king should care for his people the way I cared for mine. Each Jataka ends by telling you who was who, and in this one the monkey king was the future Buddha. Why tell stories like this? Because they show the path from the inside. Generosity. Courage. Compassion. Not as ideas, but as a monkey holding on to a branch until the last one is across.","screen":["`JATAKA — BIRTH STORIES.` · `HARE, DEER, ELEPHANT, MONKEY.` · `THE CANE TOO SHORT.` · `A KING LEARNS WHAT RULING IS FOR.` · then, large: `THE BRIDGE HE MADE OF HIS BODY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"The bell gives you about seventy-five seconds. Feel your own arms and shoulders, the part of you that would do the holding. Now bring to mind someone trying to get across something right now: a kid with an exam, a friend in a hard month, a coworker drowning in a deadline. Not a grand rescue. Just one small stretch that could help: staying late, holding the door a little longer, listening to the end of the story. Picture holding on while they cross. Notice what it costs, and what it gives.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Jataka. Birth stories: the Buddha's past lives, many as animals, showing the path from the inside. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`THE JATAKA — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: the bridge he made of his body. Small stretches count too. That's day eighteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: UPEKKHA.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"upekkha","word":"equanimity","hook":"upekkha; the steady mind in a storm","length":"7:00","carry":"let it be as it is","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Jataka, the monkey king. Your line was the bridge he made of his body. Day nineteen.","screen":["`THE JATAKA — THE BRIDGE HE MADE OF HIS BODY.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Monday, the boss calls the work brilliant, and the whole day floats. Tuesday, a client calls it the worst thing they've seen, and sleep won't come. Wednesday, a friend forgets a birthday. Thursday, a stranger compliments a coat, and suddenly it's fine again. Up, down, up, down, like a boat in weather. Plenty of weeks go like that, and it gets called being human. It is. But the Buddha named those winds, one by one, and taught a way to stand in them without being blown over. Today, the steady mind in a storm.","screen":["`MONDAY: BRILLIANT.` → `TUESDAY: THE WORST.` → `A BOAT IN WEATHER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Upekkha. Equanimity. It's the fourth of the divine abodes you met with karuna: friendliness, compassion, gladness, and this. The word comes from a root meaning to look, with a prefix meaning over or near. To look on, from a steady place. Not to look away. The Buddha named eight worldly conditions, four pairs, often pictured as winds. Gain and loss. Fame and disgrace. Praise and blame. Pleasure and pain. The text says they come to everyone, the untaught and the wise alike. The winds aren't the problem. Getting flung around by them is. The Dhammapada has the picture: \"As a solid rock is not shaken by the wind, wise people falter not amidst blame and praise.\" A rock. The wind still blows. The rock still feels it. It just doesn't move. Here's the part of upekkha that's easy to get wrong. Equanimity isn't indifference. Buddhaghosa warned of its near enemy, a dull, unknowing calm that looks like it but isn't. Some teachers today call that indifference. Indifference says, I don't care. Equanimity says, I care, and I'm not going to be knocked over. It comes last of the four, and here's one way to see why. Metta wishes well. Karuna stays with pain. Mudita is glad for others. And upekkha keeps all of that steady, so the caring doesn't burn you out. A good nurse, a good parent, a good friend in an emergency. Warm, and calm. That's upekkha.","screen":["`UPEKKHA — LOOKING ON, STEADY.` · `EIGHT WORLDLY WINDS.` · `THE ROCK IN THE WIND.` · `NOT INDIFFERENCE.` · then, large: `LET IT BE AS IT IS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"The bell gives you about seventy-five seconds. Sit tall and steady, like a rock on a hillside. Now let one wind blow: remember a moment of praise. Notice what it does in your body. Let it pass. Now let the other blow: a moment of blame. Notice that too. Let it pass. Come back to the breath, and to the weight of your body on the seat. The winds come and go. You stay.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Equanimity. Upekkha: looking on from a steady place, a rock in the eight winds. Caring, and not knocked over. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`EQUANIMITY — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: let it be as it is. Letting things be as they are, and still showing up. Day nineteen, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE STRING TOO TIGHT SNAPS, TOO LOOSE WON'T PLAY.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"the string too tight snaps, too loose won't play","word":"the middle way","hook":"the string too tight snaps, too loose won't play","length":"7:00","carry":"tune","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: equanimity, upekkha, the rock in the eight winds. Your line was let it be as it is. Day twenty.","screen":["`EQUANIMITY — LET IT BE AS IT IS.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"There's a monk named Sona, and he is trying so hard. He's been pacing up and down his walking path in meditation so intensely and for so long that his feet are bleeding. The path is stained with it. And he's getting nowhere. Sitting alone, he starts to think about quitting. Going home. Using his family's money to do some good in the world instead. The Buddha, the sutta says, knows his thought and comes to him. And he doesn't say try harder. He doesn't say give up. He asks about music.","screen":["`SONA TRIES SO HARD.` → `HIS FEET ARE BLEEDING.` → `THE BUDDHA ASKS ABOUT MUSIC.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Before he was a monk, Sona was skilled at the lute, a stringed instrument. So the Buddha asks him: when the strings were too tight, did the lute play well? No. When the strings were too loose, did it play well? No. And when they were neither too tight nor too loose, but tuned just right? Then it played beautifully. It's the same with effort, the Buddha says. Too much, and you become restless and strained. Too little, and you become lazy. Find the right tuning. Sona takes the advice, and the story says that before long, he reaches awakening. This is the middle way, and it's where the Buddha's whole teaching began. In the first sermon at the deer park, before he taught the four truths, he told his five companions there are two extremes to avoid. On one side, chasing pleasure: the life he'd had in the palace. On the other, punishing the body: the harsh fasting he'd tried as a seeker. Both, he said, lead nowhere good. Between them runs a middle way, and that middle way is the eightfold path you already know. So the middle way isn't a bland compromise, halfway between two things. It's more like tuning. A string that's in tune isn't half-tight. It's exactly right for that string, that instrument, that day. And it needs retuning, again and again, because strings drift. Some days need more effort. Some days need rest. The skill isn't finding the answer once. It's listening, and adjusting.","screen":["`TOO TIGHT, TOO LOOSE, TUNED.` · `EFFORT, THE SAME WAY.` · `TWO EXTREMES TO AVOID.` · `NOT HALFWAY. TUNED.` · then, large: `THE MIDDLE WAY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"The bell gives you about seventy-five seconds. Sit, and check your tuning. Is your body too tight? Clenched jaw, raised shoulders, gripping hands? Let a little go. Is it too loose? Slumped, drowsy, drifting? Lift the chest a little and brighten your attention. Look for the place in between: upright and relaxed, awake and easy. When you drift one way or the other, gently retune.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The middle way. Between indulgence and self-punishment, not a compromise but a tuning, adjusted again and again. Twentieth word.","screen":["`THE MIDDLE WAY — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: tune. Not halfway between two things; exactly right for today. That's day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"the mountain ahead","word":"the Dhammapada","hook":"the mountain ahead; \"mind is the forerunner\"","length":"7:00","carry":"what you think, you become","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the middle way, the lute string tuned just right. Your line was tune. Day twenty-one.","screen":["`THE MIDDLE WAY — TUNE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Three weeks. Look at your strand. Karma, the three jewels, sati, the four noble truths, dana, Vesak, rebirth. Dukkha, tanha, nirvana, the eightfold path. The precepts, anicca, anatta. Metta, karuna, the sangha, the Jataka, equanimity, the middle way. Twenty words, the everyday vocabulary of a Buddhist life. Those are the foothills. Tomorrow the path starts climbing: the Buddha's whole life, told like a serial, from a queen's dream to his last words. So today, from the top of the foothills, a look up at the mountain. One small book of verses, and its very first line.","screen":["`TWENTY WORDS ON YOUR STRAND.` → `THE FOOTHILLS, DONE.` → `THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The Dhammapada. Four hundred and twenty-three short verses, in twenty-six chapters, kept in the Pali canon and remembered as the Buddha's own sayings. Its verses are chanted, memorized and preached on in Buddhist communities to this day. The name has two halves. Dhamma, the teaching, the way things are. And pada, which means a foot, a step, a path, and also a line of verse. So it's the path of the teaching, or the verses of the teaching, and both are right. Every step is a line. You've already heard it several times in these three weeks: the fragrance that travels against the wind, nirvana as the highest happiness, hatred ceasing by love, the rock in the wind. Now here's where it starts. The very first verse. Modern translators render it something like: mind is the forerunner of all things. An old English translation reads: \"All that we are is the result of what we have thought: it is founded on our thoughts, it is made up of our thoughts.\" Then, across the first two verses, two images. If someone speaks or acts from a harmful mind, pain follows, like the wheel of a cart following the ox's foot. And if someone speaks or acts from a clear, good mind, happiness follows them, \"like a shadow that never leaves him.\" Notice what the first verse is doing. It isn't telling you what to believe about the universe. It's pointing you back at your own mind, the one place you can actually start. The whole path so far, from day one's intention to the noticing and the metta, begins right there.","screen":["`423 VERSES, 26 CHAPTERS.` · `DHAMMA + PADA: TEACHING, STEP.` · `MIND IS THE FORERUNNER.` · `THE WHEEL, AND THE SHADOW.` · then, large: `WHAT YOU THINK, YOU BECOME.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"The bell gives you about seventy-five seconds. Think back over these three weeks. Let the words drift past: the seed of an intention, three bows, the gatekeeper, the wobble in the wheel, the open hand, the passing leaf, the monkey on the branch. Don't try to remember them all. Just notice which one has stuck. Which came back to you on an ordinary day, without being asked? Hold that one word. Breathe with it. It's yours now.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Dhammapada. The path of the teaching, verses of the teaching: four hundred and twenty-three verses that start with the mind. Twenty-first word, and camp one is done.","screen":["`THE DHAMMAPADA — your 21st word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: what you think, you become. Tomorrow the story begins, with a queen's dream of a white elephant. Day twenty-one.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE WHITE ELEPHANT DREAM.`"]}]}},"CATHOLIC":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"the smallest prayer","word":"the sign of the cross","hook":"forehead, chest, shoulder, shoulder: what the hand is tracing","length":"3:00","carry":"head, heart, shoulders — all of you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A pitcher does it before the first throw. A grandmother does it when the bus passes a church. Someone does it in a hospital hallway, right after the doctor leaves. Forehead, chest, one shoulder, the other. Two seconds, no words out loud. If you grew up Catholic, your hand may know it better than your head does. It's a prayer, maybe the smallest there is, and the hand is tracing something on purpose.","screen":["`A PITCHER. A GRANDMOTHER. A HOSPITAL HALLWAY.` → `TWO SECONDS.` → `THE SMALLEST PRAYER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"It's old. Around the year 200, a Christian writer in North Africa described believers tracing a tiny cross on their foreheads all day long: getting dressed, sitting down to eat, lighting a lamp. Over the centuries that thumb-sized cross grew into the big one. And it says two things at once. The hand traces the cross, the shape Jesus died on, over your own body. The words name who Catholics believe God is. They come from the end of Matthew's Gospel, where Jesus sends his friends out, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost.\" One God: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. That's why many Catholics dip their fingers in holy water at the church door first. Those same words were said over them, with water, at their baptism. Now look where the hand goes. The forehead, where you think. The chest, where your heart is. The shoulders, where you carry things. Many hear it that way: nothing left out. Western Catholics go to the left shoulder first; Orthodox Christians go right, then left. Same sign, two old habits.","screen":["`A THUMB ON THE FOREHEAD, AROUND 200.` · `THE CROSS, AND THE NAME.` · `HOLY WATER: A REMINDER OF BAPTISM.` · `THINK, FEEL, CARRY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. If you'd like, make the sign slowly, slower than anyone ever does it: forehead, chest, left shoulder, right. Or just rest a hand on each place and notice it. Then let the breath all the way out.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The sign of the cross. The cross traced over all of you, in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. First word on your strand.","screen":["`THE SIGN OF THE CROSS — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: head, heart, shoulders — all of you. Two seconds, and nothing left out. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE CLAIM BEHIND THE BREAD.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"the claim behind the bread","word":"the Eucharist","hook":"a white dress, a party, a wafer: and the claim the day was about","length":"3:00","carry":"he stayed","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the sign of the cross, all of you in two seconds. Today, the thing at the center of every Mass.","screen":["`THE SIGN OF THE CROSS ✓` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A white dress or a clip-on tie. Photos on the church steps, a cake after. First Communion is easy to remember. What the day was actually about can be fuzzier: in surveys, a lot of Catholics describe the bread as a symbol. The Church's own claim is much bigger than a symbol. So here it is, once, plainly.","screen":["`A WHITE DRESS. A CAKE.` → `A SYMBOL?` → `THE CLAIM, ONCE, PLAINLY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"It starts the night before Jesus died, at a Passover meal. He takes bread, gives thanks, breaks it and says, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"This is my body, which is given for you. Do this for a commemoration of me.\" Gave thanks, in Greek, is where the word comes from: eucharistia, thanksgiving. Now the claim. Catholics believe that when the priest says those words at Mass, the bread and wine truly become the body and blood of Christ. They still look, taste and feel like bread and wine. What they are, underneath, has changed. Not a symbol of him: him. The Church calls it the real presence. In John's Gospel, Jesus calls himself the living bread, and many followers find that too hard and walk away. He lets them go. After Mass, the consecrated bread that's left is kept in a small box near the altar, the tabernacle, with a red lamp burning beside it. That's why people bend a knee when they walk into an empty church. They believe he's there. Christians differ here. Orthodox Christians and Lutherans also speak of a real presence; many other Protestants see the meal as a memorial and a sign.","screen":["`THIS IS MY BODY.` · `EUCHARISTIA — THANKSGIVING.` · `STILL LOOKS LIKE BREAD.` · `THE TABERNACLE AND THE LAMP.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Think of a time someone simply stayed with you: in a waiting room, on the end of your bed, on the phone. They didn't fix anything. Breathe in, remembering it. Breathe out slowly.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The Eucharist. Thanksgiving: the bread and wine Catholics believe become Christ himself, his real presence. Second word.","screen":["`THE EUCHARIST — your 2nd word` → clicks onto the strand beside the sign of the cross."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: he stayed. It's what the red lamp is saying, in an empty church on a Tuesday afternoon. Day two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MOST MISUNDERSTOOD ROOM IN THE BUILDING.`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"the most misunderstood room in the building","word":"confession","hook":"the booth, the nerves, and why a person and not just a prayer","length":"3:00","carry":"say it out loud, then it's smaller","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Eucharist, and the claim behind the bread. Today, the room people are most nervous to walk into.","screen":["`THE EUCHARIST ✓` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Bless me, Father, for I have sinned. Plenty of people raised Catholic can still say the opening line, and still remember the nerves: the line outside the booth, the list rehearsed in your head, the little screen. Movies made it a courtroom, or a punch line. And later comes the fair question: why tell a person? Why not just tell God?","screen":["`BLESS ME, FATHER.` → `THE BOOTH. THE NERVES.` → `WHY A PERSON?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Catholics call it the sacrament of reconciliation; most just say confession. The answer to why a person starts on Easter night. In John's Gospel, the risen Jesus breathes on his friends and says, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"Whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them.\" Catholics believe that from then on, forgiveness was meant to be spoken out loud, through a person, and heard out loud too. God does the forgiving; the priest speaks for him, and for the Church, since a wrong never touches just one person. What happens is simple. You can kneel behind a screen or sit face to face. You say what you've done, plainly, in your own words. The priest listens, gives a penance, usually a prayer or a small act to make things right, and then says the words of absolution: you're forgiven, in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. One rule is absolute: the seal. A priest may never reveal what he hears, to anyone, for any reason. And if it's been years, and that's the very thing keeping you away, here's what's true. Mass is open to you; you can go and simply not receive communion until you're ready. You can start with: it's been a long time, and I don't remember how. Priests hear it often, and helping you through is part of the job. Catholics believe the forgiveness is real even if you walk out feeling awkward, or nothing at all. Something huge in the dark at two in the morning gets edges once it's spoken.","screen":["`WHOSE SINS YOU SHALL FORGIVE.` · `BEHIND A SCREEN OR FACE TO FACE.` · `THE SEAL: NEVER REVEALED.` · `SPOKEN, IT HAS EDGES.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, think of one thing you've been carrying and never said out loud. On the second, name it to yourself in one plain sentence. On the third, breathe out, and notice whether it feels any different now that it has a name.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Confession. Saying it out loud, and hearing out loud that you're forgiven. Third word.","screen":["`CONFESSION — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: say it out loud, then it's smaller. That's the whole secret of the little room. Day three.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHAT THE STRING IS FOR.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"what the string is for","word":"the rosary","hook":"beads on a rearview mirror: counting, not magic","length":"3:00","carry":"hands busy, heart free","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: confession, the thing said out loud. Today, something that might be hanging from a rearview mirror near you.","screen":["`CONFESSION ✓` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A loop of beads swinging from a rearview mirror. Tangled in a grandmother's purse with the car keys and the cough drops. Laid in someone's hands at a funeral. Some people wear one like a necklace; some call it a lucky charm. People can't even agree how many beads are on it. The string is for something much plainer than luck. It's for counting.","screen":["`ON A MIRROR. IN A PURSE.` → `A LUCKY CHARM?` → `IT'S FOR COUNTING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Rosary comes from the Latin rosarium, a rose garden: a garland of prayers, like a crown of roses. Here's the layout. A cross, a short tail of beads, then the loop: five sets of ten small beads, called decades, each set apart by a single bead. On the single bead, the Our Father. On each of the ten, a Hail Mary. After each ten, a short line of praise, the Glory Be. One decade on its own takes about five minutes, and plenty of people start there. That's where the arguments about the count come from: fifty-nine beads on the whole string, fifty-three Hail Marys. Counting prayers is ancient. Stories from the early desert monks tell of one who kept track of his daily prayers with pebbles in his lap. Later, monks prayed all one hundred and fifty psalms, and tradition says people who couldn't read prayed one hundred and fifty short prayers instead. The rosary grew out of that, and was called the poor man's psalter. And here's the plain secret. While the fingers and lips are busy with the words, the mind is free to think through scenes from the lives of Jesus and Mary, called the mysteries. That's the real prayer. The beads just keep the hands busy.","screen":["`ROSARIUM — A ROSE GARDEN.` · `FIVE DECADES OF TEN.` · `PEBBLES IN A MONK'S LAP.` · `THE MIND ON THE MYSTERIES.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Rest one hand in your lap. On each breath out, touch your thumb to the next finger: one, two, three. Don't count out loud. Let the fingers keep track, so the mind doesn't have to.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The rosary. A garland of prayers on a string that keeps count, so the mind can rest on the life of Jesus. Fourth word.","screen":["`THE ROSARY — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: hands busy, heart free. The beads hold the count, so the mind can go somewhere better. That's day four.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: HONOR, NOT WORSHIP.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"honor, not worship","word":"the saints","hook":"\"isn't praying to Mary idolatry?\" — the answer is one difference","length":"3:00","carry":"the company you're in","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the rosary, a string that keeps count. Today, the question a friend may have asked you about Mary.","screen":["`THE ROSARY ✓` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Sooner or later a friend asks it, usually kindly: why do Catholics pray to Mary? Isn't that worshiping a statue? Walk into an old church and you can see why they'd wonder. Plaster figures in niches. Faces in stained glass. Candles flickering in front of a woman in blue and a man holding keys. Plenty of people raised Catholic were never given the answer either. It turns on one difference, and it's a difference people already live by.","screen":["`WHY PRAY TO MARY?` → `A WOMAN IN BLUE. A MAN WITH KEYS.` → `ONE DIFFERENCE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Catholics draw a sharp line between worship and honor. Worship belongs to God alone. Honor can be given to people: the way you'd honor a grandmother, a teacher, a hero. Saints get honor. Mary, the mother of Jesus, gets the highest honor of any saint, but still honor, not worship. So why pray to them? Because what Catholics are doing is asking: pray for me. It's what you'd ask a friend. Catholics believe in what the old creed calls the communion of saints: everyone who belongs to Christ, living and dead, is one family, and the family can still pray for each other. Death doesn't end the friendship. So a statue works like a family photo. Nobody is praying to the plaster. And the saints were never perfect. The man with the keys is Peter, who said three times, the night Jesus was arrested, that he didn't know him, then went out and wept. Catholics count him the first pope. A saint isn't someone who never fell. It's someone who kept getting up. Other Christians see it differently: many Protestants honor the saints as examples, and don't ask for their prayers.","screen":["`WORSHIP: GOD ALONE.` · `HONOR: MARY AND THE SAINTS.` · `PRAY FOR ME.` · `THE COMMUNION OF SAINTS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Think of someone, alive or gone, who failed at something and kept going. On the first breath, picture their face. On the second, remember what they got wrong. On the third, remember that they kept going.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The saints. Holy ones: honored, not worshiped, and still one family with the living. Fifth word.","screen":["`THE SAINTS — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the company you're in. Flawed people, still going, still in the family. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: ASHES, AND FORTY DAYS.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"ashes, and forty days","word":"Lent","hook":"a smudge on the forehead, fish on Friday: giving something up is a way of paying attention","length":"3:00","carry":"less, on purpose","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the saints, honored, not worshiped. Today, the one day a year Catholics walk around with a smudge on their foreheads.","screen":["`THE SAINTS ✓` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"One Wednesday a year, a coworker shows up with a black smudge on her forehead. Then the barista. Then a kid on the train. Somebody leans over to say: you've got something on your face. It's Ash Wednesday, and it starts a season. For the next forty days, a Catholic friend might be off chocolate, or off social media, or eating fish on Fridays. It can look like a diet. It isn't about the chocolate.","screen":["`A SMUDGE ON THE FOREHEAD.` → `IT'S ASH WEDNESDAY.` → `IT ISN'T ABOUT THE CHOCOLATE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Lent is the season of forty days before Easter. Forty is the number the Bible keeps reaching for when someone is being tested: Noah's rain, Moses on the mountain, and Jesus fasting in the desert for forty days before he begins his work. Lent walks alongside those forty days. It opens with ash. A cross is traced on the forehead, often with words from the Bible's first pages, in the Douay-Rheims: \"dust thou art, and into dust thou shalt return.\" It sounds grim. Catholics hear it as honest: you won't be here forever, so what will you do with the time? Then come three old practices: prayer, fasting, and giving to the poor. Catholics fast on Ash Wednesday and Good Friday, and skip meat on the Fridays of Lent, because Friday is the day Jesus died. The rule doesn't count fish as meat. Hello, Friday fish fry. Many also give something up. Here's the idea underneath: give up something small, and every reach for it becomes a moment of noticing. The empty space where the chocolate was becomes a little room for prayer, or for someone else. And the forty days only make sense because of where they end: Easter morning.","screen":["`FORTY: NOAH, MOSES, THE DESERT.` · `DUST THOU ART.` · `PRAYER, FASTING, GIVING.` · `THE REACH BECOMES A REMINDER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Think of one small thing you reach for without thinking: your phone, a snack, a complaint. Picture yourself reaching for it and stopping, hand in the air. Stay in that pause for three slow breaths, and see what you notice there instead.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Lent. Forty days before Easter, of prayer, fasting and giving, where less becomes a way of paying attention. Sixth word.","screen":["`LENT — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: less, on purpose. The ash says the time is short; the empty space makes room. Day six, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE THINGS YOUR FAMILY KEPT.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"the things your family kept","word":"sacramentals","hook":"a medal on a keychain, a palm behind the crucifix, an abuela's altarcito: the Church's one word for all of it","length":"3:00","carry":"faith you can hold","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Lent, less on purpose. Six words so far: the sign of the cross, the Eucharist, confession, the rosary, the saints, Lent. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`THE SIGN OF THE CROSS · THE EUCHARIST · CONFESSION · THE ROSARY · THE SAINTS · LENT`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"A Saint Christopher medal on a keychain. A palm from Palm Sunday tucked behind the crucifix, drying out for a year. A bottle of holy water in the kitchen cabinet. An abuela's altarcito, with candles, a rosary and photos of the people she's lost. A Santo Niño, the child Jesus, watching over a Filipino living room. A medal blessed at a quinceañera Mass. Every family's version looks different. The Church has one word for all of it.","screen":["`A MEDAL. A PALM. HOLY WATER.` → `AN ALTARCITO. A SANTO NIÑO.` → `ONE WORD FOR ALL OF IT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Sacramentals. The word is a cousin of sacrament. The seven sacraments, like baptism, confession and the Eucharist, are the big moments Catholics believe Christ himself gave the Church. Sacramentals are the smaller things the Church added around them: blessings, blessed objects, words and gestures. Look back at this week and you'll find you've been holding them all along. Holy water and the sign of the cross. The ashes. The rosary. A saint's medal. The Church is careful about what they are. They aren't magic, and they aren't lucky charms. They're reminders that help a person get ready for grace, the way a photo on the fridge reminds you to call. Around them grows what the Church calls popular piety: the customs each people builds, which it welcomes as long as they lead back to Christ. So a Filipino family getting up before dawn for nine Masses before Christmas, called Simbang Gabi. A Mexican family's altar for its dead in early November. An Irish grandmother who won't eat meat on any Friday of the year. An Italian town's feast for its patron saint. Different shapes, one faith. Whatever your family's version was, it was a way in.","screen":["`SACRAMENTS: THE BIG SEVEN.` · `SACRAMENTALS: THE SMALL SIGNS.` · `NOT MAGIC: REMINDERS.` · `DIFFERENT SHAPES, ONE FAITH.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Bring to mind one object from your family's faith: a medal, a picture, a candle, a rosary in a drawer. On the first breath, see it. On the second, see whose it was. On the third, let it remind you of them.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Sacramentals. Small blessed things and gestures: reminders that point to grace. Seventh word: a week's worth.","screen":["`SACRAMENTALS — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: faith you can hold. That's week one: seven words, and you were already holding most of them. Tomorrow, your grandmother before dinner.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: YOUR GRANDMOTHER BEFORE DINNER.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"your grandmother before dinner","word":"grace","hook":"your grandmother before dinner; a gift you didn't earn","length":"6:15","carry":"you are already held","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: sacramentals, faith you can hold. Week one is behind you. Week two starts with the thing all those small signs point to. Day eight.","screen":["`FAITH YOU CAN HOLD.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"The food is on the kitchen table, steam coming off the potatoes, and somebody's already reaching for the bread. Then a hand on the wrist. A grandmother, saying: wait. Grace first. Heads go down. A few quick words, a quick amen, and everybody eats. A kid at a table like that could grow up thinking grace means the little speech before dinner. It does. But the word is much bigger than the speech. It's the reason for the speech.","screen":["`A KITCHEN TABLE.` → `WAIT. GRACE FIRST.` → `THE WORD IS BIGGER THAN THE SPEECH.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Grace comes from the Latin gratia: a favor, a gift, something given freely, not paid for. It's the same root that gives English gratitude, and gratis, free of charge. The New Testament was written in Greek, and there the word is charis, which also means a gift freely given, and a kind of loveliness. So when Catholics talk about grace, they mean God's gift of himself, given before anybody earned it. The letter to the Ephesians says it plainly, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"For by grace you are saved through faith, and that not of yourselves, for it is the gift of God.\" Not of yourselves. That's the surprise at the heart of the word. You don't climb up to grace. It comes down to you. Catholics say it arrives in many ways: in baptism and the other sacraments, in prayer, in an ordinary Tuesday when a friend calls at exactly the right moment. Yesterday's small signs are there to get a person ready for it. And here's where the dinner table comes back in. The traditional Catholic grace before meals goes: \"Bless us, O Lord, and these thy gifts, which we are about to receive from thy bounty, through Christ our Lord. Amen.\" Listen to what it notices. Thy gifts. Thy bounty. Somebody grew the food, somebody cooked it, and before any of that, Catholics say, somebody gave the world to begin with. So saying grace isn't paying a fee before eating. It's noticing that you were given something.","screen":["`GRATIA — A FAVOR, A GIFT.` · `CHARIS — GIVEN FREELY.` · `NOT OF YOURSELVES.` · `THE TABLE IS A SMALL PICTURE.` · then, large: `A GIFT YOU DIDN'T EARN.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice, and it's small on purpose. Think of one thing you have today that you didn't earn. The air, maybe. A person. A roof. Let it come into focus, and stay with it while you breathe slowly. If you pray, you can say thank you with each breath out. If you don't, just let yourself notice the gift.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Grace. A favor, a gift freely given. Catholics say it comes first, before anyone earns it. Eighth word.","screen":["`GRACE — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you are already held. Grace means the gift came first. That's day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: A YOUNG WOMAN SAYS YES.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"a young woman says yes","word":"Mary","hook":"a young woman says yes","length":"6:15","carry":"let it be","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: grace, a gift you didn't earn. Your line was you are already held. Today the word comes back, in an angel's mouth. Day nine.","screen":["`YOU ARE ALREADY HELD.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"A small town in Galilee called Nazareth. A young woman there is promised in marriage to a man named Joseph. Luke doesn't give her age, and he doesn't describe the house. An ordinary day, as far as we know. And then, Luke's Gospel says, the angel Gabriel comes in to her, telling her she's going to have a child, and that he'll be called the Son of the Most High. She isn't married yet. Today, a yes the whole Catholic story leans on, and the young woman who said it.","screen":["`A TOWN IN GALILEE.` → `AN ANGEL IN THE ROOM.` → `SHE ISN'T MARRIED YET.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Her name, in Hebrew, was Miriam, like the sister of Moses. We say Mary. And the first thing Gabriel says to her, after the greeting, is what the angels in Luke say again and again: \"Fear not, Mary, for thou hast found grace with God.\" Grace: yesterday's word, back again. Then she does something people skip over. She asks a question. How can this be? She isn't passive. She wants to understand. The angel answers, and then she gives the reply Catholics have treasured ever since. In the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"Behold the handmaid of the Lord; be it done to me according to thy word.\" In Latin, be it done is fiat, and Catholics still call her answer Mary's fiat. Her yes. Here's why it matters so much. Catholics say God didn't force anything on her. He asked, and waited, and the whole story of Jesus turns on a young woman saying yes. That's why they call her the Mother of God, because they believe her son is God, and why she gets the highest honor of any saint: honor, as day five said, not worship. Other Christians differ on how much to say about her. Many Protestants honor her as a faithful believer and a model of trust, and don't pray to her. And Luke's page is the same for all of them: a young woman, a question, and then yes.","screen":["`MIRIAM — FEAR NOT.` · `SHE ASKS A QUESTION.` · `FIAT — BE IT DONE.` · `THE MOTHER OF GOD.` · then, large: `BEHOLD THE HANDMAID OF THE LORD.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Think of one thing in your life right now that you're resisting: something you can't change and keep pushing against. Name it. Notice how tightly you're holding it. Then, breath by breath, let your hands open a little on your lap. If you pray, you can borrow Mary's words: be it done to me.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Mary. Miriam of Nazareth, the mother of Jesus, whose yes, her fiat, Catholics honor above every other saint. Ninth word.","screen":["`MARY — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: let it be. But notice the order: first she asked her honest question. Day nine.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE WORDS, LINE BY LINE, FROM LUKE.`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"the words, line by line, from Luke","word":"the Hail Mary","hook":"the words, line by line, from Luke","length":"6:15","carry":"a greeting, then a request","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Mary, a question, and then a yes. Your line was let it be. Today, the words on the rosary's ten beads, and who said them first. Day ten.","screen":["`LET IT BE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"At a wake, a room full of voices runs it together until it sounds like one long word. A kid in Catholic school rattles it off before anyone can blink. In football, a desperate pass thrown deep with no time left still gets called a Hail Mary. Said that fast, it's easy to miss where the words came from. Half the prayer isn't new at all. It's lifted, line by line, from the first chapter of Luke's Gospel. Today, slowly.","screen":["`ONE LONG WORD AT A WAKE.` → `A HAIL MARY PASS.` → `LIFTED FROM LUKE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"The first line belongs to an angel: yesterday's Gabriel, in the room in Nazareth. In the Douay-Rheims Bible, his first words are: \"Hail, full of grace, the Lord is with thee: blessed art thou among women.\" Hail is just an old hello. Full of grace is day eight's word, filled to the brim. The next line belongs to a relative. A little later in Luke, Mary visits her cousin Elizabeth, who's also pregnant, and Elizabeth cries out: \"Blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb.\" Over the centuries Christians put those two greetings side by side, an angel's and a cousin's, and added two names: Mary, right after the angel's hail, and Jesus at the end. That's the whole first half. Every word of it, apart from those two names, is from Luke. Then the prayer turns. The second half isn't scripture. It's the Church speaking, and it came into the prayer later: Holy Mary, Mother of God, pray for us sinners, now and at the hour of our death. Amen. Listen to the shape. The first half says hello. The second half asks for something, small and huge at once: pray for us. Now, and at the very end. Catholics aren't asking Mary to do what only God does. They're asking her to pray with them. On the rosary it comes ten times in each decade, a greeting, then a request, like a heartbeat. Say it fast and it's a blur. Say it slowly and you're standing in two rooms in Luke's Gospel.","screen":["`AN ANGEL'S HELLO.` · `A COUSIN'S BLESSING.` · `THEN THE CHURCH ASKS.` · `PRAY FOR US, NOW AND AT THE END.` · then, large: `A GREETING, THEN A REQUEST.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Let your breath slow down. On the breath in, think of a greeting: hello to someone you love. On the breath out, think of a request: one thing you need right now. In, a greeting. Out, a request. If you pray the Hail Mary, you can say it once, as slowly as you've ever said it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"The Hail Mary. An angel's hello and a cousin's blessing from Luke, then the Church's request: pray for us. Tenth word.","screen":["`THE HAIL MARY — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: a greeting, then a request. Two rooms in Luke, ten times a decade. Day ten.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE FAMILY WORD HE CHOSE.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"the family word he chose","word":"the Our Father","hook":"the family word he chose","length":"6:15","carry":"not a stranger","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Hail Mary, a greeting, then a request. Today, the prayer on the single beads between the tens. Day eleven.","screen":["`A GREETING, THEN A REQUEST.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"A boss gets the best email voice. A judge gets your honor. A king, in the old days, got your majesty, said from the knees. The more powerful someone is, the more careful and formal the words. In Luke's Gospel, a disciple asks Jesus: teach us to pray. How do you talk to the one who made the stars? Titles, you might expect. A long list of them. Instead he gives them a prayer that begins with a family word.","screen":["`YOUR HONOR. YOUR MAJESTY.` → `TEACH US TO PRAY.` → `A FAMILY WORD.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"In Matthew's Gospel, in the Douay-Rheims Bible, it starts: \"Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.\" Father. Behind the Greek of the Gospels, some scholars hear the Aramaic word abba. Mark's Gospel keeps that word untranslated, on the night before Jesus died, when he prays in the garden: \"Abba, Father, all things are possible to thee.\" Abba is the word a son or daughter uses. Not a title. A relationship. And then look at the word before it: our. Not my Father. Our. Read it closely and there isn't a single I or me in the whole prayer. Give us. Forgive us. Lead us. Deliver us. Someone praying it alone at night still prays in the plural, standing in a crowd they can't see. That's the family word doing its work twice: God is not a stranger, and neither is the person next to you. At Mass, the whole room prays it together, just before communion. For a long time Catholics prayed it in Latin: Pater noster, our Father. One difference shows up in other churches. Churches that learned the prayer from the King James Bible end with: for thine is the kingdom, the power and the glory. The King James prints that line in Matthew; the Douay-Rheims, translated from the Latin, ends at deliver us from evil, and so do Catholics. At Mass, the priest adds a short prayer after it, and then everyone says a version of that ending together. Same prayer, two old habits.","screen":["`ABBA — FATHER, AT HOME.` · `OUR, NOT MY.` · `NO I, NO ME.` · `PATER NOSTER.` · then, large: `NOT A STRANGER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Say just the first two words, slowly, out loud or silently: our Father. On our, think of someone you'd rather not include in that word, and include them anyway. On Father, notice how different it sounds from a title. If you pray the whole prayer, you can pray it once, very slowly.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"The Our Father. The prayer Jesus taught, opening with a family word, with no I or me in it anywhere. Eleventh word.","screen":["`THE OUR FATHER — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: not a stranger. Not God, and not the person beside you. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY PEOPLE STAND UP WHEN THE BOOK COMES IN.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"why people stand up when the book comes in","word":"the Gospel","hook":"why people stand up when the book comes in","length":"6:15","carry":"the story enters the room","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Our Father, a prayer with no I or me in it. Your line was not a stranger. Today, back inside the Mass. Day twelve.","screen":["`NOT A STRANGER.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"A guest at a Catholic Mass spends a lot of it half a second behind. Everyone else seems to know when to sit, when to stand, when to kneel, and the guest is watching the person in front. One moment in particular catches visitors off guard. People have been sitting quietly, listening to readings. Then music starts, often the word alleluia, and the whole room stands up at once. Someone at the front lifts a book up high. Why stand for a book? Day two was the table at the center of the Mass. Today, the first half: the story.","screen":["`SIT, STAND, KNEEL.` → `THE WHOLE ROOM STANDS.` → `FIRST, THE STORY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"First, the name. The word Mass is usually traced to the old Latin words at the end of the service, ite, missa est, roughly: go, you are sent. So the whole thing may be named for the moment people walk back out the door. Now, the first half. Catholics call it the Liturgy of the Word, and it's mostly listening. On a Sunday there's usually a reading from the Old Testament, then a psalm, often sung back and forth, then a reading from one of the letters of the apostles. People sit for those. Then comes the Gospel, a reading from one of the four books about the life of Jesus, and everyone stands. The English word Gospel goes back to an Old English phrase for good news, and it translates the Greek euangelion. Catholics say that when the Gospel is read, Christ himself is speaking to the people gathered there. So you stand the way you'd stand when someone important walks into the room. Right before it, people trace three little crosses with a thumb: on the forehead, on the lips, on the heart. It's day one's sign, shrunk down. One common way to explain it: may these words be in my mind, on my lips, in my heart. Standing for the reading isn't new, either. In the Bible itself, after the exile, the scribe Ezra opens the book of the law in front of the whole people, and when he opens it, they all stand up. Then everyone sits again, and a priest or deacon preaches: the homily. The story has entered the room.","screen":["`MASS — GO, YOU ARE SENT.` · `READINGS, A PSALM, THEN THE GOSPEL.` · `THREE SMALL CROSSES.` · `EZRA OPENS THE BOOK. THEY STAND.` · then, large: `THE STORY ENTERS THE ROOM.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. If you're sitting, stand up, just for this, the way the room stands for the Gospel. Touch your forehead, your lips, your heart. Then sit back down, breathe slowly, and let each of those three places get ready to listen. You don't have to say anything.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"The Gospel. The good news: the reading about the life of Jesus that everyone stands to hear, the high point of the Mass's first half. Twelfth word.","screen":["`THE GOSPEL — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: the story enters the room. That's why everyone gets to their feet. Day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"PEACE BE WITH YOU.\"`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"\"peace be with you\"","word":"the sign of peace","hook":"\"peace be with you\" — the handshake in the middle","length":"6:15","carry":"turn to the stranger","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Gospel, and the whole room standing for the story. Today, the moment the whole room turns around. Day thirteen.","screen":["`THE STORY ENTERS THE ROOM.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"The Mass is quiet and formal. Candles, a priest at the altar, everyone facing forward, nobody talking. And then, out of nowhere, everyone turns around. The person behind you sticks out a hand. The teenager next to you, who hasn't looked up from the floor, says peace be with you. A couple kisses. A little kid waves at the whole row. For a few moments, a room full of people who mostly don't know each other shakes hands. Then everyone turns back to the front as if nothing happened. Today, the handshake in the middle of the Mass.","screen":["`EVERYONE FACING FORWARD.` → `THEN EVERYONE TURNS AROUND.` → `THE HANDSHAKE IN THE MIDDLE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"After the story comes the table. Bread and wine are carried up to the altar, the priest prays over them, and the whole room says the Our Father, from day eleven. Then, just before people come forward for communion, the sign of peace. Where does it come from? Start with Easter night. In John's Gospel, the frightened friends of Jesus are hiding behind locked doors, and the risen Jesus stands among them. His first words, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"Peace be to you.\" Not a scolding for running away. Peace. In the Greek of John's Gospel it's eirene, a word that carries the old Hebrew greeting shalom, which means more than quiet. It means whole, complete, nothing missing. The first Christians greeted each other with more than a handshake: early Christian writers describe a kiss of peace shared in worship. There's a reason it sits right before communion in the Catholic Mass. Jesus once said that if you're bringing a gift to the altar and remember that someone has something against you, leave the gift there, go and make peace first, then come back. So before people go up to the table, they turn to whoever's beside them. Not their best friend. Whoever happens to be there. It's short on purpose. It isn't a chat. It's a sign: before we go any further, we're at peace with each other. The gesture varies from place to place: a bow, a nod, a handshake. The meaning holds.","screen":["`AFTER THE STORY, THE TABLE.` · `PEACE BE TO YOU — EASTER NIGHT.` · `SHALOM — WHOLE, NOTHING MISSING.` · `MAKE PEACE, THEN COME TO THE ALTAR.` · then, large: `TURN TO THE STRANGER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Bring three people to mind, one at a time. Someone you love. Someone you barely know: a neighbor, a coworker, the person who sold you coffee. Then someone you're not at peace with right now. With each one, picture turning to them and offering your hand. If you pray, you can wish each of them peace.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"The sign of peace. Turning to whoever is beside you, just before the table, to say peace be with you. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`THE SIGN OF PEACE — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: turn to the stranger. Not your best friend: whoever happens to be beside you. Day thirteen, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE OBLIGATION NOBODY EXPLAINS AS A GIFT.`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"the obligation nobody explains as a gift","word":"Sunday","hook":"the obligation nobody explains as a gift","length":"6:15","carry":"one day that isn't yours to fill","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the sign of peace. Your line was turn to the stranger. And that's two weeks. This week: grace, Mary, the Hail Mary, the Our Father, the Gospel, the sign of peace. Day fourteen.","screen":["`TURN TO THE STRANGER.` → `TWO WEEKS.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Being woken up too early. Scratchy church clothes. A parent at the bottom of the stairs saying: we're going, it's an obligation. That's how some people who grew up Catholic remember Sunday. Catholics are asked to go to Mass every Sunday, and to a kid it can sound like homework for the soul. Meanwhile the rest of the week is full. The phone that never stops. The laundry. The emails that arrive on Saturday night. What if the obligation was never meant as homework?","screen":["`SCRATCHY CHURCH CLOTHES.` → `HOMEWORK FOR THE SOUL?` → `SUNDAY, EXPLAINED AS A GIFT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Start at the very beginning of the Bible. In Genesis, God makes the world in six days, and then, in the Douay-Rheims Bible, \"he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had done.\" God rests. Not, the tradition says, because he's tired. In the story, the day of rest is built into the world like a beam into a house. For the Jewish people it became the Sabbath, the seventh day, kept from sunset Friday to sunset Saturday, and Jewish families keep it still. Then something happened on a different day. All four Gospels put the empty tomb on the first day of the week, the day after the Sabbath. Christians began gathering on that first day to break bread, and came to call it the Lord's day. In Latin, dies Domini. In Spanish it's still domingo, in Italian domenica. An early Christian named Justin Martyr described it for outsiders: on the day called Sunday, everyone gathers, the memoirs of the apostles or the writings of the prophets are read, bread and wine are brought and blessed, and there's a collection for widows and orphans. That's recognizably the Mass. So why call it an obligation? Catholics say it's the way the Church makes sure the gift actually gets opened. Left alone, a week fills every hour. The obligation holds one day open: for worship, for family, for rest, for a long meal. Sunday Mass can also be kept on Saturday evening, at what's called the vigil. And it isn't a trap: the Church itself says a serious reason, like being sick or caring for a baby, excuses you. The idea underneath is simple. One day a week isn't yours to fill.","screen":["`HE RESTED ON THE SEVENTH DAY.` · `THE FIRST DAY: THE EMPTY TOMB.` · `DIES DOMINI — THE LORD'S DAY.` · `JUSTIN: ON THE DAY CALLED SUNDAY.` · then, large: `THE GIFT GETS OPENED.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice, and for this sit you have nothing to do. Nothing to fill. Put the phone face down, or out of reach. Let your hands rest open. Every time your mind reaches for a task, notice it, and set it down for later. If you pray, you can offer the time to God. If not, just let it be empty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Sunday. The Lord's day, the day of the empty tomb, the day the Church keeps open for worship and rest. Fourteenth word.","screen":["`SUNDAY — your 14th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: one day that isn't yours to fill. That's what the obligation was protecting all along. Two weeks, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY THERE'S FIRE IN EVERY CHURCH.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"why there's fire in every church","word":"candles","hook":"why there's fire in every church","length":"6:30","carry":"light a candle for someone","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Sunday, the gift inside the obligation. Your line was one day that isn't yours to fill. Week three starts with fire. Day fifteen.","screen":["`ONE DAY THAT ISN'T YOURS TO FILL.` → `WEEK THREE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Every building has lights now. Switches, LEDs, the glow of exit signs. And yet step into a Catholic church at noon, in a city with perfect electricity, and there's still fire. Two candles on the altar. A tall one near the front. Rows of little flames in a side corner, flickering, somebody's coins in the box beside them. And one small red lamp that never seems to go out. It isn't for seeing. Nobody needs a candle to read anymore. So why is there fire in every church?","screen":["`PERFECT ELECTRICITY.` → `AND STILL, FIRE.` → `IT ISN'T FOR SEEING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Start with what Jesus says in John's Gospel, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"I am the light of the world: he that followeth me, walketh not in darkness.\" So a candle in a church is a small sermon without words. Light in the dark. And every candle there has its own job. The candles on or near the altar are lit for Mass. The tall one is the Easter candle, the paschal candle. On the night before Easter, Catholics gather outside in the dark, light a new fire, and carry this one candle into a black church while someone sings, three times, light of Christ. Then the flame is passed from candle to candle through the whole crowd until the room is full of light. The same candle stands near the font at baptisms, and beside the coffin at funerals. The little red lamp is the one from day two: the sanctuary lamp, burning near the tabernacle as a sign that Christ is present there. And the rows in the corner? Votive candles, from the Latin votum, a vow or a prayer. Someone lights one for a sick mother, a job interview, a child who's struggling, a friend who died. The flame keeps burning after they walk out the door. Some people put it this way: the candle keeps praying after I leave. In Catholic teaching, the wax does nothing by itself. It's a way of saying: this person matters, and I'm not done hoping.","screen":["`I AM THE LIGHT OF THE WORLD.` · `THE EASTER CANDLE.` · `THE RED LAMP: HE IS HERE.` · `VOTIVE — A VOW, A PRAYER.` · then, large: `THIS PERSON MATTERS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice. If there's a candle nearby, you can light it. If not, just picture one. Think of one person who could use some light right now. See their face above the flame. You don't have to fix anything. Just hold them there while it burns, breathing slowly. If you pray, you can pray for them.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Candles. Light in the dark: the Easter candle, the sanctuary lamp, and the votive flame that keeps praying after you leave. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`CANDLES — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: light a candle for someone. The flame keeps hoping after you've gone. Day fifteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE WOMEN WENT FIRST.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"the women went first","word":"Easter","hook":"the women went first","length":"6:30","carry":"the stone is already moved","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: candles, and the Easter flame carried into a dark church. Your line was light a candle for someone. Today, the morning that flame is for. Day sixteen.","screen":["`LIGHT A CANDLE FOR SOMEONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Very early on a Sunday morning, near Jerusalem. Jesus has been executed and buried. The men who followed him are nowhere in this scene. A few women are walking to a tomb cut in the rock, carrying spices to anoint a body. That's what you did for someone you loved. On the way, they realize they have a problem. A great stone has been rolled across the entrance. In Mark's Gospel, they ask each other: \"Who shall roll us back the stone from the door of the sepulchre?\" They go anyway. They don't have a plan. They just go.","screen":["`VERY EARLY. A SUNDAY.` → `A FEW WOMEN, WITH SPICES.` → `WHO WILL ROLL BACK THE STONE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"When they get there, the stone is already rolled away. The tomb is empty. In one telling, two men in shining clothes ask them why they're looking for the living among the dead, and say, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"He is not here, but is risen.\" Every Gospel tells it a little differently: how many women, how many angels, who sees what first. But all four agree on one thing: women were the first witnesses at the empty tomb. Some scholars point out that a woman's word counted for less in that world, which makes the detail stand out. And the men, one Gospel says, didn't believe them at first: the women's words seemed like idle tales. In another telling, Mary Magdalene stays behind at the tomb, crying, and meets the risen Jesus himself, and doesn't recognize him until he says her name. Then he sends her to tell the others. That's why Catholic tradition calls her the apostle to the apostles: the one sent to the ones who would be sent. Catholics believe the resurrection is the center of everything: that Jesus truly rose from the dead, and that death is not the end of the story. It's why, as day fourteen said, Christians gather on Sunday: every Sunday keeps a little of Easter. Easter itself isn't just one day. It's a season of fifty days, longer than Lent. In Spanish, Italian and French, the feast's name comes from Pascha, the Passover: Pascua, Pasqua, Pâques. And look at the order of that morning. The women worried about the stone the whole way there. By the time they arrived, it had already been moved.","screen":["`HE IS NOT HERE, BUT IS RISEN.` · `WOMEN WERE THE FIRST WITNESSES.` · `THE APOSTLE TO THE APOSTLES.` · `FIFTY DAYS, NOT ONE.` · then, large: `THE STONE IS ALREADY MOVED.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Think of one stone you're worried about: something ahead that feels too heavy to move. A conversation, a bill, a diagnosis, a decision. Picture yourself walking toward it anyway, like the women did, without a plan. Breathe slowly as you walk. You don't have to solve it. Just keep walking. If you pray, you can bring it to God.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Easter. The feast of the resurrection, the empty tomb, and the women who were sent to tell. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`EASTER — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: the stone is already moved. The women only found out because they went anyway. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE RICH KID WHO GAVE HIS CLOTHES BACK.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"the rich kid who gave his clothes back","word":"Francis","hook":"the rich kid who gave his clothes back","length":"6:30","carry":"lord, make me an instrument","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Easter, the women, and the stone already moved. Today, one of the saints from day five, up close. Day seventeen.","screen":["`THE STONE IS ALREADY MOVED.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Italy, the Middle Ages. A town on a hill called Assisi. There's a young man there everyone knows: the son of a rich cloth merchant, good clothes, good parties, the one who pays for everybody's dinner. He dreams of being a knight. He goes off to a war with the next town, gets captured, spends a long stretch in a prison cell, and comes home sick. And something in him has changed. He starts giving things away. Money, cloth from his father's shop. His father is furious, and drags him in front of the bishop of Assisi, in the town square, to get his property back. What the son does next is why people still know his name.","screen":["`ASSISI, A TOWN ON A HILL.` → `THE RICH MERCHANT'S SON.` → `DRAGGED BEFORE THE BISHOP.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"His name was Francesco. We say Francis. According to the stories written down by his early followers, he stood in front of the bishop and the whole town, took off his clothes, every piece, and handed them back to his father. And he said, in effect: until now I called you my father. From now on I'll say our Father, who art in heaven. The bishop covered him with his own cloak. Francis walked out of town with nothing. What came next is the part people forget. He didn't become a hermit. He went toward the people everyone avoided. The stories say that once, riding outside town, he met a man with leprosy, a disease that made people outcasts, and instead of riding past, he got down and kissed his hand. Later he wrote that what had seemed bitter to him was turned into sweetness. He gathered friends who lived poor, preached in the streets and cared for the sick. They became the Franciscans, who still carry his name. Near the end of his life he wrote a song, the Canticle of the Creatures, praising God for brother sun and sister moon, even sister death. Now today's line: lord, make me an instrument. It opens the prayer that bears his name, make me an instrument of your peace. Here's the honest part. As far as anyone can tell, Francis didn't write it. Its first known printing is in a small French magazine in 1912, with no author's name. His name was attached to it later. Francis's own words are in the Canticle.","screen":["`HE GAVE THE CLOTHES BACK.` · `TOWARD THE PEOPLE EVERYONE AVOIDED.` · `BROTHER SUN, SISTER MOON.` · `THE PRAYER HE PROBABLY DIDN'T WRITE.` · then, large: `MAKE ME AN INSTRUMENT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Get comfortable and let your breath slow down. Think of one thing you're holding on to a little too tightly. It doesn't have to be big. A grudge counts. With each breath out, imagine loosening your grip on it, the way Francis handed his clothes back. If you pray, you can say the line: lord, make me an instrument.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Francis. Francis of Assisi, the rich kid who gave it all back and went toward the outcasts. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`FRANCIS — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: lord, make me an instrument. The prayer borrowed his name; the life was his own. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE WORD POPE FRANCIS KEPT SAYING.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"the word Pope Francis kept saying","word":"mercy","hook":"the word Pope Francis kept saying","length":"6:30","carry":"start with yourself","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Francis of Assisi, the rich kid who gave it all back. Your line was lord, make me an instrument. Today, the pope who took his name. Day eighteen.","screen":["`MAKE ME AN INSTRUMENT.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"In 2013, a man from Buenos Aires who used to ride the bus to work became pope and took a name no pope had ever used: Francis, after the saint from Assisi you met yesterday. One word kept coming up, in sermons, in interviews, off the cuff to crowds. He called a whole year after it. He titled a book after it. Asked once, early on, to describe himself, he said, in effect: a sinner the Lord has looked upon. Pope Francis led the Church until his death in 2025. Today, his word: mercy.","screen":["`A POPE WHO RODE THE BUS.` → `ONE WORD KEPT COMING UP.` → `A SINNER THE LORD LOOKED UPON.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"The Latin word is misericordia. Inside it are two smaller words: miser, wretched, in trouble, and cor, the heart. Mercy is a heart that goes out to someone in misery. Not a verdict. A heart. The Bible's Hebrew has a word for it that comes from the word for a mother's womb, the kind of love you feel for a child you carried. Luke's Gospel puts it at the center of what Jesus asks, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"Be ye therefore merciful, as your Father also is merciful.\" Pope Francis took his motto from an old sermon by Bede, an English monk, about the day Jesus called Matthew, a tax collector everyone despised. Jesus looked at him, Bede says, with mercy, and chose him. In Latin: miserando atque eligendo. By having mercy, and by choosing. Francis said that was his own story. That's where today's line comes from. Francis kept saying that people who can't show mercy have often never let themselves receive it. You can't give away what you've never been handed. So the first step is to accept it: to let God, or someone, look at you with mercy and choose you anyway, the way Jesus looked at Matthew. For some people, the hardest one to show mercy to is themselves. Then it flows outward: forgiving, feeding, visiting, going toward people in trouble. Catholics call these the works of mercy. Francis of Assisi kissing the leper's hand was the same heart. And confession, from day three, is the Church saying it out loud: you're forgiven.","screen":["`MISERICORDIA — A HEART FOR THE WRETCHED.` · `BE YE THEREFORE MERCIFUL.` · `BY HAVING MERCY, AND BY CHOOSING.` · `YOU CAN'T GIVE WHAT YOU WERE NEVER HANDED.` · then, large: `START WITH YOURSELF.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Bring to mind one thing you haven't quite forgiven yourself for, if there is one. Something small is fine. Picture someone who loves you looking at it, and at you, without flinching. Not excusing it. Just looking at you with a heart that goes out to you. Breathe slowly, and let yourself be looked at like that. If you pray, you can let that look be God's.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Mercy. Misericordia: a heart that goes out to someone in trouble, received first, then given away. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`MERCY — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: start with yourself. Mercy received is the only kind you can pass on. Day eighteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: ONE, HOLY, CATHOLIC.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"one, holy, catholic","word":"the Church","hook":"one, holy, catholic — the small-c word means universal","length":"6:30","carry":"everywhere at once","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: mercy, a heart that goes out to someone in trouble. Your line was start with yourself. Today, the small word in the Church's own name. Day nineteen.","screen":["`START WITH YOURSELF.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"A Catholic Mass in Lagos. In Manila. In a tiny village in Poland. In a strip-mall parish in Texas. The language is different. The music is wildly different: drums, organs, guitars, choirs in five-part harmony. But a regular would know where they were almost at once. The readings, the peace, the table. The same shape, in a language you don't speak. Every Sunday, Catholics say a creed that calls the Church one, holy, catholic and apostolic. Today, the small-c word in that line, and what it actually means.","screen":["`LAGOS, MANILA, POLAND, TEXAS.` → `THE SAME SHAPE.` → `ONE, HOLY, CATHOLIC.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Start with church. In the New Testament the Greek word is ekklesia, from a verb meaning to call out. It meant an assembly, people called out of their houses to gather. So the Church, first of all, isn't a building. It's the people who were called. Now the small-c word. Catholic comes from the Greek katholikos, from kath' holou: according to the whole. Universal. Early in Christian history, a bishop named Ignatius of Antioch was being taken to Rome under guard, to be executed. On the way he wrote letters to the churches. In one of them he writes that wherever Jesus Christ is, there is the catholic Church. He meant the whole Church, everywhere, gathered around Christ. The creed said at Sunday Mass puts four words side by side. One: many countries, one faith. Holy: set apart for God, even though, as day five said, its people are far from perfect. Catholic: for everyone, everywhere. Apostolic: going back to the apostles. Catholics believe their Church is one family under the pope, the bishop of Rome, successor of Peter. And that family is more varied than it looks. Alongside the Latin Church there are Eastern Catholic Churches, with their own liturgies and traditions, united with Rome. Other Christians say these creed words too. Orthodox Christians say catholic and mean the Orthodox Church. In Protestant churches that say the creed, it usually means the whole Church of Christ across every denomination. For Catholics, Ignatius's line still sets the size of the word: wherever Christ is.","screen":["`EKKLESIA — THE ONES CALLED OUT.` · `KATHOLIKOS — ACCORDING TO THE WHOLE.` · `IGNATIUS, ON THE ROAD TO ROME.` · `ONE, HOLY, CATHOLIC, APOSTOLIC.` · then, large: `EVERYWHERE AT ONCE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Picture the globe, slowly turning. Somewhere it's morning, and people are walking into a church. Somewhere it's midnight, and someone is praying alone by a candle. Somewhere a baby is being baptized, and somewhere a funeral is ending. Let the picture be as big as it wants to be. You're one person on it, breathing. If you pray, you can pray for all of them at once.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Church. The ekklesia, the people called out, and catholic, meaning according to the whole: universal. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`THE CHURCH — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: everywhere at once. Lagos, Manila, Texas, and wherever you're sitting. Day nineteen, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE SAINT WHO WROTE ABOUT PRAYER LIKE A FRIENDSHIP.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"the saint who wrote about prayer like a friendship","word":"Teresa of Ávila","hook":"the saint who wrote about prayer like a friendship","length":"6:30","carry":"talk to him like a friend","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Church, the ones called out, and catholic, meaning universal. Your line was everywhere at once. Today, one woman in one walled Spanish city. Day twenty.","screen":["`EVERYWHERE AT ONCE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Sit down to pray, or just to be quiet, and within a minute the mind has gone to lunch, or an argument from last week, or whether the car is locked. A woman in Spain, long ago, admitted in writing that for years prayer went like that for her. She would listen for the clock to strike, wishing the hour were over. She was a nun, and prayer was her whole job. Later the Church would call her a teacher of prayer for everyone.","screen":["`SIT DOWN TO PRAY.` → `WITHIN A MINUTE: LUNCH.` → `SHE LISTENED FOR THE CLOCK.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Her name was Teresa, from the walled city of Ávila in Spain. She grew up in a big, lively family, and as a girl, by her own account, she cared more for books of knights and adventure, and for nice clothes, than for holiness. As a young woman she entered a Carmelite convent. And for years, she says in her autobiography, prayer was a struggle. Distracted, dry, going through the motions. Then, after a long time, something changed, and the way she described prayer afterward is the reason she's still read. Prayer, as she understands it, is nothing else than friendship: spending time often, alone, with the one we know loves us. Not a performance. Not a speech. Time with a friend. She wrote the way she talked: funny, blunt, full of kitchen pictures. She said God walks among the pots and pans. In another book, she pictures the soul as a castle made of a single diamond, with many rooms, and God living in the center. Prayer is the door you go in by. She also got a lot done. She founded new convents across Spain, traveling on bad roads in rough carts, arguing with bishops, and working with a young friar we know as John of the Cross. There's a story people love to tell: that after a cart tipped her into a muddy river, she complained to God, if this is how you treat your friends, no wonder you have so few. Nobody knows whether she really said it. But it sounds exactly like someone who talked to God like a friend. Long after her death, the Church named her a Doctor of the Church: a teacher for everyone.","screen":["`ÁVILA. BOOKS OF KNIGHTS.` · `YEARS OF DRY PRAYER.` · `PRAYER IS FRIENDSHIP.` · `A CASTLE WITH MANY ROOMS.` · then, large: `TALK TO HIM LIKE A FRIEND.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice, with no speech to give. Imagine a friend sitting across from you, one who knows you well and likes you anyway. Tell them, silently, one plain thing about your day. Not the polished version. Then just sit together without talking. When your mind wanders, and it will, come back the way you'd turn back to a friend after a distraction. If you pray, you can make that friend God.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Teresa of Ávila. The Carmelite nun who struggled with prayer for years, then described it as friendship with the one who loves us. Twentieth word.","screen":["`TERESA OF ÁVILA — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: talk to him like a friend. No speech, no polish: just time together. Day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"the mountain ahead","word":"the Psalms","hook":"the mountain ahead; the prayers Jesus himself prayed","length":"6:30","carry":"150 doors","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Teresa of Ávila, and prayer as friendship. Your line was talk to him like a friend. Today is the last day of camp one. Day twenty-one.","screen":["`TALK TO HIM LIKE A FRIEND.` → `THE LAST DAY OF CAMP ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Look back down the hill for a second. Three weeks ago you started with the sign of the cross. Then the Eucharist. Confession. The rosary. The saints. Lent. Sacramentals. Grace. Mary. The Hail Mary. The Our Father. The Gospel. The sign of peace. Sunday. Candles. Easter. Francis. Mercy. The Church. Teresa. Twenty words: the words a Catholic hears every week. Now look up. There's a mountain ahead, and it's made of stories. But first, one more word, and it's the prayer book Jesus himself grew up praying.","screen":["`TWENTY WORDS, DOWN THE HILL.` → `THE WORDS OF EVERY WEEK.` → `A MOUNTAIN MADE OF STORIES.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"The Psalms are a book of one hundred and fifty songs and prayers in the middle of the Bible. The Hebrew name is Tehillim: praises. Our word psalm comes from Greek, a song sung to a plucked string. They were Israel's prayer book and hymnal, and Jesus, a Jewish boy in Nazareth, likely grew up hearing them. Here's what surprises people: they aren't all nice. Some are joyful. Some are furious. Some are people in the dark asking God where he's gone. On the cross, in Matthew's Gospel, Jesus cries out the first line of one of those, in the Douay-Rheims Bible: \"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\" That's a psalm. In that hour, he prays in words that were already old. And in another Gospel, his last words are from a psalm too: \"Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit.\" One detail, if you ever look them up in an older Catholic Bible: the numbers are often off by one. The Douay-Rheims follows the ancient Greek and Latin count, so the psalm Jesus cried on the cross is Psalm 21 there, and Psalm 22 in Bibles that follow the Hebrew. Same words, different count. The Church still prays them every day. At every Mass there's a psalm between the readings, the one from day twelve, sung back and forth. Priests, monks and nuns pray the psalms through the day in what's called the Liturgy of the Hours. And the rosary from day four, the poor man's psalter, grew up in their shadow. One hundred and fifty psalms, one for nearly every feeling a person can have. One hundred and fifty doors.","screen":["`TEHILLIM — PRAISES.` · `NOT ALL OF THEM NICE.` · `MY GOD, WHY HAST THOU FORSAKEN ME?` · `THE NUMBERS ARE OFF BY ONE.` · then, large: `150 DOORS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Here's the practice for the end of camp one. Ask yourself what you're actually feeling today, honestly. Glad. Tired. Angry. Lost. Grateful. Whatever it is, the Psalms have a door for it, and you don't have to clean it up first. Name the feeling in one word. Then breathe with it, not against it. If you pray, you can hand God that one word, just as it is.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Psalms. One hundred and fifty praises, laments and songs, the prayer book Jesus himself prayed. Twenty-first word, and the last of camp one.","screen":["`THE PSALMS — your 21st word` → the strand complete → `CAMP 1. FIRST WORDS. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: 150 doors. Tomorrow, camp two: the stories, starting with two pregnant women. Day twenty-one.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: TWO PREGNANT WOMEN (LUKE 1:39–56).`"]}]}},"CHRISTIANITY":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"what the bread and the cup are doing","word":"communion","hook":"a small square of bread and a tiny cup, passed down the row","length":"3:15","carry":"a meal is a memory","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A tray comes down the row. A small square of bread, or a thin wafer. Then a second tray, full of tiny cups: wine in some churches, grape juice in others. Some people eat right away. Some hold it and wait for everyone. Nobody stops to explain it, and somehow everybody knows what to do. All of it goes back to one dinner, on the night before Jesus died.","screen":["`A TRAY COMES DOWN THE ROW.` → `BREAD. A TINY CUP.` → `ONE DINNER, THE NIGHT BEFORE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"The night before he was executed, Jesus ate a Passover meal with his friends. He took bread, gave thanks, broke it, and gave it to them: this is my body. Then the cup. Both times, one request: \"this do in remembrance of me.\" Here's the surprise. Those words aren't only in the Gospels. A letter Paul wrote to a young church has them too, and it may be older than any Gospel. His word for remembrance is anamnesis, and many Christians hear more in it than a memory in your head: remembering with your hands, so the thing is present again. That's why the meal has more than one name. Communion means sharing: one loaf, many people. The Eucharist comes from the Greek for giving thanks, the first thing he did. Some churches call it the Lord's Supper. And here churches honestly differ. Catholic and Orthodox Christians believe Christ is truly present in the bread and wine. Many Protestant churches see them as signs, with the remembering at the heart. Others hold careful words of their own.","screen":["`THE NIGHT BEFORE HE DIED.` · `ANAMNESIS — REMEMBERING.` · `COMMUNION. EUCHARIST. SUPPER.` · `WHO BELIEVES WHAT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Picture a table you've shared: a holiday, a kitchen, a last dinner before someone moved away. See one face at it. Breathe in. On the way out, let yourself remember them, not just think about them.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Communion. Sharing: one loaf, many people, and a meal that remembers. First word on your strand.","screen":["`COMMUNION — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: a meal is a memory. The tray that came down the row was carrying one. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE THREE NAMES YOU SAY IN ONE BREATH.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"the three names you say in one breath","word":"the Trinity","hook":"Father, Son and Holy Spirit: said at every baptism, rarely explained","length":"3:15","carry":"three, and still one","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: communion, the meal that remembers. Today, three names many Christians say in a single breath.","screen":["`COMMUNION ✓` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"In the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. It's said over a baby at a baptism, at the end of a service, with a hand moving forehead, chest, shoulder, shoulder. Ask someone who has said it their whole life what it means, though, and you may get a long pause, and then a picture of a shamrock. First, a guess.","screen":["`FATHER. SON. HOLY SPIRIT.` → `SAID A THOUSAND TIMES.` → `EXPLAINED?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Christians say there is one God, and that God is Father, Son and Holy Spirit. Three persons, one God. Not three gods. Not one God wearing three masks. The word Trinity isn't in the Bible. In the early 200s, a North African writer named Tertullian used the Latin trinitas to name something Christians saw all through the Gospels: Jesus praying to the Father, and promising to send the Spirit. At the end of one Gospel, he sends his friends out baptizing in the name, singular, of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. One name. Three. Every easy picture breaks somewhere. Water, ice and steam make it one God changing forms, an idea early Christians turned down. A shamrock makes each person a third of God. So some teachers, as far back as Augustine, start somewhere else. One letter in the Bible says simply: God is love. And love needs someone to love. Many Christians hear the Trinity that way: at the very center of God, a relationship.","screen":["`ONE GOD, THREE PERSONS.` · `THE WORD CAME LATER.` · `IN THE NAME — ONE NAME, THREE.` · `EVERY EASY PICTURE BREAKS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Breathe in, and think of someone who loves you. Breathe out, and think of someone you love. Then notice the love between you: something real, though you can't hold it in your hand.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The Trinity. One God, three persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. Second word on your strand.","screen":["`THE TRINITY — your 2nd word` → clicks onto the strand beside communion."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: three, and still one. It's why that blessing says in the name, not in the names. Day two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE VISITOR WHO CAME AT NIGHT.`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"the visitor who came at night","word":"born again","hook":"the altar call at camp, and a phrase that's older than any altar call","length":"3:30","carry":"never too old to start again","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Trinity, three and still one. Today, a phrase you've heard about people, and maybe about yourself.","screen":["`THREE, AND STILL ONE.` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"The music slows down. Every head bowed, every eye closed. The speaker says: if you want to give your life to Jesus tonight, raise your hand, or come forward. Some people walked down that aisle at summer camp, some in a stadium, some never did. The phrase people used afterward was born again. That phrase is much older than any altar call. It comes from a conversation at night.","screen":["`EVERY HEAD BOWED.` → `COME FORWARD.` → `BORN AGAIN?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"A respected teacher named Nicodemus comes to Jesus after dark. Jesus tells him: \"Except a man be born again, he cannot see the kingdom of God.\" The Greek word for again, anothen, has a second meaning: from above. Nicodemus hears the first one and asks the obvious question: \"How can a man be born when he is old?\" Climb back into the womb? Jesus means the second: a birth from above, by God's Spirit, which he compares to the wind. You can't see where it comes from; you only hear it. A few lines later comes the verse on the signs at football games, John 3:16: \"For God so loved the world.\" The altar call itself is much newer. Revival preachers in the 1800s began inviting people to come forward, and big stadium crusades made it famous. Ask Jesus into your heart isn't a Bible phrase either; it's a simple way churches put it. And churches differ on how new birth happens: in a moment of decision, in baptism, over a lifetime. But notice the picture Jesus chose. Nobody gives birth to themselves.","screen":["`A TEACHER, AFTER DARK.` · `ANOTHEN — AGAIN, FROM ABOVE.` · `LIKE THE WIND.` · `THE ALTAR CALL CAME LATER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, think of something in your life that feels tired or stuck. On the second, notice you can't force it to be new, any more than a baby can give birth to itself. On the third, just leave a little room for something new to come.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Born again. In the Greek of the story, born from above, too. Third word.","screen":["`BORN AGAIN — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: never too old to start again. It answers the question Nicodemus asked that night. That's day three.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: UNDER THE WATER AND BACK UP.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"under the water and back up","word":"baptism","hook":"a baby in a white gown, a teenager in a pool: same word, an old disagreement","length":"3:30","carry":"under the water, up again","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: born again, born from above. Today, the water many churches tie to that new birth.","screen":["`BORN AGAIN ✓` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A baby in a long white gown, a little water poured over her head while the grandparents take photos. A teenager in a T-shirt, held by the shoulders and leaned back into a pool, coming up to cheering. Same word for both: baptism. Same faith. And a disagreement, centuries old, about when and how to do it. Both sides have their reasons.","screen":["`A WHITE GOWN.` → `A T-SHIRT, A POOL.` → `SAME WORD.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Baptism comes from the Greek baptizo: to dip, to plunge, to immerse. It's older than the church. A wild preacher named John was dipping people in the Jordan River as a sign of turning their lives around, and Jesus himself walked into that river and was baptized. Later he sent his friends out to baptize, and from the start, baptism was how someone joined. So why two pictures? Catholic, Orthodox, Lutheran, Anglican, Methodist and Presbyterian churches baptize babies. The idea: God's promise comes first, before the child can answer, and the family promises to raise them in it. Baptist, Pentecostal and many non-denominational churches baptize people old enough to say yes for themselves, usually all the way under. Paul's picture fits that well: down into the water like a burial, up like rising from the dead. Whether baptism itself saves is a real difference too. Many churches call it the moment God makes a person new. Many others call it an outward sign of something God has already done inside. Either way, the shape is the same.","screen":["`BAPTIZO — TO DIP, TO PLUNGE.` · `JOHN, THE JORDAN, JESUS.` · `BABIES, OR THOSE WHO CHOOSE.` · `A SIGN, OR THE MOMENT ITSELF.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, breathe all the way out, slowly, like going under. On the second, pause a moment in the stillness before you breathe in. On the third, breathe in deep, like coming up into the air. Notice how that first breath feels.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Baptism. From the Greek for dip or plunge: the way into the church, with water. Fourth word.","screen":["`BAPTISM — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: under the water, up again. A white gown or a T-shirt in a pool, it's the same picture. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: CHURCH WORDS NOBODY DEFINED.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"church words nobody defined","word":"testimony","hook":"quiet time, on fire, testimony: the church words you picked up like a family's inside jokes","length":"3:30","carry":"your story is a witness","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: baptism, under the water and up again. Today, the words people used around it, the ones nobody ever looked up.","screen":["`BAPTISM ✓` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Quiet time. On fire for God. A hedge of protection. Traveling mercies. If you grew up in church, you probably learned these the way you learn a family's inside jokes: by hearing them a lot. People even have a name for it: Christianese. Today, three of the most common, and what's actually behind them.","screen":["`QUIET TIME. ON FIRE.` → `TRAVELING MERCIES.` → `CHRISTIANESE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Quiet time means a few minutes alone each day to read the Bible and pray. It has a model. One Gospel says Jesus got up \"a great while before day\" and went off to a solitary place to pray. On fire means eager, all in. It echoes two friends on a road who said, after a stranger turned out to be Jesus, that their hearts had burned inside them while he talked. And testimony. At camp or youth group, someone stands by the bonfire and tells how they came to faith. Testimony is a courtroom word. In Greek it's martyria, from martys: a witness, someone who says what they saw. Early Christians who died rather than take back what they'd witnessed were called by that same word, and it came into English as martyr. So a testimony isn't a performance, and it isn't a sales pitch. It's a witness statement: here's what happened to me. Some people love telling theirs to a crowd. Plenty of Christians keep theirs for a friend, or for nobody but God.","screen":["`QUIET TIME — A HABIT OF JESUS.` · `ON FIRE — A BURNING HEART.` · `TESTIMONY — A WITNESS.` · `MARTYS — WITNESS, THEN MARTYR.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, think of one thing that really happened to you, something that changed you even a little. On the second, say to yourself, in one plain sentence, what happened. On the third, let it rest. A witness doesn't add anything.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Testimony. From the Greek for witness: here's what I saw happen. Fifth word.","screen":["`TESTIMONY — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: your story is a witness. A quiet one counts as much as a bonfire one. That's day five.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WIND, BREATH AND FIRE.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"wind, breath and fire","word":"the Holy Spirit","hook":"the third name nobody explained, and what speaking in tongues is","length":"3:30","carry":"closer than your own breath","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: testimony, a witness statement. Today, the third name in the blessing from day two.","screen":["`YOUR STORY IS A WITNESS.` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Of the three names, Father and Son come with pictures. The Holy Spirit can feel like the one nobody explained. Maybe you saw people speaking in tongues at a friend's church and wondered what was happening. Maybe you wondered why it never happened to you. The words underneath are plainer than you'd think: breath, and wind.","screen":["`THE THIRD NAME.` → `SPEAKING IN TONGUES?` → `BREATH. WIND.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"In Hebrew the word is ruach; in Greek, pneuma. Both mean breath, wind and spirit, all at once. On the Bible's first page, the spirit of God, or the wind of God, moves over the waters. In John's Gospel, the risen Jesus breathes on his friends and says: receive the Holy Spirit. He calls the Spirit the Paraclete: the one called alongside you. A helper, an advocate, someone in your corner. Then fire. Fifty days after Passover, at the festival of Pentecost, his friends hear a sound like a rushing wind, something like flames rests on each of them, and they speak in languages the visiting crowd can understand. Speaking in tongues today is understood in different ways. Pentecostal and charismatic churches treasure it as a living gift, often as a way of praying. Some churches believe that gift belonged mainly to the first generation; many others are open to it but rarely practice it. And Paul, writing to a church that prized it, asks: \"do all speak with tongues?\" He expects the answer no. His list of what the Spirit grows is quieter: love, joy, peace, patience, kindness.","screen":["`RUACH, PNEUMA — BREATH, WIND.` · `THE ONE CALLED ALONGSIDE.` · `PENTECOST — WIND AND FIRE.` · `DO ALL SPEAK WITH TONGUES?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, just notice the air: in, and out. On the second, remember that in the old languages, breath and spirit are one word. On the third, breathe slowly, and let it be enough.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The Holy Spirit. Ruach, pneuma: breath, wind, spirit, the helper called alongside. Sixth word.","screen":["`THE HOLY SPIRIT — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: closer than your own breath. In the old languages, it's the same word. Day six, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: A LIBRARY THAT TELLS ONE STORY.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"a library that tells one story","word":"the Bible","hook":"not one book but a shelf of them, read as one story from a garden to a city","length":"3:30","carry":"one story, many books","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Holy Spirit, breath and wind and fire. Six words so far: communion, the Trinity, born again, baptism, testimony, the Holy Spirit. Today, the seventh, where all of them come from.","screen":["`COMMUNION · TRINITY · BORN AGAIN · BAPTISM · TESTIMONY · HOLY SPIRIT`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"It's in a hotel drawer, on a phone app, in a grandmother's lap with the pages gone soft. It's easy to picture one thick book. Open the table of contents, though, and it's a whole shelf: poems, letters, laws, family stories, songs of complaint, four lives of Jesus. Written over many centuries, by many hands. And Christians read it as one story.","screen":["`A HOTEL DRAWER. A PHONE.` → `ONE BOOK?` → `A WHOLE SHELF.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"The word Bible comes from the Greek ta biblia: the books. Plural. A library. If you've ever started on page one and gotten lost somewhere in the laws, that's why: nobody reads a library front to back, and a lot of people start with one of the Gospels instead. Protestant Bibles have sixty-six books, Catholic Bibles seventy-three, Orthodox Bibles a few more. There are two big parts. The Old Testament is the Jewish scriptures, the ones Jesus himself read. The New Testament was written by his first followers. Testament means covenant: a binding promise. Here's a surprise. The chapter and verse numbers aren't original. Chapters came in the 1200s, and the New Testament's verse numbers in the 1500s, so for most of Christian history nobody could look up day three's verse as John 3:16. Then the shape. Many Christians tell it in a few big moves: a world made good, a family chosen, slaves set free, kings and prophets, exile and return, then Jesus, and his followers sent out. And the bookends. Near the very start there's a garden with a tree of life. On the last page, the tree of life is back, growing beside a river in a city made new. The story ends where it began.","screen":["`TA BIBLIA — THE BOOKS.` · `TESTAMENT — A PROMISE.` · `THE NUMBERS CAME LATER.` · `A GARDEN, THEN A CITY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, think of where your own story started. On the second, think of a chapter that was hard. On the third, picture one thing you hope gets made new. Three breaths, one story.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The Bible. Ta biblia, the books: a library Christians read as one story. Seventh word, and a week's worth.","screen":["`THE BIBLE — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one story, many books. That's week one: seven words, all from that one shelf. Tomorrow, a word you've said a thousand times.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: YOU'VE SAID IT A THOUSAND TIMES.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"you've said it a thousand times","word":"amen","hook":"you've said it a thousand times — at a table, at a funeral","length":"5:00","carry":"let it be so","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Bible. Your line was one story, many books. Week one is behind you. Week two opens with a short word, the one that ends so many prayers.","screen":["`ONE STORY, MANY BOOKS.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"At the end of grace, right before anyone's allowed to eat. At a funeral, quietly, along with everyone else. Amen to that, as a joke. You've said it more times than you could count, and it's easy to hear it as the end: the prayer's over, you can open your eyes. It means something closer to the opposite.","screen":["`AMEN.` → `YOU'VE SAID IT A THOUSAND TIMES.` → `IT DOESN'T MEAN THE END.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Amen is Hebrew, from a root, aman, that means firm. Solid. The kind of thing you can put your weight on. The same root gives Hebrew its words for faithfulness and trust. So amen isn't a sign-off. It's yes. That's solid. So be it. It's old. In one scene in the books of Moses, a list of rules is read aloud to a whole people, and after each one: \"And all the people shall answer and say, Amen.\" A room answering yes. Then a detail that's easy to miss. In the Gospels, Jesus puts the word in a strange place: at the start. The old English wording is \"Verily, verily, I say unto you.\" Underneath verily is amen, amen. Usually amen came after, to agree with something already said. He said it first, vouching for his words before he spoke them. And the last book of the Bible goes further still. It calls him \"the Amen, the faithful and true witness.\" Not just someone who says yes. The yes itself.","screen":["`AMEN — FIRM. SOLID.` · `NOT GOODBYE. YES.` · `THE ROOM ANSWERS.` · `HE SAID IT FIRST.` · then, large: `THE YES ITSELF.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and let your breathing settle. Bring to mind one thing that's true and good in your life right now, something solid enough to put your weight on. Stay with it. Each time you breathe out, let the answer be yes. If you pray, you can let that yes be an amen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Amen. Firm, truly, so be it: a yes, not a goodbye. Eighth word.","screen":["`AMEN — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: let it be so. Next time a room says it, you'll know what everyone is agreeing to. Day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: A GIFT YOU DIDN'T EARN.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"a gift you didn't earn","word":"grace","hook":"a gift you didn't earn; your grandmother said it before dinner","length":"5:15","carry":"you are already held","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: amen, and your line was let it be so. Today, the prayer that comes right before so many of those amens.","screen":["`AMEN — LET IT BE SO.` → `DAY 9.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"The food is on the table, everybody's hungry, and somebody's grandmother says: wait. We haven't said grace. Heads go down. A few words, maybe the same ones she's said every night for years. Then amen, and the forks move. Saying grace meant saying thanks. But Christians use the word for something much larger: a gift you didn't earn.","screen":["`WE HAVEN'T SAID GRACE.` → `NOT JUST THANKS.` → `A GIFT YOU DIDN'T EARN.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"The Greek word in the New Testament is charis: a gift, a favor, kindness given freely. It's where English gets charisma, a gift someone just has. And it hides inside a word from day one: Eucharist, thanksgiving. In Greek, the gift and the thank-you belong to one family. Now the part that cuts against the grain. So much of life runs on earning. You work, you get paid. You study, you pass. Grace doesn't work like that. One of Paul's letters says it plainly: \"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God.\" Not a wage. A gift. Paul also describes a problem he begged the Lord three times to take away. He never says what it was. The answer he says he got was this: \"My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness.\" Not the problem removed. Enough, given right inside it. Churches have long argued about how grace reaches a person: through the sacraments, through faith, through both. The word itself keeps its meaning: given, not earned. The grandmother at the table wasn't paying for dinner. She was saying: this came to us. Thank you.","screen":["`CHARIS — A GIFT, FREELY GIVEN.` · `IN GREEK, GIFT AND THANKS ARE KIN.` · `NOT OF YOURSELVES.` · `ENOUGH, INSIDE THE PROBLEM.` · then, large: `GIVEN, NOT EARNED.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and notice your breathing. You didn't make this breath. It just came, the way it's been coming all day while you weren't paying attention. Let each breath in be a gift you didn't earn. Let each breath out be a thank-you, said inside or not said at all.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Grace. Charis: a gift you didn't earn, freely given. Ninth word.","screen":["`GRACE — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: you are already held. That's what grace says, before anyone has earned anything. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: A CARPENTER'S SON FROM A NOWHERE TOWN, NOT A STAINED-GLASS WINDOW.`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"a carpenter's son from a nowhere town, not a stained-glass window","word":"Jesus","hook":"a carpenter's son from a nowhere town, not a stained-glass window","length":"5:30","carry":"he ate with the wrong people on purpose","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: grace, a gift you didn't earn. Today, the name at the center of everything on this path.","screen":["`GRACE — GIVEN, NOT EARNED.` → `DAY 10.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"35 sec","voice":"A stained-glass window, lit from behind. A painting with a golden circle around his head. A calm face on a card in a hospital waiting room. Those are the usual pictures of Jesus. Now try a different one. A man with rough hands, dust from his trade on his clothes. A family the neighbors knew by name. When he started to teach, the neighbors weren't impressed. They were confused. Isn't that just the builder?","screen":["`NOT A STAINED-GLASS WINDOW.` → `ROUGH HANDS. A KNOWN FAMILY.` → `ISN'T THAT JUST THE BUILDER?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Start with the name. Jesus is the English form of a Greek name, which comes from the Hebrew Yeshua, the same name as Joshua. It means, roughly, God saves. It wasn't a rare name; one of Paul's letters mentions another man called Jesus. Then the job. When he comes home to teach, the neighbors ask, in the old English: \"Is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary?\" The Greek word is tekton: a builder, a craftsman. It could mean wood, and it could mean stone. Either way, a working man with a trade. Then the town. Nazareth, in Galilee, a place nobody in the story expects much from. When one man first hears where Jesus is from, he says: \"Can there any good thing come out of Nazareth?\" His friend answers: \"Come and see.\" But in Mark's Gospel, what gets people talking isn't where he came from. It's who he eats with. He sits down at the house of a tax collector, at a table full of people others avoided. Some religious teachers ask his followers how he can eat with tax collectors and sinners. He answers: healthy people don't need a doctor. Sick people do. The meal itself is the message. Christians say much more about Jesus: that he is the Son of God, that he died and rose again. The path gets to all of that. It starts at a table.","screen":["`YESHUA — THE NAME JOSHUA.` · `TEKTON — A BUILDER.` · `NAZARETH — COME AND SEE.` · `A TABLE WITH THE WRONG PEOPLE.` · then, large: `IT STARTS AT A TABLE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and picture a table you've eaten at. Notice who usually sits there. Now picture one person who usually doesn't: someone you'd never think to invite. Give them a chair, just in your mind, and stay with the fuller table for a while. If you pray, you can bring them into it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Jesus. Yeshua: God saves. A builder's name from a small town, at the center of the whole Christian path. Tenth word.","screen":["`JESUS — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: he ate with the wrong people on purpose. In the Gospels, that's what got him talked about. Day ten.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE PRAYER A ROOM SAYS WITHOUT A PAGE.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"the prayer a room says without a page","word":"the Lord's Prayer","hook":"\"Our Father\": the family word, plain asks, and two endings","length":"5:30","carry":"you're not addressing a stranger","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Jesus, the builder who ate with the wrong people on purpose. Today, the prayer he taught his friends.","screen":["`JESUS — THE WRONG PEOPLE, ON PURPOSE.` → `DAY 11.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"35 sec","voice":"Weddings, funerals, a locker room before a big game. Our Father, who art in heaven. A whole room can say it without anyone holding a page. Then, near the end, a small awkward moment. Some people stop at deliver us from evil. Others keep going: for thine is the kingdom. Nobody got it wrong. Here's why, and what the very first word is doing.","screen":["`A PRAYER SAID BY HEART.` → `SOME STOP. SOME KEEP GOING.` → `NOBODY GOT IT WRONG.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"It opens, in the old English: \"Our Father which art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.\" Start with Father. Jesus spoke Aramaic, and once, praying the night before he died, a Gospel keeps his own word: Abba. The family word, what a child says at home and a grown son still says to his dad. You may have heard it means daddy; scholars push back, since adults used it too. But it isn't a courtroom title. Then our. Not my. The whole prayer never once says I, me or my. Even said alone in a quiet room, it assumes a family around you. Then come the asks: that God's name be honored, that his kingdom come on earth as in heaven, bread for today, forgiveness as we forgive, and rescue from evil. One of those lines changes from church to church. Some say forgive us our debts, the older wording of the Gospel. Others say trespasses, from an old English prayer book. And the ending. The oldest copies of the Gospel stop at deliver us from evil. An early Christian handbook has a line of praise after it, and later copies carry the longer ending. So Catholics usually stop at evil, and at Mass the praise comes a moment later. Protestants usually say it straight through to the amen.","screen":["`ABBA — THE FAMILY WORD.` · `OUR, NOT MY.` · `DEBTS OR TRESPASSES?` · `TWO ENDINGS, BOTH OLD.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and let your breathing settle. Think of the word our, and let one person you love come to mind. Then widen it to the people you'll pass today, strangers included. If you pray, you can say the opening line slowly: Our Father, which art in heaven, hallowed be thy name. If you don't, rest in the word our, and everyone it takes in.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"The Lord's Prayer. The family word, Abba, Father, then a few plain asks. Eleventh word.","screen":["`THE LORD'S PRAYER — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: you're not addressing a stranger. That's what the first word says. Day eleven, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHO YOUR NEIGHBOR TURNS OUT TO BE.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"who your neighbor turns out to be","word":"the Good Samaritan","hook":"a good Samaritan on the local news, and who the Samaritans actually were","length":"5:45","carry":"the neighbor is whoever is in front of you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Lord's Prayer, which opens with our, not my. Today, a story Jesus told when someone asked where our ends.","screen":["`OUR, NOT MY.` → `DAY 12.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"A good Samaritan stops at a car crash, and it's on the local news. There are laws named after the phrase, and hospitals. Hardly anyone who uses it knows who the Samaritans were, or why, to the first people who heard it, a good Samaritan sounded almost like a contradiction. It starts with a man who knew his Bible very well. He and Jesus agree on the heart of it: love God with everything, and love your neighbor as yourself. Then the man asks the follow-up everybody quietly wants to ask. \"And who is my neighbour?\"","screen":["`A GOOD SAMARITAN, ON THE NEWS.` → `WHO WERE THE SAMARITANS?` → `\"AND WHO IS MY NEIGHBOUR?\"`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 50","voice":"Jesus answers with a story, in Luke's Gospel. \"A certain man went down from Jerusalem to Jericho, and fell among thieves.\" They strip him, beat him and leave him half dead in the ditch. A priest comes down the road, sees him, and passes by on the other side. Then a Levite, who served in the Temple, does the same. The story never says why. They're characters in a story, not a verdict on anyone's faith; Jesus and the man asking him were Jews too. Then a third traveler: a Samaritan. Samaritans and Judeans were related peoples who shared the books of Moses, but they worshipped on different mountains and had centuries of bad blood between them. So the hero of the story comes from across the line. And he's the one who stops. The old English says \"he had compassion on him.\" He bandages the wounds, pours in oil and wine, puts the man on his own animal, takes him to an inn, and pays for his stay, with a promise to cover whatever else it costs. Then the twist. The man had asked: who counts? Jesus turns it around: \"Which now of these three, thinkest thou, was neighbour unto him that fell among the thieves?\" Not who qualifies. Who acted like one. The man answers, \"He that shewed mercy on him.\" And Jesus says: \"Go, and do thou likewise.\"","screen":["`A MAN IN THE DITCH.` · `TWO PASS BY.` · `THE ONE FROM ACROSS THE LINE STOPS.` · `NOT WHO QUALIFIES. WHO ACTED.` · then, large: `GO, AND DO THOU LIKEWISE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and let a breath out. Think back over this week and find one moment when someone needed something and you passed by on the other side: a text you didn't answer, a coworker who looked lost. No need to judge yourself. Everyone passes by sometimes. Just see the moment clearly, and picture what stopping would have looked like. If you pray, you can hold that person in prayer. If you don't, hold them in mind.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"The Good Samaritan. The man from across the line who stopped, and turned the question from who counts to who acts. Twelfth word.","screen":["`THE GOOD SAMARITAN — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the neighbor is whoever is in front of you. Not who's close. Who's there. That's day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE FATHER RUNS.`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"the father runs","word":"the Prodigal Son","hook":"the father runs — old men didn't run","length":"5:45","carry":"you can come home","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Good Samaritan, the man from across the line who stopped. Today, another story Jesus told, about a road home.","screen":["`THE GOOD SAMARITAN ✓` → `DAY 13.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"35 sec","voice":"A grown son is walking up a dusty road toward the house he grew up in. He left badly. He took money that wasn't really his yet, and it's gone. Now he's broke, and he's practicing a speech in his head, the way you might rehearse a hard phone call. He isn't hoping to be forgiven. He's hoping to be hired. And while he's still far off, before he can say a word, he sees his father, an old man, running toward him.","screen":["`A SON WHO LEFT BADLY.` → `A SPEECH, REHEARSED.` → `AN OLD MAN, RUNNING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 55","voice":"It's in Luke's Gospel, and it's usually called the Prodigal Son. Prodigal isn't in the story. It's an old word for wasteful, and the name came later. A man has two sons. The younger asks for his share of the inheritance now, while his father is still alive; some readers hear in that: I wish you were already gone. The father gives it to him. The son goes off to a far country and spends every coin. Then a famine hits, and he ends up feeding pigs, hungry enough to envy what they eat. Then, in the old English, \"when he came to himself,\" he rehearses his speech: I've sinned; make me one of your hired workers. Now the moment. \"But when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him.\" Some scholars of the period say a dignified older man didn't run in public. The text only says he ran. Had compassion is the same Greek word Luke uses for yesterday's Samaritan: his gut turned over. The son starts the speech, and the father never lets him reach the part about being a servant. He calls for the best robe, a ring, shoes and a feast. \"For this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found.\" But the older brother, who stayed and worked, hears the party and won't go in. The father goes out to him too, and pleads. The story ends there. We never find out if he goes in.","screen":["`HIS SHARE, NOW.` · `FEEDING PIGS, FAR FROM HOME.` · `THE FATHER RUNS.` · `THE OLDER BROTHER, OUTSIDE.` · then, large: `HE WAS LOST, AND IS FOUND.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and let a breath out. Think of a place, or a person, you've drifted from. Maybe you left badly. Maybe it just faded. Picture the road back. Now picture them seeing you from far off and coming toward you before you've said anything. Stay with that picture. If you pray, you can hold the father's words: he was lost, and is found. If you don't, just let the picture be.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"The Prodigal Son. The one who wasted everything, and the father who ran to meet him anyway. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`THE PRODIGAL SON — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: you can come home. Christians have long heard the father as a picture of God: running to the one who left, walking out to the one who stayed. Day thirteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"BLESSED ARE THE POOR IN SPIRIT.\"`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"\"blessed are the poor in spirit\"","word":"the Beatitudes","hook":"\"blessed are the poor in spirit\" — the upside-down list","length":"5:45","carry":"the last shall be first","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Prodigal Son, and a father running down the road. Your line was you can come home. Two weeks today.","screen":["`YOU CAN COME HOME.` → `TWO WEEKS.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"35 sec","voice":"Hashtag blessed. It turns up under the new car, the beach photo, the engagement ring. Now a hillside in Galilee. Crowds have followed a teacher out of the towns, many of them sick. He sits down and starts a long talk with a list of who's blessed. And every name on it is the one you wouldn't expect. The poor. The ones who are crying. The gentle ones who never win an argument. It opens what Christians call the Sermon on the Mount.","screen":["`WHO GETS CALLED BLESSED?` → `A HILLSIDE. A CROWD.` → `EVERY NAME IS UNEXPECTED.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"They're called the Beatitudes, from the Latin word for blessed. In Matthew's Gospel they're usually counted as eight, and each has the same shape: blessed are these people, because this will happen to them. The first, in the old English: \"Blessed are the poor in spirit: for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.\" The second: \"Blessed are they that mourn: for they shall be comforted.\" Then the meek, who will inherit the earth. The hungry for what's right. The merciful. The pure in heart. The peacemakers. And the people hounded for doing right. Then the word itself. The Greek word for blessed, makarioi, wasn't a churchy word. In ordinary Greek it described the fortunate, the happy. Old poets even used it for the gods, who had nothing to worry about. So the sentence lands like this: the lucky ones are the broke ones. The lucky ones are the ones at the funeral. It's meant to make you blink. Another Gospel has a shorter, blunter version: blessed are you poor, with no in spirit at all. Poor in money, poor in pride, or both: Christians have long talked about the difference. Either way, the list turns the ladder over. Later Jesus says it in a way that became a saying: \"the last shall be first, and the first last.\"","screen":["`EIGHT BLESSINGS, ONE SHAPE.` · `MAKARIOI — THE LUCKY ONES.` · `BLESSED ARE YOU POOR.` · `THE LADDER TURNED OVER.` · then, large: `THE LAST SHALL BE FIRST.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit comfortably, and let one long breath out. Think of one person you tend to overlook. Not someone you dislike; someone you just don't see. The person who cleans the building. The quiet one in the group chat. Hold their face in your mind. If you pray, you can hold them there the way you would in a prayer. If you don't, just let them be first in your mind for a little while.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"The Beatitudes. Eight blessings that start at the bottom. Fourteenth word. Amen, grace, Jesus, the Lord's Prayer, the Good Samaritan, the Prodigal Son, the Beatitudes: that's week two.","screen":["`THE BEATITUDES — your 14th word` → `WEEK TWO. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the last shall be first. Week three brings love, faith, songs that shout, a cross and an empty tomb. Day fourteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: AGAPE: THE WORD FOR LOVE THAT ISN'T A FEELING.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"agape: the word for love that isn't a feeling","word":"love","hook":"agape: the word for love that isn't a feeling","length":"6:00","carry":"love is what you do when you don't feel it","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Beatitudes, the list that starts at the bottom. Week three begins with the most familiar word on this path.","screen":["`THE LAST SHALL BE FIRST.` → `WEEK THREE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"At a lot of weddings, someone's cousin stands up, a little nervous, and reads: love is patient, love is kind. It's on cards, on mugs, on the wall above somebody's couch. It's so familiar it sounds soft. But it wasn't written for a wedding. It was written to a church that was falling apart: people taking each other to court, splitting into camps behind their favorite teachers, showing off who was more spiritual. Paul had started that church, in the Greek city of Corinth. And in the middle of a letter full of scolding, he stopped and wrote this.","screen":["`LOVE IS PATIENT, LOVE IS KIND.` → `NOT WRITTEN FOR A WEDDING.` → `WRITTEN TO A CHURCH IN A FIGHT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Love. English has one word for it. You love your mother, your dog, pizza, a song. Greek had several. Eros, the love that wants. Philia, the love between friends. And the one Paul chose here: agape. Christian teachers have long filled it with a particular meaning: love as a choice, not a mood. Love that acts for someone's good whether or not you feel warm about them. Listen to how Paul describes it, in the old English: \"Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up.\" Charity. That's the old translation's word, from the Latin caritas, related to the word for dear, even costly. Today charity sounds like a donation box, so newer Bibles say love. But the old word tells you something: this is love with its sleeves rolled up. Notice something else. In Paul's Greek, almost every line in the passage is a verb. Suffers long. Is kind. Doesn't envy. Doesn't brag. He isn't describing a feeling. He's describing behavior, the kind you could watch someone do. That's why it fit a church full of people who didn't like each other. He wasn't asking them to feel warm. He was asking them to be patient and kind, and to stop showing off. He ends: \"And now abideth faith, hope, charity, these three; but the greatest of these is charity.\" Christians say this is how God loves first. Another letter puts it simply: \"We love him, because he first loved us.\"","screen":["`GREEK HAD SEVERAL WORDS.` · `AGAPE — LOVE AS A CHOICE.` · `CHARITY — LOVE WITH SLEEVES UP.` · `EVERY LINE IS A VERB.` · then, large: `THE GREATEST OF THESE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and breathe out slowly. Think of someone who's hard to love right now. Not the hardest person in your life; someone mildly difficult. A coworker. A relative. A roommate. You don't have to feel anything warm. Just picture them, and ask one plain question: what would be kind, for them? Something small and real. Let an answer come, or don't. Then just breathe, and let the question rest.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Love. In Greek, agape: love that's a choice, not a mood. The old English calls it charity. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`LOVE — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: love is what you do when you don't feel it. Paul wrote it to people who didn't like each other. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT CERTAINTY.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"not certainty","word":"faith","hook":"not certainty; a mustard seed, and a father who believed and doubted at once","length":"6:00","carry":"small is enough","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: love, the kind you do when you don't feel it. Today, a word for the days you don't feel much of anything.","screen":["`LOVE ✓` → `DAY 16.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"You pray, and feel nothing. You sit in a service where everyone around you seems moved, and wonder what you're missing. Next to people whose faith looks like a wall, solid and never shaken, it's easy to decide faith belongs to other people. Here's a scene from Mark's Gospel. A father brings his son, who has suffered since childhood, and the disciples couldn't help. The father says: if you can do anything, help us. Jesus tells him all things are possible to the one who believes. And the father cries out, in tears: \"Lord, I believe; help thou mine unbelief.\" Both at once.","screen":["`YOU PRAY, AND FEEL NOTHING.` → `A FATHER, OUT OF OPTIONS.` → `\"I BELIEVE; HELP MY UNBELIEF.\"`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Faith. The Greek word is pistis, and it's less about certainty than about trust. It's the word for trusting a friend, or being faithful to one. You have faith in someone the way you have faith in a bridge: not because you've proved it, but because you're willing to put your weight on it. Amen, on day eight, came from a root meaning firm, solid. Same idea. Now, how much of it do you need? Another Gospel tells the same story, and afterward the disciples ask why they couldn't help the boy. Jesus answers: \"If ye have faith as a grain of mustard seed, ye shall say unto this mountain, Remove hence to yonder place; and it shall remove.\" He doesn't say: if you had enormous faith. He says: if you had even this much. The mountain is big. The seed is tiny. The point is the seed. A letter in the Bible puts it another way: \"faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.\" Not seen. And not always felt. Mother Teresa's private letters, published after her death, describe years of feeling God's absence when she prayed. She kept praying. So some Christian writers have said that doubt, and even dryness, isn't simply faith's opposite. The father in Mark didn't bring a perfect faith. He brought a half-sure, crying, both-at-once faith, and the story doesn't send him away. Jesus heals the boy.","screen":["`PISTIS — TRUST, NOT PROOF.` · `A MUSTARD SEED.` · `THINGS NOT SEEN, NOT FELT.` · `DOUBT ISN'T THE OPPOSITE.` · then, large: `SMALL IS ENOUGH.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. If there's something tiny nearby, a grain of rice, a seed, a crumb, put it in your palm. If not, just open your hand. Sit, and breathe. Think of one thing you're unsure about. Don't solve it. Just admit it. Now think of one small thing you do trust: the floor holding you up, the next breath coming, one person. Let that small thing be enough, for now. If you pray, the father's words are there for you: Lord, I believe; help thou mine unbelief.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Faith. Pistis. Not certainty: trust, even the size of a seed. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`FAITH — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: small is enough. On a day you feel nothing, a seed's worth still counts. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: DAVID SHOUTING AT GOD.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"David shouting at God","word":"the Psalms","hook":"David shouting at God — anger is allowed","length":"6:00","carry":"you can say anything to him","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: faith, and a father who believed and doubted in the same breath. Your line was small is enough. Today, a whole book of honest prayers.","screen":["`SMALL IS ENOUGH.` → `DAY 17.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"35 sec","voice":"Anger at a person. Anger at a life. Sometimes, anger at God, or the universe, or whoever runs things. It can seem like the one feeling nobody brings to church, where everyone shows up polite. Now open the middle of the Bible, and there's a whole book of people who didn't. \"How long wilt thou forget me, O LORD? for ever?\" That's not a complaint about God. It's a complaint to God, out loud, and it's scripture.","screen":["`ANGER, EVEN AT GOD.` → `POLITE FACES.` → `\"HOW LONG?\"`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"The Psalms. A hundred and fifty poems, the songbook of ancient Israel, still prayed by Jews and by Christians. The name comes from Greek, psalmos, a song sung to a plucked string. The Hebrew title is Tehillim: praises. Tradition ties many of them to David, the shepherd boy who became king; the headings put his name on about half, and scholars debate how many he actually wrote. For a book called praises, a great deal of it is complaint. Scholars call these laments, and there are a lot of them. That first line asks how long four times in two verses. Another psalm opens with a cry you'll hear again tomorrow, from the cross: \"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\" And one psalm ends in darkness, with no comfort at all. If prayer has ever felt like talking to the ceiling, that one is in the book too. These weren't hidden away. Many carry headings addressed to the chief musician: songs to be sung. Laments often have a shape. First the honest part: where are you, this isn't fair, I'm going under. Then, often, a turn. The how long psalm ends by saying the singer has trusted in God's mercy and will sing again. In these songs, the anger doesn't have to be cleaned up first. It's the way in.","screen":["`TEHILLIM — PRAISES.` · `SO MUCH OF IT IS COMPLAINT.` · `\"HOW LONG?\" FOUR TIMES.` · `FIRST THE HONEST PART. THEN A TURN.` · then, large: `YOU DON'T HAVE TO BE POLITE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and breathe. Think of something you're upset about. Not a crisis, but something real, something you've been keeping polite about. Silently, say it straight. No softening. How long. Why. This isn't fair. If you pray, you can say it to God, the way the Psalms do. If you don't, say it to the quiet. Then breathe, and notice what's left once it's been said. You don't have to fix anything.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"The Psalms. A hundred and fifty songs, with praise and complaint side by side. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`THE PSALMS — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: you can say anything to him. The Psalms were saying it long before anyone told you to be polite. Day seventeen, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY THE SYMBOL IS AN EXECUTION DEVICE.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"why the symbol is an execution device","word":"the cross","hook":"why the symbol is an execution device","length":"6:00","carry":"the worst thing is not the last thing","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Psalms, and a cry that opens one of them. Today, the place that cry was heard again. The hardest word in this camp.","screen":["`THE PSALMS ✓` → `DAY 18.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"35 sec","voice":"On a steeple. On a necklace. Tattooed on a forearm. Hanging from a rearview mirror. The cross is everywhere, and it's easy to stop seeing it. So try a swap. A movement whose symbol was an electric chair. A tiny gold noose on a chain. It sounds unthinkable. But that's what the cross was to the people who first saw it: the way Rome killed the people it wanted everyone to watch die. So why would anyone wear one?","screen":["`ON A STEEPLE. ON A NECKLACE.` → `NOW SWAP IN AN ELECTRIC CHAIR.` → `WHY WOULD ANYONE WEAR ONE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Crucifixion was how Rome executed slaves, rebels and anyone it saw as a threat to order. It was slow, and it was public on purpose. The Gospels say Jesus was arrested at night, questioned, and handed to Pontius Pilate, the Roman governor, who sentenced him. Roman soldiers carried it out. Over his head was written the charge: the King of the Jews. A mockery, and a warning. The Gospels keep a few things he said there. One has him pray: \"Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do.\" In Mark's Gospel he cries out, in his own language, yesterday's line: \"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\" The Gospels don't smooth that over. Some Christian readers say it's there so that anyone who has felt abandoned knows he felt it too. Then a turn in the history. Historians of early Christian art say the oldest surviving images more often show other signs: a fish, an anchor, a shepherd. The cross became the main symbol later, after Rome stopped crucifying people. Christians say something happened there that changed what the worst thing means. Churches put it in different words: a sacrifice, a rescue, love going all the way down. And they show it differently. Catholic and Orthodox churches often show the crucifix, with Jesus on it. Some Protestant churches show the cross empty. Both are saying something about that Friday: it happened, and Christians say it wasn't the end.","screen":["`ROME'S PUBLIC EXECUTION.` · `\"FATHER, FORGIVE THEM.\"` · `FIRST A FISH, AN ANCHOR.` · `THE CRUCIFIX AND THE EMPTY CROSS.` · then, large: `IT WASN'T THE END.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and breathe out. Think of a hard thing from this year. Not the hardest. Just name it, quietly, without reliving it. Now notice one plain fact: you're still here. Something came after it, even if it's only this minute, sitting and breathing. You don't have to make it mean anything. Just hold both: it happened, and it wasn't the last thing. If you pray, you can bring it with you.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"The cross. Rome's execution, turned into the sign on the steeple. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`THE CROSS — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the worst thing is not the last thing. Tomorrow is a Sunday morning, at first light. Day eighteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE WOMEN WENT TO THE TOMB FIRST.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"the women went to the tomb first","word":"Easter","hook":"the women went to the tomb first","length":"6:00","carry":"the stone is already moved","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the cross, Rome's execution on a Friday. Your line was the worst thing is not the last thing. Now, the Sunday.","screen":["`THE WORST THING IS NOT THE LAST THING.` → `DAY 19.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"It's very early on a Sunday, first light. Three women are walking to a grave, carrying spices. Their friend was executed on Friday, and there wasn't time to prepare his body before the Sabbath, so they're going now. It's the last thing you can do for someone you love. And on the way, they realize they have a problem. The tomb is sealed with a big stone, and none of them can move it. Mark's Gospel keeps the question they ask each other on the road: \"Who shall roll us away the stone from the door of the sepulchre?\"","screen":["`SUNDAY. FIRST LIGHT.` → `THREE WOMEN, CARRYING SPICES.` → `WHO WILL MOVE THE STONE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Easter. In most of the world its name comes from Pascha, from the Hebrew word for Passover, because it happened in Passover week. Where the English word Easter comes from, nobody is completely sure. In Mark, the women arrive, and the stone is already rolled back. Inside sits a young man dressed in white, who tells them: \"he is risen; he is not here: behold the place where they laid him.\" And the oldest copies of Mark end almost right there. The women run from the tomb, trembling, and say nothing to anyone, \"for they were afraid.\" Fear, and an empty room. Most scholars agree the longer endings in many Bibles were added later, and many churches still read them. Another Gospel tells it closer up. Mary Magdalene comes alone, while it's still dark, and finds the stone gone. She stays outside, crying. A man asks why she's weeping, and she thinks he's the gardener. Then he says one word. Her name. \"Mary.\" And she knows him. Now notice who's there first. Not Peter. Not the twelve. Women, in a world where a woman's word counted for little. Every Gospel puts them at the tomb first. Easter is the center of the Christian year. Christians say Jesus rose from the dead, and that death doesn't get the last word. On Easter morning many greet each other: Christ is risen. And the answer comes back: he is risen indeed.","screen":["`PASCHA — FROM PASSOVER.` · `\"HE IS RISEN; HE IS NOT HERE.\"` · `MARY. SHE KNOWS HIM.` · `THE WOMEN WERE THERE FIRST.` · then, large: `THE STONE IS ALREADY MOVED.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and breathe. Think of a stone in your life right now: something heavy in the way, that you've been worrying about on the road before you've even gotten there. Name it. Now, just for this minute, picture walking up and finding it already moved. Don't plan how. Don't argue with it. Just let yourself feel the relief, and breathe into it. If you pray, you can hand the stone over. If you don't, notice how much of the weight was the worrying.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Easter. Pascha. The morning the stone was moved and the tomb was empty. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`EASTER — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the stone is already moved. The women worried about it the whole way there. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT A BUILDING.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"not a building","word":"the church","hook":"not a building — the people; the Greek word means \"the called-out ones\"","length":"6:00","carry":"find two or three","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Easter, and the women at the tomb at first light. Today, what those first witnesses became.","screen":["`EASTER ✓` → `DAY 20.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Say the word church and a picture comes up. A steeple. Stained glass. A cathedral so big your voice echoes, or a strip-mall storefront with folding chairs. Everybody pictures a building. But for about the first two hundred years, Christians had almost no church buildings at all. They met in kitchens and courtyards, around somebody's table. Paul ends one of his letters with greetings to a married couple, and adds: \"greet the church that is in their house.\" The church was in the house. Which means it wasn't the house.","screen":["`SAY \"CHURCH.\" WHAT DO YOU SEE?` → `200 YEARS, ALMOST NO BUILDINGS.` → `\"THE CHURCH THAT IS IN THEIR HOUSE.\"`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"The word in the New Testament is Greek: ekklesia. It's made of two small pieces: ek, out, and a word for calling. The called-out ones. In a Greek city, the ekklesia was the assembly: the citizens called out of their homes to gather and decide things together. It wasn't a place. It was people, showing up. So when the first Christians needed a word for themselves, that's the one they picked. Not the temple. Not a sacred site. The gathering. Paul pushes it further. He tells one of his churches: \"ye are the body of Christ.\" Hands, feet, eyes, ears. All different. None of them the whole thing, and none able to say to the others, I don't need you. That's how he pictures a church: not a crowd of the same person, but a body of very different ones. Then the smallest church there is. In Matthew's Gospel, Jesus promises: \"For where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst of them.\" Two or three. Not two or three hundred. Christians today gather in every size there is. Cathedrals that took centuries to build. House churches still meeting around a kitchen table. A hospital room, a prison, a video call. Churches differ a lot about how they're led and how they're joined. But almost all of them would say this much: the building is where the church meets. The people are the church.","screen":["`EKKLESIA — THE CALLED-OUT ONES.` · `AN ASSEMBLY, NOT A PLACE.` · `\"YE ARE THE BODY OF CHRIST.\"` · `TWO OR THREE.` · then, large: `THE PEOPLE ARE THE CHURCH.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and breathe out slowly. Think of the two or three people you'd call if something went wrong tonight. The ones who'd show up. Picture them, one at a time: their faces, where they are right now. Then widen it a little, to someone who showed up for you once and never knew what it meant. If you pray, you can gather them in your prayer. If you don't, just let them be gathered here with you for a minute.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"The church. Ekklesia. Not a building: the called-out ones, gathered. Twentieth word.","screen":["`THE CHURCH — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: find two or three. That's the smallest church there is, and it counts. Day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE SHORTEST GOSPEL, THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"the shortest gospel, the mountain ahead","word":"Mark 1:1","hook":"the shortest gospel, the mountain ahead; \"the beginning of the good news\"","length":"6:30","carry":"start here","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the church, the people, not the building. Now the whole camp, quickly: communion, the Trinity, born again, baptism, testimony, the Holy Spirit, the Bible, amen, grace, Jesus, the Lord's Prayer, the Good Samaritan, the Prodigal Son, the Beatitudes, love, faith, the Psalms, the cross, Easter, the church. Twenty words. Today makes twenty-one.","screen":["`FIND TWO OR THREE.` → the strand, twenty words lit."]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"35 sec","voice":"So many of those words lead back to four short books: the Gospels. Matthew, Mark, Luke and John. On day seven you heard that a lot of people start reading the Bible with a Gospel. The shortest of the four is Mark. Sixteen chapters. You could read the whole thing aloud in one sitting, in somebody's living room. It's fast. It's blunt. It skips the parts you'd expect. There's no Christmas in it, no shepherds, no star. It starts with a man shouting in the desert. And its very first line is today's word.","screen":["`FOUR SHORT BOOKS.` → `MARK: THE SHORTEST.` → `NO CHRISTMAS. A DESERT.` → `THE FIRST LINE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Mark, chapter one, verse one. In the old English: \"The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.\" That's the whole verse. It's a title, and a door. Start with gospel: good news. Mark's Greek word is euangelion, and it wasn't a church word yet. Historians point to Roman-era inscriptions that use it for public announcements: a victory won, news about an emperor, news that changes the day for everyone who hears it. Mark takes that word and puts it on a builder from Galilee. Now the shape, so you know the mountain ahead. First, Galilee: a man baptized in a river, fishermen dropping their nets, healings, arguments, and the stories he told. A word Mark uses again and again here is straightway. Immediately. Everything happens fast. Then, right in the middle, the turning point. Jesus asks his friends: who do you say that I am? And from there the road turns toward Jerusalem. The last third of the book is one week: the entry into the city, the table and the bread, the cross, and the empty tomb, where the women run away, afraid. Which brings us back to the book's first word. Beginning. Some readers notice that Mark calls all of it, the whole book, just the beginning. As if the good news isn't finished when the book ends. As if the reader is supposed to pick it up from there.","screen":["`EUANGELION — GOOD NEWS.` · `GALILEE: STRAIGHTWAY.` · `THE TURN: WHO DO YOU SAY I AM?` · `ONE LAST WEEK.` · then, large: `THE BEGINNING OF THE GOOD NEWS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Last sit of the camp. Sit, and breathe, the way you did on day one, when the first word was communion, a meal that remembers. Now let one word from these three weeks come back to you on its own. Don't pick it. Let it arrive. Grace, maybe. Or the stone already moved. Or small is enough. Hold it for a few breaths. Then ask, gently: where does this start for me? If you pray, you can offer that word back as your prayer, with an amen. If you don't, just hold it, and let it be a beginning.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Mark 1:1. The beginning of the gospel: good news, and only the beginning of it. Twenty-first word, and the strand is complete.","screen":["`MARK 1:1 — your 21st word` → the strand complete → `CAMP 1. FIRST WORDS. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: start here. Tomorrow, Camp 2 begins: the stories, and the question of why he told them at all. Twenty-one days. Day twenty-one.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY HE TOLD STORIES.`"]}]}},"HINDUISM":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"the one at every doorway","word":"Ganesha","hook":"an elephant-headed figure on the dashboard, by the door, at the top of the page","length":"3:00","carry":"clear the road, then begin","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Every Hindu beginning starts with him, so this one does too. A small statue on a taxi dashboard. A picture above a shop counter. His name printed at the top of a wedding invitation, before anyone else's. Elephant head, round belly, one tusk broken. He sits wherever things start, and that's the clue to who he is.","screen":["`EVERY BEGINNING STARTS WITH HIM.` → `DASHBOARDS. DOORWAYS. INVITATIONS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Start with the name. Ganesha is gana plus isha: lord of the ganas, the attendants of the god Shiva. But the title that explains the doorways is Vighnaharta. Vighna, an obstacle; harta, one who removes. The remover of obstacles. That's why he's honored first: before a ceremony, a wedding, a new shop or an exam. Some people write shri ganeshaya namah at the top of a page before they begin: namah means a bow. There's a popular way of reading his body: big ears, listen more than you talk; small eyes, focus; a big belly, take in what life serves, good and hard. And the broken tusk: tradition says he wrote down the Mahabharata as the sage Vyasa dictated it, and one story says that when his pen gave out, he snapped off his own tusk and kept writing.","screen":["`LORD OF THE GANAS.` · `VIGHNAHARTA — REMOVER OF OBSTACLES.` · `SHRI GANESHAYA NAMAH.` · `THE BROKEN TUSK.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Rest a hand on something that marks a start: a door, a laptop, a notebook. Name, once, what's in the way of the next thing you're beginning. Then breathe out and lift your hand.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Ganesha. Lord of the ganas, remover of obstacles, the one at the door. First word on your strand, and the first name.","screen":["`GANESHA — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: clear the road, then begin. It's why his name comes first on so many invitations, and why he came first here. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE SOUND THE UNIVERSE HUMS.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"the sound the universe hums","word":"om","hook":"the sound a room makes together; it's three sounds and a silence","length":"3:00","carry":"one sound, before words","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Ganesha, the one who goes first. Today, the sound that opens almost every Hindu prayer.","screen":["`GANESHA ✓` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Om. A yoga class ends with it, and a lot of Hindu prayers begin with it. Most people treat it as one long hum. One of the oldest Hindu texts hears four parts in it: three sounds, and a fourth that isn't a sound at all. First, a guess.","screen":["`OM.` → `ONE HUM?` → `FOUR PARTS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Say it slowly and you can feel three sounds. A, with the mouth open. U, as the lips round. M, as they close. That's why it's sometimes written aum. An old text of just twelve verses starts by saying om is all of this: past, present, future, and whatever lies beyond time. Then it maps the sounds onto your day. A is waking, the world you can see and touch. U is dreaming, the world made of the mind. M is deep, dreamless sleep, where nothing is wanted. The fourth part has no sound. It's the silence after the m runs out, and the text calls it simply the fourth: the self, at peace. So om isn't a sign-off. It's a map of a whole day in one breath, with the quiet underneath it. The Bhagavad Gita says acts of offering, giving and discipline begin with it.","screen":["`A — WAKING.` · `U — DREAMING.` · `M — DEEP SLEEP.` · `THE FOURTH — THE SILENCE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Breathe in. Let it out as the sound, aloud or under your breath: a, u, m. Let the m run out on its own, then stay a second in the quiet after it. That quiet is the fourth part.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Om. A, u and m for waking, dreaming and deep sleep, and the silence after it for the fourth. Second word on your strand.","screen":["`OM — your 2nd word` → clicks onto the strand beside namaste."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one sound, before words. Not a task. Something to notice the next time a room goes quiet before it begins. Day two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE PRAYER YOU KNOW BY HEART.`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"the prayer you know by heart","word":"Gayatri","hook":"the prayer you can say start to finish, and what it asks for","length":"3:00","carry":"a prayer for a clear mind","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: om, three sounds and a silence. Today it opens a prayer you might already know by heart.","screen":["`OM.` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Om bhur bhuvah svah. If you grew up in a Hindu home, there's a good chance you can say the rest without thinking. Maybe someone said it every morning. Maybe it played at every puja. Now the hard part: what does it mean? Most people go quiet. Today, line by line. First, a guess.","screen":["`OM BHUR BHUVAH SVAH…` → `YOU CAN SAY IT.` → `WHAT DOES IT MEAN?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"It's called the Gayatri mantra, and it's one of the oldest verses Hindus still say every day. Take it a line at a time. Om: the sound from yesterday. Bhur bhuvah svah: earth, sky, and everything beyond: the whole world. Tat savitur varenyam: that bright, worthy light of the sun. Bhargo devasya dhimahi: we meditate on that divine radiance. Dhiyo yo nah prachodayat: may it wake up and guide our minds. Notice what it asks for. Not money. Not luck. Not to win. A clear mind. That's what a mantra is: the word comes from man, to think, and tra, a tool. A tool for the mind, said on purpose. Many families say the Gayatri at dawn, as the light comes up, and some say it a set number of times on beads. Some traditions teach it at a coming-of-age ceremony; in many homes, everyone simply says it. If you grew up saying it, you weren't doing it wrong. Once you know the lines, it stops being a sound you repeat and becomes a request you mean.","screen":["`OM — THE SOUND FROM YESTERDAY.` · `EARTH, SKY, AND BEYOND.` · `MAY IT WAKE UP OUR MINDS.` · `A TOOL FOR THE MIND.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Breathe in. As you breathe out, say the Gayatri once, slowly, out loud or in your head, or just its last line: dhiyo yo nah prachodayat, may it wake up and guide our minds. This time, mean the words.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Gayatri. The morning prayer for a clear mind, and a mantra: a tool for the mind. Third word on your strand.","screen":["`GAYATRI — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: a prayer for a clear mind. Next time you hear it, you'll know what's being asked. Day three.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE OLDEST HELLO.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"the oldest hello","word":"namaste","hook":"you've said it after yoga class; the words inside say more than hello","length":"3:00","carry":"the light in me sees the light in you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Gayatri, a prayer for a clear mind. Today, a word you've said a thousand times as hello.","screen":["`A PRAYER FOR A CLEAR MIND.` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"You've said it at the end of a yoga class, and you've probably heard it means the light in me sees the light in you. The word itself says something much simpler. The longer meaning is a reading of it, and that reading comes from somewhere specific. First, a guess.","screen":["`NAMASTE.` → `WHAT IT SAYS.` → `WHAT IT'S READ AS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Namaste is Sanskrit, and it splits in two. Namas: a bow. Te: to you. Word for word, I bow to you. That's the whole dictionary meaning. So where does the light come from? From a reading of the word. One of the oldest Hindu texts describes a light that shines above the heavens, above everything, and then says that same light is the light inside a person. Out there and in here: one light. Put that next to a bow and you get the popular line: the light in me sees the light in you. Not a translation. An interpretation, with a real source behind it. What the reading changes is who you're bowing to. Not someone's job, mood or résumé, but whatever in them is the same as what's in you. And nobody has to earn it first. In this reading, it's already there. The same bow goes further at home. When a kid touches a grandparent's feet at the door, that's pranam, from pra, forward, and nam, to bend: the same root as namaste. And the elder's hand comes down on the child's head: a blessing, given back. Respect goes one way, a blessing comes the other.","screen":["`NAMAS — A BOW.` · `TE — TO YOU.` · `THE SAME LIGHT, INSIDE A PERSON.` · `A BLESSING, GIVEN BACK.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Palms together if you like, or just sit up straight. Breathe in. As you breathe out, tip your head slightly forward: the bow the word is named for. Nothing to feel, nothing to get right. Just the gesture, done once, on purpose.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Namaste. Word for word, I bow to you. Read deeper, the light in me sees the light in you. Fourth word on your strand.","screen":["`NAMASTE — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the light in me sees the light in you. Not a task. Just a way to look at the next person you meet. That's day four.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: LIGHT PASSED HAND TO HAND.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"light passed hand to hand","word":"arti","hook":"a flame on a plate, moved in circles, then carried around the room","length":"3:00","carry":"light passed hand to hand","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: namaste, I bow to you, and the bow that touches an elder's feet. Today, the flame that comes around on a plate.","screen":["`I BOW TO YOU.` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Evening at a temple. Or the steps down to a river, as dusk comes in. Or a living room, when the lamp comes out. A small flame sits on a metal plate, and someone moves it in slow circles in front of the shrine. A bell rings. People sing. Then the plate is carried around the room, and one by one, people pass their hands over the flame and bring their palms to their eyes and forehead. It takes a few minutes, start to finish. Today's word is that flame, and what happens when it leaves the shrine.","screen":["`A FLAME ON A PLATE.` → `SLOW CIRCLES. A BELL.` → `THEN IT COMES TO THE PEOPLE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 5","voice":"Arti. The waving of a lamp; the offering of light. After the flowers and food are offered, the lamp is circled before the image of God, and a song is sung. The best known begins: Om jai jagadish hare. Victory to you, lord of the world. An old Hindu text has a line that helps with what the lamp might mean: the sun doesn't shine there, nor the moon and stars, let alone this little fire; everything shines after that one shining. So a small flame held up before God is light offered back to where light comes from. Then the flame leaves the shrine and goes out to the people. Each person draws its warmth toward their face. The same small fire touches every face in the room, and nobody gets more of it. Often there's one more step: prasad. Food or sweets offered before God, then handed out. The sweet you got after temple as a kid was that: an offering, given back as a blessing.","screen":["`ARTI — THE OFFERING OF LIGHT` · `EVERYTHING SHINES AFTER THAT ONE` · `THE SAME FIRE TOUCHES EVERY FACE` · `PRASAD — GIVEN BACK AS A BLESSING`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. If there's a candle or a lamp near you, look at it; any light in the room will do. Cup your hands toward it, as if gathering warmth, and bring them to your eyes and forehead. Then bring one person to mind and turn your cupped hands toward them, as if passing the plate.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Arti. The offering of light, and then the light shared with everyone. Fifth word.","screen":["`ARTI — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: light passed hand to hand. Next time the plate comes around, you'll know what you're doing. Day five.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY SO MANY GODS?.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"why so many gods?","word":"ishta devata","hook":"why there are so many gods, and which one is yours","length":"3:00","carry":"one truth, many names","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: arti, light passed hand to hand. Today, the question every Hindu kid gets asked at school.","screen":["`LIGHT PASSED HAND TO HAND.` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Someone at school or work asks it, half curious, half teasing: how many gods do you have? Ganesha on the dashboard, Krishna in the calendar, Durga at Navratri, Lakshmi at Diwali, a whole shelf at your grandparents' place. It's a fair question, and most people never got a good answer to give. Here's one. First, a guess.","screen":["`HOW MANY GODS DO YOU HAVE?` → `A FAIR QUESTION.` → `HERE'S AN ANSWER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"One of the oldest Hindu verses says it in five words: ekam sat vipra bahudha vadanti. Truth is one; the wise call it by many names. That line is how a lot of Hindus answer the question. The many gods are many faces and names of one reality, the way one person can be a mother, a boss and a friend and still be one person. Not every Hindu puts it the same way. Some families give their whole devotion to Vishnu, or Shiva, or the Goddess, and see the others as part of that one. Some honor many, each for a different part of life. Almost everyone has an ishta devata: a chosen deity, the form of God that feels like yours. Often it comes from your family. Sometimes you find your own. So the answer to the question at school can be simple: one truth, many names, and one of them feels like home.","screen":["`TRUTH IS ONE.` · `THE WISE CALL IT BY MANY NAMES.` · `MANY FACES, ONE REALITY.` · `THE ONE THAT FEELS LIKE YOURS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Think of the image of God you saw most growing up: on a wall, a dashboard, a shelf. Breathe in. As you breathe out, say its name once, quietly, as if it were the name of someone who knows you.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Ishta devata. Your chosen deity: the form of God that feels like yours. Sixth word.","screen":["`ISHTA DEVATA — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one truth, many names. Next time someone asks how many gods you have, you've got an answer. Day six.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT FATE, NOT PAYBACK.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"not fate, not payback","word":"karma","hook":"the word everyone borrows, and what it actually says","length":"3:00","carry":"what you do, you become","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: one truth, many names, and the one that's yours. Six words so far: Ganesha, om, Gayatri, namaste, arti, ishta devata. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`GANESHA · OM · GAYATRI · NAMASTE · ARTI · ISHTA DEVATA`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Karma. Someone says it when the car that cut them off gets stopped at the next light. Someone says it when a person who was unkind finally has a bad week. It turns up in song lyrics, on coffee mugs, under photos. Used like that, it means payback: the universe keeping a ledger and settling it on schedule, ideally where you can watch. It's a satisfying idea. It is also not quite what the word means. The older meaning is less about getting even and more about getting made, and it turns the whole thing around to face the one who's using it.","screen":["`KARMA.` → `THE CAR AT THE RED LIGHT.` → `PAYBACK?` → `NOT QUITE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Karma is the Sanskrit word for action. A deed. Something done. In the Bhagavad Gita, Krishna points out that no one can stay without acting, even for a moment: breathing, eating, speaking, choosing, all of it is action. So the first thing to know is that karma isn't something that happens to you. It's what you do. The second thing is what action does back. One of the oldest Hindu texts says it like a proverb: as a person acts, so that person becomes. Not: so the person is punished. Becomes. Patience, practiced, turns into a patient person. Sharpness, practiced, turns into a sharp one. A mango seed isn't judged worthy of a mango tree; it grows one because that's what the seed is. The payback version keeps your eyes on other people: did they get what's coming? The older version turns you around: what am I growing with my next word? Hindu teachers also speak of karma across lifetimes, and that's a bigger conversation for later. For today: as you act, so you become.","screen":["`KARMA — ACTION, A DEED` · `NO ONE STAYS WITHOUT ACTING` · `AS ONE ACTS, SO ONE BECOMES` · `WHAT AM I GROWING?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Bring to mind one small thing you did this week: a kind word, a sharp one, a promise kept or skipped. Don't grade it. Just ask what it's growing. Then pick one small action you'd be glad to see grow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Karma. Action, and what action makes of the one who does it. Not payback. Seventh word: a week's worth.","screen":["`KARMA — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: what you do, you become. That's week one: seven words, and what's inside each. Tomorrow, what your grandmother was actually doing.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHAT YOUR GRANDMOTHER WAS ACTUALLY DOING.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"what your grandmother was actually doing","word":"puja","hook":"a flower, a flame, a bell: what's happening at the home shrine","length":"6:00","carry":"attention is an offering","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: karma. Your line was what you do, you become. Week one is behind you. Week two starts at home. Day eight.","screen":["`WHAT YOU DO, YOU BECOME.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Before the kettle goes on, someone lights a small brass lamp. Beside it: a marigold, fresh this morning, a bell with a handle worn smooth, a stick of incense, a little water in a cup, a sweet on a tiny plate. Some Hindu homes keep a shelf like this, or a corner, or a whole small room, and someone stops there each morning. Walk past it on an ordinary morning and it can look like decoration. Stand and watch for a minute, though, and the person in front of it is busy: pouring, lighting, ringing, setting things down. Their hands are working through something in order. Today's word is the name for what they're doing.","screen":["`A LAMP. A FLOWER. A BELL.` → `SOMEONE STOPS THERE EACH MORNING.` → `WHAT ARE THEY DOING?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Puja. It's usually translated worship, and that's not wrong, but in English the word can sound formal and far away. A home puja is close and small, and it's done with the hands. Watch the steps. Water is offered. A flower is set down. Incense is lit. A lamp is waved. A bell is rung. Food is offered, and afterward it comes back as prasad, a gift to be eaten. Families and regions do it differently, and some teachers describe the whole thing as welcoming God as an honored guest. Now listen to the Bhagavad Gita. Krishna, speaking as God, says that whoever offers him a leaf, a flower, a fruit, or water, with devotion, he accepts it. In Sanskrit it begins: patram pushpam phalam toyam. A leaf. A flower. A fruit. Water. Nothing on that list costs much. What the verse cares about is the devotion, the heart that comes with the gift. So here's one way in, for anyone, whatever they believe. Puja gives a person's attention one place to land, and walks it through one sense after another: the cool of the water, the color of the flower, the smell of the smoke, the sound of the bell, the taste of the sweet. For those few minutes, the whole person is in one place. Maybe that's why the shrine can be so simple: a picture, a small stone, a lamp. The size of the offering was never the point. The care is. Seen that way, a grandmother at that shelf every morning isn't only keeping a custom. She's spending her attention on purpose, before anything else can claim it.","screen":["`WATER, FLOWER, FLAME, BELL, FOOD` · `A LEAF, A FLOWER, A FRUIT, WATER` · `ONE SENSE AFTER ANOTHER` · then, large: `ATTENTION IS AN OFFERING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: honor one ordinary thing. No shelf needed. Look around and choose one object you can see: a cup, a plant, your own open hand. Give it your eyes first, its color and its edges. Then, if you can reach it, your touch. Then close your eyes and keep the picture of it. If you pray, you can offer this small attention to God. If you don't, just let one thing have all of you for a little while. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Puja. Honor, offered with the hands and the senses: a leaf, a flower, a fruit, water. Eighth word.","screen":["`PUJA — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: attention is an offering. Today, give one ordinary thing your whole attention: a meal, a face, a cup of tea. One minute, nothing else in your hands. Day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE THING THAT'S YOURS TO DO.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"the thing that's yours to do","word":"dharma","hook":"a warrior puts down his bow and says he no longer knows what's right","length":"3:00","carry":"do the thing that's yours to do","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: puja. Your line was attention is an offering. Today, the word at the center of the Bhagavad Gita.","screen":["`ATTENTION IS AN OFFERING.` → `DAY 9.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Bhagavad Gita opens on a battlefield. A warrior named Arjuna asks his charioteer to drive him out between the two armies, and he sees his own family on the other side: teachers, uncles, cousins. His bow slips from his hand and he sits down in the chariot. The word he reaches for, to say what he's lost, is today's word.","screen":["`KURUKSHETRA.` → `THE BOW SLIPS.` → `WHAT HE'S LOST.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Dharma gets translated as duty, law or religion, and each of those catches only a piece. It comes from a Sanskrit root meaning to hold, or to support. Dharma is what holds things up: teachers say the dharma of fire is to burn and the dharma of water is to flow. It's what a thing is for, the part it holds in the whole. In the Gita's second chapter, Arjuna says his mind is confused about dharma, and he asks his charioteer, Krishna, to teach him. The rest of the Gita is that teaching. One line from the third chapter shows where it starts: better your own dharma, even done imperfectly, than someone else's done well. Your own dharma is svadharma. So dharma isn't only rules handed down. It's also a question: what is mine to do here? Arjuna isn't missing information. He's lost the thread of what's his to do.","screen":["`DHARMA — WHAT HOLDS THINGS UP.` · `I AM YOUR STUDENT. TEACH ME.` · `YOUR OWN, EVEN IMPERFECTLY.` · `WHAT IS MINE TO DO HERE?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, bring to mind one thing you've been putting off. On the second, ask it once, silently: what's mine to do here? On the third, let the question go. No plan needed yet.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Dharma. From a root meaning to hold: what holds things up, and your share of the holding. Ninth word.","screen":["`DHARMA — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: do the thing that's yours to do. It's the question the whole Gita answers, and this path comes back to it. Day nine.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE ONE BEHIND YOUR EYES.`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"the one behind your eyes","word":"atman","hook":"what stays when everything else about you changes","length":"6:00","carry":"someone is in there","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: dharma. Your line was do the thing that's yours to do. Today we turn from what gets done to the one doing it. Day ten.","screen":["`DO THE THING THAT'S YOURS TO DO.` → `DAY 10.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A question with no trick in it. Who is hearing this sentence? Not what. Who. The quick answer is: me. But try pointing at the me. The body? It has been changing since childhood. The mood? It may have shifted twice since breakfast. The thoughts? They're moving faster than this voice. Everything you can point to keeps moving. And yet, the Upanishads say, something has been there for all of it, the one all of it happened to. A teacher in an old Hindu text was pressed on exactly this, and his answer turned the question around. Today's word is what he was pointing at.","screen":["`WHO IS HEARING THIS?` → `NOT THE BODY. NOT THE MOOD.` → `SOMETHING HAS BEEN THERE FOR ALL OF IT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Atman. The self. Not the self in selfie, the personality you show the world. The Upanishads use atman for the deepest you: the one that is aware. And they notice something strange about it. You can't look straight at it. In the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad, a questioner keeps pressing the teacher Yajnavalkya: show me this self, plainly, the way you'd point and say, that's a cow, that's a horse. And Yajnavalkya answers, in effect: you can't see the seer of seeing. You can't hear the hearer of hearing. The one who looks can't become one more thing on the table to be looked at. So, in other passages, the same Upanishad describes the self by saying what it is not: neti, neti. Not this, not this. Whatever you can point to, the body, the breath, the busy mind, the self is not that. One way to put the Upanishad's point: take away everything that can be watched, and what's left is the watching. Put plainly: someone is in there. Today that someone gets its name. Here's where it turns practical. When worry comes, it comes as something seen: a thought, a tight chest, a story about tomorrow. The Upanishad points past it, to the one who sees. Hindu schools describe the atman in different ways. Some teach that the self is one with the whole. Others teach that it is a distinct soul, forever dear to God and dependent on God. Tomorrow brings the word for the whole. Today, just the turn this word makes: away from what's being watched, toward the one who is watching.","screen":["`ATMAN — THE SELF THAT IS AWARE` · `THE SEER OF SEEING` · `NETI, NETI — NOT THIS, NOT THIS` · `THE WATCHING, NOT THE WATCHED` · then, large: `SOMEONE IS IN THERE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today's practice is to notice the noticer. Let your eyes close or soften. Let a thought arrive, any thought. Don't follow it. Just see that it's there. Now notice that something saw it. Rest there for a moment, not in the thought, in the seeing. Another thought will come; that's what thoughts do. Same move: see it, then notice the one who saw. No need to get anywhere. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Atman. The self: not the body, not the thoughts, but the one aware of them. Tenth word.","screen":["`ATMAN — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: someone is in there. When worry shows up today, ask once, quietly: who is seeing this? Just the question. Day ten.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE OCEAN THE WAVE FORGETS IT IS.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"the ocean the wave forgets it is","word":"Brahman","hook":"salt in a cup of water, and rivers that reach the sea","length":"6:15","carry":"not two","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: atman, the one behind your eyes. Your line was someone is in there. Did you catch yourself watching a thought? Today the word gets bigger, so we'll go slow. Day eleven.","screen":["`SOMEONE IS IN THERE.` → `DAY 11.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A father hands his son a lump of salt and says: put this in a cup of water, and come back in the morning. The son does. Next morning the father says: bring me the salt. The boy looks. It's gone. Sip from the top, says the father. Salty. From the middle. Salty. From the bottom. Salty. The salt is in every sip, and he can't find it anywhere. The father is Uddalaka, the son is Shvetaketu, and the story is told in the Chandogya Upanishad. It's one of the old ways of pointing at today's word, and the word is a big one.","screen":["`A LUMP OF SALT. A CUP OF WATER.` → `WHERE DID IT GO?` → `IT'S IN EVERY SIP.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Brahman. Not Brahma, the creator god in the stories, and not Brahmin, the priestly class. Different words. Brahman is the name the Upanishads give to the whole: the reality underneath everything, what everything comes from and returns to. Like the salt, it isn't one more thing you can pick out. It's in every taste. The same chapter of the Chandogya has another picture. Rivers flow, some east, some west, and in the end they reach the sea and become the sea. And once there, the Upanishad says, they don't know: I am this river, I am that one. Now the line the father keeps saying to his son, again and again in that chapter: tat tvam asi. That's what you are. Yesterday's word, atman, the self, and today's word, Brahman, the whole: the Upanishad brings them face to face. Hindu thinkers read the line in more than one way. Advaita Vedanta, taught by Shankara, reads it as identity: the self and the whole are not two. Ramanuja's school reads it as one whole with real differences inside it, the self living in God and God as the self's inner ruler. Madhva's school keeps the self and God distinct, the self always dependent on God. These readings sit side by side in the tradition, and nobody here has to choose. Just notice what the picture does. In the Upanishad, the rivers don't dry up somewhere along the way. They become the sea. And today's title borrows the picture for one way of hearing it: a wave that is ocean the whole time, even while it forgets.","screen":["`BRAHMAN — THE WHOLE` · `SALT IN WATER, RIVERS IN THE SEA` · `TAT TVAM ASI — THAT'S WHAT YOU ARE` · `READ MORE THAN ONE WAY` · then, large: `NOT TWO.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: let the river reach the sea. Sit and breathe. Bring a river to mind, any one you've seen or can imagine. Let it carry this life, this name, today's worries. Follow it as it moves. Then let it reach the sea, and let the edges go. Not lost: arrived. Rest there, in the big water. Some rest there with one name of God in mind. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Brahman. The whole, the reality under everything. Not Brahma, not Brahmin. Eleventh word.","screen":["`BRAHMAN — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: not two. When you feel cut off from someone today, try the rivers and the sea for one breath. Day eleven.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: ANYONE WHO TURNS A LIGHT ON.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"anyone who turns a light on","word":"guru","hook":"the word got borrowed for salesmen; the old meaning is closer to a lamp","length":"6:00","carry":"thank a teacher today","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Brahman. Your line was not two. If it felt too big, that's all right; it's a word to grow into. Today is smaller and closer to home. Day twelve.","screen":["`NOT TWO.` → `DAY 12.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Marketing guru. Fitness guru. Some guru with an online course and a very confident voice. In everyday English the word has drifted toward a person with something to sell. In Hindu tradition it names something far more personal, and the thing it describes may already be in your life without the word ever being used. A grandparent at the kitchen table. A coach. A teacher who stayed after class. A friend who said one true sentence at exactly the right time. Today's word is for that person.","screen":["`MARKETING GURU. FITNESS GURU.` → `THE OLDER MEANING.` → `ONE TRUE SENTENCE, AT THE RIGHT TIME.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Guru. A teacher, but in this tradition more than a teacher of facts. A guru is someone who helps you see. An Upanishad called the Advaya Taraka plays with the sound of the word: the syllable gu, it says, means darkness, and ru is what stops it. Wordplay, not a dictionary entry, but a vivid one. A guru is the one who removes the dark. Yesterday you met one without the title. Uddalaka, with the cup of salt water, was a father acting as a guru, teaching his son by letting him taste. In stories like that one, learning happens up close, in a relationship, one person to another. The Mundaka Upanishad says that someone who wants to know should go to a teacher with fuel in hand, firewood for the teacher's fire, a small gift and a sign of willingness to serve. And the teacher, it says, should be learned in the texts and steady in Brahman. Two things there. The student comes humbly. The teacher has to be the real thing. Hindu traditions honor gurus in more than one way. Some follow one teacher for life, inside a long line of teachers. For some, God is the first guru, or the guru within. And on Guru Purnima, a full-moon festival, students thank their teachers. But keep the Advaya Taraka's picture close: someone stood where you were stuck, and the dark lifted. Today's title says it: anyone who turns a light on. A guru isn't only a job title. It can be a moment.","screen":["`GU — DARKNESS, RU — WHAT STOPS IT` · `A FATHER WITH A CUP OF SALT WATER` · `GO WITH FUEL IN HAND` · `A MOMENT, NOT JUST A TITLE` · then, large: `ANYONE WHO TURNS A LIGHT ON.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today, remember one light. Rest your hands in your lap. Bring to mind one time someone helped you see. One sentence, one afternoon, one look. Let their face come. Hear it again, if you can. Then, silently, thank them. If you pray, you can thank God for them too. Sit for a moment in what the room looked like once the light was on. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Guru. A teacher who helps you see; in one Upanishad's play on the sound, the one who removes the dark. Twelfth word.","screen":["`GURU — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: thank a teacher today. The actual person, if you can reach them. Two lines in a message are plenty. Day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY 108.`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"why 108","word":"japa","hook":"beads moving through someone's fingers, and the number on the string","length":"6:00","carry":"count what you want to keep","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: guru. Your line was thank a teacher today. Did a name come to mind? Today a mantra gets a counter. Day thirteen.","screen":["`THANK A TEACHER TODAY.` → `DAY 13.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Ask why a mala has a hundred and eight beads and you may hear a different answer each time. Watch one in use first. Beads move through someone's fingers, one at a time: wooden beads, or seeds, or stones, on a string, with one larger bead at the top that, by custom, the fingers don't pass over. The lips move without sound. The thumb draws the next bead forward. By tradition, a full Hindu mala carries a hundred and eight beads, plus that one at the top. The number can wait. A better question comes first: what is the person with the beads actually doing?","screen":["`BEADS ON A STRING.` → `LIPS MOVING, NO SOUND.` → `WHY 108?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Japa. Repeating a mantra, or a name of God, again and again, often counted. On day three, the Gayatri showed what a mantra is: a tool for the mind. Japa is using that tool again and again. The beads are the counter: one bead, one repetition. The hand keeps count so the mind doesn't have to, and the mind is free to stay with the sound. The Bhagavad Gita names japa among offerings. Here is the verse around it. In the tenth chapter, Krishna names where he can be found. Among things that don't move, the Himalaya. Among words, the one syllable, om. And among offerings, he says, I am the offering of japa. The Sanskrit is yajnanam japa-yajno 'smi. Of all the ways to make an offering, here is one that needs no fire and no flowers. Just a name, said again. Now, why a hundred and eight? People give answers from the sky, from lists of divine names, from the body. None of them comes from a text this lesson can put in front of you, so we'll leave the question open, honestly. But there's something simpler worth noticing about any fixed count. It gives the practice an end. You know when you're done. You don't have to decide, halfway through, whether you've done enough; the string decides. And a practice that can be finished today can be picked up again tomorrow, and the day after. That's how a practice gets kept. So the number on the string may hold mysteries, but it also does a plain job: it turns a good intention into something you can count.","screen":["`JAPA — A NAME, SAID AGAIN` · `THE HAND COUNTS, THE MIND RESTS` · `I AM JAPA, AMONG OFFERINGS` · `A FIXED COUNT GIVES AN END` · then, large: `COUNT WHAT YOU WANT TO KEEP.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: count on your fingers. No beads needed. Each finger has three sections. Touch your thumb to the top section of your index finger: one. The middle section: two. The base: three. Then on to the next finger. With each touch, say om silently on the out-breath, or, if you pray, a name of God you use. Go slowly. You won't reach a hundred and eight today, and you don't need to. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Japa. Repeating a mantra or a name, counted, so the hand keeps count and the mind can rest. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`JAPA — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: count what you want to keep. Once today, under a desk or on a train, count a few quiet oms on your fingers. Nobody will know. Day thirteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY IT'S SAID THREE TIMES.`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"why it's said three times","word":"shanti","hook":"om shanti shanti shanti: three times, never once, and there's a reason","length":"3:00","carry":"peace, peace, peace","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: japa. Your line was count what you want to keep. Two weeks today. Today, a word you've probably heard said three times in a row.","screen":["`COUNT WHAT YOU WANT TO KEEP.` → `TWO WEEKS.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Om shanti shanti shanti. It closes a lot of Hindu prayers, and more than a few yoga classes. Not once, not twice: three times. That isn't just for the rhythm. Traditional teachers read the three as a list. First, a guess at the word.","screen":["`OM SHANTI SHANTI SHANTI.` → `WHY THREE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"55 sec","voice":"Shanti means peace, the settled kind: calm water with nothing stirring it. The Bhagavad Gita puts it bluntly: for someone without peace, how can there be happiness? Now the three. Traditional teachers read the three shantis as three kinds of trouble, the three places a bad day can come from. Trouble from forces beyond anyone's control: storms, floods, the world. Trouble from other beings: the friend who didn't call, the driver who cut in. And trouble from within: your own body and your own mind. Three sources, three shantis. The teachers add something practical. There's little anyone can do about the first, more about the second, and the third is the one most within reach, and the easiest to skip. So the chant isn't padding. It goes down the list: peace out there, peace between us, peace in here.","screen":["`SHANTI — THE SETTLED KIND.` · `THE WORLD.` · `OTHER PEOPLE.` · `YOUR OWN MIND.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell, one shanti on each, said silently. First out-breath: toward the world. Second: toward one person you're not at peace with. You don't have to forgive them. Third: toward your own mind.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Shanti. Peace, the settled kind, said three times: the world, other people, your own mind. Fourteenth word. Puja, dharma, atman, Brahman, guru, japa, shanti: that's week two.","screen":["`SHANTI — your 14th word` → `WEEK TWO. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: peace, peace, peace. Next time something rattles you, there are three places it could have come from. Next week brings the doing words. Day fourteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: HANUMAN'S WHOLE PERSONALITY.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"Hanuman's whole personality","word":"seva","hook":"a mountain carried across the sky, because one herb was needed","length":"6:30","carry":"do something for someone, quietly","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, shanti. Your line was peace, peace, peace. Which of the three rattled you most? Week three starts now, and it opens with words you do with your hands. Day fifteen.","screen":["`PEACE, PEACE, PEACE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"On a battlefield in Lanka, Lakshmana, Rama's brother, lies wounded, barely breathing. A healer says there is a cure: certain herbs that grow on a mountain far to the north, and they have to be here fast. Hanuman goes. He crosses the sky, finds the mountain, and then he stops. The herbs are hiding from him, the story says, and he can't tell which ones are needed. There's no time to go back and ask. So he solves it another way. He lifts the whole peak out of the ground and carries it back through the air, herbs and all. Lakshmana breathes. Hanuman has a monkey's face, the strength of the wind, and a picture of him hangs in homes and shops. Today's word is why.","screen":["`A BROTHER WOUNDED. NO TIME TO LOSE.` → `HE CAN'T TELL WHICH HERB.` → `SO HE BRINGS THE MOUNTAIN.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Seva. Service. Not service as a job title, and not help with a receipt attached. Seva is doing what's needed because it's needed, with the self stepped out of the way. Hanuman is the tradition's living picture of it. Notice what the mountain story leaves out. There's no speech. No moment where he explains how hard it was. The whole story points at one thing: a man on the ground starts breathing again. The strength is enormous, and it's all aimed outward. The Ramayana does this with him more than once. When he leaps the ocean and finds Sita in Lanka, what he finally puts in Rama's hand is hers, not his: her jewel, and her message. Later devotional stories push the picture even further; people tell of him opening his own chest to show that Rama and Sita live inside it. Whatever he has, it's for someone else. And seva doesn't need a mountain. In temples and homes it's very ordinary work. Washing the floor before the festival. Chopping vegetables for a meal that strangers will eat. Sitting with a sick neighbor so the family can sleep. Nobody hands out credit for it, and that's part of the point. There's something else folded into the word. Some who practice it say seva done quietly works on the one doing it too: for as long as the hands are busy with someone else's need, the head gets a rest from itself. Hanuman's gifts were huge. The practice works with whatever size yours are.","screen":["`SEVA — SERVICE, SELF OUT OF THE WAY.` · `NO SPEECH. A MAN BREATHES AGAIN.` · `HER JEWEL, NOT HIS ADVENTURE.` · `A FLOOR, A MEAL, A NIGHT'S SLEEP.` · then, large: `WHATEVER HE HAS, IT'S FOR SOMEONE ELSE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today's practice: find your herb. Let your shoulders drop. Bring one thing to mind that you're good at. Cooking. Listening. Fixing a bike. Staying calm in a mess. Now let one person come to mind who could use exactly that this week. Don't plan it all out. Just decide: I'll do it, and I won't mention it. Then rest in the decision for the length of the sit. If you pray, you can offer the work before you do it. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Seva. Service done because it's needed, with the self out of the way. Hanuman carrying the mountain. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`SEVA — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: do something for someone, quietly. One small thing today. No post. No mention. Just a mountain moved, and nobody told. Day fifteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: LOVE AS A METHOD.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"love as a method","word":"bhakti","hook":"a leaf, a flower, a fruit, a little water: what the Gita says is enough","length":"6:30","carry":"devotion is a verb","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, seva, and Hanuman with a mountain in his hands. Your line was do something for someone, quietly. If you did it, did some part of you want to tell somebody? Day sixteen.","screen":["`DO SOMETHING FOR SOMEONE, QUIETLY.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A leaf. A flower. A piece of fruit. A little water. That list came up on day eight, at the home shrine, and back then the point was how small the gifts are. Today, read the rest of the sentence. In the ninth chapter of the Bhagavad Gita, Krishna says that whoever offers him one of these with love, he accepts it, because it was offered with love. The gift isn't what makes it count. The love is. And the Sanskrit word for that love sits right inside the verse. A whole way of walking this path is built on it, a way full of songs, drums, bells and late nights of singing. It's today's word.","screen":["`A LEAF, A FLOWER, AGAIN.` → `OFFERED WITH LOVE.` → `THE WORD INSIDE THE VERSE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Bhakti. Devotion. Love turned toward God and practiced on purpose, the way you'd practice an instrument. You've spent some of this camp with the head: atman, Brahman, the self and the whole. Bhakti is the heart, and the Gita treats it as a full path of its own. In the Gita's telling, nobody has to master the ideas first. The way in is love. Devotees aim that love at God in different forms, Krishna, Rama, Shiva, the Goddess, and the move is the same. Tradition remembers a woman who showed it. Mirabai was a princess in what is now Rajasthan, and the stories say that from childhood she thought of Krishna as her own. Married into a royal family, she kept singing to him in the temple, in public, with ordinary devotees. Her in-laws, the stories go, were furious. They sent her poison, and it did her no harm. Whatever you make of those details, her songs are still sung. Here's why bhakti is a method and not only a mood. Moods come and go. The lamp gets lit anyway. The song gets sung on the tired night too. Some who practice it say the feeling catches up with the doing, not the other way around. Go back to the leaf: what the verse looks for is the love it's offered with, and love, on this path, is something you keep doing. That's part of why this path can be so loud. Bells, drums, a crowd singing the same name over and over. That's bhakti with the volume up.","screen":["`BHAKTI — LOVE, PRACTICED ON PURPOSE.` · `THE HEART IS A PATH TOO.` · `MIRABAI KEPT SINGING.` · `THE LAMP GETS LIT ANYWAY.` · then, large: `DEVOTION IS A VERB.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"The practice today: offer a leaf. Find one small thing within reach. A sip of water, a flower, a breath, even just your open hand. Hold it for a moment. If you pray, you can offer it, with love, to God in whatever form you know. If you don't, offer it to whatever you love most in the world, and let the feeling be as big as it wants to be. Nobody's watching. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Bhakti. Devotion. Love you practice, not only love you feel. A leaf, offered whole. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`BHAKTI — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: devotion is a verb. Today, do one loving thing on a day you don't feel like it. That's the method, all of it. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE COURAGE NOT TO STRIKE.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"the courage not to strike","word":"ahimsa","hook":"the yoga list that starts before any pose: not-harming","length":"6:30","carry":"do no harm — start with your mouth","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, bhakti, love practiced like an instrument. Your line was devotion is a verb. Did you do the loving thing on a day you didn't feel like it? Today's word asks for a different kind of strength. Day seventeen.","screen":["`DEVOTION IS A VERB.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A message comes in late at night. It's unfair, and it stings, and your thumbs already know the reply. The sharp one. The one that would land. Here's a strange fact about yoga. Before the Yoga Sutras of Patanjali teach anything about posture, they set out a short list of restraints, the ground the rest is built on, and the first word on that list isn't stretch, or breathe. It's not-harming. So the first restraint in that text has something to say to those thumbs.","screen":["`A MESSAGE. A SHARP REPLY READY.` → `YOGA BEFORE THE POSES.` → `THE FIRST WORD: NOT-HARMING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Ahimsa. Himsa is harm, violence. Ahimsa is its opposite: not-harming. And it's easy to hear that as a soft word, a word for people who never stand up for anything. Gandhi, who put this word beside truth at the center of his public life, heard it the other way around. He was not a quiet man. He confronted an empire, and he refused the one weapon everyone expected him to pick up. Ahimsa takes nerve. When something hits a person, the body often wants to hit back, and ahimsa is the half-second where you don't add more harm to a moment that already has some. The Jains, close neighbors on the same soil, hold ahimsa at the heart of their path too. Now look at what Patanjali says comes of it. The Yoga Sutras teach that when someone is truly settled in not-harming, hostility falls away in their presence. Not because they won the argument. Because they stopped feeding it. Some teachers name three places ahimsa lives: in thought, in word, and in deed. Deeds are the obvious ones. Today's line starts with the middle one, words, because that's where the half-second is easiest to miss. The cutting joke. The thing said about someone who isn't in the room. The reply sent at night. One more layer. The vow points inward too. A voice in the head that calls a person worthless is himsa as well. Not-harming includes the one doing the not-harming.","screen":["`AHIMSA — NOT-HARMING.` · `NOT SOFT. IT TAKES NERVE.` · `HOSTILITY FALLS AWAY.` · `THOUGHT, WORD, DEED.` · then, large: `START WITH YOUR MOUTH.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Now, find the half-second. Bring back the last time you said something sharp, to someone or about someone. Don't punish yourself for it; that would be harm too. Just replay it, slowly, and this time stop at the moment before the words came out. Feel the push to strike. Let it pass through without acting on it. What could have been said instead? Maybe nothing. Rest there. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Ahimsa. Not-harming, in thought, word and deed. The courage not to strike. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`AHIMSA — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: do no harm — start with your mouth. Today, let one sharp sentence go unsaid, and notice what happens to the room. Day seventeen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY THE WORLD LOOKS SOLID.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"why the world looks solid","word":"maya","hook":"a rope on a dark path, and the snake that was never there","length":"6:30","carry":"hold it lightly","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, ahimsa, the courage not to strike. Your line was do no harm — start with your mouth. Did a sharp sentence go unsaid? Today we go back to the big ideas for a day. Day eighteen.","screen":["`DO NO HARM — START WITH YOUR MOUTH.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Dusk, on a path, and something is coiled across it. Your heart jumps before your mind has a word for it. Snake. You freeze. Your pulse is real. The fear is real. Then someone brings a lamp, and it's a rope. Just a rope, lying where it always was. Nothing about the rope changed. The snake was never there. And yet for a few seconds it ran your whole body. Teachers in this tradition tell that small story because it holds a very big word. A thing that is not what it seems, and works on you anyway.","screen":["`SOMETHING COILED ON THE PATH.` → `THE FEAR IS REAL.` → `A LAMP. A ROPE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Maya. It often gets translated as illusion, and that one word causes a lot of confusion. It sounds like the tradition is saying the world is fake. Your kitchen, your friends, your job, none of it real. That isn't what it says. The Svetasvatara Upanishad puts it this way: know that nature is maya, and that the great Lord is the one who works the maya. So maya isn't nothing. It's the power that makes the world appear the way it does, so convincingly that we stop looking past it. Schools of the tradition read this differently, and they've argued about it with great care. Advaita Vedanta, the school of Shankara, says the world we see is an appearance laid over Brahman, the way the snake is laid over the rope. The rope is what's really there. Other schools, like those of Ramanuja and Madhva, hold that the world is fully real, made by God, and that maya names God's wondrous power. Both readings agree on one thing. The surface is not the whole story. And that's where the word walks into an ordinary day. A post makes someone's life look perfect. A comment feels like an attack on all of you. A bad morning looks like a bad life. Each one is a snake on the path, until the lamp comes. Neither reading asks anyone to stop living in the world. Only to stop gripping it as if the surface were everything. Hold it lightly.","screen":["`NOT FAKE. NOT THE WHOLE STORY.` · `THE POWER THAT MAKES IT APPEAR.` · `SNAKE ON A ROPE, OR GOD'S POWER?` · `A POST. A COMMENT. A BAD MORNING.` · then, large: `HOLD IT LIGHTLY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today's practice: bring the lamp. Breathe once, slowly. Recall one thing this week that made your heart jump. A message, a look, a number, a picture of someone else's life. Feel the jump again, just a little. Don't push it away. Now look at it again, slowly, as if someone had brought a lamp. What is actually there? Let the tightness loosen as much as it wants to. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Maya. The power that makes the world appear, so well that we stop looking past the surface. The snake that turns out to be rope. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`MAYA — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: hold it lightly. Today, when something makes you jump, take one breath and ask: snake, or rope? Day eighteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE EXIT DOOR.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"the exit door","word":"moksha","hook":"a boy at Death's door turns down every treasure for one question","length":"6:30","carry":"freedom is the point","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, maya, and the snake that was a rope. Your line was hold it lightly. Did you catch a snake on the path? Today, the word for where this whole path is headed. Day nineteen.","screen":["`HOLD IT LIGHTLY.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A boy named Nachiketa is sitting at the door of Death's house. In the Katha Upanishad, his father, in a burst of temper, has said: I give you to Death. So the boy goes. Yama, the lord of death, is away, and Nachiketa waits on the doorstep without food for three nights. When Yama comes home, he's embarrassed to have kept a guest waiting, and he offers the boy three wishes. The first two are easy. The third is a question: what happens to a person after death? Yama tries to talk him out of it. Ask for something else, he says. Ask for long life, sons and grandsons, cattle, gold, a kingdom. Nachiketa says no.","screen":["`A BOY AT DEATH'S DOOR.` → `THREE WISHES.` → `TAKE THE GOLD INSTEAD.` → `NO.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Nachiketa's answer is the heart of today's word. He tells Death that all those treasures wear out, that wealth will never satisfy a person, that even a long life is short. Keep your horses, he says. I want to know. And Yama, impressed, begins to teach him. That turn, from the list of things to the thing under the list, points straight at today's word. Moksha means release. Liberation. Freedom. The tradition speaks of the soul moving through birth after birth, bound by its own actions and desires, and moksha is the knot finally coming untied. Hindu thought names several aims of a good life: dharma, doing what's yours to do; artha, making a living; kama, love and pleasure. None of those are bad. Moksha sits beyond them, as the freedom the others point toward. What that freedom is, the schools describe differently. Advaita Vedanta says it's waking up to what day ten and day eleven already hinted: that atman, the self, is Brahman, and always was. Devotional schools, such as those centered on Krishna, describe it as living forever in the loving presence of God, never dissolved, never alone. Some schools teach that a person can be free before death, liberated while still living. Notice the shape of Nachiketa's choice. He doesn't hate gold. He just won't trade the real question for it. That's the word in an ordinary day too: the moment you see that the next thing on the list was never going to be enough.","screen":["`KEEP YOUR HORSES. I WANT TO KNOW.` · `MOKSHA — RELEASE. THE KNOT UNTIED.` · `BEYOND DUTY, WEALTH AND PLEASURE.` · `ATMAN IS BRAHMAN, OR LOVE WITH GOD.` · then, large: `FREEDOM IS THE POINT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: set one thing down. Sit and breathe. Notice one thing you want right now. A message back, a better week, a number on a screen. Let the wanting be there. Now, just for the length of this sit, set it down beside you. Not forever. Just: for now, I don't need it. Breathe, and notice what's left when the wanting rests. If you pray, you can hold that space open to God. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Moksha. Release. Liberation. The knot untied, the freedom the other aims of life point toward. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`MOKSHA — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: freedom is the point. Once today, when you catch yourself chasing the next thing, set it down for one breath, the way Nachiketa set down the gold. Day nineteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: GOOD COMPANY.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"good company","word":"satsang","hook":"an old chain of verses that starts with who you sit with and ends in freedom","length":"6:30","carry":"good company changes you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, moksha, and a boy who told Death to keep his gold. Your line was freedom is the point. Did you set something down, even for a breath? One more word before the camp ends. Day twenty.","screen":["`FREEDOM IS THE POINT.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Yesterday's word was the top of the mountain: freedom. Fair question, then. Where do you start? There's an old Sanskrit song called Bhaja Govindam, traditionally credited to the teacher Shankara, and one of its verses answers that question like a row of dominoes. Each step knocks over the next, all the way up to freedom. You'd expect the first domino to be something hard. Years of study. Fasting. A cave in the mountains. It isn't. The very first domino is who you spend your time with.","screen":["`WHERE DO YOU START?` → `A ROW OF DOMINOES.` → `THE FIRST ONE: YOUR COMPANY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Satsang. Sat means true, real, good. Sang means company, being together. Satsang is good company, the company of what's true and of people turned toward it. Here's the chain from Bhaja Govindam, in plain words. From good company comes letting go. From letting go comes freedom from delusion. From freedom from delusion comes a mind that stays steady. And from a steady mind comes freedom while you're still alive. Look at what's in that chain. Delusion: think of the snake on the rope from day eighteen. Freedom is moksha from yesterday. And the thing that starts it all isn't a technique. It's a room. In Hindu life, a satsang is often an actual gathering. People meet in a home or a temple hall to sing, to hear a teacher, to read a text together, to sit in silence side by side. Nobody has to be advanced. You come, you sit, you listen. The idea underneath the verse is simple and a little uncomfortable: company shapes a person. A friend's worries can become your worries; a grandmother's patience can become your patience. The verse doesn't say go find perfect people. It puts company first in the chain, and leaves the question with you: what is your company doing to you, and how much time do you spend near what's true? That first domino has a name and a face.","screen":["`SAT — TRUE. SANG — COMPANY.` · `GOOD COMPANY, THEN LETTING GO, THEN FREEDOM.` · `A ROOM, NOT A TECHNIQUE.` · `WE TAKE THE SHAPE OF OUR COMPANY.` · then, large: `GOOD COMPANY CHANGES YOU.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: sit in company. Sit and breathe. Bring to mind one person whose presence makes you steadier. A friend, a grandparent, a teacher, someone in a story. Let their face come. Notice what happens to your breathing near them. Then widen it, to anyone, anywhere, who stops today to pray or to sit still. Let yourself be one of them. If you pray, you can hold them all before God. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Satsang. Good company. The company of what's true, and the first domino on the way to freedom. Twentieth word.","screen":["`SATSANG — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: good company changes you. Today, spend ten unhurried minutes with one person who steadies you. In person, if you can. Day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"the mountain ahead","word":"the Gita","hook":"back to the battlefield: a warrior drops his bow, and the conversation begins","length":"6:45","carry":"you have the right to act. Not to the fruit","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, satsang. Your line was good company changes you. Now, the whole camp, quickly: Ganesha, om, Gayatri, namaste, arti, ishta devata, karma, puja, dharma, atman, Brahman, guru, japa, shanti, seva, bhakti, ahimsa, maya, moksha, satsang. Twenty words. Today makes twenty-one. Day twenty-one.","screen":["`GOOD COMPANY CHANGES YOU.` → the strand, twenty words lit."]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Go back to day nine. A battlefield, two armies drawn up, conch shells blowing. Arjuna, a great archer, asks his charioteer to drive him out between the lines so he can see who he's about to fight. He looks across and sees teachers, uncles, cousins, friends. His mouth goes dry. His bow slips from his hand. And he sits down in the chariot and says he will not fight. We left him there, with his driver, Krishna, beside him. Today we find out what happens next. Because the whole conversation that follows has a name, and it's the last word of this camp.","screen":["`BACK TO THE BATTLEFIELD.` → `HIS BOW SLIPS.` → `WHAT DOES THE DRIVER SAY?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The Bhagavad Gita. The Song of the Lord. It sits inside the great epic, the Mahabharata, and it's a conversation between two people in a chariot. On day nine you heard that the driver is Krishna, and a great deal more than a driver. Krishna does more than give orders. He teaches. He talks about the self that cannot be killed, which you met as atman. He talks about acting without grabbing, about loving God, about seeing clearly. Look back at this week and you'll hear echoes. Action with the self out of the way, the way seva works. Love as a path of its own, bhakti. The Gita holds these paths side by side. Then the line this camp saved for last. In the second chapter Krishna says, in plain words: your right is to the action alone, never to its fruits. Don't let the fruit be your reason for acting. And the verse keeps going: don't let that become a reason to do nothing, either. It isn't a way out of the fight. It's a way to stand up and act without being owned by the result. And near the end of the Gita comes a surprise. After everything, Krishna tells Arjuna to think it over fully, and then do as he chooses. He hands the choice back. In the fourth camp you'll read this text itself, line by line. For now you get the door, and the sentence that opens it.","screen":["`THE SONG OF THE LORD, IN A CHARIOT.` · `THE WHOLE CAMP IS IN THERE.` · `NOT A WAY OUT. A WAY TO STAND UP.` · `THINK IT OVER. THEN CHOOSE.` · then, large: `YOU HAVE THE RIGHT TO ACT. NOT TO THE FRUIT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: pick up the bow. Sit and breathe. Press your palms together at your chest, the way you did on day one. Bring to mind one thing that's yours to do this week, the thing you've been sitting down in the chariot about. Picture doing it, fully. Then open your hand, and let go of how it turns out. The action is yours. The fruit isn't. If you pray, you can offer the result to God. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Gita. The Song of the Lord, a conversation in a chariot that gets a warrior back on his feet. Twenty-first word, and the door to the mountain.","screen":["`THE GITA — your 21st word` → the strand complete → `CAMP 1. FIRST WORDS. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you have the right to act. Not to the fruit. Do one thing today that's yours to do, and let the result go. Tomorrow the stories begin, with a goddess, a bath, and a boy made of earth. Day twenty-one.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE BOY AT THE DOOR.`"]}]}},"ISLAM":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"the words before every beginning","word":"bismillah","hook":"the words before every beginning — a meal, a drive, an exam","length":"3:00","carry":"start in the name of","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Before the first bite of dinner. Before the car pulls out of the driveway. At the top of an exam, before the first answer. Under the breath, fast, so it's easy to miss: bismillah. Many Muslims have said it since before they could read. It goes before a beginning, so it goes first here too. First, a guess.","screen":["`BEFORE THE FIRST BITE.` → `BEFORE THE CAR PULLS OUT.` → `BISMILLAH.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Bismillah is three pieces. Bi: in, or with. Ism: name. Allah: God. In the name of God. Allah isn't the name of a separate god; it's the Arabic word for God, and Arabic-speaking Christians and Jews say it too. The full line is bismillah ar-rahman ar-rahim: “In the name of Allah, the Beneficent, the Merciful.” All but one of the Qur'an's chapters, called surahs, open with it. So what does it do? Starting in someone's name means you're not starting alone. It's like working under a name you respect: the work carries that name now, so you want it to be worth carrying. That's why it goes before small things, not only big ones. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ once told a boy eating beside him: say bismillah, and eat with your right hand. And for the times you forget, because everyone does, another of his sayings gives a way back in: say it when you remember, in the name of God, at its beginning and its end. Nobody gets locked out of their own lunch.","screen":["`BI — IN. ISM — NAME. ALLAH — GOD.` · `ALL BUT ONE SURAH OPENS WITH IT.` · `YOU'RE NOT STARTING ALONE.` · `FORGOT? SAY IT WHEN YOU REMEMBER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Think of the very next thing you'll do after this: coffee, an email, the car. Breathe in. On the way out, say the word for it, aloud or under your breath: bismillah. Or simply give it one full pause before it begins.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Bismillah. In the name of God: the words before a beginning, so nothing starts alone. First word on your strand.","screen":["`BISMILLAH — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: start in the name of. It's why the word comes before a meal, a drive, an exam, and why it came first here. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHAT YOUR PRAYER IS ACTUALLY SAYING.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"what your prayer is actually saying","word":"al-Fatiha","hook":"seven verses said in every prayer, many times a day, and what they actually say","length":"3:00","carry":"a conversation, answered line by line","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: bismillah, in the name of God. Today, the prayer that opens with it.","screen":["`BISMILLAH ✓` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"It's said in every unit of every prayer: seventeen times on an ordinary day. Many Muslims learned it by heart as children and could say it half asleep. Ask what it means, line by line, and a lot of people go quiet. There's a question people really ask: what's the point of praying in Arabic if I don't understand it? Today, the whole thing, in plain English.","screen":["`SEVENTEEN TIMES A DAY.` → `WHAT DOES IT SAY?` → `IN PLAIN ENGLISH.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"It's called al-Fatiha, the opening: the first surah of the Qur'an, from a root meaning to open. A key, in Arabic, is a miftah. Seven short verses. Here they are, in plain words. In the name of God, the Beneficent, the Merciful. Praise be to God, Lord of the Worlds. The Beneficent, the Merciful. Owner of the Day of Judgment. You alone we worship, and You alone we ask for help. Show us the straight path: the path of those You have favored, not of those who earn anger, nor of those who go astray. That's all of it. Notice the shape. It opens in yesterday's name. Then praise, and mercy said twice. Then it turns from talking about God to talking to God: You. And when it finally asks for something, it asks for one thing. Not money, not health. Directions. A saying the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ reported from God describes it as a conversation. When someone praying says, praise be to God, Lord of the Worlds, God answers: My servant has praised Me. Line after line, an answer comes back. So here's the answer to the Arabic question. The words don't change. Knowing them changes how you say them. Even one line, known while you say it, gives a wandering mind somewhere to stand.","screen":["`AL-FATIHA — THE OPENING.` · `SEVEN VERSES, IN PLAIN ENGLISH.` · `PRAISE FIRST, THEN ONE REQUEST.` · `A CONVERSATION, ANSWERED.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Breathe in. On the way out, say the one request, in Arabic or in English: ihdina s-sirat al-mustaqim. Show us the straight path. It's the line the whole prayer leans toward.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Al-Fatiha. The opening: seven verses, praise first, then one request. Second word on your strand.","screen":["`AL-FATIHA — your 2nd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: a conversation, answered line by line. Next time you hear it, you'll know what's being said. Day two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE WATER BEFORE THE WORDS.`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"the water before the words","word":"wudu","hook":"washing before prayer: why these parts, in this order","length":"3:00","carry":"rinse the day off","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: al-Fatiha, a conversation, answered line by line. Today, what comes before a single word of it.","screen":["`AL-FATIHA ✓` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Near the door of many mosques there's a row of taps along a wall, a low ledge to sit on, a drain along the floor. Before the prayer, people stop here first. Sleeves up, shoes off. Water over the hands, into the mouth, over the face, up the arms, over the feet. If you grew up with it, you could do it with your eyes closed. So: why these parts, and why this order?","screen":["`A ROW OF TAPS BY THE DOOR.` → `HANDS, FACE, ARMS, FEET.` → `WHY THESE? WHY THIS ORDER?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"It's called wudu, from a root with the sense of brightness, the way a washed face shines. The core is one verse of the Qur'an: “When ye rise up for prayer, wash your faces, and your hands up to the elbows,” then wipe the head, and then the feet. Face, arms, head, feet: that's the order, and it comes from the verse itself. The rest, the hands first, the mouth and nose, the ears, families learned from the way the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ showed his companions. Here's the surprising part. It isn't really about dirt. People make wudu when they're already clean. A saying of the Prophet pictures it this way: as the water runs off your face, the wrongs your eyes looked at run off with it; off the hands, what the hands did; off the feet, where the feet went. So it's a reset, done with the body: the step between the street and the prayer. It lasts until something breaks it, like sleep, so it isn't always a fresh wash for every prayer. And when there's no water, or water would harm you, the same verse allows clean earth instead. That's called tayammum. The reset is never out of reach.","screen":["`WUDU — FROM A ROOT OF BRIGHTNESS.` · `FACE, ARMS, HEAD, FEET.` · `THE WRONGS RUN OFF WITH THE WATER.` · `NO WATER? CLEAN EARTH.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. If there's water near you, wash your hands slowly and feel it run off your fingers. If not, rest your hands open and picture cool water over them, then over your face. With each breath out, let one thing from today run off with it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Wudu. The washing before prayer: face, arms, head and feet. A reset with water. Third word.","screen":["`WUDU — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: rinse the day off. The day doesn't have to come with you into the prayer. Day three.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: INSHALLAH OR MASHALLAH?`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"inshallah or mashallah?","word":"inshallah","hook":"two words people mix up: one looks ahead, one looks at what's already here","length":"3:00","carry":"inshallah for tomorrow, mashallah for today","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: wudu, a reset with water. Today, two words that get mixed up all the time.","screen":["`WUDU ✓` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"See you tomorrow? Inshallah. Will the report be done by Friday? Inshallah. A cousin's new baby, a friend's new apartment, a kid's first goal: mashallah. They sound alike, they both have Allah in them, and people swap them around, Muslims included. There's even a family joke that inshallah is the politest way to say probably not. The real difference fits in one line.","screen":["`SEE YOU TOMORROW? INSHALLAH.` → `A NEW BABY? MASHALLAH.` → `WHAT'S THE DIFFERENCE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"In sha' Allah: if God wills. It looks ahead, at something that hasn't happened yet. It comes from the eighteenth surah of the Qur'an, the Cave: “And say not of anything: Lo! I shall do that tomorrow, Except if Allah will.” So it isn't maybe. Maybe is about your mood. Inshallah is about what you don't control. You plan completely, and admit that tomorrow isn't yours to hand out. It was never a reason to skip the work, either. A man once asked the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ whether he should leave his camel untied and trust God. Tie it, came the answer, and trust. Now its twin. Ma sha' Allah: what God has willed. It looks at something good that's already here: a baby, a new job, a view that stops you. It says, this is a gift, not only my doing. And it comes from the same surah. A few verses on, a man walks proudly through his gardens, and his friend asks why he didn't say, on the way in, what God wills. So: inshallah for tomorrow. Mashallah for what's already here.","screen":["`INSHALLAH — IF GOD WILLS.` · `TIE YOUR CAMEL, AND TRUST.` · `MASHALLAH — WHAT GOD HAS WILLED.` · `SAME SURAH, TWO DIRECTIONS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, think of one thing you've planned for tomorrow. On the second, notice the part of it that's up to you, and the part that isn't. On the third, think of one good thing already in your life, and let it be a gift.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Inshallah. If God wills: a real plan, held with an open hand. And its twin, mashallah, for what's already here. Fourth word.","screen":["`INSHALLAH — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: inshallah for tomorrow, mashallah for today. That's the whole difference. That's day four.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: IS DOUBT OKAY?`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"is doubt okay?","word":"iman","hook":"the question people type late at night: is it haram to doubt?","length":"3:00","carry":"ask, so the heart can rest","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: inshallah for tomorrow, mashallah for today. Today, a question many people only ever ask a search bar.","screen":["`INSHALLAH ✓` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"It's late, the phone is the only light in the room, and someone types a question they'd never ask out loud: is it haram to doubt your faith? A question from a class that won't go away. A stretch when prayer felt empty. Underneath it is one worry: if I have questions, am I still in? Before any rule, this path starts there, because the oldest answers are gentler than people expect.","screen":["`LATE AT NIGHT, ONE QUESTION.` → `IS IT HARAM TO DOUBT?` → `AM I STILL IN?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Start with the word for faith: iman. Its root, a-m-n, is the root of safety and of trust; the same root gives amana, something entrusted to you. So iman is less a certificate you hold and more a trust you live in. The Qur'an even speaks of believers adding faith to their faith, as if it can grow. That's why people say my iman is low when prayer goes dry for a while: a faith that can grow can also dip, and come back. Now the question. Some of the Prophet Muhammad's ﷺ companions once came to him, troubled. We find thoughts in ourselves, they said, so bad we can hardly bring ourselves to say them. Do you really find that? he asked. Yes. That, he said, is clear faith. Not the thought itself: being troubled by it. Their discomfort showed how much they cared. And the Qur'an tells of a prophet who asked. Ibrahim, Abraham, says: my Lord, show me how You give life to the dead. Don't you believe? comes the reply. And he answers: “Yea, but (I ask) in order that my heart may be at ease.” He isn't scolded. He's shown. Scholars have a lot to say about the difference between a passing question and turning away, and this path will get there. For today, the plain part: in these stories, asking is a way of looking for rest.","screen":["`IMAN — FROM A ROOT OF SAFETY AND TRUST.` · `THAT IS CLEAR FAITH.` · `SHOW ME, SO MY HEART MAY BE AT EASE.` · `ASKING, TO FIND REST.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, let one honest question come up, whatever it is. On the second, don't answer it; just hold it, the way Ibrahim held his. On the third, breathe out, and let your heart rest a moment, with the question still open.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Iman. Faith: from a root of safety and trust, a faith that can grow, with room for questions. Fifth word.","screen":["`IMAN — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: ask, so the heart can rest. Ibrahim asked, and he wasn't turned away. Day five, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: ONE DAY OF RAMADAN.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"one day of Ramadan","word":"Ramadan","hook":"a kitchen at four in the morning: one day of the fast, from suhoor to iftar","length":"3:00","carry":"hunger teaches","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: iman, a faith with room to ask. Today, a month many Muslims know from the inside, told start to finish.","screen":["`IMAN ✓` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Four in the morning, and the kitchen light is on. A family, half asleep, eating dates and eggs and drinking as much water as they can before the sky starts to pale. Then, from dawn, nothing: no food, not even a sip of water, until sunset. If you grew up with it, you know every hour of that day. It's rarely told start to finish, with what it's for. So here's one day.","screen":["`A KITCHEN AT FOUR IN THE MORNING.` → `THEN NOTHING UNTIL SUNSET.` → `ONE DAY, START TO FINISH.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Ramadan is a month of the Islamic calendar, which follows the moon, so it slides earlier every year and passes through every season. Here's one day of it. Before dawn, suhoor, the early meal. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said there's a blessing in it, so people eat even when they'd rather sleep. At first light comes the dawn prayer, and the fast begins: no food, no drink, until sunset. That's the fourth pillar, sawm. Then the day itself: work, school, errands, and a hunger that keeps tapping you on the shoulder. Here's what it's for. The Qur'an says it plainly: “Fasting is prescribed for you, even as it was prescribed for those before you, that ye may ward off (evil).” The word underneath is taqwa, a careful awareness. The hunger is a tool, not the point. Another of the Prophet's sayings makes that sharp: whoever doesn't give up lying, God has no need of their giving up food and drink. At sunset, iftar: the fast breaks, often with a date and water, the way the Prophet broke his. And it's built for real people: the sick and travelers are excused, and make the days up later. Why this month? The Qur'an names it as the month the Qur'an was revealed.","screen":["`SUHOOR — THE EARLY MEAL.` · `DAWN TO SUNSET: NO FOOD, NO WATER.` · `THAT YE MAY WARD OFF EVIL.` · `IFTAR — A DATE AND WATER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, notice your body: is there any hunger or thirst in it, even a little? On the second, picture the next thing you'll eat or drink. On the third, picture that first sip, taken slowly, the way it's taken at sunset.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Ramadan. The month of the fast, dawn to sunset, and the month the Qur'an was revealed. Sixth word.","screen":["`RAMADAN — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: hunger teaches. The empty hours are where the attention grows. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: TWO EIDS, NOT ONE.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"two Eids, not one","word":"Eid","hook":"new clothes, an envelope from an uncle, and the second Eid people forget","length":"3:00","carry":"joy that comes back around","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: one day of Ramadan, dark to dark. Six words so far: bismillah, al-Fatiha, wudu, inshallah, iman, Ramadan. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`BISMILLAH · AL-FATIHA · WUDU · INSHALLAH · IMAN · RAMADAN`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The morning after Ramadan ends, kids are up early in new clothes. An uncle hands out envelopes of money. The family heads to a prayer held in the morning, and then it's visits, sweets, and Eid Mubarak said so many times it blurs. And yet plenty of people, Muslims included, half forget there's a second Eid a couple of months later, or what makes it different. Today, both.","screen":["`NEW CLOTHES. AN ENVELOPE.` → `EID MUBARAK.` → `AND A SECOND EID.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Eid is tied to a root meaning to return: a day that comes back around, every year. When the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ arrived in Medina, a saying reports, people there had two old days of games and celebration. God has given you two better ones, he told them: the day of breaking the fast, and the day of sacrifice. The first is Eid al-Fitr. Fitr means breaking the fast. It closes Ramadan, and before the Eid prayer, each household gives a small gift of food, zakat al-fitr, so people in need can celebrate too. The second is Eid al-Adha, the feast of sacrifice. It falls during the hajj, the pilgrimage to Mecca, and it remembers Ibrahim, Abraham, and the ram given in place of his son. Families who are able offer an animal, and share the meat with relatives, neighbors and people in need. So one Eid closes a month of fasting, and the other comes with the pilgrimage. Both begin with a prayer, and both are built so nobody is left out of the feast. And Eid Mubarak? A blessed Eid.","screen":["`EID — A DAY THAT COMES BACK AROUND.` · `EID AL-FITR — BREAKING THE FAST.` · `EID AL-ADHA — THE FEAST OF SACRIFICE.` · `NOBODY LEFT OUT OF THE FEAST.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, picture a table set for a feast. On the second, picture who's around it: your people. On the third, add one more chair, for someone who might not have a table of their own today.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Eid. A feast that comes back around: al-Fitr after the fast, al-Adha with the pilgrimage. Seventh word: a week's worth.","screen":["`EID — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: joy that comes back around. That's week one: seven words, and what's inside each. Tomorrow, the greeting that is a wish.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE GREETING THAT IS A WISH.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"the greeting that is a wish","word":"salaam","hook":"the greeting that is a wish; s-l-m — salaam, Muslim, Islam","length":"3:15","carry":"peace, upon you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Eid, joy that comes back around. That was week one. Week two starts with the first word two Muslims say to each other. Day eight.","screen":["`EID — JOY THAT COMES BACK AROUND.` → `DAY 8.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"In a shop, at an airport gate, across a dinner table: two people meet, and before how are you, before the weather, one says as-salamu alaykum. The other answers straight back: wa alaykum as-salam. It happens so fast it sounds like hello. It isn't hello. Hello says I see you. This greeting hands the other person something. And the word at its center is hiding inside the name of the faith itself.","screen":["`AS-SALAMU ALAYKUM.` → `IT SOUNDS LIKE HELLO.` → `IT'S A WISH.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Salaam means peace. As-salamu alaykum: peace be upon you. A wish, given in the first second of meeting someone. Here's the part that surprises people. Arabic builds its words from roots, usually three letters. This one is s, l, m. Put different vowels around it and you get salaam: peace. You get Islam: the surrender, the making of peace with God. And you get Muslim: a person who does that. So every time two Muslims greet each other, they say a piece of the faith's own name. The Qur'an even gives the word to God: among God's names is As-Salam, Peace itself. And it sets a rule for answering: “When ye are greeted with a greeting, greet ye with a better than it or return it.” The minimum is to give the peace back. Better is to add to it: and upon you peace, and the mercy of God, and His blessings. The greeting grows as it goes around. A saying of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ ties it to love: you won't truly love one another, he told his companions, until you spread salaam among yourselves. Not a feeling first and then a greeting. The greeting first, and the love grows from it.","screen":["`SALAAM — PEACE.` · `S-L-M: SALAAM, ISLAM, MUSLIM.` · `ANSWER WITH BETTER, OR THE SAME.` · `THE GREETING FIRST, THEN THE LOVE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Sit comfortably and let your breathing slow. Bring to mind one person you'll see today. Breathe in, and on the way out send them the word, silently: salaam, peace upon you. Then someone harder to greet. The same word, the same breath. Stay with it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Salaam. Peace: the greeting that's a gift, and the root inside Islam and Muslim. Eighth word.","screen":["`SALAAM — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: peace, upon you. The Qur'an's rule for answering: with better, or at least the same. Day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: A HOUSE BUILT ON FIVE.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"a house built on five","word":"the five pillars","hook":"not a checklist: a building, and the building is made of time","length":"3:30","carry":"five things that hold a life up","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: salaam. Your line was peace, upon you. Today, the whole shape of a Muslim life, in one sitting. Day nine.","screen":["`SALAAM — PEACE, UPON YOU.` → `DAY 9.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The five pillars of Islam often get learned like a list for a test. Witness. Prayer. Giving. Fasting. Pilgrimage. Five boxes, done. Said that way, it sounds like a checklist. But the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ didn't describe them as a list. He described them as a building. And the building turns out to be made of time.","screen":["`FIVE WORDS, FIVE BOXES?` → `NOT A CHECKLIST.` → `A BUILDING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Islam is built on five, a saying of the Prophet goes. Built, like a house; pillars are what keep the roof up. One: the shahada, the witness. There is no god but God, and Muhammad is the messenger of God. One sentence, said to become Muslim and said inside every prayer. Its clock is always. Two: salat, prayer, five times a day, at times that move with the sun. Its clock is the day. Three: zakat, giving a set share of savings to people in need, once a year. Four: sawm, the Ramadan fast you met on day six. Their clock is the year. Five: hajj, the pilgrimage to Mecca, once in a lifetime. Its clock is a life. Now look at the shape. Always. Every day. Every year. Once in a life. Circles inside circles. And the house fits the person living in it: the Qur'an lets the sick and the traveler make up missed fast days, and asks for the hajj only from those able to make the journey. Muslims also draw the house in more than one way. Sunni teaching usually names these five as the pillars. Shia teaching usually speaks of roots, the core beliefs, and branches, the practices: the witness lives in the roots, and prayer, fasting, giving and pilgrimage among the branches. Same house, drawn two ways.","screen":["`ISLAM IS BUILT ON FIVE.` · `ALWAYS, DAILY, YEARLY, ONCE.` · `BUILT TO FIT THE PERSON.` · `ROOTS AND BRANCHES: DRAWN TWO WAYS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Sit comfortably. The pillars are circles of time, so borrow the shape. Bring to mind something that holds up your day, and let it stay a moment. Then something that holds up your year. Then something you'd want to do once in your life. Nothing to do about any of them. Just see what's holding you up.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The five pillars. Witness, prayer, giving, fasting, pilgrimage: not five boxes but a house built on five. Ninth word.","screen":["`THE FIVE PILLARS — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: five things that hold a life up. Always, daily, yearly, once. Day nine.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY FIVE TIMES.`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"why five times","word":"salat","hook":"why five times; standing, bowing, forehead to the ground","length":"3:30","carry":"the body prays too","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the five pillars, a house built on five. Today, the second pillar, up close. Day ten.","screen":["`THE FIVE PILLARS — A HOUSE BUILT ON FIVE.` → `DAY 10.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"Ten past one on a weekday, in the stockroom behind a hardware store. One of the staff flattens a clean cardboard box on the floor, turns to face a particular direction, and stands very still. Then he bows, hands on his knees. Then he goes all the way down, forehead to the cardboard, between the paint cans and the delivery crates. A few minutes later he folds the box away and goes back to the register. Why five times a day? And why the whole body?","screen":["`A STOCKROOM, JUST AFTER ONE.` → `FOREHEAD TO THE FLOOR.` → `WHY FIVE? WHY THE BODY?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Salat. It's the second pillar, and it isn't quite what English means by prayer. Du'a is asking, in your own words, any time. Salat is a set shape, at set times, with set words. The Qur'an says: “Lo! worship preserveth from lewdness and iniquity.” The five times follow the sun. Fajr before sunrise. Dhuhr after midday. Asr in the afternoon. Maghrib just after sunset. Isha at night. Where does five come from? A saying of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ tells it. On the Night Journey he was given fifty daily prayers. On the way back, Musa, Moses, asked what he'd been given, and said: your people can't manage that; go back and ask for less. He went back, again and again, until it was five. And God said: five, with the reward of fifty. Many pray them at five separate times; Shia practice also allows joining them into three sittings, and the prayers are still five. Now the body. Each prayer is made of units called rak'ahs, and each moves through the same shapes. Standing, and reciting al-Fatiha, the seven verses from day two. Bowing, back flat, hands on the knees. Standing again. Then sujud: forehead, nose, palms, knees and toes on the ground. Another saying tells that a person is never nearer to God than there. The part of you that thinks, set down where your feet were.","screen":["`SALAT — A SET SHAPE, AT SET TIMES.` · `FIVE TIMES, FOLLOWING THE SUN.` · `FIFTY, THEN FIVE.` · `STANDING, BOWING, FOREHEAD DOWN.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"45 sec","voice":"If you're able, stand for a moment, then bow a little, hands on your knees, and notice how your breath changes in each shape. If you'd rather stay seated, lower your head toward your chest, slowly, and feel what the body says when it bows. Stay there, and let the body do the praying for once, without any words.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Salat. The prayer in its set shape: five times a day, standing, bowing, forehead to the ground. Tenth word.","screen":["`SALAT — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the body prays too. And if you prayed for a while and then stopped, you're in plenty of company: many come back one prayer at a time. Day ten.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THANK-YOU AS A REFLEX.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"thank-you as a reflex","word":"alhamdulillah","hook":"thank-you as a reflex","length":"3:15","carry":"praise, whatever the day did","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: salat. Your line was the body prays too. Today, a word many Muslims say once the prayer is done, and all day long. Day eleven.","screen":["`SALAT — THE BODY PRAYS TOO.` → `DAY 11.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"Ask a Muslim friend how they are, and there's a good chance the first word back isn't fine. It's alhamdulillah. Got the job? Alhamdulillah. Didn't get the job? Very often, also alhamdulillah. Someone sneezes: alhamdulillah. A grandmother, asked about her knees, which are terrible: alhamdulillah. It comes out so fast it sounds like a reflex. It is one, but a reflex people build on purpose, and the word says more than thanks.","screen":["`HOW ARE YOU?` → `GOT THE JOB. DIDN'T GET THE JOB.` → `THE SAME WORD, EITHER WAY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Al-hamdu li-llah. Al: the. Hamd: praise. Li-llah: belongs to God. The praise belongs to God. Not quite thanks, God. Arabic has a separate word for thanks, shukr, and scholars draw a line between the two. Thanks answers something done for you. Praise answers who someone is. You can only give thanks for the good day; you can give praise on any day. That's why the word keeps turning up on the bad ones. The root of hamd, h-m-d, is in a name you know. Muhammad means the one who is praised, again and again. Ahmad, Mahmoud, Hamid: the same three letters. It's woven into the day. A saying of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ says: when you sneeze, say alhamdulillah, and whoever hears you answers, may God have mercy on you. After the prayer, many count it thirty-three times, on their fingers or on beads, among other short phrases. And another saying explains why it fits any kind of day. The believer's situation is a wonder, the Prophet said: when good comes, they're grateful, and that's good for them; when hardship comes, they're patient, and that's good for them too. Alhamdulillah is the two-second version. It doesn't pretend the bad day was good. It says the praise still belongs where it belonged this morning.","screen":["`THE PRAISE BELONGS TO GOD.` · `PRAISE, NOT ONLY THANKS.` · `H-M-D — THE ROOT OF MUHAMMAD.` · `GOOD DAY OR HARD DAY, BOTH COUNT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Sit comfortably. Bring to mind one thing that went right today, and stay with it. Then one thing that didn't. Then hold both at once, the good one and the hard one, and notice they belong to the same day. If you want, let the word ride out on a breath: alhamdulillah.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Alhamdulillah. The praise belongs to God. Praise, not only thanks, so it fits every kind of day. Eleventh word.","screen":["`ALHAMDULILLAH — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: praise, whatever the day did. It fits the good days and the hard ones. Day eleven.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"PRAISE BE TO ALLAH, LORD OF THE WORLDS.\"`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"\"Praise be to Allah, Lord of the Worlds\"","word":"Rabb","hook":"the lord who raises and tends every world, not just yours","length":"3:15","carry":"every world is tended","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: alhamdulillah, the praise belongs to God. Today it turns out to be the first word of a verse you say in every prayer. Day twelve.","screen":["`ALHAMDULILLAH — PRAISE, WHATEVER THE DAY DID.` → `DAY 12.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"On day two you heard al-Fatiha whole, all seven verses in one go. For the next five days it slows down: one verse at a time, inside words you may have said a thousand times. Verse two begins with yesterday's word. Praise be to God. Then it points that praise somewhere enormous. Not at your day. At every world there is.","screen":["`AL-FATIHA, SLOWED DOWN.` → `VERSE TWO: ALHAMDULILLAH.` → `EVERY WORLD THERE IS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Verse two: “Praise be to Allah, Lord of the Worlds.” In Arabic, al-hamdu lillahi rabbi l-'alamin. The first half is yesterday's word. The new word is Rabb. English says Lord, which is right, but thin. Rabb is the one who owns and the one who looks after. Commentators explain it with the picture of raising something: bringing a child, or a seedling, along step by step until it's grown. So a rabb isn't a distant owner with paperwork. It's the one who feeds, shelters and brings you up. Then al-'alamin: the worlds. Not the world: the worlds, plural. Commentators have listed people, angels, animals, every kind of created thing. The ant colony under the sidewalk. The deep sea. The inside of a stranger's life on the train. All of it, the verse says, is being raised and tended by one Rabb. And remember the conversation from day two. At this verse, God answers: My servant has praised Me. The praise isn't said into empty air. It's heard, and answered.","screen":["`ALHAMDULILLAH — ALL PRAISE.` · `RABB — THE ONE WHO RAISES.` · `AL-'ALAMIN — THE WORLDS, PLURAL.` · `MY SERVANT HAS PRAISED ME.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Sit comfortably. First, your own world: one thing in it that's being looked after, even today. Stay with it. Then a world that isn't yours: a bird, a stranger, a field somewhere you'll never see. Then hold both at once. If you want, the Arabic can ride out on a breath: al-hamdu lillahi rabbi l-'alamin.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Rabb. Lord: the one who owns, raises and looks after, here the Lord of all the worlds. Twelfth word.","screen":["`RABB — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: every world is tended. Yours, and every one you'll never see. Day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"THE BENEFICENT, THE MERCIFUL.\"`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"\"The Beneficent, the Merciful\"","word":"rahma","hook":"mercy, named twice, before the word judgment ever appears","length":"3:15","carry":"mercy twice before anything else","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Rabb, the one who raises and looks after every world. Your line was every world is tended. Day thirteen.","screen":["`RABB — EVERY WORLD IS TENDED.` → `DAY 13.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A mare stands in a field with her foal pressed close against her legs. She's about to shift her weight, and she stops, lifts one hoof, and holds it in the air, so she won't step on him. A saying of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ uses exactly that picture: the gentleness creatures show each other comes from one small share of a much greater mercy. Today's verse is two words long, and both of them are that mercy.","screen":["`A MARE LIFTS HER HOOF.` → `ONE SHARE OF MERCY.` → `VERSE THREE: TWO WORDS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Verse three: “The Beneficent, the Merciful.” Ar-Rahman ar-Rahim. If that sounds familiar, it should: they're the two names from verse one, the bismillah, said again. Both come from one root, r-h-m, and the noun is today's word: rahma, mercy. The same root makes rahim, the womb. In this language, mercy has the shape of the place where you were held before you were born. Why two names? One common explanation: ar-Rahman is mercy as wide as everything, reaching every creature. Ar-Rahim is mercy that stays, that keeps coming back to you in particular. And the mare? The same saying says God made mercy in a hundred parts and sent one part into the world. With that one part, creatures are gentle with each other. The other ninety-nine are kept back. Now look at where the verse sits. Praise, then mercy, then mercy again, and only after that, tomorrow, the word judgment. Another saying puts it plainly: when God created the world, He wrote that His mercy outstrips His anger. And in the conversation from day two, God answers this verse too: My servant has extolled Me.","screen":["`THE TWO NAMES FROM VERSE ONE.` · `R-H-M — THE ROOT OF WOMB.` · `WIDE MERCY, MERCY THAT STAYS.` · `MERCY OUTSTRIPS ANGER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Sit comfortably. First, the wide mercy: something good that reaches everyone, sunlight, rain, the air in this breath. Stay with it. Then the mercy that stays: someone who kept coming back for you, even when you didn't make it easy. Let their face come. If you pray, you can hold the two names: ar-Rahman, ar-Rahim. If you don't, hold the two pictures.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Rahma. Mercy, from the root of the womb, named twice in a row: ar-Rahman, ar-Rahim. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`RAHMA — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: mercy twice before anything else. That's the order the prayer keeps. Day thirteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"OWNER OF THE DAY OF JUDGMENT.\"`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"\"Owner of the Day of Judgment\"","word":"yawm ad-din","hook":"a day when every account is settled, held by the one just named merciful","length":"3:15","carry":"the account is in kind hands","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: rahma, mercy, named twice in a row. Your line was mercy twice before anything else. Two weeks today. Day fourteen.","screen":["`RAHMA — MERCY, TWICE.` → `DAY 14.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"A shopkeeper's ledger has two columns: what came in, and what went out. Most of a life never makes it into a ledger. Dishes washed that weren't yours. An angry text deleted before it was sent. A night spent sitting up with someone, never mentioned again. Nobody writes the quiet things down. And on the other side, the things nobody caught. Today's verse says both columns are kept. Dropped into a prayer about mercy, that can sound like a warning. Listen to where it sits.","screen":["`A LEDGER WITH TWO COLUMNS.` → `THE GOOD NOBODY WROTE DOWN.` → `BOTH COLUMNS ARE KEPT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Verse four: “Owner of the Day of Judgment.” In Arabic, maliki yawmi d-din. Today's word is the day itself: yawm ad-din. Yawm is day. And din, translated judgment, is also the everyday Arabic word for religion, a way of life. At its root it carries the sense of a debt coming due, an account settled. So this is the day when every account is squared: both columns, the unseen good and the unfinished wrong. Now, where it sits. Praise, then mercy, then mercy again, and only then the Day. The one just named merciful, twice, is the one who holds the account. Some readers take that order as the point: the reckoning isn't in the hands of a stranger, or an enemy, or a machine. It's held by mercy. And in that reading, nothing quiet is lost: the dishes, the unsent message, the night beside someone. In the conversation from day two, God answers this verse too: My servant has glorified Me. Some tellings add: My servant has entrusted his affairs to Me. Read that way, the verse isn't dread. It's handing the account over.","screen":["`YAWM AD-DIN — THE DAY OF THE ACCOUNT.` · `DIN — A DEBT COMING DUE.` · `HELD BY THE ONE NAMED MERCIFUL.` · `HANDING THE ACCOUNT OVER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Sit comfortably. Bring to mind one good thing you did that nobody noticed. Let it be small. Hold it, and let it be enough that it happened. Then one thing you'd like to settle well: an apology, a debt, a promise. Don't solve it. Just set it down in front of you, honestly.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yawm ad-din. The Day of Judgment, held by the one just named merciful. Fourteenth word. Salaam, the five pillars, salat, alhamdulillah, Rabb, rahma, yawm ad-din: that's week two.","screen":["`YAWM AD-DIN — your 14th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the account is in kind hands. Nothing quiet is lost. Two weeks, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"THEE (ALONE) WE WORSHIP; THEE (ALONE) WE ASK FOR HELP.\"`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"\"Thee (alone) we worship; Thee (alone) we ask for help\"","word":"tawhid","hook":"the hinge of the prayer: it turns from talking about God to talking to God, and it says one","length":"3:30","carry":"one","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: yawm ad-din, the day every account is settled. Your line was the account is in kind hands. Today, the prayer turns around. Day fifteen.","screen":["`YAWM AD-DIN — THE ACCOUNT IS IN KIND HANDS.` → `DAY 15.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"Mecca, in the early years. A man named Bilal is enslaved there, and he has become a Muslim. The early biographies say the man who owned him would drag him out into the noon heat, lay him on the burning sand, put a heavy rock on his chest, and tell him to give up his faith. And Bilal, crushed under the stone, would say only one word, over and over. Ahad. Ahad. One. One. He comes back in two days, as the voice of the call to prayer. Today, his word, and the verse where al-Fatiha says it.","screen":["`BILAL, UNDER THE ROCK.` → `AHAD. AHAD.` → `ONE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Verse five: “Thee (alone) we worship; Thee (alone) we ask for help.” In Arabic, iyyaka na'budu wa iyyaka nasta'in. Two things happen here. First, the prayer turns around. For four verses it has talked about God: praise be to Him, the Merciful, the Owner of the Day. Now, suddenly, it talks to God. You. In the conversation from day two, God's answer changes here too: this is between Me and My servant, and My servant shall have what he asked. Second, listen to the order of the Arabic. It doesn't say we worship You. It says You, we worship. Putting You first makes it exclusive: only You, and only You for help. That's today's word in miniature. Tawhid. The root is w-h-d, one; wahid is the number one. Tawhid means making one, affirming one. It's the first half of the shahada from day nine: there is no god but God. It even has its own surah, four lines long, that opens: “Say: He is Allah, the One!” Here's why it matters on an ordinary Tuesday. If God is one, everything else, however big it looms, isn't God. Your job, your phone, your fear, other people's opinion of you: real, and none of them ultimate. One way to put tawhid: one center, instead of a dozen. That's what Bilal was saying under the rock. Not an argument. The center, holding.","screen":["`THE PRAYER TURNS: ABOUT GOD, TO GOD.` · `YOU, WE WORSHIP — YOU ALONE.` · `W-H-D — ONE.` · `ONE CENTER, NOT A DOZEN.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Sit still, and gather your attention into one place: the breath at the tip of your nose. Just that. When your mind splits off into a dozen places, the list, the text, the worry, notice it, and bring it back to the one breath. Again and again. In the prayer, at the words of witness, many raise one finger of the right hand; you can rest one finger up while you sit, if you like. If you pray, let the word rest there: ahad.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Tawhid. The oneness of God, affirmed: You alone we worship, You alone we ask for help. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`TAWHID — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one. One center, instead of a dozen things pulling at you. Day fifteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"SHOW US THE STRAIGHT PATH.\"`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"\"Show us the straight path\"","word":"as-sirat al-mustaqim","hook":"the whole prayer is a request for directions, and the road has footprints on it","length":"3:30","carry":"show us the way","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: tawhid, You alone. Your line was simply one. Today, al-Fatiha finally asks for something. Day sixteen.","screen":["`TAWHID — ONE.` → `DAY 16.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"Think about the last time you were properly lost. Not a little turned around. Lost. The phone at two percent, the street signs in a language you can't read, every corner looking like the one before. What did you finally do? You stopped someone and asked: which way? It's a humbling question. And at the center of the prayer Muslims say most, after praise and mercy and trust, the one thing it asks for is directions.","screen":["`PROPERLY LOST.` → `WHICH WAY?` → `THE PRAYER ASKS FOR DIRECTIONS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Verse six: “Show us the straight path.” In Arabic, ihdina s-sirata l-mustaqim. One word at a time. Ihdina: show us, guide us, from the root h-d-y, the same root as hidaya, guidance, and as hadiya, a gift. Guidance, in this language, is something handed to you. Sirat is a road, a wide, clear way. Mustaqim: straight, upright, not bending. Together, today's word: as-sirat al-mustaqim, the straight path. Notice the small word us. Even someone praying completely alone, at three in the morning, says show us. Then verse seven says whose path it is: “The path of those whom Thou hast favoured; Not (the path) of those who earn Thine anger nor of those who go astray.” Not an abstract rule, then, but a road with footprints on it. Another verse names those favored as the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs and the righteous: the best of company. And two ways of missing it, which one common reading hears as knowing the way and walking off it, and never finding it at all. Muslims who pray ask for this road many times a day, for a lifetime. Why keep asking for a road you're already on? A simple answer: the path isn't found once. You ask to be shown it, and kept on it, at every corner. That's all seven verses, walked.","screen":["`IHDINA — SHOW US, A GIFT.` · `SIRAT — A WIDE, CLEAR ROAD.` · `A ROAD WITH FOOTPRINTS ON IT.` · `ASKED AGAIN AT EVERY CORNER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Sit comfortably. Bring to mind one place in your life where you honestly don't know which way to go: a decision, a relationship, a next step. Don't solve it. Stand at that corner for a moment and admit you don't know. Then picture one person who walked a hard road well, and the way they carried themselves. If you pray, you can carry the line in Arabic or English: show us the straight path.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"As-sirat al-mustaqim. The straight path: a wide, clear road, asked for in every prayer, with footprints on it. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`AS-SIRAT AL-MUSTAQIM — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: show us the way. Even praying alone, the prayer says us, not me. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS. AL-FATIHA, ALL SEVEN VERSES.` → `TOMORROW: THE CALL TO PRAYER, AND WHY IT'S BEAUTIFUL.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"the call to prayer, and why it's beautiful even if you don't know the words","word":"the adhan","hook":"the call to prayer, and why it's beautiful even if you don't know the words","length":"3:30","carry":"you are being called","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: as-sirat al-mustaqim, the straight path, and the last of al-Fatiha's seven verses. Your line was show us the way. Today, the call that brings people to the prayer. Day seventeen.","screen":["`AS-SIRAT AL-MUSTAQIM — SHOW US THE WAY.` → `DAY 17.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Before dawn, in a city still dark, one voice starts up from a minaret. A single voice, rising, holding a note longer than seems possible, then falling away. Then a second voice, farther off, a beat behind. Then another. For a few minutes the rooftops are full of the same words, never quite in sync. It isn't a song, exactly, and it isn't an alarm. It's an invitation, sent out at each of the five prayer times.","screen":["`ONE VOICE, HOLDING A NOTE.` → `THEN ANOTHER, A BEAT BEHIND.` → `WHAT IS IT SAYING?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"The adhan. Its root, a-dh-n, is the root of the ear, udhun: the call is literally the thing you hear. In the first years in Medina, the sayings report, the young community didn't have one. People gathered by guessing the time. The companions talked it over: a bell? A horn? A fire? Then a companion named Abdullah ibn Zayd dreamed of a man teaching him the words. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said it was a true dream, and told him: teach it to Bilal, his voice carries. Bilal is the man from day fifteen, saying ahad, one, under the rock. By now he was free, and his voice called the city to prayer. The words: Allahu akbar, God is greater. Then two witnesses: there is no god but God, and Muhammad is the messenger of God. Then the heart of it. Hayya 'ala s-salah: come to prayer. Hayya 'ala l-falah: come to success. Falah is flourishing, and its root is the root of the farmer, the one who splits the ground so something can grow. At dawn, Sunni callers add a line: prayer is better than sleep. The Shia call carries a line of its own at every prayer: come to the best of deeds. That's why it can be beautiful even without the Arabic. Its whole job is to turn toward you and say: come. You, now.","screen":["`ADHAN — FROM THE ROOT FOR EAR.` · `A DREAM, AND BILAL'S VOICE.` · `COME TO PRAYER. COME TO SUCCESS.` · `COME. YOU, NOW.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Close your eyes and listen. Not to anything in particular: just to what's calling right now. A car outside, the fridge, your own breathing, someone's voice in another room. Each one is asking for a little of your attention. Notice what you turn toward. If you pray, you can let the old words sit under the sound: come to prayer, come to success.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The adhan. The call to prayer, from the root for ear. God is greater; come to prayer; come to success. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`THE ADHAN — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: you are being called. Come to prayer, come to flourishing, said to you. Day seventeen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: FRIDAY.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"friday","word":"Jumu'ah","hook":"Friday; why the week has a hinge","length":"3:30","carry":"gather","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the adhan. Your line was you are being called. Today, the one call a week that gathers everyone. Day eighteen.","screen":["`THE ADHAN — YOU ARE BEING CALLED.` → `DAY 18.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"Just after noon on a Friday. A cab driver parks and locks the car. A nurse swaps her shift. A young guy slips out of the office in a borrowed tie. A shopkeeper pulls the shutter halfway down. They're all walking the same way, and the sidewalk outside the mosque fills until people are praying on mats laid out on the street. An hour later they're all walking back, and the city picks up exactly where it left off.","screen":["`FRIDAY, JUST AFTER NOON.` → `EVERYONE WALKING ONE WAY.` → `THEN BACK TO WORK.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Jumu'ah. The Arabic name for Friday comes from the root j-m-', to gather. The same root gives jami', the big gathering mosque, and jama'ah, the congregation. Friday is, literally, gathering day. The Qur'an sets it up in a surah named after it: “When the call is heard for the prayer of the day of congregation, haste unto remembrance of Allah and leave your trading.” Leave your trading. Stop the sale, close the laptop. But listen to the next verse: “And when the prayer is ended, then disperse in the land and seek of Allah's bounty.” Go back to work. So Friday isn't a day off. It's a hinge: the week swings on it for an hour, then carries on. What happens in that hour? A sermon, the khutbah, given in two parts, and then a prayer of two units that takes the place of the usual noon prayer. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ taught the preparation too: wash the whole body, wear your best, put on perfume, come early. One of his sayings calls Friday the best day the sun rises on, the day Adam was created. Another says there's an hour on Friday when any prayer is answered, and nobody knows exactly which hour. The schools work out the details of who must attend and when. The point is in the name: once a week, you show up, together.","screen":["`JUMU'AH — FROM A ROOT FOR GATHERING.` · `LEAVE YOUR TRADING.` · `THEN GO BACK TO WORK.` · `ONCE A WEEK, TOGETHER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Think of the people you actually gather with. A family table, a team, a group chat, a few friends at the same café. Picture their faces, one at a time. Notice who you haven't gathered with in a long while. Let one name come up and stay. If you pray, you can remember them in it. If you don't, just hold their face a little longer.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Jumu'ah. Friday, gathering day: a sermon and a prayer at noon, then back to work. The hinge of the week. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`JUMU'AH — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: gather. Once a week the city bends around it, then carries on. Day eighteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE PILLAR THAT'S ABOUT MONEY.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"the pillar that's about money","word":"zakat","hook":"the pillar that's about money; 2.5% and why","length":"3:30","carry":"what you give purifies what you keep","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Jumu'ah, gathering day. Your line was gather. Today, the pillar about a bank balance. Day nineteen.","screen":["`JUMU'AH — GATHER.` → `DAY 19.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Five pillars hold up a Muslim life. One is a statement of faith, one is prayer, one is fasting, one is a journey. And one is about a bank balance. Not a general hope that people will be generous: a number, worked out once a year like a tax return, with a calculator or a pencil at the kitchen table. Mosques post online calculators for it. It can look like paperwork. And its name means something close to purity.","screen":["`FIVE PILLARS, ONE ABOUT MONEY.` → `A NUMBER, ONCE A YEAR.` → `ITS NAME MEANS PURITY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Zakat. Its root, z-k-w, holds two ideas at once: to be pure, and to grow. It's like pruning a plant. You cut some away, and what's left is cleaner, and grows better. The Qur'an names prayer and zakat together again and again: “Establish worship, pay the poor-due.” God and neighbor in one breath. How does it work? Once a lunar year, a Muslim whose savings stay above a set threshold, the nisab, traditionally measured in gold or silver, gives a share of that wealth. The early rules set it, for silver, at a quarter of a tenth: one fortieth, two and a half percent. Not of income: of what you've held on to for a year. Why so small? One way to read it: it's sized so anyone above the line can give it every year without being ruined by it, so it never stops. Where does it go? The Qur'an names eight kinds of people, starting with the poor and the needy, and including people crushed by debt and the stranded traveler. And it speaks of a known right in people's wealth for the one who asks and the one who goes without. So the money isn't framed as the giver's gift. It's a right, already inside the wealth, being handed back. Shia practice adds khums, a fifth of the year's surplus. And beyond all of it is sadaqa, giving from the heart, any amount, anytime. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ counted even a smile at your brother as sadaqa.","screen":["`ZAKAT — TO PURIFY, TO GROW.` · `PRAYER AND ZAKAT, SIDE BY SIDE.` · `TWO AND A HALF PERCENT, ONCE A YEAR.` · `THE POOR'S RIGHT, HANDED BACK.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Think about what you have right now. Not only money: time this week, food in the kitchen, a skill, a coat you haven't worn in two years. Let one thing come to mind that you've held on to longer than you need. Picture the pruning: that one thing cut away and handed on. Notice how the rest of what you have feels after it. Lighter, or heavier? Just notice.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Zakat. The third pillar: two and a half percent of savings, given once a year, from a root meaning to purify and to grow. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`ZAKAT — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: what you give purifies what you keep. Like pruning: less, and it grows better. Day nineteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: AN ORPHAN, A MERCHANT, A MAN WHO WAS TRUSTED BEFORE HE WAS BELIEVED.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"an orphan, a merchant, a man who was trusted before he was believed","word":"the Prophet ﷺ","hook":"an orphan, a merchant, a man who was trusted before he was believed","length":"3:30","carry":"al-Amin — the trustworthy","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: zakat. Your line was what you give purifies what you keep. Today, the man at the center of it all. Day twenty.","screen":["`ZAKAT — WHAT YOU GIVE PURIFIES WHAT YOU KEEP.` → `DAY 20.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"Who holds the spare key to your home? Probably not the most impressive person you know. The most trustworthy one, the one who has never once let you down. The early biographies say a whole city felt that way about one man. Clans who'd fight over a camel all agreed on him, and gave him a nickname for it. Years later, many of those same people refused to believe what he told them, and still left their valuables in his care. Today, told the way Islam tells him: in words only.","screen":["`WHO HOLDS YOUR SPARE KEY?` → `A WHOLE CITY TRUSTED ONE MAN.` → `TRUSTED, NOT YET BELIEVED.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, peace be upon him. In Muslim tradition he isn't drawn or pictured; he's known through words, so words are all we'll use. The early biographies say he was born in Mecca, around the year 570. His father died before he was born, his mother when he was six, and the grandfather who took him in two years later. His uncle Abu Talib raised him after that. So when the Qur'an speaks to him, it can say: “Did He not find thee an orphan and protect (thee)?” He grew up herding sheep, then traveled with the trade caravans. A merchant and widow named Khadijah hired him to lead hers, and was so struck by his honesty that she proposed marriage. By then Mecca had a name for him: al-Amin, the trustworthy. The biographies tell a story about it. The clans were rebuilding the Ka'ba and nearly came to blows over who would set the sacred Black Stone back in its place. They agreed to let the next man through the gate decide. It was him. He spread out a cloak, set the stone on it, and had a man from each clan lift a corner, together. At forty, he began to receive the Qur'an. A saying records how he stood on a hill and asked his people: if I told you an army was behind this hill, would you believe me? We've never known you to lie, they said. Then he told them his message, and many turned away. Trusted first. Believed, by many, only later.","screen":["`AN ORPHAN IN MECCA.` · `A MERCHANT, AND KHADIJAH.` · `AL-AMIN — AND THE BLACK STONE.` · `WE'VE NEVER KNOWN YOU TO LIE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Think of someone who trusts you with something. A secret, a key, a child, a job. Picture them handing it over, and notice what it feels like to be trusted. Then think of one small promise you've made lately and haven't kept yet. Nothing dramatic: a call, a book to return, a message you said you'd send. Just hold it. If you pray, you can ask for help keeping it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The Prophet, peace be upon him. An orphan, a shepherd, a merchant, called al-Amin, the trustworthy, long before most believed him. Twentieth word.","screen":["`THE PROPHET ﷺ — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: al-Amin, the trustworthy. He was trusted first, and believed by many only later. Day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE SHORT SURAHS.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"the short surahs","word":"juz' 'amma","hook":"the short surahs; the mountain ahead","length":"3:30","carry":"the ones every Muslim child knows","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Prophet, peace be upon him, al-Amin, the trustworthy. Now the whole camp, quickly: bismillah, al-Fatiha, wudu, inshallah, iman, Ramadan, Eid, salaam, the five pillars, salat, alhamdulillah, Rabb, rahma, yawm ad-din, tawhid, as-sirat al-mustaqim, the adhan, Jumu'ah, zakat, the Prophet. Twenty words. Today makes twenty-one.","screen":["`TWENTY WORDS ON YOUR STRAND.` → `DAY 21.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Twenty words: the everyday vocabulary of a Muslim life. That's the foothills. Tomorrow the path starts climbing, into the stories of the prophets, one after another. So today, from the top of the foothills, look up at the mountain itself, the Qur'an, and find the place where almost every Muslim starts to climb it. Oddly enough, it's at the very end.","screen":["`THE FOOTHILLS, DONE.` → `THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.` → `START AT THE END.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"The Qur'an has a hundred and fourteen surahs, roughly longest first, shortest last. To make it possible to read the whole thing in a month, especially in Ramadan, it's divided into thirty equal parts. Each part is a juz'. The last one is called juz' 'amma, after its very first word. Surah seventy-eight opens: “Whereof do they question one another?” In Arabic, 'amma yatasa'alun. So 'amma, whereof, names the whole part. It holds the surahs from seventy-eight to the end. Most are short, some only three or four lines, and many scholars place most of them among the earliest revelations in Mecca: vivid, rhythmic, full of oaths by the dawn, the night, the fig. And this is where children begin. Not at page one: at the back. After al-Fatiha, a child often learns an-Nas, the last surah, then al-Falaq, then al-Ikhlas, the surah of oneness from day fifteen, and on backward from there. These are the surahs most often recited in the daily prayers, for a lifetime. Grandparents and five-year-olds know the same ones by heart. For some, the climb goes on for years, until the whole Qur'an is held in memory; someone who does that is called a hafiz, a keeper. But almost everyone starts here, at the small end of the mountain.","screen":["`THIRTY PARTS, FOR A MONTH.` · `'AMMA — WHEREOF DO THEY QUESTION?` · `CHILDREN START AT THE BACK.` · `THE SMALL END OF THE MOUNTAIN.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Think back over these three weeks. Let the words drift past: the name before a beginning, the opening, the water, the fast, the feast, the greeting, the one. Don't try to remember all of them. Just notice which one has stuck, which one came back to you on an ordinary day, in the kitchen or the car, without being asked. Hold that one word. It's yours now.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Juz' 'amma. The thirtieth part of the Qur'an, the short surahs, where nearly everyone begins. Twenty-first word, and camp one is done.","screen":["`JUZ' 'AMMA — your 21st word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the ones every Muslim child knows. Tomorrow the path starts climbing, and the stories begin.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS. CAMP ONE, DONE.` → `TOMORROW: THE ONE WHO WOULD BE A STEWARD.`"]}]}},"JUDAISM":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"the words on the doorpost","word":"the mezuzah","hook":"the little case on the doorframe; what's actually inside it","length":"3:00","carry":"take the words with you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"That little case on the doorframe. Right side as you walk in, about shoulder height, often tilted. Some people touch it on the way out and kiss their fingertips, as quick as checking for keys. Most people assume the case is the thing. It isn't. The case is just a case. First, a guess: what's inside?","screen":["`THE CASE ON THE DOORFRAME.` → `TOUCHED ON THE WAY OUT.` → `WHAT'S INSIDE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Inside is a tiny scroll, written by hand on parchment by a trained scribe with a quill. It carries a passage from the Torah that opens with six famous words, the Shema: hear, O Israel, the Lord our God, the Lord is one. The same passage says to write these words on the doorposts of your house. So people did, literally. Mezuzah is simply Hebrew for doorpost: the word for the frame became the word for the scroll. On the back, many scribes write one of God's names, Shaddai, which some read as the initials of guardian of the doors of Israel. Now the tilt. A famous medieval teacher, Rashi, said hang it upright. His grandson said lay it flat. Many Ashkenazi Jews, whose families come from central and eastern Europe, hang it at a slant: a little of both. Many Sephardi Jews hang it upright. And the touch? Not a lucky charm; the great teacher Maimonides warned against treating it as one. It's a reminder. Every time you cross the threshold, going out or coming home, you pass the words.","screen":["`A SCROLL, WRITTEN BY HAND.` · `HEAR, O ISRAEL.` · `UPRIGHT OR FLAT? A SLANT.` · `A REMINDER, NOT A CHARM.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Rest your fingertips on the nearest doorframe, or just picture your own front door. Breathe in. As you breathe out, think of one thing you'd like to carry through that door today. Then lift your hand.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The mezuzah. Doorpost, and the handwritten words fixed to it: hear, O Israel. First word on your strand.","screen":["`THE MEZUZAH — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: take the words with you. That's what the touch on the way out means. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: TWO FLAMES, EIGHTEEN MINUTES BEFORE SUNSET.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"two flames, eighteen minutes before sunset","word":"the candles","hook":"Friday afternoon, a match, two flames, and hands over the eyes","length":"3:00","carry":"light, then rest","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the mezuzah, the words on the doorpost. Your line was take the words with you. Today, two small flames on a Friday.","screen":["`TAKE THE WORDS WITH YOU.` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Friday afternoon, and the clock is running. The oven's on, someone's still in the shower, a kid can't find their shoes. Then, at a minute printed on a calendar on the fridge, someone strikes a match and lights two candles. They circle their hands over the flames, cover their eyes, and whisper a blessing. When they look up, the week is over.","screen":["`FRIDAY, THE CLOCK RUNNING.` → `TWO CANDLES.` → `HANDS OVER THE EYES.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"This is how Shabbat begins: the day of rest, from Friday sunset until the stars come out on Saturday night. Why sunset? In the Torah's first chapter, each day closes the same way: there was evening and there was morning. Evening comes first, so a day starts in the dark. And since lighting a fire is one of the kinds of work that stop on Shabbat, the candles go on while it's still light, in many towns eighteen minutes early, as a margin. Why two? The rest day is commanded twice, in two wordings: remember the day, and observe it. Zachor and shamor. One flame for each. Then the hands over the eyes. Usually you say a blessing and then do the thing. But once this blessing is said, Shabbat has begun, and no new fire can be lit. So people light first, cover their eyes, bless, and then look, as if the light were new. And why light at all? The rabbis said it's for shalom bayit, peace in the home: a light so the family can see each other's faces at dinner.","screen":["`EVENING FIRST, THEN MORNING.` · `REMEMBER AND OBSERVE.` · `LIGHT, COVER, BLESS, LOOK.` · `PEACE IN THE HOME.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Look at the nearest light: a lamp, a window, even this screen. Breathe in. Close your eyes, the way people do after lighting. As you breathe out, open them slowly, as if the light were new.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The candles. Two flames before sunset on Friday: remember, and observe. Second word.","screen":["`THE CANDLES — your 2nd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: light, then rest. First the flame, then the week can stop. That's day two.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: YOU ALREADY SPEAK A LITTLE YIDDISH.`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"you already speak a little Yiddish","word":"mensch","hook":"mazel tov, oy vey, schlep: a whole language hiding in English","length":"3:00","carry":"be a mensch","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the candles, two flames before sunset. Your line was light, then rest. Today, words you may have been saying for years.","screen":["`LIGHT, THEN REST.` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Mazel tov. Oy vey. Schlep, kvetch, chutzpah, nosh. Plenty of people say these without a second thought, Jewish or not. They aren't slang. They're Yiddish, a whole language with its own songs, newspapers and novels, and a lot of English speakers have been using a little of it all along.","screen":["`MAZEL TOV. OY VEY. SCHLEP.` → `NOT SLANG.` → `A WHOLE LANGUAGE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Yiddish was the everyday language of Ashkenazi Jews in central and eastern Europe for centuries. It's built mostly from old German, with Hebrew and Slavic words mixed in, and it's written in Hebrew letters. Immigrants carried it to American cities, and English kept a handful. Schlep: to drag. Kvetch: to complain, at length. Nosh: a little snack. Chutzpah: nerve, rude or brave depending on who's telling it. Oy vey is short for oy vey iz mir: oh, woe is me. And mazel tov? Mazel comes from a Hebrew word for a star, and luck; tov means good. Good luck, word for word. But it isn't said before the exam. It's said after the good news: a birth, a wedding, a new job. It means: a good thing happened. Then there's the word worth keeping. In German, Mensch just means a person. In Yiddish, a mensch is a real human being: decent, honest, someone who does the right thing when nobody's checking. The great rabbi Hillel taught it long before Yiddish existed: where nobody is acting like a person should, try to be that person.","screen":["`A LANGUAGE IN HEBREW LETTERS.` · `SCHLEP, KVETCH, NOSH.` · `MAZEL TOV — AFTER, NOT BEFORE.` · `A MENSCH — A REAL HUMAN BEING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. First breath: someone you'd call a mensch. Second breath: one thing they did when nobody was checking. Third breath: let the word land on them, quietly, like a compliment they'll never hear.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Mensch. A real human being: decent, honest, there when it counts. Third word.","screen":["`MENSCH — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: be a mensch. Of everything Yiddish lent to English, that may be the best one. Day three, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE PLATE THAT TELLS A STORY.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"the plate that tells a story","word":"seder","hook":"a bone, an egg, parsley and horseradish: the Passover plate, and why the answer starts with we","length":"3:00","carry":"we were slaves","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: mensch, a decent human being. Your line was be a mensch. Today, a plate where every food is part of a story.","screen":["`BE A MENSCH.` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A spring night, a long table, chairs borrowed from the neighbors. In the middle sits a plate with odd little things on it: a roasted bone, an egg, some parsley, horseradish, a brown sweet paste. Nobody's eating them yet. Then the youngest kid stands up, a little nervous, and asks: why is this night different from all other nights?","screen":["`A BONE, AN EGG, PARSLEY.` → `THE YOUNGEST STANDS UP.` → `WHY IS THIS NIGHT DIFFERENT?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Seder means order. Passover night follows a set order of steps, read from a little book called the Haggadah, which means the telling. The story it tells is the Exodus: slaves in Egypt, a stubborn king, ten plagues, and a night of leaving so rushed the bread had no time to rise. That's the matzah. The plate is the story laid out as food. Bitter herbs, often horseradish, for how bitter slavery was. Charoset, a sweet paste often made of apples, nuts and wine, looks like the mortar the slaves used for bricks. A green vegetable, often parsley, dipped in salt water, like tears. The roasted bone recalls the lamb of the first Passover; the egg, an offering brought on festivals long ago. You taste the story before anyone explains it. Then comes the surprise. The answer to the child's question doesn't start with once upon a time. It starts: we were slaves. We. The old rule is that in every generation, each person should see themselves as if they personally walked out of Egypt. Not a history lesson. A memory you're handed.","screen":["`SEDER — ORDER.` · `THE BREAD THAT DIDN'T RISE.` · `THE STORY LAID OUT AS FOOD.` · `WE WERE SLAVES.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. First breath: something bitter from this year. Second breath: something sweet that came anyway. Third breath: hold the two together, the bitter and the sweet, the way the plate does.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Seder. Order: Passover night told step by step, with the story laid out on a plate. Fourth word.","screen":["`SEDER — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: we were slaves. Not they. We. That one word carries the whole night. Day four.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY THE GLASS BREAKS.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"why the glass breaks","word":"the glass","hook":"one stomp, a crunch, and everyone shouts mazel tov: why break something at a wedding?","length":"3:00","carry":"even in joy, remember","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the seder, a story laid out as food. Your line was we were slaves. Today, the loudest second of a Jewish wedding.","screen":["`WE WERE SLAVES.` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The end of the ceremony. The couple stands under a canopy. Someone sets a glass, wrapped in a cloth, on the floor. One stomp, a crunch, and the whole room shouts mazel tov. Plenty of people have cheered for it without knowing why it happens. And there's more than one answer: families tell it different ways.","screen":["`A GLASS IN A CLOTH.` → `ONE STOMP.` → `MAZEL TOV!`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Start with the canopy. It's called a chuppah: four poles and a cloth, open on every side, like the roof of a home that's just beginning. Then the glass. Its roots go back to rabbis in ancient Babylon who broke a precious cup at a wedding feast, on purpose, when the celebrating got out of hand. Later teachers tied the custom to Jerusalem, and the Temple that stood there, destroyed nearly two thousand years ago. An old psalm promises to keep Jerusalem above even your highest joy. So at the happiest moment, something breaks: a reminder that even on the best day, something in the world is still broken. Families add their own readings. Love is precious and breakable, so handle it with care. A glass, once broken, stays broken; the promise is meant to be just as permanent. In some old customs, the noise scared off bad luck. Traditionally the groom breaks it; at many weddings today, either partner does, or both together. More than one meaning can be true at once, and a Jewish wedding is happy to hold them all. Then comes the shout, the words you met two days ago: mazel tov.","screen":["`THE CHUPPAH — OPEN ON EVERY SIDE.` · `A PRECIOUS CUP, BROKEN.` · `REMEMBER JERUSALEM.` · `MORE THAN ONE MEANING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. First breath: the happiest moment you can remember. Second breath: someone or something missing from it. Third breath: let the two sit side by side, without either one canceling the other.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The glass. Broken at the height of the joy, so the joy remembers. Fifth word.","screen":["`THE GLASS — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: even in joy, remember. The crunch comes right before the cheering, never instead of it. That's day five.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT JUST A PARTY.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"not just a party","word":"mitzvah","hook":"a thirteen-year-old, an ancient scroll, then the DJ: which part is the real thing?","length":"3:00","carry":"it's yours now","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the glass, broken right before the cheering. Your line was even in joy, remember. Today, a day many people remember for the DJ.","screen":["`EVEN IN JOY, REMEMBER.` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A thirteen-year-old in a suit that doesn't quite fit, or a dress picked out months ago, stands in front of everyone they know and chants from a handwritten scroll, in Hebrew they've practiced for a year. Then comes the party: the DJ, the candy bar, the chair lifted into the air. So which part was the real thing? Neither one, it turns out, makes it happen.","screen":["`THIRTEEN, AND A SCROLL.` → `THEN THE DJ.` → `WHICH PART IS REAL?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Bar mitzvah means son of the commandment. Bat mitzvah, daughter of the commandment. A mitzvah is one of the things the Torah asks a person to do, and in everyday talk it's also a good deed: that was a real mitzvah. Here's the twist. You don't have a bar or bat mitzvah. You become one. In traditional Jewish law it happens on its own, on a birthday: thirteen for boys, twelve for girls, and many communities mark both at thirteen. Ceremony or no ceremony, party or no party, the day comes. An old teaching lays out a life by ages: five for starting scripture, ten for the rabbis' teachings, thirteen for the commandments. Until then, the responsibility sat with your parents. From that birthday on, it's yours. So the ceremony isn't what makes it happen. It's the first time it's shown in public: called up to read from the Torah, often with a short talk about what that week's reading means to you. And the party? Celebrating a mitzvah with a festive meal is a tradition too. The DJ is newer. Some people take it up as adults, at thirty or sixty or eighty. There's no deadline.","screen":["`SON OR DAUGHTER OF THE COMMANDMENT.` · `YOU BECOME ONE.` · `THIRTEEN FOR THE COMMANDMENTS.` · `SHOWN IN PUBLIC, THEN A MEAL.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. First breath: think back to being twelve or thirteen. Second breath: one thing you're responsible for now that you weren't then. Third breath: let it be yours, without the weight. Just yours.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Mitzvah. A commandment, a good deed, and at a bar or bat mitzvah, a responsibility that becomes yours. Sixth word.","screen":["`MITZVAH — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: it's yours now. The day comes on its own; the ceremony just says so out loud. Day six.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: AN ARGUMENT FOR THE SAKE OF HEAVEN.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"an argument for the sake of heaven","word":"machloket","hook":"a holy book that writes down its arguments, and keeps the losing side","length":"3:00","carry":"argue to learn, not to win","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: mitzvah, a responsibility that becomes yours. Your line was it's yours now. Six words this week: the mezuzah, the candles, mensch, seder, the glass, mitzvah. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`MEZUZAH · CANDLES · MENSCH · SEDER · THE GLASS · MITZVAH`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Open a page of the Talmud, the great library of the rabbis' discussions, and you'll find something you might not expect in a holy book: arguments. One rabbi says this. Another says no, that. Then a third weighs in. And very often the side that lost is written down too, and kept, for centuries. A holy book that saves its disagreements.","screen":["`A PAGE OF THE TALMUD.` → `ONE SAYS THIS, ONE SAYS THAT.` → `THE LOSING SIDE, KEPT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Machloket means a disagreement. And there's a famous pair at the center of it: Hillel and Shammai, two great teachers about two thousand years ago, and the schools of students who followed them. You met Hillel on day three. The two schools argued for years, about prayer, about Shabbat, about how to live. Finally, the story goes, a voice from heaven settled it: both of these are the words of the living God. And the law follows Hillel. Wait. Both are right, but one wins? Why Hillel? Because, the story says, his students were kind and humble. They taught Shammai's view along with their own, and even taught Shammai's first. An old teaching names the difference between a good argument and a bad one. An argument for the sake of heaven will last, and its example is Hillel and Shammai. An argument to win, to beat someone, won't. Remember the slanted mezuzah from day one: an argument where nobody had to lose. So when Jews argue over a text, often out loud, in pairs, across a table, it isn't a lack of faith. It's how people learn.","screen":["`MACHLOKET — A DISAGREEMENT.` · `HILLEL AND SHAMMAI.` · `BOTH ARE THE WORDS OF THE LIVING GOD.` · `FOR THE SAKE OF HEAVEN.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. First breath: someone you disagree with. Second breath: the best version of their side, put as fairly as you can. Third breath: notice that you can hold it without letting go of your own.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Machloket. A disagreement, and in this tradition a way of learning: argued for the sake of heaven, with both sides kept. Seventh word: a week's worth.","screen":["`MACHLOKET — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: argue to learn, not to win. That's week one: seven words, and what's inside each. Tomorrow, a man who argued with God himself.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MAN WHO ARGUED WITH GOD ABOUT SODOM.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"the man who argued with God about Sodom","word":"Abraham","hook":"the man who argued with God about Sodom","length":"6:45","carry":"you're allowed to argue","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: machloket, rabbis arguing for the sake of heaven. Your line was argue to learn, not to win. Week two starts with a bigger argument. Day eight.","screen":["`ARGUE TO LEARN, NOT TO WIN.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"An old man is sitting at the door of his tent in the heat of the day. Three visitors arrive, he feeds them, and as they leave, God tells him something. The cities of the plain, Sodom and Gomorrah, have done terrible things, and God is going to see about them. He could bow, or nod, or quietly go back inside. He doesn't. The text says he comes closer. And then he starts to argue. With God. Today: an argument with God, and the man who made it.","screen":["`A TENT IN THE HEAT OF THE DAY.` → `GOD SPEAKS OF SODOM.` → `ABRAHAM COMES CLOSER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Abraham. Born Abram, the text says, and renamed when God promised he'd be the father of many nations. Jews call him Avraham avinu, Abraham our father. And here, he bargains. What if there are fifty good people in the city? Will you sweep them away with the rest? Then comes the line at the center of the story: \"Shall not the Judge of all the earth do justly?\" That takes nerve. He's holding God to God's own standard. And God answers: for fifty, I'll spare it. So Abraham keeps going. Forty-five? Forty? Thirty? Twenty? Each time he gets more polite, and more stubborn. He calls himself \"but dust and ashes,\" and still doesn't back down. He stops at ten. Why ten? The text doesn't say. Some teachers note that ten is the smallest number that makes a community. In the end, ten good people aren't found. But the argument is never scolded. God listens to every round. And Abraham isn't the last. Moses argues with God later. So does Job. The psalms complain out loud. There's a Hasidic story of a rabbi who put God on trial on behalf of his people. Arguing with God, in this tradition, isn't the opposite of faith. It's one of the ways faith talks.","screen":["`ABRAM BECOMES ABRAHAM.` · `WHAT IF THERE ARE FIFTY?` · `FROM FIFTY DOWN TO TEN.` · `ARGUING IS HOW FAITH TALKS.` · then, large: `SHALL NOT THE JUDGE OF ALL THE EARTH DO JUSTLY?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Bring to mind one thing that seems unfair to you right now, in your life or in the world. Don't smooth it over. Say it plainly, inside. If you pray, you can say it to God the way Abraham did: direct, respectful, and not backing down. If you don't, say it honestly to yourself.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Abraham. Father of many nations, who argued with God on behalf of strangers. Eighth word.","screen":["`ABRAHAM — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you're allowed to argue. Direct, respectful, and on behalf of someone else: that's how Abraham did it. Day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WRESTLING ALL NIGHT, LIMPING, RENAMED.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"wrestling all night, limping, renamed","word":"Jacob","hook":"wrestling all night, limping, renamed","length":"6:45","carry":"you'll limp and you'll be blessed","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Abraham, bargaining with God from fifty down to ten. Your line was you're allowed to argue. Today, his grandson goes further. Day nine.","screen":["`YOU'RE ALLOWED TO ARGUE.` → `DAY 9.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"It's the night before the hardest meeting of his life. Twenty years ago Jacob cheated his twin brother Esau out of their father's blessing and ran. Now he's coming home, and Esau is on the road with four hundred men. Jacob sends his family across a river called the Jabbok and stays behind, alone, in the dark. And then, the text says, a man wrestles with him until the break of dawn. No name. No warning. No explanation. Just two bodies in the dirt all night long. Today: the wrestler, and the name the whole people still carries.","screen":["`TWENTY YEARS AFTER THE LIE.` → `ALONE AT THE RIVER.` → `A MAN WRESTLES HIM TILL DAWN.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Jacob. In Hebrew, Yaakov, from the word for heel, because he was born holding on to his twin brother's heel. He's been grabbing ever since. Who is he fighting? The text calls him a man. The prophet Hosea calls him an angel. Jacob himself names the place Peniel, the face of God, because he says he saw God face to face. One old midrash says it was Esau's angel. The Torah doesn't settle it, and maybe that's the point: some nights you can't tell who you're fighting. As dawn comes, the stranger can't win, so he touches Jacob's hip and wrenches it. And Jacob, hurt, still won't let go: \"I will not let thee go, except thou bless me.\" The blessing is a new name. \"Thy name shall be called no more Jacob, but Israel; for thou hast striven with God and with men, and hast prevailed.\" Israel: one who wrestles with God. A whole people named not for someone who obeyed quietly, or never doubted, but for someone who fought all night and held on. And he doesn't walk away clean. The sun rises, and Jacob is limping. The Torah says that's why the children of Israel don't eat the sinew of the thigh, and observant Jews still don't. A food law that remembers an injury. He's hurt, and he's renamed, in the same morning.","screen":["`YAAKOV — THE ONE WHO GRABS THE HEEL.` · `A MAN, AN ANGEL, OR GOD?` · `ISRAEL — WRESTLES WITH GOD.` · `THE BLESSING AND THE LIMP.` · then, large: `I WILL NOT LET THEE GO, EXCEPT THOU BLESS ME.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Think of one thing you're wrestling with right now: a decision, a person, a question. Don't try to win it in the next minute. Just hold on to it, the way Jacob held on, and ask what blessing might be in it. If you pray, you can ask for that blessing.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Jacob. The heel-grabber who wrestled until dawn, came away limping, and was renamed Israel. Ninth word.","screen":["`JACOB — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you'll limp and you'll be blessed. The mark and the new name came in the same morning. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"WE WILL DO AND WE WILL HEAR\".`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"\"we will do and we will hear\"","word":"Sinai","hook":"\"we will do and we will hear\" — practice before belief","length":"6:45","carry":"do first","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Jacob, who wrestled till dawn and was renamed Israel. Your line was you'll limp and you'll be blessed. Today, his descendants, out of Egypt, at a mountain. Day ten.","screen":["`YOU'LL LIMP AND YOU'LL BE BLESSED.` → `DAY 10.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Three months out of Egypt, a crowd of former slaves is camped at the foot of a mountain in the desert. They've been told to wash, to wait, to keep back from the slopes. On the third morning there's thunder, lightning, a thick cloud on the peak, and the sound of a horn growing louder and louder until the whole camp is shaking. Later, what this new life with God will ask is read out loud to them. And the people give an answer, all together, that the rabbis would keep coming back to. It's two words in Hebrew, and they're in the wrong order. Today: the mountain, and the answer.","screen":["`A MOUNTAIN IN THE DESERT.` → `THUNDER, CLOUD, A HORN.` → `AN ANSWER IN THE WRONG ORDER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Sinai is the mountain where, in the Torah's story, God gives the Torah to Israel. Where exactly it stood is uncertain, and the text isn't interested in the map. What matters is what happens there. After the laws are read, the people say: \"All that the LORD hath spoken will we do, and obey.\" In Hebrew: na'aseh v'nishma. We will do, and we will hear. Look at the order. The usual order runs the other way: explain it to me first, then I'll decide whether to do it. Here the doing comes first, and the understanding follows. The rabbis tell a story about that moment: when Israel put doing before hearing, a voice from heaven asked, who revealed to my children this secret that the angels use? Teachers hear in it practice before belief. You light the candles, as on day two, before you have a theory of candles. The meaning grows out of the doing. The rabbis also argued about that day, and they wrote the argument down. One teacher said the mountain was held over the people like a barrel, as if they had no real choice. Others answered that they accepted freely, and accepted again in later generations. Both readings are kept. And one more teaching: every Jewish soul, even of people not yet born, stood at Sinai, converts too. The whole people, past and future, at the foot of one mountain. Jews mark that day every year, at the festival of Shavuot.","screen":["`THE MOUNTAIN WHERE TORAH IS GIVEN.` · `NA'ASEH V'NISHMA — DO, THEN HEAR.` · `THE SECRET THE ANGELS USE.` · `EVERY SOUL STOOD THERE.` · then, large: `WE WILL DO, AND WE WILL HEAR.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Think of one good thing you've been putting off until you understand it better, or feel more ready. A call. An apology. A habit. Picture yourself doing it first, and letting the understanding come afterward.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Sinai. The mountain of the covenant, where the people answered: we will do, and we will hear. Tenth word.","screen":["`SINAI — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: do first. The understanding tends to arrive a little later. Day ten, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE OLDEST LABOR LAW.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"the oldest labor law","word":"Shabbat","hook":"the oldest labor law; a day the world can't have","length":"6:45","carry":"stop. it's already Friday.","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Sinai, and the answer we will do, and we will hear. Your line was do first. On day two you lit the candles; today, the day they open. Day eleven.","screen":["`DO FIRST.` → `DAY 11.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A phone on a Saturday looks like a phone on a Tuesday. Work email. The group chat. A sale that ends tonight. The world never really closes anymore. Now take a family that, every week, from Friday sunset until the stars come out on Saturday, lets none of that in. No office. No shopping. In some homes, not even a light switch. Not a holiday, not a sick day. Just the seventh day, arriving on schedule. Some people call it the oldest labor law there is. Today: the day the world can't have.","screen":["`THE WORLD NEVER CLOSES.` → `A DAY IT CAN'T REACH.` → `THE SEVENTH DAY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Shabbat comes from a Hebrew root, shin, bet, tav, that means to stop. To cease. Not first to relax, or to worship: to stop. At the end of the creation story, even God stops, and the verb there comes from the same root. English borrowed the word as Sabbath. Then the rabbis asked a practical question: what counts as work? They listed thirty-nine kinds: planting, baking, writing, building, lighting a fire, and more, drawn from the work it took to build the tent for God in the desert. So work, on Shabbat, means making and changing things, not how tired you get. Now the part that earns the nickname. The rest day is commanded twice, and the second time it gives a different reason. Not creation. Egypt. You were slaves, so rest, \"that thy man-servant and thy maid-servant may rest as well as thou.\" As well as you. Not after you. And the list of who rests keeps going: your children, the stranger in your town, even the ox and the donkey. A weekly day off, owed to everyone, written into an ancient law. Jews keep the day in different ways. Orthodox Jews keep the thirty-nine categories carefully: no driving, no cooking, no phones. Conservative, Reform and other Jews keep Shabbat in their own ways, through the candles, the meal, synagogue and a slower day, with each community drawing its own lines. The twentieth-century rabbi Abraham Joshua Heschel described Shabbat as a palace built in time rather than in space. You don't travel to it. It arrives, every week, wherever you are.","screen":["`SHABBAT — TO STOP.` · `THIRTY-NINE KINDS OF WORK.` · `REST, BECAUSE YOU WERE SLAVES.` · `A PALACE IN TIME.` · then, large: `AS WELL AS THOU.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Put one thing down with each breath. The thing you're working on. The thing you're worried about. The phone, if it's in your hand. For these few breaths, nothing is being made or fixed. That's the whole idea of Shabbat, very small.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Shabbat. To stop: the seventh day, Friday sunset to Saturday stars, a rest owed to everyone in the house. Eleventh word.","screen":["`SHABBAT — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: stop. it's already Friday. Rest isn't a prize for finishing; it's owed. Day eleven.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: HELLO, GOODBYE, PEACE.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"hello, goodbye, peace","word":"shalom","hook":"hello, goodbye, peace — and a fourth meaning: whole","length":"6:45","carry":"be whole","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Shabbat, the day of stopping. Your line was stop. it's already Friday. Today, the word people say once the candles are lit: Shabbat shalom. Day twelve.","screen":["`STOP. IT'S ALREADY FRIDAY.` → `DAY 12.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"One word, three jobs. Someone walks in: shalom. Someone leaves: shalom. On a card, at the end of a letter, over a door: shalom, peace. Hello, goodbye, peace. The same word opens a conversation and closes it. But there's a fourth meaning hiding underneath the other three, and it's the one the rest of them grow from. Today, the wish folded inside the hello.","screen":["`HELLO. GOODBYE. PEACE.` → `ONE WORD, THREE JOBS.` → `AND A FOURTH MEANING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Shalom is Hebrew, and it grows from a root of three letters, shin, lamed, mem, and the root's first sense isn't quiet. It's whole. Complete. Nothing missing. The same root gives shalem, whole, and a verb for paying back what you owe, so the account is square again. So peace, in this language, isn't only the absence of a fight. It's the presence of everything that should be there. A house with shalom has nothing broken in it. A body with shalom is well. In the Torah, when Jacob sends his son Joseph to check on his brothers, the Hebrew says, more or less: go and see the shalom of your brothers. How whole are they? The word runs all through Jewish prayer. The priestly blessing, said over congregations, and in some homes over the children on Friday night, ends with it: \"and give thee peace.\" Peace is the last word. And an old psalm doesn't treat peace as something that just happens to you: \"Seek peace, and pursue it.\" The rabbis noticed that verb. Other commandments, they taught, you do when the chance comes to you. Peace you go after. So when someone says shalom at a door, coming or going, underneath the hello is an old wish: may you be whole. May nothing be missing.","screen":["`SH-L-M — WHOLE, COMPLETE.` · `SEE THE SHALOM OF YOUR BROTHERS.` · `THE BLESSING ENDS IN PEACE.` · `SEEK PEACE, AND PURSUE IT.` · then, large: `MAY NOTHING BE MISSING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Breathe slowly, and notice one place in you that feels not quite whole right now. Tired. Worried. Rushed. Don't fix it; just let it be noticed. On the last breath, if you want to, say the word, out loud or inside: shalom. Not to fix anything. Just to wish it whole.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Shalom. Hello, goodbye, peace, and underneath all three: whole, with nothing missing. Twelfth word.","screen":["`SHALOM — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: be whole. It's the wish folded inside every hello and goodbye. That's day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: SIX WORDS.`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"six words","word":"the Shema","hook":"six words; the sentence a Jew says with eyes covered","length":"6:45","carry":"hear","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: shalom, and underneath the hello, whole. Your line was be whole. Today, the words rolled up inside the mezuzah from day one. Day thirteen.","screen":["`BE WHOLE.` → `DAY 13.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A synagogue on an ordinary morning. People are reading, murmuring, some standing, some swaying. Then, at one moment, many of them do the same small thing. They raise a hand and cover their eyes. For a few seconds a room full of adults sits like children playing hide and seek. And under the hands, six Hebrew words. Some children learn it as their first prayer, at bedtime, and tradition hopes it will be among the last words a person says in this life. Six words, said at the beginning and the end. Today: the sentence under the hand.","screen":["`A HAND OVER THE EYES.` → `SIX HEBREW WORDS.` → `THE FIRST PRAYER AND THE LAST.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The six words are: Shema Yisrael, Adonai Eloheinu, Adonai echad. \"Hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one.\" They come from the Torah, from the same passage that's rolled up inside the mezuzah, and the prayer is named for its first word. Shema. Hear. But the Hebrew verb is bigger than hearing with your ears. It means listen, pay attention, understand, even take it to heart and act on it. It's not here is a fact. It's are you listening? Notice who it's said to. Not to God. To Israel, to the people, to the person saying it. It's a sentence you say to yourself, out loud, to wake yourself up. A detail about the name. The word written there is God's four-letter name, which Jewish tradition doesn't pronounce. Reading aloud, people say Adonai, my Lord, instead. And a detail about the page. In a handwritten Torah scroll, two letters in this verse are written extra large: the last letter of the first word and the last letter of the last word. Together they spell ed, the Hebrew word for witness. Saying it, some teachers say, is a kind of testimony. Why the hand over the eyes? An old story remembers that Rabbi Judah the Prince, whom tradition credits with gathering the rabbis' early teachings into one book, passed his hand over his eyes when he said it. People still do: shut out everything you can see, just for a moment, so you can hear. The same passage says to speak these words when you lie down and when you rise up. So it's said morning and night, at bedtime, and at the end.","screen":["`SHEMA — HEAR, LISTEN, TAKE IT IN.` · `SAID TO ISRAEL, NOT TO GOD.` · `TWO BIG LETTERS SPELL WITNESS.` · `A HAND OVER THE EYES, TO HEAR.` · then, large: `HEAR, O ISRAEL.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Cover your eyes with one hand, the way people do for the Shema. Let the room disappear. Breathe slowly and just listen, to whatever sound is left: a fan, traffic, your own breath. If you pray, you can say the six words on the way out. If not, let the breath out and simply hear.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Shema. Six words that begin hear, O Israel, said morning and night with the eyes covered. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`THE SHEMA — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: hear. Not only with the ears: listen, and take it in. Day thirteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: INTENTION.`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"intention","word":"kavanah","hook":"intention; the difference between saying and meaning","length":"6:45","carry":"mean it","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Shema, six words said with the eyes covered. Your line was hear. Day fourteen, the end of week two.","screen":["`HEAR.` → `DAY 14.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Someone drives home and pulls into the driveway without remembering a single turn. Someone reads a whole page, reaches the bottom, and has no idea what it said. Someone says love you at the end of a phone call the way they'd say bye. The mouth did the job. Nobody was there. Now imagine a tradition with words it says every day, morning and night, for a lifetime. The danger isn't forgetting them. It's saying them perfectly while you're somewhere else entirely. Judaism has a word for what's missing. Today: intention.","screen":["`HOME, AND NO MEMORY OF THE DRIVE.` → `THE MOUTH DID THE JOB.` → `NOBODY WAS THERE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Kavanah comes from a root that means to aim, to direct, to point something at a target. Intention is aiming your heart where your words are going. The rabbis, discussing the Shema from yesterday, said that if a person directed their heart while reading it, they had done what was asked. They also remembered that the early pious ones would wait an hour before praying, just to direct their hearts. An hour of aiming, for a few minutes of prayer. The Bible's clearest picture of kavanah is a woman named Hannah, praying at the sanctuary for a child. She speaks in her heart; only her lips move, and no sound comes out. The priest thinks she's drunk. She isn't. She's praying as hard as anyone ever has, and the rabbis learned much of how to pray from her. One old line puts it simply: the Merciful One wants the heart. Then the honest part. The rabbis argued about whether a commandment done without intention still counts, and the discussion went on for centuries. Many say yes, the act counts, but it isn't the whole thing. And one teaching holds both sides together: do the good thing even when your heart isn't in it, because from doing it without the right intention, you come to do it with the right one. That's day ten again, do first, with a promise attached. This week you met Abraham, Jacob, Sinai, Shabbat, shalom, the Shema, and now the thing that makes any of them more than motions.","screen":["`KAVANAH — TO AIM THE HEART.` · `AN HOUR OF AIMING FIRST.` · `HANNAH'S LIPS MOVE, NO SOUND.` · `DOING LEADS TO MEANING.` · then, large: `THE MERCIFUL ONE WANTS THE HEART.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Forty-five seconds, with the bell. Pick one short thing you say every day without thinking: thank you, good morning, love you, or, if you pray, the Shema. For the first part of the sit, just aim: who is it for, and what do you actually mean by it? Then say it once, slowly, meaning every word.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Kavanah. Intention: aiming your heart where your words and actions are going. Fourteenth word. Two weeks.","screen":["`KAVANAH — your 14th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: mean it. Two weeks in, that's the thread running through all of them. Week two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE BLESSING BEFORE EATING.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"the blessing before eating","word":"bracha","hook":"the blessing before eating; saying thank you to the source","length":"7:00","carry":"notice before you bite","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: kavanah, aiming the heart where the words go. Your line was mean it. Week three starts at the table. Day fifteen.","screen":["`MEAN IT.` → `WEEK THREE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Watch someone who keeps this tradition pick up an apple. Before the first bite there's a pause, about two seconds long, and a murmur you'd miss if you weren't looking. Then the bite. Same with a glass of water. A cookie. A piece of bread at a wedding. A lot of eating happens the way scrolling does: mid-sentence, standing up, already on to the next thing. This is the opposite. A tiny speed bump between wanting something and having it. And the words inside that pause have a surprising reason behind them. Today: the two-second blessing.","screen":["`AN APPLE. A PAUSE.` → `TWO SECONDS, A MURMUR.` → `THEN THE BITE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"A bracha is a blessing. The standard form opens with the same words each time: baruch atah Adonai, eloheinu melech ha-olam, roughly, blessed are you, Lord our God, ruler of the world. Then the ending changes with the food. For bread: who brings forth bread from the earth. For fruit: who creates the fruit of the tree. For water, or almost anything else: by whose word all things came to be. Here's the surprise. It's not the food being blessed. The blessing is said to the source of the food. Why bother? The rabbis noticed two psalms that seem to disagree. One says: \"The earth is the LORD'S, and the fulness thereof.\" Another says: \"The heavens are the heavens of the LORD; but the earth hath He given to the children of men.\" Which is it? Does the world belong to God, or to us? Their answer: both, and the blessing is the hinge. Before the blessing, the apple is still God's. After it, it's been given to you. To eat without a blessing, they said, is a little like taking something that isn't yours yet. There's a blessing after eating too, and the Torah puts it in the order people actually eat: \"And thou shalt eat and be satisfied, and bless the LORD thy God.\" First eat, then be full, then say thanks. One rabbi, Rabbi Meir, taught that a person should say a hundred blessings every day. It sounds like a lot until you count how many things in a day get done without a second look. A hundred small pauses.","screen":["`BLESSED ARE YOU, THE SOURCE.` · `THE EARTH IS THE LORD'S.` · `BUT THE EARTH HATH HE GIVEN.` · `A HUNDRED BLESSINGS A DAY.` · then, large: `THE BLESSING IS THE HINGE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. If there's something to eat or drink near you, hold it, even a glass of water. Look at it. Think of the hands it passed through to reach you: a farmer, a driver, someone stocking a shelf. If you pray, you can say a blessing you know. If not, just say thank you, to wherever it came from. Then drink, slowly.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Bracha. A blessing, said to the source before you eat, and after you're full. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`BRACHA — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: notice before you bite. Two seconds is all the pause asks for. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT CHARITY.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"not charity","word":"tzedakah","hook":"not charity — justice; the box on the counter","length":"7:00","carry":"give before you feel like it","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: bracha, a blessing said to the source. Your line was notice before you bite. Today, the box on the counter. Day sixteen.","screen":["`NOTICE BEFORE YOU BITE.` → `DAY 16.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"On the counter in some Jewish kitchens sits a small tin box with a slot in the top. Nobody makes a speech about it. A kid drops in a coin after finding one on the sidewalk. A grandmother drops a few in on Friday afternoon, before she lights the candles. When it's full, it goes to a food bank, a school, a family down the street. It's easy to call that charity. But the word written on the box doesn't mean charity. It means something sharper. Today: the box on the counter, and why it isn't a favor.","screen":["`A TIN BOX WITH A SLOT.` → `COINS BEFORE THE CANDLES.` → `IT ISN'T CHARITY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Tzedakah comes from tzedek: justice, righteousness, what's right. Charity, in English, sounds like a kindness you choose when you feel moved to. Tzedakah assumes something else: that part of what you have was never fully yours. It's the poor person's share, and giving it is setting things right. That's why, in Jewish law, it isn't optional. The rabbis said that even someone who lives on tzedakah should give some. The Torah says: \"Justice, justice shalt thou follow.\" The word there is tzedek, said twice. The rabbis asked why twice, and one answer is: pursue justice, and pursue it justly, even in how you give. The legal scholar Maimonides laid out eight levels of tzedakah, like the rungs of a ladder. Near the bottom: giving, but grudgingly. Higher: giving before you're asked. Higher still: giving so that neither side knows the other, so nobody has to feel ashamed. And the top rung, above all the others: helping someone so they won't need help again. A loan, a partnership, a job. Tzedakah at its best ends the need for tzedakah. Maimonides also made an observation about habit. A person who gives a thousand coins at once, he said, grows less than a person who gives one coin a thousand times, because it's the repeated act that shapes the giver. That's what the box on the counter is for. Not a feeling of generosity, waited for. A habit, built one coin at a time, until justice is just something your hands do.","screen":["`TZEDEK — JUSTICE, NOT CHARITY.` · `EVEN THE POOR GIVE.` · `EIGHT RUNGS OF A LADDER.` · `ONE COIN, A THOUSAND TIMES.` · then, large: `JUSTICE, JUSTICE SHALT THOU FOLLOW.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. Think of one need near you: a food bank, a neighbor, a cause you care about. Picture one small, specific amount, of money or of time. Not what feels generous. Just what's fair. Hold that amount in mind, and notice how it sits.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Tzedakah. Giving as justice, the poor person's share, not a favor. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`TZEDAKAH — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: give before you feel like it. The habit builds the giver, one coin at a time. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY SOME PRAYERS NEED TEN PEOPLE.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"why some prayers need ten people","word":"minyan","hook":"why some prayers need ten people","length":"7:00","carry":"some things need company","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: tzedakah, giving as justice. Your line was give before you feel like it. Today, a count. Day seventeen.","screen":["`GIVE BEFORE YOU FEEL LIKE IT.` → `DAY 17.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"It's seven in the morning on a weekday, and a man is standing at the door of a small synagogue, looking up and down the street. Inside, eight people are waiting. He counts them again. Eight, nine with him. Then a stranger walks past with a coffee, and the man calls out, a little embarrassed: excuse me, are you Jewish? Do you have ten minutes? We need one more. In synagogues that are short a few people, scenes like this are part of the morning. Today: why some prayers can't be said until there are ten.","screen":["`SEVEN IN THE MORNING.` → `EIGHT, NINE.` → `WE NEED ONE MORE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"A minyan is a count: ten adults, the smallest number Jewish law calls a community. A person can pray alone, anywhere, and Jews do. But certain prayers, the ones that speak as a public, need the ten. The call to prayer, Barchu. The reading of the Torah from the scroll. And the Kaddish, including the mourner's Kaddish, said by someone who has lost a parent or a close relative. Why ten? The rabbis found it in the Torah, in a roundabout way. When twelve spies return from the promised land, ten of them bring back a frightening report, and God calls those ten a congregation. So the rabbis said: ten make a congregation. Some teachers also point back to day eight, when Abraham bargained for Sodom and stopped at ten. Who counts toward the ten is something Jews answer differently. In Orthodox communities, the minyan is ten men, thirteen and older: the age of bar mitzvah, from day six. In Reform and Reconstructionist communities, and in egalitarian Conservative ones, women count equally. Now think about what the rule does for a mourner. By custom, for eleven months after a parent dies, a traditional mourner says Kaddish every day. And because it needs a minyan, they can't say it alone. Every single day, nine other people have to show up, and answer amen. The rule makes sure grief is never carried in an empty room. There's an old teaching that when ten gather to pray, the divine presence is among them. And the tenth person doesn't have to know the words. Being there is what makes it count.","screen":["`MINYAN — A COUNT OF TEN.` · `TEN SPIES, ONE CONGREGATION.` · `WHO COUNTS, COMMUNITIES DIFFER.` · `NO MOURNER SAYS KADDISH ALONE.` · then, large: `WE NEED ONE MORE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. Count your own minyan. Think of the people who would show up if you called: at a hospital, at a funeral, at a table. Name them in your head, one at a time, and see how far you get. Ten, or three, or one. Then think of someone who might not reach ten, and whose count you could join.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Minyan. A count of ten, the smallest community, so some prayers are never said alone. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`MINYAN — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: some things need company. Sometimes being the tenth is the whole job. Day seventeen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE DAY THE WHOLE PEOPLE SAYS SORRY TOGETHER.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"the day the whole people says sorry together","word":"Yom Kippur","hook":"the day the whole people says sorry together","length":"7:00","carry":"you can start over","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: minyan, the count of ten. Your line was some things need company. Today, a day when the room fills up. Day eighteen.","screen":["`SOME THINGS NEED COMPANY.` → `DAY 18.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"It's the tenth day of the seventh month, just after sunset, and the synagogue is full, with faces that don't come every week. Some are dressed in white. Some are wearing sneakers under their good clothes. The fast began before sunset, and it won't end until the stars come out tomorrow night. Then a cantor begins a melody so old and so familiar that some people cry before a word is sung, and it's sung three times, each time a little louder. From this evening to the next, a whole people will say sorry, together. Today: Yom Kippur.","screen":["`A FULL ROOM AT SUNSET.` → `WHITE CLOTHES, NO FOOD.` → `A MELODY SUNG THREE TIMES.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Yom Kippur means the day of atonement. The Torah sets it on the tenth day of the seventh month, in the autumn, and tells the people to afflict their souls, which the rabbis understood as a fast. So from evening to evening: no food, no water, no leather shoes, no washing for pleasure. White clothes are common, like angels, some say, who don't need to eat. The evening begins with Kol Nidre, the melody from the start, whose Aramaic words release vows. And then the confessions. They're in the plural. Not I have sinned. We have. One confession runs through the Hebrew alphabet, a wrong for every letter, and everyone says every line, lightly tapping their chest, including lines about things they personally never did. The whole community stands together as one. Nobody is singled out, and nobody gets to pretend they're above it. But the day has a limit, and the rabbis wrote it down plainly. Yom Kippur atones for what's between a person and God. For what's between you and another person, it doesn't, until you've made it right with them. So in the days before, the custom is to call, write, knock on doors, and ask forgiveness. The fast doesn't replace the phone call. In the afternoon, the story of Jonah is read: a whole city that turns around and is given another chance. As the sun goes down, the last service is called Neilah, the closing of the gates. The doors of the ark stay open, the room stands, and at nightfall one long blast of the ram's horn ends it. Then everybody goes home and eats.","screen":["`THE DAY OF ATONEMENT.` · `WE HAVE SINNED, NOT I.` · `THE FAST DOESN'T REPLACE THE CALL.` · `NEILAH — THE CLOSING OF THE GATES.` · then, large: `ONE LONG BLAST OF THE SHOFAR.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. Bring to mind one person you owe an apology, big or small, recent or old. Don't write the speech yet. Just picture their face, and picture the moment of saying it. Notice what makes it hard. If you pray, you can ask for the courage to make the call.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Yom Kippur. The day of atonement: a day of fasting, confessing together, and closing gates. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`YOM KIPPUR — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you can start over. A whole people says so, out loud, every year. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: RETURN, NOT REPENTANCE.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"return, not repentance","word":"teshuvah","hook":"return, not repentance — you're not lost, you're facing the wrong way","length":"7:00","carry":"turn around","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Yom Kippur, a whole people saying sorry together. Your line was you can start over. Today, how. Day nineteen.","screen":["`YOU CAN START OVER.` → `DAY 19.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"You're driving somewhere new, and you miss the exit. The map on your phone doesn't lecture you. It doesn't tell you you're a bad driver or that you've ruined the trip. It just says: recalculating. And then, somewhere up ahead: make a U-turn. You were never lost, exactly. You were going the wrong way, and the way back was always there. English has a heavy word for what happens after we do wrong: repentance. It sounds like sackcloth and shame. The Hebrew word underneath it is much closer to that voice on the map. Today: teshuvah.","screen":["`YOU MISSED THE EXIT.` → `RECALCULATING.` → `MAKE A U-TURN.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Teshuvah comes from shuv, to turn, to return. It doesn't say you're broken. It says you're facing the wrong way, and you can turn around. The prophet Hosea calls out: \"Return, O Israel, unto the LORD thy God.\" Return. As if home were still there. The same Hebrew word also means an answer, a reply. Turning around is answering a call. The rabbis thought teshuvah was so basic to the world that they listed it among the things created before the world itself, before anyone had done anything wrong. The way back was built before the road. So how does a person actually turn? Maimonides lays it out plainly. Stop doing the thing. Regret it, honestly. Say it out loud, in words, not just in your head. And decide not to do it again. Then his test for complete teshuvah: you find yourself in the same situation, with the same temptation and the same strength, and this time you don't do it. Not because you can't. Because you've turned. Two bold teachings go further. One rabbi says: in the place where a person who has returned stands, even the completely righteous cannot stand. The one who turned has walked a road the others never had to walk. And another says that when teshuvah comes from love, even deliberate wrongs are turned into merits, as if the wrong turn became part of the way home. The month before the new year, and the ten days from Rosh Hashanah to Yom Kippur, are set aside for exactly this. But tradition doesn't lock the door the rest of the year. It holds it open any day.","screen":["`SHUV — TO TURN, TO RETURN.` · `BUILT BEFORE THE WORLD.` · `STOP, REGRET, SAY IT, DECIDE.` · `WHERE THE ONE WHO RETURNS STANDS.` · then, large: `RETURN, O ISRAEL.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. If you can, stand up and slowly turn around, all the way, so you're facing the other side of the room. Then sit. Bring to mind one habit or direction in your life that's pointing the wrong way. Not a verdict on yourself. Just the direction. Picture the U-turn. If you pray, you can ask for help turning.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Teshuvah. Return: not shame, but turning around, and answering the call home. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`TESHUVAH — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: turn around. No shame required, only the U-turn, one at a time. Day nineteen, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT A BOOK.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"not a book","word":"Torah","hook":"not a book — a scroll, hand-written, read to the end and rolled back","length":"7:00","carry":"the world's oldest book club","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: teshuvah, turning around. Your line was turn around. Today, the scroll a thirteen-year-old reads from. Day twenty.","screen":["`TURN AROUND.` → `DAY 20.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Here is a book nobody is allowed to print. Every copy is written by hand, letter by letter, with a quill, on parchment, by a scribe trained for the work. One copy can take many months. If a single letter is missing, or two letters touch, the whole thing can't be used until it's fixed. Readers don't run a finger along the writing; they follow it with a pointer, often silver. When it's carried through a room, people stand, and some reach out to kiss it. When one is too worn to use, it isn't thrown away. It's buried. Today: the scroll at the center of Jewish life.","screen":["`A BOOK NOBODY CAN PRINT.` → `MONTHS TO WRITE ONE COPY.` → `CARRIED, KISSED, BURIED.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Torah comes from a Hebrew root that means to aim, to point, to teach. Torah is teaching, instruction, the way pointed out. In its narrowest sense, it's the five books of Moses, Genesis to Deuteronomy, written in a scroll by a scribe called a sofer. Tradition says the scribes of old counted every letter. Wider, Torah means the whole of the Hebrew Bible. Wider still, all of Jewish teaching, down to a good answer given at a kitchen table last night. A psalm describes it like this: \"Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path.\" Not a floodlight. A lamp, enough for the next step. Here's the part that makes it different from a book on a shelf. The scroll is read aloud, in public, a section at a time, from beginning to end. It's the scroll a bar or bat mitzvah reads from. And on the autumn festival called Simchat Torah, the joy of the Torah, the reader finishes the last lines of Deuteronomy, and right away, without a pause, the reading begins again at Genesis, often from a second scroll already open to the first word. People dance with the scrolls in their arms. The story is never finished. There's a teaching about the letters, too. The Torah ends with the letter lamed and begins with the letter bet. Put the end next to the beginning, and they spell lev, heart. The scroll runs in a circle, and the circle is a heart.","screen":["`TORAH — TEACHING, THE WAY POINTED OUT.` · `A LAMP, ENOUGH FOR THE NEXT STEP.` · `FINISHED, ROLLED BACK, BEGUN AGAIN.` · `LAMED AND BET SPELL HEART.` · then, large: `A LAMP UNTO MY FEET.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell. Think of one teaching that has lit the next step for you: a line from a parent, a teacher, a book, a text you love. Not a whole philosophy. One sentence. Say it to yourself slowly, like a scribe copying it one letter at a time. Then ask where it's pointing you next.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Torah. Teaching: a handwritten scroll, read from end back to beginning, year after year. Twentieth word.","screen":["`TORAH — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: the world's oldest book club. Nobody ever finishes it, and nobody's meant to. Day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THIS WEEK'S PORTION.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"this week's portion","word":"the parsha","hook":"this week's portion; the mountain ahead is a calendar","length":"7:00","carry":"join the cycle wherever it is","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Torah, the handwritten scroll, read to the end and rolled back. Your line was the world's oldest book club. Day twenty-one, the last day of camp one.","screen":["`THE WORLD'S OLDEST BOOK CLUB.` → `DAY 21.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"This Saturday morning, in a synagogue in Buenos Aires, one in Melbourne, one in a Brooklyn basement and one in a Jerusalem neighborhood, the scroll will be opened, most weeks, to the same place. The same chapters, read in the same week, in the same order, by people who will never meet. Some people could tell you this week's portion by name without checking. It's a calendar made of text. And as camp one ends, it's also the road ahead of you on this path. Today: the portion, and where it's about to take us.","screen":["`ONE SATURDAY, EVERY CITY.` → `THE SAME CHAPTERS, THE SAME WEEK.` → `A CALENDAR MADE OF TEXT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"A parsha is a portion. The five books of the Torah are divided into fifty-four of them, and one is read each Sabbath, so the whole scroll is read in about a year. There are fewer Sabbaths than portions in some years, so some weeks two are read together. Each portion is named for one of its first distinctive words. The first is Bereshit, in the beginning. Then Noach, Noah. Then Lech Lecha, go forth, when Abraham leaves home. Some people date things by it, the way others use the month: that happened the week of Noach. The reading isn't only on Saturday morning. Short pieces are read on Monday and Thursday mornings too. The rabbis traced that custom back to the time of Moses, so that no one would go three days without Torah, and credited the scribe Ezra with shaping it. They even suggested a way to study it at home: read the week's portion twice, and once in translation. And here's a detail that says a lot about a scattered people. After some holidays, Israel and the rest of the world fall out of step by a week, and then quietly catch up again. The cycle bends, but it holds. That's why the carry for today is what it is. You don't start the Torah on page one when you're ready. You find out where everyone is, and you step in there. Camp one gave you twenty-one words. Next, camp two: the stories, and it begins exactly where the cycle does, at the first word, Bereshit.","screen":["`FIFTY-FOUR PORTIONS, ONE A WEEK.` · `EACH NAMED FOR AN EARLY WORD.` · `MONDAY, THURSDAY, SABBATH.` · `THE CYCLE BENDS, BUT HOLDS.` · then, large: `STEP IN WHERE EVERYONE IS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min","voice":"Seventy-five seconds, with the bell, to walk back through camp one. Let the words come one by one: the mezuzah, the candles, mensch, seder, the glass, mitzvah, machloket. Abraham, Jacob, Sinai, Shabbat, shalom, the Shema, kavanah. Bracha, tzedakah, minyan, Yom Kippur, teshuvah, Torah, the parsha. Stay with whichever one pulls at you.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The parsha. This week's portion, one of fifty-four, read by a whole people in step. Twenty-first word. Camp one, complete.","screen":["`THE PARSHA — your 21st word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: join the cycle wherever it is. Tomorrow, the stories begin, at the very first word. Day twenty-one.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: BEFORE ANYTHING, A VOICE.`"]}]}},"SIKHISM":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"the word you already say","word":"Waheguru","hook":"said at good news and bad, all day, at home; the word Sikhs say for God, and it means \"wow, the teacher\"","length":"3:00","carry":"wow","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Good news on the phone: Waheguru. Someone stands up after sitting on the floor too long: Waheguru. Hard news, said softer: Waheguru. In many Sikh homes it's said all day long, and hardly ever translated. It turns out to be a name for God built from the smallest word people say when something is bigger than they are.","screen":["`GOOD NEWS: WAHEGURU.` → `HARD NEWS: WAHEGURU.` → `HARDLY EVER TRANSLATED.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Break it in two. Wahe: wow, wonderful, the cry of amazement. Guru: the teacher, the one who leads from darkness into light. So Waheguru is something like: wow, the teacher. Notice what kind of name that is. It isn't a title, like king or judge. It's a reaction. Sikh scripture speaks of a God beyond counting and beyond description, so instead of a description, the name is a response: when words run out, you say wow. That's why the same word fits joy, relief and grief. It isn't reporting how you feel. It's pointing past the feeling, to what's bigger. It's the word Sikhs repeat in meditation, slowly, on the breath. And it's inside the greeting Sikhs trade: Waheguru ji ka Khalsa, Waheguru ji ki fateh. The Khalsa, the community of committed Sikhs, belongs to Waheguru, and so does the victory. Ji is a small word of respect, like dear. So the word you've heard all your life carries a whole idea: wonder isn't only a mood. It's a way of paying attention. And no Punjabi needed here: every word on this path comes with what's inside it.","screen":["`WAHE — WOW. GURU — TEACHER.` · `NOT A TITLE. A REACTION.` · `THE SAME WORD FOR JOY AND GRIEF.` · `INSIDE THE SIKH GREETING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Look at something near you: your hand, a window, a cup. Breathe in and look at it as if you'd never seen one before. On the way out, if you like, let the word go with the breath, aloud or silently: Waheguru. Wow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Waheguru. Wahe, wow; guru, the teacher. The name Sikhs say for God, made of wonder. First word on your strand.","screen":["`WAHEGURU — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: wow. It's the whole name, said the way it feels, and now you know what's inside it. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: EVERYONE ON THE FLOOR, EVERYONE THE SAME MEAL.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"everyone on the floor, everyone the same meal","word":"langar","hook":"everyone on the floor, everyone the same meal","length":"3:00","carry":"nobody sits higher","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Waheguru. Wahe, wow; guru, the teacher. Today, a meal plenty of people have eaten sitting on a gurdwara floor.","screen":["`WAHEGURU ✓` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Next to the Golden Temple in Amritsar there's a kitchen that never closes. Anyone can walk in and eat: no ticket, no questions. People at the temple speak of tens of thousands of meals on an ordinary day. And there are no chairs. A banker sits beside a laborer, a first-time visitor beside a grandmother who's come for sixty years, all on the floor in long rows. Where you sit usually says who you are. Here, it can't.","screen":["`A KITCHEN THAT NEVER CLOSES.` → `NO TICKET. NO CHAIRS.` → `LONG ROWS ON THE FLOOR.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Langar is the free community kitchen, and the meal that comes out of it. Gurdwaras keep one. Tradition traces it to Guru Nanak, who shared food with everyone who came to his town of Kartarpur, and the Gurus after him built it into an institution. Here's why it mattered. In Nanak's Punjab, caste rules decided who could eat with whom, and who could even cook for whom. Eating side by side across those lines could be a scandal. The langar did it on purpose, every day. The row everyone sits in even has its own name: pangat. Three details carry the idea. The floor: nobody sits higher, because there's nothing to sit higher on. The food: simple and vegetarian, so someone of any faith or diet can eat without asking what's in it. And the guest list: there isn't one. Any faith, or none. You sit down, hold out your hands, and someone puts a roti in them. The meal is a teaching you can taste: one God, one row.","screen":["`LANGAR — THE KITCHEN AND THE MEAL.` · `EATING ACROSS THE LINES OF CASTE.` · `PANGAT — THE ROW.` · `THE FLOOR, THE FOOD, THE GUEST LIST.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Picture yourself in the row: on your left, someone you'd never usually sit beside; on your right, someone else. Breathe in. As you breathe out, hold out your hands, palms up, the way people do when the roti comes down the row.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Langar. The free kitchen, and the meal where everyone sits in one row and eats the same food. Second word.","screen":["`LANGAR — your 2nd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: nobody sits higher. That's the whole teaching of the meal, in three words. Day two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: IS THAT A HAT?`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"is that a hat?","word":"the turban","hook":"what's under the turban, what the color means, and the plain steel circle on the wrist","length":"3:00","carry":"a crown you tie yourself","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: langar, where nobody sits higher. Today, the questions a lot of Sikhs get asked at school and at work.","screen":["`NOBODY SITS HIGHER.` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Is that a hat? What's under there? Does the color mean something? Sikhs who wear a turban hear these at school, at work and in the airport security line. The real answers are better than most people expect. And there's a second thing that often needs explaining, on the wrist: a plain steel circle.","screen":["`IS THAT A HAT?` → `WHAT'S UNDER THERE?` → `AND THE CIRCLE ON THE WRIST.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Start underneath. Many Sikhs keep their hair uncut all their lives, as a way of accepting the body as it was made. That's kesh. The turban, the dastar or pagg, covers and looks after that hair. So no, it isn't a hat that comes off with the weather. Most who wear one tie it fresh each morning, over hair combed and knotted on top, yards of cloth wrapped layer by layer. Sikh tradition remembers that in the Gurus' world a fine turban marked kings and nobles. The tenth Guru, Guru Gobind Singh, made it part of his community's identity, so every Sikh who ties one wears a crown, whatever family they come from. The color? For most, simply a choice, or what goes with the day. And not every Sikh wears one: some women do, many cover their hair with a scarf, and Sikhs keep the faith in different ways. If you've cut your hair, you're every bit as welcome on this path. Now the wrist. The kara is a plain steel bracelet: a circle with no beginning and no end, like the one God. Steel, not gold, on purpose. It isn't jewelry. Sikhs often say that when your hand reaches for something, the kara gets there first and asks: should my hand be doing this?","screen":["`KESH — HAIR, LEFT AS IT GREW.` · `THE DASTAR — A CROWN YOU TIE.` · `THE COLOR IS USUALLY A CHOICE.` · `THE KARA — STEEL, NOT GOLD.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, sit up and let the crown of your head rise, as if something precious rested there. On the second, touch your wrist, where a kara would sit. On the third, think of one thing your hands will reach for today.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The turban. A crown tied fresh over hair left as it grew, and beside it, the steel kara. Third word.","screen":["`THE TURBAN — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: a crown you tie yourself. Next time someone asks if it's a hat, that's the short answer. Day three.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: FIVE WHO SAID YES.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"five who said yes","word":"Panj Pyare","hook":"the five people dressed alike at the front of the parade, and the day in 1699 they stand in for","length":"3:00","carry":"five strangers, one family","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the turban, a crown you tie yourself, and the steel kara. Today, the morning in 1699 that both trace back to.","screen":["`A CROWN YOU TIE YOURSELF.` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"At the front of a Sikh parade, ahead of the float carrying the scripture, walk five people dressed alike, swords held upright. They aren't security, and they aren't there for show. They're standing in for five people from more than three hundred years ago, who did something nobody in the crowd that day expected.","screen":["`FIVE, DRESSED ALIKE, AT THE FRONT.` → `NOT SECURITY. NOT FOR SHOW.` → `STANDING IN FOR FIVE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 20","voice":"Spring, 1699, at Anandpur in the hills of Punjab. A huge crowd has gathered for the harvest festival, Vaisakhi. The tenth Guru, Guru Gobind Singh, walks out with a drawn sword and asks a question that silences everyone: who will give their head for the faith? One man stands, Daya Ram. The Guru takes him into a tent and comes back out alone. Then he asks again. Four more stand, one at a time. What happened inside that tent, the tellings differ. What they agree on is what came next: all five walked out alive, dressed alike, beside the Guru. They're remembered as the Panj Pyare: panj, five, pyare, beloved. And look who they were. A shopkeeper from Lahore. A farmer from near Delhi. A water-carrier from Puri, on the eastern coast. A cloth printer from Dwarka, in the far west. A barber from Bidar, in the south. Castes that, around them, would never have shared a meal, from homes a thousand miles apart. They became the first of the Khalsa, the community of committed Sikhs: one family. The men took one name, Singh, lion, and Sikh women carry the name Kaur. Two names that rank no one.","screen":["`WHO WILL GIVE THEIR HEAD?` · `ALL FIVE WALK OUT ALIVE.` · `FIVE CASTES, FIVE CORNERS OF INDIA.` · `SINGH AND KAUR — ONE FAMILY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, picture the crowd on the hillside, gone quiet. On the second, picture one person standing up when nobody else will. On the third, sit a little taller yourself, the way someone does when they stand for what they love.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Panj Pyare. The five beloved: strangers from across India who stood up, and walked out one family. Fourth word.","screen":["`PANJ PYARE — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: five strangers, one family. The five at the front of every parade are standing in for them. That's day four.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE STANDING PRAYER.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"the standing prayer","word":"Ardas","hook":"the standing prayer; \"sarbat da bhala\" — the good of all","length":"3:00","carry":"for everyone, not just us","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Panj Pyare, five strangers, one family. Today, the prayer that remembers them every time it's said.","screen":["`FIVE STRANGERS, ONE FAMILY.` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"End of the service. Someone steps forward, and the whole room rises: palms together, facing the scripture. Plenty of people have stood for it since they were small without knowing what's being said. Then a voice calls out, bole so nihal, and the room shouts back, sat sri akal. Today, what everyone is standing for.","screen":["`THE ROOM RISES.` → `PALMS TOGETHER.` → `WHAT IS BEING SAID?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"It's the Ardas. The word comes from Persian, for a petition, a humble request. Sikhs say it standing, facing the Guru Granth Sahib, at the end of services and before anything important: a wedding, a journey, a new home. It has a shape. It opens by remembering the Gurus, one by one, from Nanak on. Then it remembers more: the scripture, the five beloved from yesterday, and the men and women who suffered for their faith. A prayer with a long memory. Then it turns personal. The one leading adds the day's requests: a new baby, a grandmother in the hospital, thanks for a new job. So no two are quite the same. And the end never changes. It asks for chardi kala, ever-rising spirits, and then three words: sarbat da bhala. The good of all. Not the good of Sikhs, or of this room. Everyone. After all that memory, the last thing asked for is the well-being of the whole world. Then comes the shout. Bole so nihal: whoever says it is blessed. And the answer, sat sri akal: true is the timeless one. The same words many Sikhs use to say hello.","screen":["`ARDAS — A HUMBLE REQUEST.` · `A PRAYER WITH A LONG MEMORY.` · `THE DAY'S OWN REQUESTS.` · `SARBAT DA BHALA — THE GOOD OF ALL.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Stand if you can, palms together. On the first, bring to mind the people closest to you. On the second, widen it to strangers. On the third, widen it once more, to someone you find hard to wish well.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Ardas. The standing prayer: it remembers, it asks, and it ends by asking for the good of all. Fifth word.","screen":["`ARDAS — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: for everyone, not just us. Those are the prayer's last words, and they leave nobody out. Day five, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: ARE SIKHS HINDU OR MUSLIM?`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"are Sikhs Hindu or Muslim?","word":"Sikh","hook":"the question Sikhs get asked from both sides, and the plain answer: a learner, on a path of its own","length":"3:00","carry":"a learner, on a path of its own","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Ardas, the standing prayer that ends with the good of all. Today, a question many Sikhs get asked, and a plain answer to it.","screen":["`FOR EVERYONE, NOT JUST US.` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"So, are Sikhs a kind of Hindu? Or a kind of Muslim? Sikhs hear both, sometimes in the same week. Surveys in the United States have found many people guessing Sikhism is a branch of Islam; others assume it's part of Hinduism. The plain answer is neither. And why people mix it up turns out to be a good story.","screen":["`A KIND OF HINDU?` → `A KIND OF MUSLIM?` → `THE PLAIN ANSWER: NEITHER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 20","voice":"Start with the word. Sikh is Punjabi for a learner, a student: someone who learns from the Guru. So nobody starts out finished. The path began with Guru Nanak, born, tradition says, in 1469, in Punjab, and grew through ten Gurus over about two hundred years. It has its own scripture, its own initiation, which you met on day four, and its own houses of worship. So it's a religion of its own, not a branch of another. Why the mix-up? Nanak was born into a Hindu family and grew up among Hindu and Muslim neighbors. His companion on years of travel, Mardana, was a Muslim musician. Sikh scripture uses names for God that both neighbors used, and it even holds hymns by holy poets born into Hindu and Muslim families, beside the Gurus' own. Sharing words isn't the same as being the same. Then there's the turban and the beard, which people who don't know often read as Muslim, and that mistake hasn't always been harmless. The first words Nanak is remembered saying, as his mission began, were: there is no Hindu, there is no Muslim. Some hear in that: beneath the labels, everyone is made by the one. Others hear a challenge: nobody was living up to the name they carried. Either way, it put no one down. It was a starting point.","screen":["`SIKH — A LEARNER.` · `ITS OWN PATH, FROM GURU NANAK.` · `SHARING WORDS ISN'T BEING THE SAME.` · `THERE IS NO HINDU, THERE IS NO MUSLIM.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, notice one label people put on you. On the second, notice one you put on someone else. On the third, set both down for a breath, and see the person underneath.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Sikh. A learner, a student of the Guru, on a path that began with Guru Nanak. Sixth word.","screen":["`SIKH — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: a learner, on a path of its own. That's the plain answer, and it needs nobody else's faith put down to say it. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE SYMBOL ON THE FLAG.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"the symbol on the flag","word":"the Khanda","hook":"the symbol on the flag outside the gurdwara, on car windows and pendants: what each piece means","length":"3:00","carry":"the inner and the outer, held together","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Sikh, a learner on a path of its own. Six words so far: Waheguru, langar, the turban, Panj Pyare, Ardas, Sikh. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`WAHEGURU · LANGAR · THE TURBAN · PANJ PYARE · ARDAS · SIKH`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"It's on the flag outside the gurdwara. On car window stickers, pendants, gates, phone cases and forearms. A circle, a straight double-edged sword through the middle, and two curved swords wrapped around the outside. Plenty of people could draw it from memory. Fewer could say what each piece means.","screen":["`ON THE FLAG. ON THE CAR. ON THE ARM.` → `A CIRCLE, AND THREE SWORDS.` → `WHAT EACH PIECE MEANS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"It's called the Khanda, after the sword in the middle. A khanda is a straight, double-edged sword, the very kind of blade that stirred the water at the first initiation, with the five from day four. Sikhs often read its two edges as the power to cut truth from falsehood. Around it, the circle, the chakkar. It was once a real steel ring, thrown in battle, but in the emblem it's read as the one with no beginning and no end, like the kara on the wrist. Then the two curved swords on the outside. They're usually read as miri and piri: the outer life and the inner life, the world and the spirit, held together. The sixth Guru, Guru Hargobind, wore two swords to say exactly that. So at the center there's no crown and no face: just a blade that cuts lies. The flag it flies on, the Nishan Sahib, is saffron or blue, on a tall pole, so anyone, from far off, can find shelter and a meal. And at Vaisakhi, the spring harvest festival and the day the five stood up, many gurdwaras lower the flag, wash the pole and dress it in fresh cloth.","screen":["`THE KHANDA — A DOUBLE-EDGED SWORD.` · `THE CIRCLE — NO BEGINNING, NO END.` · `MIRI AND PIRI — WORLD AND SPIRIT.` · `THE FLAG SAYS: SHELTER AND A MEAL.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, think of your outer life: work, bills, the list of things to do. On the second, your inner life: what you hope for, what you love. On the third, hold both at once, the way the two swords hold the circle.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The Khanda. A double-edged sword, a circle without end, and two swords for world and spirit. Seventh word: a week's worth.","screen":["`THE KHANDA — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the inner and the outer, held together. That's week one: seven words, and what's inside each. Tomorrow, the hands nobody sees.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE HANDS NOBODY SEES.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"the hands nobody sees","word":"seva","hook":"the shoes handed back, the plates scrubbed, the roti rolled by someone you'll never meet; the word for that","length":"3:30","carry":"one quiet thing for someone","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Last week ended with the Khanda: the inner and the outer, held together. Week two starts with the hands. Day eight.","screen":["`THE INNER AND THE OUTER.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"You leave your shoes at the gurdwara door, and when you come back they're lined up waiting, sometimes wiped clean. Someone scrubbed the plate you ate langar from. Someone rolled the roti. In the great kitchen in Amritsar, a banker chops onions beside a teenager washing steel plates. Nobody's keeping score, and nobody asks your name. There's a word for what those hands are doing.","screen":["`YOUR SHOES, HANDED BACK.` → `A BANKER CHOPPING ONIONS.` → `NOBODY KEEPING SCORE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"The word is seva: selfless service, done for others with no reward in mind. Here's what makes it different from just being nice. In Sikh life it's part of the spiritual life, not something beside it. The Gurus return to the idea again and again: service done with a humble heart is a way of drawing near to God. Not a warm-up for the real practice. The practice. Hands in the dishwater count. And seva has a condition: it's meant to be done without ego. The hymns warn that service done to be seen, thanked or admired quietly turns into something else. That's why so much of it happens where nobody's watching. The person who scrubbed your plate doesn't know your name, and that isn't an accident. Sikh teaching names three kinds: with your body, like cooking or cleaning; with your mind, like teaching or listening; and with what you have, like food or money. So there's no one who can't do it. And if you've seen Sikh volunteers turn up with hot meals after a flood or a fire, now you know the word they'd use.","screen":["`SEVA — SELFLESS SERVICE.` · `NOT BESIDE THE PRACTICE. THE PRACTICE.` · `DONE WITHOUT EGO.` · `BODY, MIND, AND WHAT YOU HAVE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Breathe, and bring to mind one person who could use a hand. Not a grand gesture: a dish, a message, a door held. Picture yourself doing it without telling anyone. Stay with that picture for a while: one person, one quiet thing.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Seva. Selfless service, done without ego, and part of the spiritual life itself. Eighth word.","screen":["`SEVA — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one quiet thing for someone. Seva works best when nobody hears about it, not even over dinner. Day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE FIRST SYMBOL OF THE SCRIPTURE.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"the first symbol of the scripture","word":"Ik Onkar","hook":"the first symbol of the scripture — one","length":"3:30","carry":"one","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: seva. Your line was one quiet thing for someone. Today, the very first mark in the Sikh scripture. Day nine.","screen":["`ONE QUIET THING FOR SOMEONE.` → `DAY 9.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Most books start with a title, or once upon a time. The Sikh scripture, the Guru Granth Sahib, starts with a symbol. You may have seen it without knowing: on a gold pendant, a dashboard, over a shop door, on a forearm. A Gurmukhi numeral with a sweeping curve flying off the top. Before any story, any rule, any song: this.","screen":["`A LONG BOOK OF HYMNS.` → `THE FIRST THING ISN'T A WORD.` → `IT'S A SYMBOL.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"It's called Ik Onkar. It starts with a numeral: the digit one. Not the word, the number, the one you'd write on a price tag. That's Ik. Then Onkar, a very old sound for the divine, related to Om, which Nanak's Hindu neighbors chanted. And the curve, sweeping up and out, which some Sikhs read as a line with no end. Together: there is one. One God, one reality, one source behind everything. Why a numeral? One way Sikh teachers explain it: a word belongs to one language, and can be argued with. Everyone agrees on one. And notice what that one includes. On this path, the one isn't far off, looking down on creation. The hymns say it's in everything: in you, in the person across the street, in the river and the dust. So if there's one, nobody stands outside it. You can hear Guru Nanak's line from day six underneath: there is no Hindu, there is no Muslim. If there's one, the labels come second.","screen":["`IK — THE NUMERAL ONE.` · `ONKAR — AN ANCIENT SOUND FOR GOD.` · `A NUMBER NOBODY CAN ARGUE WITH.` · `IF THERE'S ONE, NO ONE IS OUTSIDE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Breathe, and notice yourself: your hands, your chest rising. Then notice something beyond you: a sound in the room, someone you love. Then hold both at once, and if you like, let one word ride out on the breath: one.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Ik Onkar. The numeral one, joined to an ancient sound for God: there is one, and it opens the whole scripture. Ninth word.","screen":["`IK ONKAR — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one. Same source, for you and for the person who annoys you most. That's day nine.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"ONE CREATOR, TRUTH IS THE NAME.\"`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"\"one creator, truth is the name\"","word":"the Mool Mantar, line 1","hook":"\"one creator, truth is the name\"","length":"3:30","carry":"the name is truth","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Ik Onkar, the numeral one that opens the scripture. Today, the words that come right after it. Day ten.","screen":["`IK ONKAR — ONE.` → `DAY 10.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Describe God in a single breath. Not a book, not a sermon: a few words, short enough for a child to memorize and deep enough for a lifetime. Right after the symbol for one, at the top of the scripture, Guru Nanak does exactly that. Sikhs call it the Mool Mantar, the root verse, and many say it every morning. Today, its first line.","screen":["`DESCRIBE GOD IN ONE BREATH.` → `THE ROOT VERSE.` → `A SEED THAT HOLDS A TREE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Mool means root. Mantar is the Punjabi form of mantra, a sacred phrase. The scripture's language is centuries old, and even fluent Punjabi speakers look words up, so one line at a time is how everyone reads it. After Ik Onkar, it says: Sat Naam. Karta Purakh. Sat means truth, and something deeper than true as opposed to false: it comes from an old root meaning what really is, what lasts. Naam means name. Sat Naam: truth is the name, or the one whose name is truth. The Victorian scholar Max Arthur Macauliffe, who translated with Sikh scholars beside him, gave the opening as: \"There is but one God whose name is true, the Creator.\" Here's the surprise in naam. On this path the name isn't just a label, like yours printed on a bill. It comes to mean the whole presence of God, running through everything. Then Karta Purakh. Karta, the maker. Purakh, the being. The creator: not a force that set the world going and walked away, but the one still making. Three ideas in five words: there is one, its name is truth, and it creates. And a quiet challenge inside: if truth is God's name, then telling the truth is a way of saying it.","screen":["`MOOL MANTAR — THE ROOT VERSE.` · `SAT — WHAT REALLY IS.` · `NAAM — MORE THAN A LABEL.` · `KARTA PURAKH — THE ONE STILL MAKING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Breathe, and think of one true thing about your life right now: not the flattering version, just the true one. Sit with it without fixing it. If you like, let two words go out with the breath: Sat Naam.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The Mool Mantar, the root verse. Line one: Ik Onkar, Sat Naam, Karta Purakh. There is one, truth is the name, the creator. Tenth word.","screen":["`THE MOOL MANTAR — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the name is truth. Which makes an honest sentence a small way of saying it. Day ten.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: \"WITHOUT FEAR, WITHOUT HATE.\"`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"\"without fear, without hate\"","word":"the Mool Mantar, line 2","hook":"\"without fear, without hate\"","length":"3:30","carry":"nirbhau, nirvair","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Mool Mantar's first line. Your line was the name is truth. Today, the two words that come next. Day eleven.","screen":["`SAT NAAM — THE NAME IS TRUTH.` → `DAY 11.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A boss with someone's job in their hands. A bully at the bus stop. And on the other side, a grudge kept for years. Fear and hate are strange twins: people fear those with power over them, resent those who wronged them, and between the two they can fill a whole life. In the root verse, Guru Nanak describes God with two words that cut right through both.","screen":["`A BOSS, A BULLY.` → `A GRUDGE KEPT FOR YEARS.` → `TWO WORDS THAT CUT THROUGH BOTH.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Nirbhau. Nirvair. Nir means without. Bhau means fear. Vair means enmity, the settled hostility of an enemy. So: without fear, without hate. Why together? Fear is what you feel toward something that could hurt you. Hate is what you feel toward something you think is against you. A God who is one, who holds everything, has nothing outside to fear and no enemy to hate. The verse goes on: beyond time, never born, existing by itself, known by the Guru's grace. But these two words have a life of their own, because Sikhs don't only say them about God. They take them as a picture of who a person can become. The ninth Guru, Guru Tegh Bahadur, wrote a short verse describing the wise person as one who frightens no one, and is frightened by no one. Both halves matter. Fearless isn't enough if it makes you frightening. Gentle isn't enough if it makes you afraid. So nirbhau, nirvair isn't only a description to admire from a distance. It's a direction.","screen":["`NIR — WITHOUT.` · `BHAU, FEAR. VAIR, HATE.` · `FRIGHTENS NO ONE, FEARS NO ONE.` · `NOT JUST ABOUT GOD. A DIRECTION.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Breathe, and name one thing you're afraid of. Then bring to mind one person you hold something against. Loosen your grip on both, only a little. If you like, let the words go out with the breath: nirbhau, nirvair.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The Mool Mantar, line two. Nirbhau, nirvair: without fear, without hate. Said of God, and taken as a direction for a life. Eleventh word.","screen":["`NIRBHAU, NIRVAIR — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: nirbhau, nirvair. Less fear and less hate, both at once, which is harder than either alone. Day eleven.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: HONEST WORK.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"honest work","word":"kirat karni","hook":"honest work; Nanak's three pillars start here","length":"3:30","carry":"earn it clean","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Mool Mantar, line two: without fear, without hate. Today, the first of three pillars Sikhs build a life on. Day twelve.","screen":["`NIRBHAU, NIRVAIR.` → `DAY 12.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A mountaintop. A begging bowl. A cave. The classic pictures of a holy man: someone who gave everything away, or left his family to meditate alone. Guru Nanak, back from his years on the road, chose differently. He spent the rest of his life on a farm by a river, plowing fields, raising a family, growing food. The founder of a faith, with his hands in the dirt.","screen":["`THE MOUNTAINTOP. THE BEGGING BOWL.` → `NANAK CHOOSES A FARM.` → `THE FIRST OF THREE PILLARS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Kirat karni. Kirat means honest labor, the work of your hands and your head. Karni means to do. So: do honest work. Earn your living, and earn it cleanly. Sikhs often describe daily life as resting on three pillars, and this is the first. In Nanak's day, some seekers left ordinary life behind: work, family and home, living on alms. Nanak honored sincere seekers wherever he met them. But the life he taught was the householder's. You don't have to leave the world to find God; you find God in the middle of it, in the field, in the shop, at the kitchen table. So in his last years, at Kartarpur, the Guru farmed. A line in the scripture sums it up. In paraphrase: the one who eats what they've earned by their own effort, and gives some of it away with their own hand, recognizes the path. Notice the word honest. Work without cheating, without exploiting anyone, without taking what isn't yours. The first pillar isn't in a temple. It's on Monday morning.","screen":["`KIRAT — HONEST LABOR. KARNI — TO DO.` · `NOT LEAVING THE WORLD. LIVING IN IT.` · `THE GURU WHO FARMED.` · `EARNED BY EFFORT, WITHOUT CHEATING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Look at your hands for a moment. Think about the work they do: paid work, housework, schoolwork, caring for someone. Pick one piece of it that's coming up, and picture doing it cleanly: no corner cut, nothing taken that isn't yours. Stay with that picture.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Kirat karni. Honest work: earning a living by your own effort, without cheating anyone. The first pillar. Twelfth word.","screen":["`KIRAT KARNI — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: earn it clean. The shortcut nobody would notice is exactly where this pillar stands. Day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: SHARE WHAT YOU EARN.`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"share what you earn","word":"vand chakna","hook":"share what you earn","length":"3:30","carry":"share it first","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: kirat karni. Your line was earn it clean. Earning honestly is half of it. Today, the other half. Day thirteen.","screen":["`KIRAT KARNI — EARN IT CLEAN.` → `DAY 13.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A kid on the school bus has a bag of chips, and a friend is looking at it. There's a tiny moment where the kid decides: hold it tighter, or tip the bag over and say, want some? Most of us are still making that decision, just with bigger bags: a paycheck, a spare room, an afternoon. Today, the second pillar.","screen":["`A BAG OF CHIPS ON THE BUS.` → `HOLD IT TIGHTER, OR TIP IT OVER?` → `THE SECOND PILLAR.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Vand chakna. Vand means to share, to divide into portions. Chakna means to eat, to take your part. Share, and then eat. Notice the order. Not eat, and share what's left over. Share first, then enjoy what's yours. Yesterday's line from the scripture praised the one who earns by effort and gives some away with their own hand. Today is that second half. Your own hand: not a tax, not a charity you've never seen. Many Sikhs give in a particular way called dasvandh, a tenth: a tenth of what they earn, set aside for others and the community, the langar, the gurdwara, someone in need. Some give exactly a tenth, some give what they can, and some give their time instead. And the two pillars lean on each other. Honest work alone could make a hard worker who keeps everything. Sharing alone could be generous with money that wasn't fairly earned. Together: earn cleanly, then let some of it go. The langar from day two runs on exactly this.","screen":["`VAND — SHARE. CHAKNA — EAT.` · `SHARE FIRST, THEN ENJOY.` · `DASVANDH — A TENTH.` · `EARN CLEAN, THEN LET SOME GO.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Think about what came into your hands this week: money, food, time, a skill, a kindness. Pick one thing, and picture dividing it, the way you'd tip the bag over. Picture who you'd hand a portion to, and their face when you do. Stay with that.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Vand chakna. Share, then eat: dividing what you've earned so others have a portion too. The second pillar. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`VAND CHAKNA — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: share it first. Not what's left over at the end, but the first portion. Day thirteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE THIRD PILLAR.`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"the third pillar","word":"naam japna","hook":"the third pillar — remember the name, on the breath","length":"3:30","carry":"earn it clean, share it first, keep the name","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: vand chakna. Your line was share it first. Today, the third pillar, the one that holds the other two up. Day fourteen.","screen":["`VAND CHAKNA — SHARE IT FIRST.` → `DAY 14.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A stool with two legs holds someone for a moment, if they're careful. Add a third leg, and it holds all day. Honest work, sharing what you earn: both solid. But a person could do both and still run on empty: busy, generous, and forgetting what any of it is for. In the Sikh telling, there's a third leg.","screen":["`A STOOL WITH TWO LEGS.` → `BUSY, GENEROUS, AND EMPTY.` → `THE THIRD LEG.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 20","voice":"Naam japna. Naam, the name from day ten: not just a label, but the whole presence of God. Japna means to repeat, to keep saying. So: keep the name. Remember God, again and again, all through the day. Its everyday form has its own word: simran, remembrance. How do you remember something all day long? You tie it to something you do all day anyway. You breathe. Wahe on the in-breath, guru on the out-breath: the word from day one. Some Sikhs do it sitting still before dawn, some with prayer beads, some while they drive or cook. If you've ever wondered whether simply repeating Waheguru counts, it's one of the most common ways Sikhs practice. It can be silent, or a whole room singing the scripture to drums and harmonium. Here's why it sits beside work and sharing. Without it, honest work can turn into pride: look how hard I work. Sharing can turn into a show: look how much I give. Remembering keeps both humble, because it reminds you where the strength to work, and everything you share, came from. And remembering without working or sharing would float off into the clouds. Earn it clean, share it first, keep the name. Three legs.","screen":["`NAAM — THE NAME. JAPNA — TO REPEAT.` · `SIMRAN — WAHE IN, GURU OUT.` · `IT KEEPS WORK AND GIVING HUMBLE.` · `WORK, SHARE, REMEMBER, TOGETHER.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Feel what you're sitting on, and how it holds you up without your thinking about it. Breathe slowly. If you'd like, let the name ride on the breath: Wahe in, guru out. If that word isn't yours, let each breath carry one thing you want to remember is real. Keep coming back to it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Naam japna. Keeping the name: remembering God through the day, often on the breath, as simran. The third pillar. Fourteenth word. Seva, Ik Onkar, the root verse twice, kirat karni, vand chakna, naam japna: that's week two.","screen":["`NAAM JAPNA — your 14th word` → `WEEK TWO. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: earn it clean, share it first, keep the name. That's the whole stool. Next week: the company, the door, the spirit and the book. Day fourteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE CONGREGATION.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"the congregation","word":"sangat","hook":"the congregation; why Sikhs never practice alone for long","length":"3:30","carry":"good company","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Last week ended with the three pillars: earn it clean, share it first, keep the name. Week three starts with the people around you. Day fifteen.","screen":["`EARN IT CLEAN, SHARE IT FIRST, KEEP THE NAME.` → `WEEK THREE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Around certain people, a person gets funnier, kinder, braver. Around others, more anxious and quicker to gossip. Nobody gives an order; the room just does it. It's why parents want to meet their kids' friends. People become, a little at a time, like the people they sit with. The Gurus built that plain fact into the center of Sikh life.","screen":["`FUNNIER AROUND SOME PEOPLE.` → `ANXIOUS AROUND OTHERS.` → `WE BECOME OUR COMPANY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Sangat. The congregation, the company you keep. You'll often hear sadh sangat, the company of the holy. Everywhere Guru Nanak traveled, the stories say, he left a sangat behind: a small circle of people who met to sing the hymns, share food and look after one another. Long before there were grand buildings, there were these circles of ordinary people. So what makes a sangat true? The scripture asks that very question, and the answer, in paraphrase, is simple: where the one name is spoken. Not where the building is finest or the people richest. Sikhs speak of the sangat with a startling kind of respect. There's a long tradition that the Guru is present wherever the sangat gathers, and that the congregation, deciding together, carries real weight. That's why so much of Sikh life happens with others. Not because time alone is bad; remembering on your own breath matters. But a person alone tends to drift, and a person in good company gets carried, the way a small boat is carried by a current.","screen":["`SANGAT — THE COMPANY YOU KEEP.` · `NANAK LEFT A CIRCLE BEHIND.` · `WHERE THE ONE NAME IS SPOKEN.` · `A PERSON ALONE DRIFTS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Bring to mind the people you spend the most time with. Not to judge them; just notice. Who do you leave feeling lighter? Who helps you remember what matters? Picture one of them clearly, and let yourself be grateful for them, and stay there a while.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Sangat. The congregation, the company you keep, gathered around the one name. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`SANGAT — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: good company. You can't choose every room you're in, but you can choose some of them. Day fifteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE DOOR OF THE GURU.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"the door of the guru","word":"the gurdwara","hook":"the door of the guru; why it's open to anyone","length":"3:30","carry":"cover your head, take off your shoes, come in","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: sangat. Your line was good company. Today, the place where that company meets. Day sixteen.","screen":["`SANGAT — GOOD COMPANY.` → `DAY 16.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"On a street you know, there's a tall flag, saffron or blue, on a pole outside a building: maybe a white one with domes, maybe a converted warehouse or an old church. Through the door you can hear singing and drums, and smell something cooking. And someone walking by might wonder: can I just walk in? Today, the answer.","screen":["`A TALL FLAG ON A POLE.` → `SINGING, DRUMS, SOMETHING COOKING.` → `CAN I JUST WALK IN?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Gurdwara. Gur, the Guru. Dwara, a door. The door of the Guru. The flag from day seven, the Nishan Sahib, is there so it can be found from far away. And yes: anyone can walk in, of any faith or none. In Amritsar, the Golden Temple was built with doors on all four sides, which Sikhs explain as a welcome from every direction. There are a few things everyone does, Sikh or not. Cover your head; gurdwaras usually keep scarves at the door. Take off your shoes. Wash your hands. Leave tobacco and alcohol outside. That's all. Inside, the last two weeks meet under one roof. At the front, on a raised platform under a canopy, rests the scripture, the Guru Granth Sahib. People bow, then sit on the floor, all at one level: the sangat. Musicians sing the hymns: naam japna, out loud. Near the end comes the Ardas, then karah prashad, a warm sweet of flour, butter and sugar, placed in cupped hands. Then the langar. And the people cooking, cleaning and minding the shoes? That's seva. A gurdwara is the whole path, given a building and a door that stays open.","screen":["`GUR, THE GURU. DWARA, A DOOR.` · `OPEN TO ANYONE, FROM EVERY SIDE.` · `HEAD COVERED, SHOES OFF, HANDS WASHED.` · `THE WHOLE PATH UNDER ONE ROOF.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Do the threshold with your body. Lift your hands to your head, as if laying a cloth over it. Rub your palms together slowly, as if rinsing them. Feel your feet on the floor, bare or as if they were. Then stay at the door a while, listening: singing somewhere inside, and nobody asking who you are.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The gurdwara. The door of the Guru: the Sikh place of worship, open to anyone. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`THE GURDWARA — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: cover your head, take off your shoes, come in. That's all a first visit asks of anyone. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: EVER-RISING SPIRITS.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"ever-rising spirits","word":"chardi kala","hook":"ever-rising spirits — optimism as a discipline","length":"3:30","carry":"rise anyway","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the gurdwara. Your line was cover your head, take off your shoes, come in. Today, a two-word answer to how are you. Day seventeen.","screen":["`THE GURDWARA — COME IN.` → `DAY 17.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Ask a Sikh grandfather how he's doing. He's eighty-one, his knees are bad, and the doctor's news this week wasn't great. There's a decent chance he'll smile and say two words: chardi kala. Not I'm fine. Something more like: spirits rising. He's noticed everything. He's just decided which way his spirit is going to face.","screen":["`BAD KNEES, HARD NEWS.` → `CHARDI KALA.` → `NOT A MOOD. A DISCIPLINE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Chardi kala. Chardi means rising, climbing. Kala means spirit, energy, a state of being. Ever-rising spirits. Here's the difference between chardi kala and just being cheerful. Cheerfulness depends on circumstances. Chardi kala is meant to hold when circumstances collapse. And Sikh history has asked a great deal of it. There were long stretches, especially in the eighteenth century, when Sikhs were hunted, driven into forests and hills. Out of those years came a whole defiant way of speaking: tradition remembers that a lone Sikh might call himself sava lakh, a hundred and twenty-five thousand. One man, a whole army. Not denial: a refusal to let circumstances write your spirit for you. Where does it come from? From trust. Sikhs speak of hukam, the will of God running through everything. If all of life, even the hard parts, is held inside the one, despair doesn't get the last word. You heard the phrase on day five: the Ardas asks for it near its very end. And chardi kala doesn't forbid sadness. Sikhs grieve, and weep. It just keeps pointing up, like a plant that grows toward the light in a dark room.","screen":["`CHARDI — RISING. KALA — SPIRIT.` · `NOT CHEERFULNESS. IT HOLDS WHEN THINGS FALL.` · `ONE SIKH, A WHOLE ARMY.` · `TRUST IN HUKAM, THE WILL OF GOD.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Let one heavy thing come to mind, something that's actually hard right now. Don't push it away. Then, without denying any of it, let your posture rise a little: lift your chest, soften your face, breathe in as if drawing yourself upward. You're not saying it's fine. You're choosing which way your spirit faces. Stay there, with the weight and the rising together.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Chardi kala. Ever-rising spirits: hope practiced as a discipline, holding even when life is hard. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`CHARDI KALA — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: rise anyway. Not pretending it's fine; just choosing which way to face. Day seventeen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHAT A SIKH CARRIES AND WHY.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"what a sikh carries and why","word":"the five Ks","hook":"what a Sikh carries and why","length":"3:30","carry":"reminders you can touch","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: chardi kala. Your line was rise anyway. Today, five things some Sikhs carry every day. Day eighteen.","screen":["`CHARDI KALA — RISE ANYWAY.` → `DAY 18.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A wedding ring turned during a hard meeting. A photo in a wallet. A grandmother's watch that doesn't keep time anymore. Small things people carry to remember. They don't do anything, exactly. Touch one, and something comes back: a promise, a person, who someone meant to be. Initiated Sikhs carry five of them, every day.","screen":["`A RING, A PHOTO, A WATCH.` → `TOUCH IT, AND SOMETHING COMES BACK.` → `A SIKH CARRIES FIVE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"They're the five Ks, because in Punjabi each one begins with the sound k. Sikhs initiated into the Khalsa, the family that began with the five on day four, keep all five. Kesh: uncut hair, from day three, under the turban. Kanga: a small wooden comb tucked into the hair. Keep the hair clean and ordered, and the life too. Kara: the steel circle on the wrist, also from day three. Kachera: a cotton undergarment, like simple shorts: self-restraint, and readiness to move. Kirpan: a small sword, worn in a sheath, often under the clothes. It stands for the duty to protect people who can't protect themselves, and for dignity and courage. It isn't there for aggression. Notice what all five have in common. None is a decoration. A comb, a bracelet, underwear, hair: plain, ordinary things. The reminders aren't locked away in a temple. They're on the body, at work, at the gym, all day. Faith you can touch. Many Sikhs who aren't initiated still wear the kara, and wherever you are with the rest, this path starts from there.","screen":["`FIVE THINGS THAT BEGIN WITH K.` · `KESH AND KANGA — HAIR, AND ITS COMB.` · `KARA — THE HAND REMEMBERS.` · `KACHERA AND KIRPAN — RESTRAINT, PROTECTION.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Choose one thing you're wearing or carrying: a ring, a watch, a hair tie, your sleeve. Put your hand on it and decide, just for now, what it will remind you of: something you believe, a person, a promise. Close your hand around it and breathe with that meaning for a while.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The five Ks. Kesh, kanga, kara, kachera, kirpan: five reminders a committed Sikh carries every day. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`THE FIVE KS — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: reminders you can touch. Faith that goes where you go, not left behind at the door. Day eighteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE TENTH GURU, THE KHALSA, THE SAINT-SOLDIER.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"the tenth guru, the khalsa, the saint-soldier","word":"Guru Gobind Singh","hook":"the tenth Guru, the Khalsa, the saint-soldier","length":"3:30","carry":"sword and saint","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the five Ks. Your line was reminders you can touch. Today, the Guru they trace back to. Day nineteen.","screen":["`THE FIVE KS — REMINDERS YOU CAN TOUCH.` → `DAY 19.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"Nine years old. A father leaving home, knowing he may not come back. He has gone to the capital to stand up for the right of people from another faith to practice theirs, and he doesn't come back. Now the community turns to the boy and tells him: you are the Guru. That's how the tenth Guru's leadership began, in 1675, as Sikh tradition tells it. The boy grew into a poet, a warrior and the founder of the Khalsa.","screen":["`NINE YEARS OLD.` → `A FATHER WHO DOESN'T COME BACK.` → `YOU ARE THE GURU.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Guru Gobind Singh, the tenth and last human Guru of the Sikhs. His father was the ninth Guru, Guru Tegh Bahadur. Sikh tradition remembers that he went to Delhi on behalf of Hindu Kashmiri pandits who were being pressed to convert, and was executed there by the Mughal authorities. Sikhs honor him as a martyr who gave his life for the freedom of others to worship. That memory shaped his son. Guru Gobind Singh taught that a person of faith should be two things at once. The Sikh phrase is sant sipahi: saint and soldier. Saint, because the inner life comes first: remembrance, humility, the name. Soldier, because when the weak are being crushed, a saint doesn't look away. The kirpan from yesterday comes from exactly this. And the order matters. A soldier with no saint in him is only violence; a saint with no courage may stand by while others suffer. He wrote devotional poetry Sikhs still recite every day, and in 1699 he called the five from day four out of the crowd. He lost his father, and all four of his sons, to the violence of his time, and is still remembered for chardi kala. And near the end of his life he decided there would be no eleventh human Guru.","screen":["`THE TENTH AND LAST HUMAN GURU.` · `A FATHER WHO DIED FOR OTHERS' FREEDOM.` · `SANT SIPAHI — SAINT AND SOLDIER.` · `THE SAINT COMES FIRST.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Let one hand rest open on your lap: that's the saint, quiet, open, remembering. Let the other close into a gentle fist: that's the courage to stand up for someone. Feel both at once. Now think of one person in your life who might need someone to stand up for them, and hold them in mind, with both hands.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Guru Gobind Singh. The tenth Guru, founder of the Khalsa, poet and warrior: the saint-soldier. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`GURU GOBIND SINGH — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: sword and saint. The saint comes first, and the courage serves it. Day nineteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: A SCRIPTURE THAT IS THE GURU.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"a scripture that is the guru","word":"the Guru Granth Sahib","hook":"a scripture that is the Guru; why it's carried, covered, put to bed","length":"3:30","carry":"a living book","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Guru Gobind Singh. Your line was sword and saint. Today, who he named to come after him. Day twenty.","screen":["`GURU GOBIND SINGH — SWORD AND SAINT.` → `DAY 20.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"Late evening at a gurdwara. The last hymn has been sung. Someone gently closes a very large book, wraps it in embroidered cloth, and lifts it onto their head. Someone else waves a whisk of long white hair over it, the way attendants once did for a king. It's carried to its own room, laid on a bed and covered for the night. At dawn it's carried back out and opened, and the day begins. Why would anyone put a book to bed?","screen":["`A LARGE BOOK, WRAPPED IN CLOTH.` → `CARRIED ON SOMEONE'S HEAD.` → `PUT TO BED FOR THE NIGHT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 20","voice":"The Guru Granth Sahib. Granth means book. Sahib is a word of deep respect. And Guru, the teacher from day one. The Guru who is a book. Here's how that happened. The fifth Guru, Guru Arjan, gathered the hymns of the Gurus before him and his own, and in 1604 installed the collection at the Harmandir Sahib in Amritsar. Then, in 1708, near the end of his life, Guru Gobind Singh declared there would be no more human Gurus. From then on, Sikhs would find their Guru in the scripture. So when Sikhs bow before it, they aren't bowing to paper and ink. They're bowing to the Guru, present in the words. That's why it's treated like a revered teacher in the room: higher than everyone, under a canopy, attended with a whisk, put to rest at night. And inside? Hymns by six of the Gurus, and, remarkably, by holy poets who weren't Sikhs at all: Kabir, a weaver; Ravidas, from a caste many looked down on; Sheikh Farid, a Muslim Sufi. Almost all of it is poetry, arranged by musical mode, meant to be sung. Every morning, gurdwaras open it and read the first hymn on the page as the hukamnama, the order of the day. Sikhs don't only read this book. They listen to it.","screen":["`GRANTH — BOOK. SAHIB — RESPECT.` · `1708: NO MORE HUMAN GURUS.` · `TREATED LIKE A TEACHER IN THE ROOM.` · `GURUS AND SAINTS, SIDE BY SIDE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Think of words that have spoken to you: a line from a book, a letter, something someone once said that you've never forgotten. Bring it to mind as exactly as you can. Now imagine those words not as print, but as someone sitting beside you, saying them to you now. Let them speak, and just listen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The Guru Granth Sahib. The Sikh scripture, honored as the living Guru since 1708, holding the Gurus' hymns and the words of other saints. Twentieth word.","screen":["`THE GURU GRANTH SAHIB — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: a living book. Read as if someone were speaking to you today. Day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"the mountain ahead","word":"Japji Sahib","hook":"the mountain ahead; Nanak's morning prayer","length":"4:00","carry":"38 stanzas, verse by verse","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Guru Granth Sahib. Your line was a living book. Today, the poem it opens with, and the last day of camp one.","screen":["`THE GURU GRANTH SAHIB — A LIVING BOOK.` → `DAY 21.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"30 sec","voice":"It's still dark outside. Four in the morning, maybe five. In homes in Punjab, London, Toronto, California and Nairobi, someone is already up. They've bathed. They sit quietly, cover their head, and begin to recite a long poem, often from memory. Some learned it as children, a verse at a time, beside a grandparent before school. It's the first thing in the scripture and the first thing in their day.","screen":["`FOUR IN THE MORNING.` → `A LONG POEM, FROM MEMORY.` → `THE FIRST THING IN THE DAY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 30","voice":"Japji Sahib. Jap, to recite, the same root as naam japna. Ji and Sahib, words of love and respect. It's Guru Nanak's great poem, and it opens the Guru Granth Sahib. It begins with the root verse from days ten and eleven. Then a short verse that the translator Macauliffe gave like this: \"The True One was in the beginning; the True One was in the primal age.\" Then come thirty-eight stanzas, called pauris, steps or rungs, like a ladder, and a closing verse that pictures air as the Guru, water as the father, and the earth as the great mother of all. Inside, it's full of questions. Can you think your way to God? Not if you think a hundred thousand times, the first stanza says. What will do it? Living within hukam, God's will, the word from day seventeen. It's the first of the daily prayers, and nobody has to start with all of it: many begin with the root verse, then a stanza, then a little more. That's why your line is a promise. Later on this path, we'll climb Japji verse by verse. And look how far you've come. Waheguru, langar, the turban, the Panj Pyare, Ardas, Sikh, the Khanda. Seva, Ik Onkar, the root verse, both lines, kirat karni, vand chakna, naam japna. Sangat, the gurdwara, chardi kala, the five Ks, Guru Gobind Singh, the Guru Granth Sahib. And now Japji. Twenty-one words. Camp one is done.","screen":["`JAP — TO RECITE.` · `THE TRUE ONE WAS IN THE BEGINNING.` · `THIRTY-EIGHT STEPS, LIKE A LADDER.` · `TWENTY-ONE WORDS. CAMP ONE DONE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min","voice":"Sit tall, the way someone sits in the early morning to recite. Picture a mountain path ahead of you, thirty-eight steps rising into the light. You don't have to climb today. Just stand at the bottom and look up. Then look back down the path you've already walked: twenty-one days, twenty-one words. Breathe with both, what's behind and what's ahead.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Japji Sahib. Guru Nanak's morning poem, the first in the scripture: the root verse, thirty-eight stanzas and a closing verse. Twenty-first word.","screen":["`JAPJI SAHIB — your 21st word` → the strand complete → `CAMP ONE. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: 38 stanzas, verse by verse. One rung at a time, starting whenever you like. Tomorrow camp two begins: the stories, starting with a baby born in a small village in 1469.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS.` → `CAMP ONE COMPLETE.` → `TOMORROW: BORN AT TALWANDI, 1469.`"]}]}},"SPIRITUAL":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"everything happens for a reason","word":"meant to be","hook":"the line everyone says after a breakup, and nobody can source","length":"3:00","carry":"not why, but what now","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Everything happens for a reason. It gets said after a breakup, a missed flight, a job that fell through. It's on mugs and in captions. It often gets pinned on Aristotle, but you won't find the line in anything he wrote. So where does it come from, and why does it feel so true, even to people who aren't sure what they believe?","screen":["`EVERYTHING HAPPENS FOR A REASON.` → `NOT ARISTOTLE.` → `SO WHERE'S IT FROM?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Start with the mind. Researchers asked young children why rocks are pointy: because of how they formed, or so animals could scratch on them? Many picked the animals. Children see purpose everywhere, and adults never fully grow out of it. In one set of studies, adults were asked about big events in their own lives. Believers often said those events happened for a reason, and so did a surprising number of atheists. A mind that's always reading other people's intentions starts reading them into events, too. Then the old ideas. Aristotle wrote that nature does nothing in vain: an eye is for seeing. That's about how living things are built, not about your breakup. The Stoics went further and saw the whole universe ordered by reason. Many religions speak of providence, God at work in what happens, often in ways no one can see at the time. Those are beliefs, held by serious people for a very long time, and no experiment can settle them. And here's the part that works either way. Why did this happen? often has no answer you can reach. What now? always does. Whether or not there's a reason built in, there's a meaning you can make with what comes next.","screen":["`WHY ARE ROCKS POINTY?` · `NATURE DOES NOTHING IN VAIN.` · `PROVIDENCE: AN OLD BELIEF.` · `NOT WHY, BUT WHAT NOW.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Bring to mind one thing from this year you still can't explain. Don't try to. Breathe in with it. As you breathe out, let the question turn from why to what now. No answer needed yet.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Meant to be. A feeling nearly every mind makes, and, for many people, a belief. First word on your strand.","screen":["`MEANT TO BE — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: not why, but what now. It works whatever you believe about the why. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NO BLANK MIND REQUIRED.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"no blank mind required","word":"breathe","hook":"meditation isn't clearing your mind; the word spirit means breath","length":"3:00","carry":"wander, notice, come back","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: meant to be, and the question that always has an answer: not why, but what now. Today, a word hiding inside spiritual.","screen":["`MEANT TO BE ✓` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The line that makes people quit meditating is clear your mind. They sit down, the mind does the opposite, and they decide they're bad at it. If that's you, here's the good news. Nobody's mind goes blank on command, and the old teachers didn't ask it to. They asked for something much smaller.","screen":["`CLEAR YOUR MIND?` → `NOBODY'S DOES.` → `SOMETHING SMALLER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Spiritual comes from the Latin spiritus, which means breath. Inspire once meant to breathe into; expire, to breathe out for the last time. Greek, Hebrew and Sanskrit each have a word that means breath and spirit at once. So the spiritual in spiritual but not religious starts, at its root, with breathing. Now the technique, the way breath meditation is actually taught. Feel one breath: cool air at the nose, the belly rising. Within a few breaths the mind wanders off: an email, a song, an argument from Tuesday. Then, at some point, you notice. That moment of noticing isn't the failure. It's the practice. In 1890 the psychologist William James wrote that \"bringing back a wandering attention, over and over again, is the very root of judgment, character, and will.\" Over and over. Wander, notice, come back: that's one rep, like lifting a weight. A mind that wanders twenty times in a minute just did twenty reps. And breath has one more trick. You can't slow your heart by deciding to. You can slow your breath, and a long breath out tends to settle the body.","screen":["`SPIRITUS — BREATH.` · `FEEL ONE BREATH.` · `WANDER, NOTICE, COME BACK.` · `EVERY RETURN IS A REP.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Breathe in slowly and feel where the air goes. Let it out a little longer than it came in. If a thought shows up halfway, that's fine. Notice it, and finish the breath. That's one rep.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Breathe. The root under the word spirit, and the place you come back to. Second word on your strand.","screen":["`BREATHE — your 2nd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: wander, notice, come back. The wandering is part of it, not proof you're bad at it. Day two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE TATTOO THAT'S REALLY ABOUT TODAY.`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"the tattoo that's really about today","word":"memento mori","hook":"a skull, an hourglass, a wilting rose: a death reminder that's really about living","length":"3:00","carry":"while you can, be good","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: breathe, and wander, notice, come back. Today, two Latin words people get tattooed.","screen":["`MEANT TO BE · BREATHE` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"A skull on a forearm. An hourglass. A wilting rose with two Latin words underneath. Memento mori tattoos are everywhere, and they can look a little goth. The idea behind them is older and warmer than the ink suggests. A Roman emperor practiced it in a private notebook, and his version ends somewhere you might not expect. First, a guess.","screen":["`A SKULL. AN HOURGLASS. A ROSE.` → `MEMENTO MORI.` → `GOTH? NOT QUITE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Memento mori is Latin: remember that you will die. It sounds grim. It was meant as the opposite. Marcus Aurelius, emperor of Rome, kept a notebook of reminders to himself, now called the Meditations, and he came back to death again and again. In one old translation: \"Do not act as if thou wert going to live ten thousand years. Death hangs over thee. While thou livest, while it is in thy power, be good.\" Listen to where it lands. Not on fear. On be good, while you can. One honest note: Marcus wrote in Greek. The Latin phrase is better known from later Christian art, the skulls and hourglasses tucked into old paintings. And no single school owns the idea. Early Buddhist texts teach mindfulness of death as a regular practice, and a psalm asks to be taught to number our days. Here's the napkin math that makes it land. Eighty years is a little over four thousand weeks. Put the end in view, and the middle comes clear: the call, the apology, the trip stop waiting for next month.","screen":["`REMEMBER THAT YOU WILL DIE.` · `WHILE YOU CAN, BE GOOD.` · `NOT ONE SCHOOL'S IDEA.` · `ABOUT FOUR THOUSAND WEEKS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, notice that this breath is one of a limited number: not a threat, just a fact. On the second, let one thing come to mind that you keep saving for next month. On the third, let it come a little closer.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Memento mori. Remember that you will die, so that today counts. Third word on your strand.","screen":["`MEMENTO MORI — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: while you can, be good. That's where the emperor's sentence lands, not on the skull. That's day three.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHAT NAMASTE ACTUALLY SAYS.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"what namaste actually says","word":"namaste","hook":"the word at the end of yoga class is how a lot of the world says hello","length":"3:00","carry":"mean the bow","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: memento mori, and while you can, be good. Today, the word at the end of yoga class.","screen":["`WHILE YOU CAN, BE GOOD.` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Class ends. Everyone sits up, palms together, and the teacher says namaste. Then there's the mug that says namaste in bed. In much of India and Nepal, it isn't a yoga word at all. It's how you greet your aunt, your neighbor, a guest at the door. And what it says is plainer, and kinder, than most people think. First, a guess.","screen":["`PALMS TOGETHER. NAMASTE.` → `NOT A YOGA WORD.` → `HOW YOU GREET YOUR AUNT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Namaste is Sanskrit, in two pieces. Namas, a bow. Te, to you. A bow to you. People say it to elders, guests and strangers, usually with the palms pressed together at the chest and a small dip of the head. Sometimes the gesture says it with no word at all. You may have heard it means the light in me honors the light in you. That's a modern reading, not a translation. It draws on a real idea some Hindu teachers find in the bow: that something sacred lives in every person, so you're bowing to that, not to someone's job or mood. So is it okay to say it in a yoga class? Hindus don't all feel the same way about that, and it's worth knowing they differ. What tends to sting is the word as a punchline: on a beer can, or as a joke for leave me alone. What tends to land is the plain thing it is: a greeting, meant. The difference isn't who's saying it. It's whether the bow is real.","screen":["`NAMAS — A BOW.` · `TE — TO YOU.` · `THE LIGHT LINE IS A READING.` · `A GREETING, MEANT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Bring your palms together at your chest, if you'd like. On the first breath, feel your hands meet. On the second, bring to mind someone you'll see today. On the third, a small bow of the head, toward them, wherever they are.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Namaste. Namas, a bow; te, to you. A hello with respect inside it. Fourth word.","screen":["`NAMASTE — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: mean the bow. Said that way, it's what it has always been in India and Nepal: a real greeting. Day four.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: MANIFESTING, HONESTLY.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"manifesting, honestly","word":"manifesting","hook":"vision boards and billions of views: what's psychology, what's belief, and where it came from","length":"3:00","carry":"picture the wish, then the wall","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: namaste, a bow to you. Today, the word on a lot of vision boards.","screen":["`NAMASTE ✓` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Vision boards. Scripting in a journal. Saying it like it's already yours. Manifesting has billions of views online, and lately a lot of people say it's started to feel a little fake. That's a fair instinct. It isn't ancient, and it isn't a secret. The idea grew up in America about a hundred and fifty years ago. Some of it holds up surprisingly well. Some of it doesn't. Here's which is which.","screen":["`VISION BOARDS. SCRIPTING.` → `NOT ANCIENT. NOT A SECRET.` → `WHAT HOLDS UP?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 20","voice":"In the 1800s, an American movement called New Thought taught that the mind shapes health and fortune: change the thought, change the life. Its writers are mostly forgotten now, but their phrases aren't. In 2006 a book called The Secret gathered their ideas under one name, the law of attraction, and sold tens of millions of copies. Now, honestly. There's no good evidence that a thought, on its own, pulls a thing toward you. And the strong version has a cruel flip side: if things go wrong, you must have thought wrong. Nothing backs that, and it isn't kind. But two pieces hold up. First, naming what you want changes what you notice. Decide you want a new job, and openings start jumping out at you. Second, the psychologist Gabriele Oettingen found something surprising. People who only daydream about the dream tend to put in less effort, and often get less of it. What works better is to picture the wish, then picture what's in the way, and make a plan: when this happens, I'll do that. So keep the vision board, if you love it. Just give it a second column: the wall, and the way over it.","screen":["`NEW THOUGHT, 1800S AMERICA.` · `A THOUGHT ALONE DOESN'T PULL.` · `WANTING CHANGES WHAT YOU NOTICE.` · `THE WISH, THEN THE WALL.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, picture one thing you want this year, as clearly as you can. On the second, picture the main thing in the way: a habit, a fear, a full calendar. On the third, make one small plan: when that happens, I'll…","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Manifesting. An American idea from the 1800s with a new name. Keep the wish; add the wall. Fifth word.","screen":["`MANIFESTING — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: picture the wish, then the wall. The second half is the part the research backs. Day five, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: SHADOW WORK, MINUS THE JARGON.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"shadow work, minus the jargon","word":"the shadow","hook":"TikTok's favorite inner-work word comes from a Swiss psychiatrist, and from an older line about a speck and a beam","length":"3:00","carry":"what bugs me may be mine","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: manifesting, and picture the wish, then the wall. Today, a word from the inner-work corner of the internet.","screen":["`THE WISH, THEN THE WALL.` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Shadow work. Journaling prompts, sixty-second videos, a workbook on every shelf. It sounds dark and a little mystical. It's neither, exactly. The word comes from a Swiss psychiatrist, and the idea is one you've already lived: the coworker who drives you up the wall for reasons you can't quite name.","screen":["`SHADOW WORK.` → `DARK? MYSTICAL?` → `THE COWORKER WHO DRIVES YOU UP THE WALL.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 20","voice":"About a century ago, the psychiatrist Carl Jung used the word shadow for the parts of yourself you'd rather not claim. Not only the ugly bits. Anger you learned to hide, yes, but also ambition, softness, a talent that didn't fit the family. What we don't own, Jung thought, doesn't disappear. It tends to show up somewhere else, often in how strongly other people bother us. He called that projection. The idea is older than him. In the Sermon on the Mount, Jesus asks: \"why beholdest thou the mote that is in thy brother's eye, but considerest not the beam that is in thine own eye?\" A speck in theirs, a plank in yours. So shadow work, minus the jargon, is one simple move. When someone gets under your skin more than they should, ask: is any of this mine? Not always. Sometimes people really are just annoying. And the higher self you hear about online is a looser idea, gathered from many places. Jung's own aim was plainer: to become whole, not perfect. For the light version, a notebook is plenty. For old, heavy pain, a good therapist is better company than any app.","screen":["`THE PARTS YOU'D RATHER NOT CLAIM.` · `PROJECTION.` · `A SPECK IN THEIRS, A PLANK IN YOURS.` · `IS ANY OF THIS MINE?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, think of someone who gets under your skin. On the second, name the trait that bugs you, in one word. On the third, ask, gently, whether any of that word lives in you, hidden or wanted. No verdict needed.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"The shadow. The parts of you that you'd rather not claim, the dark ones and the bright ones. Sixth word.","screen":["`THE SHADOW — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: what bugs me may be mine. Not always. Often enough to be worth asking. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHAT KARMA ACTUALLY KEEPS TRACK OF.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"what karma actually keeps track of","word":"karma","hook":"instant karma, the comments say; the word is older, and it points at you","length":"3:00","carry":"the intention is the seed","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the shadow, and what bugs me may be mine. Six words so far: meant to be, breathe, memento mori, namaste, manifesting, the shadow. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`MEANT TO BE · BREATHE · MEMENTO MORI · NAMASTE · MANIFESTING · THE SHADOW`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Karma. The ex who cheated gets a flat tire. Someone cuts the line and spills their coffee. Instant karma, the comments say. Used like that, it's the universe keeping score and paying out where you can watch. It's a satisfying idea. The word is far older than that use, and it points the other way: not at them. At you.","screen":["`KARMA.` → `THE FLAT TIRE. THE SPILLED COFFEE.` → `PAYBACK?` → `NOT QUITE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Karma is Sanskrit for action. A deed. Hindu, Buddhist, Jain and Sikh teachers all use the word, and they don't all use it the same way. Many teach that what we do shapes what comes after, in this life and, as a matter of faith, in lives to come. The Buddha made a sharp move. Intention, he told his monks, is what I call karma. Not only what your hands did: what you meant by it. The choice is the seed. Then a surprise. He listed the exact workings of karma among a few things not worth trying to figure out, because chasing them only leads to frustration. So the version that keeps score on other people, did they get what's coming, is the one the old teachers spent the least time on. The older word turns you around. Not, what will happen to them? But, what am I planting with this choice, right now? It's the one piece you can actually see, and the one piece that's yours.","screen":["`KARMA — ACTION, A DEED.` · `INTENTION IS WHAT I CALL KARMA.` · `DON'T TRY TO CALCULATE IT.` · `WHAT AM I PLANTING?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, bring to mind one choice you'll make today: a reply, a tone of voice, a small favor. On the second, notice the intention underneath it. On the third, choose the seed you'd like it to be.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Karma. Action, and the intention inside it. Not a scoreboard. Seventh word: a week's worth.","screen":["`KARMA — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: the intention is the seed. That's week one: seven words people borrow every day, and what's actually inside them. Tomorrow, the thoughts that aren't you.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE THOUGHTS THAT AREN'T YOU.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"the thoughts that aren't you","word":"thoughts","hook":"not you; weather","length":"5:30","carry":"let it pass","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: karma, and the intention is the seed. Week one is behind you. Week two starts inside your head.","screen":["`THE INTENTION IS THE SEED.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Here's a thought for you: you left the stove on. Did you? Probably not. But for half a second, your stomach believed it. Thoughts arrive like that all day, uninvited, sounding completely sure of themselves. One large study checked in with people through their phones, at random moments, and asked what they were doing and what they were thinking about. Their minds were somewhere else almost half the time. Two words help with that. Not you. Weather.","screen":["`YOU LEFT THE STOVE ON.` → `ALMOST HALF THE TIME, ELSEWHERE.` → `NOT YOU. WEATHER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"In 1890, William James described something everyone lives with and few had put well: the stream of thought. Consciousness, he wrote, \"is nothing jointed; it flows.\" A river, not a row of boxes. Thoughts come and go on it, and you didn't send most of them. Nearly two thousand years earlier, the Greek teacher Epictetus, who was born enslaved, came at it another way. In an old translation: \"Men are disturbed not by the things which happen, but by the opinions about the things.\" The trouble is less the event than the judgment we add on top of it, and that judgment is ours to examine. Many Buddhist teachers, especially in the Tibetan tradition, use a picture you may like: the mind is like the sky, and thoughts are like weather. Clouds, storms, bright spells. They move through. The sky isn't the clouds, and it isn't harmed by them. A stream, an opinion you can question, weather in the sky. Three different pictures, and each one treats a thought as something that happens in you, not the whole of you.","screen":["`IT IS NOTHING JOINTED; IT FLOWS.` · `NOT THINGS, BUT OPINIONS.` · `THE SKY AND THE WEATHER.` · `SOMETHING THAT HAPPENS IN YOU.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Here's the practice. The bell gives you about forty-five seconds. Sit, and let the breath settle. When a thought shows up, name it quietly: thinking. Don't follow it, and don't fight it. Watch it move on, the way weather does. Then you're back here, in the body that's breathing.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Thoughts. Things that happen in you, like weather. Not the whole of you. Eighth word.","screen":["`THOUGHTS — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: let it pass. Not every thought that sounds sure of itself gets a vote. Day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: FEET ON THE FLOOR, HANDS ON THE KNEES.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"feet on the floor, hands on the knees","word":"body","hook":"feet on the floor, hands on the knees — you have one","length":"5:30","carry":"come back down","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: thoughts, and let it pass. Today, the place that sits underneath them.","screen":["`THOUGHTS.` → `LET IT PASS.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Quick check. Where are your feet right now? If that took a second to answer, no surprise. A day can be lived about a foot above your own shoulders: in a screen, in a conversation that already happened, in tomorrow's list. Meanwhile there's a whole body down here, keeping you alive, carrying you around, sending messages that mostly go unopened. Feet on the floor. Hands on the knees. You have one.","screen":["`WHERE ARE YOUR FEET?` → `A FOOT ABOVE YOUR SHOULDERS.` → `YOU HAVE ONE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Start with an old instruction for sitting. The Yoga Sutras, compiled in India and traditionally credited to Patanjali, say almost nothing about the poses people now call yoga. On posture they're very brief: the seat should be steady and easy. The word is asana, and there it means simply a seat, the place you sit. Steady, so you're not fidgeting. Easy, so you're not straining. One of the oldest Buddhist teachings on mindfulness puts the body first too: breathing in, breathing out, standing, walking, sitting, lying down. Knowing, plainly, what the body is doing while it's doing it. Then, in 1890, William James turned common sense around. Common sense says we cry because we're sad. James said it may run the other way: \"we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble.\" Psychologists have argued about how far that goes ever since. The smaller point is one you can check for yourself: body and feeling are tangled together, and a change in one tends to show up in the other. That's why the body makes such a good doorway. You can't always choose your thoughts. You can usually choose to put your feet flat on the floor.","screen":["`ASANA: A SEAT, STEADY AND EASY.` · `MINDFULNESS STARTS WITH THE BODY.` · `AFRAID BECAUSE WE TREMBLE.` · `FEET FLAT ON THE FLOOR.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Here's the practice. Before the bell, set your feet flat on the floor if you can, and rest your hands on your knees or in your lap. For about forty-five seconds, feel three things in turn: your feet, whatever is holding you up, and your hands, just resting. Steady and easy. That's the whole seat.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Body. Where mindfulness starts, and a place you can always come back to. Ninth word.","screen":["`BODY — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: come back down. Your feet are always somewhere to land. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: PASCAL: THE QUIET ROOM.`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"pascal: the quiet room","word":"sit","hook":"Pascal: all the unhappiness of men arises from one single fact, that they cannot stay quietly in their own chamber (Pensées 139, Trotter's 1910 translation)","length":"5:30","carry":"sit anyway","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: body, and come back down. Today, the hardest easy thing there is.","screen":["`BODY.` → `COME BACK DOWN.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"Try this, just in your head. You're in a plain room. No phone. No book. Nobody else. Nothing to do for fifteen minutes. How long before you reach for something? Researchers at the University of Virginia tried a version of this in 2014. They left people alone with nothing but their thoughts and a button that gave a mild electric shock. People who had already tried the shock, and said they'd pay to avoid it, still pushed the button: most of the men, and about a quarter of the women. Anything, rather than just sitting. More than three hundred and fifty years earlier, a French mathematician saw this coming.","screen":["`A PLAIN ROOM. NOTHING TO DO.` → `A BUTTON.` → `ANYTHING BUT SITTING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 50","voice":"His name was Blaise Pascal. As a young man he built one of the first mechanical calculators and did serious work on probability. Later, after an intense religious experience, he filled his last years with notes toward a defense of Christian faith. He died at thirty-nine, and his friends published the notes as the Pensées, the thoughts. You may have seen one on a poster: all of humanity's problems stem from man's inability to sit quietly in a room alone. That's a modern paraphrase. An old English translation has it this way: \"I have discovered that all the unhappiness of men arises from one single fact, that they cannot stay quietly in their own chamber.\" Not problems. Unhappiness. And listen to his reason. A man will chase a hare all day, Pascal says, that he wouldn't take if you handed it to him. He doesn't want the hare. He wants the chase, because it keeps him from thinking about himself. Pascal's own answer to what waits in the quiet room was a Christian one. But his observation stands by itself: the restlessness isn't a sign something is wrong with you. He saw it in everyone. Zen Buddhism in Japan built a whole practice around sitting, called zazen. The teacher Dogen taught a form called shikantaza, just sitting: no goal, nothing to win. You sit, and the restlessness comes and goes like anything else. Two very different reasons. Both noticed the chair.","screen":["`THE POSTER LINE IS A PARAPHRASE.` · `THE HARE HE WOULDN'T TAKE.` · `ZEN: JUST SITTING.` · `THE RESTLESSNESS IS EVERYONE'S.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Here's the practice. The bell gives you about forty-five seconds. Let your hands rest in your lap. Sit. Breathe. Notice the small pull to do something else. You don't have to fight it, and you don't have to obey it. That pull is exactly what Pascal was writing about, and you're sitting with it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Sit. The simplest instruction there is, and somehow one of the hardest. Not to do anything. Just to stay. Tenth word.","screen":["`SIT — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: sit anyway. A restless sit still counts as a sit, every time. Day ten.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: 144 TIMES A DAY.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"144 times a day","word":"the phone","hook":"144 times a day; putting it face down is a practice","length":"5:30","carry":"face down for three minutes","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: sit, and sit anyway. Today, the thing that makes sitting so hard. It may be in your hand.","screen":["`SIT.` → `SIT ANYWAY.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"45 sec","voice":"A hundred and forty-four. That's how many times a day, according to one survey of Americans, people check their phones. Spread over sixteen waking hours, it's about once every six or seven minutes. And there's a decent chance you're listening to this on one. A lot of those checks don't feel like decisions. The hand just goes. Nobody here is going to tell you to throw it in a lake. It's how you talk to people you love, and it's how you got here today. But today's idea is small and a little radical: putting it face down is a practice.","screen":["`ABOUT 144 TIMES A DAY.` → `THE HAND JUST GOES.` → `FACE DOWN IS A PRACTICE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Remember Pascal from yesterday, who traced the unhappiness of men to one fact: \"they cannot stay quietly in their own chamber\"? He was writing about hunting and gambling and the royal court. He never saw a phone. But he'd recognize the reach of the hand. In 2017, researchers at the University of Texas ran a simple experiment. People did tests of attention and memory with their phones either on the desk face down, in a pocket or bag, or in another room. All the phones were silent. The people whose phones were in another room did best. The phone didn't have to buzz. Just being nearby seemed to pull at part of the mind. So face down is a start, and the next room is even better. And an old practice already knows this shape. In Jewish tradition, the Sabbath is a day each week when work stops, from sundown Friday to nightfall Saturday. The rabbi Abraham Joshua Heschel described it as a kind of palace built in time rather than in space: not a place you go, but a stretch of hours you protect. For Sabbath-observant Jews, the phone is set down with the rest of the week's work. Some people outside Judaism now keep what they call a tech sabbath. You don't have to start with a whole day. The point isn't that the phone is bad. The point is that the hand should be yours.","screen":["`PASCAL NEVER SAW A PHONE.` · `ANOTHER ROOM DID BEST.` · `A PALACE BUILT IN TIME.` · `THE HAND SHOULD BE YOURS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Here's the practice. If you're not listening on your phone, put it face down now, or in a bag. If you are, set it down, screen down, as the bell starts. Then sit for about forty-five seconds. Notice any pull toward it: the hand, the itch, the thought that something might be waiting. Just notice the pull, and let it pass, like weather. It'll still be there after the bell.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"The phone. A tool you love, and a hand that reaches on its own, until you put it face down on purpose. Eleventh word.","screen":["`THE PHONE — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: face down for three minutes. Three minutes is enough to find out whose hand it is. That's day eleven.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE QUIET THE MYSTICS SHARE.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"the quiet the mystics share","word":"silence","hook":"mystics disagree about almost everything; they all got quiet","length":"5:30","carry":"quiet is where it is","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the phone, face down for three minutes. Today, what's left when the noise stops.","screen":["`THE PHONE.` → `FACE DOWN FOR THREE MINUTES.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"When was the last time you heard nothing? Not quiet with a podcast. Not quiet with the fridge humming and a phone buzzing in the next room. Nothing. It's easy to go a long time without it. The car, the shower, the walk to the store all get filled. Here's something odd. The mystics of the world's traditions disagree about a great deal: God, the soul, what happens after death. But read what they actually did, and one thing keeps showing up, from monasteries to meeting houses to mountain caves. They got quiet.","screen":["`WHEN DID YOU LAST HEAR NOTHING?` → `THEY DISAGREE ABOUT A LOT.` → `AND THEY GOT QUIET.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Start with a prophet on the run. Elijah hides in a cave on a mountain. A great wind comes, tearing rocks apart, but God is not in the wind. Then an earthquake, and God is not in the earthquake. Then fire, and God is not in the fire. And then, in the King James wording, \"after the fire a still small voice.\" That Hebrew phrase is hard to carry into English; it has also been rendered a gentle whisper, and a sound of thin silence. Either way, what comes after the noise is quiet. Eastern Orthodox monks on Mount Athos in Greece are known for a way of prayer called hesychasm, from a Greek word meaning stillness. In traditional Quaker meetings, Friends worship by sitting together in silence, speaking only when someone feels moved to. Early Buddhist texts describe the Buddha telling monks that when they gather, they should either talk about the teaching or keep what he called noble silence. And an old Chinese classic puts it sharply: those who know don't talk about it, and those who talk about it don't know. These traditions don't agree about what's found in the silence. For some it's God; for some, the mind seeing itself clearly; for some, the Tao, which can't be named. But it's a room each of them walks into. For each, in its own way, quiet is where it is.","screen":["`NOT THE WIND, NOT THE FIRE.` · `STILLNESS, MEETING, NOBLE SILENCE.` · `THOSE WHO KNOW DON'T TALK.` · `DIFFERENT THINGS, THE SAME ROOM.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Here's the practice. If you can, turn off one sound near you: the music, the TV, the notifications. Then sit. For the next forty-five seconds, don't try to make the mind silent. Just listen to the silence that's already around the sounds. The hum, and the space around the hum. If you pray, you can hold a line from the Psalms: be still, and know. If you don't, just listen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Silence. A room that prophets, monks, Friends and sages all walk into, even when they find different things there. Twelfth word.","screen":["`SILENCE — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: quiet is where it is. A stretch you don't fill can be a room of its own. That's day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE TAO: THOSE WHO KNOW THEY HAVE ENOUGH ARE RICH.`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"the Tao: those who know they have enough are rich","word":"enough","hook":"the Tao: those who know they have enough are rich","length":"5:30","carry":"enough","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: silence, and quiet is where it is. Today, a word that's surprisingly hard to say about your own life.","screen":["`SILENCE.` → `QUIET IS WHERE IT IS.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"The shoes arrive. Or the phone, or the upgrade. For a few days there's a glow. Then, often, it's just a thing that's owned, and the wanting has already moved on to the next one. Kurt Vonnegut told a story about this. He and his fellow novelist Joseph Heller were at a party given by a billionaire. Vonnegut pointed out that their host might have made more in a single day than Heller's best-known novel had earned in its whole life. Heller said he had something the billionaire could never have: the knowledge that he had enough.","screen":["`THE GLOW, THEN THE NEXT THING.` → `A PARTY GIVEN BY A BILLIONAIRE.` → `SOMETHING HE CAN NEVER HAVE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Heller was in old company. The Tao Te Ching, the short Chinese classic traditionally credited to Lao-tzu, has a chapter of paired sayings about strength and knowledge. In the middle of it, in an old translation: \"He who is satisfied with his lot is rich.\" In the Chinese it's four characters, and the first two mean knowing enough. Not having enough. Knowing it. Richness, in this picture, isn't a number in a bank account. It's a kind of recognition. The Roman Stoic Seneca made the same turn in a letter to a friend: the poor person isn't the one who has too little, but the one who keeps craving more. And the Greek philosopher Epicurus, whose name now gets attached to fine dining, is reported to have been content with bread and water, and to have prized friendship above almost anything. Modern psychologists have a name for what all three were pushing against: the hedonic treadmill. When we get something we wanted, we get used to it, fast, and our sense of normal moves up. So the chase never finishes, because the finish line walks away as we approach it. Knowing enough doesn't mean you stop wanting anything or never improve your life. It means that now and then you step off the treadmill and say the word. The traditions that teach it don't promise it'll make you rich. They say it's what rich actually is.","screen":["`SATISFIED WITH HIS LOT IS RICH.` · `THE POOR ONE KEEPS CRAVING MORE.` · `THE HEDONIC TREADMILL.` · `STEP OFF, AND SAY THE WORD.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and let the breath settle. For about forty-five seconds, let your eyes or your mind move over what you have right now. Not the whole house, just what's in reach. The chair holding you up. The clothes you're wearing. The breath coming in without being asked. For each one, say inside: enough. Not forever. Just, right now, enough.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Enough. Not having it: knowing it. The quiet partner to every vision board. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`ENOUGH — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: enough. The one thing in the story the billionaire couldn't buy. Day thirteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THREE THINGS, OUT LOUD, BADLY.`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"three things, out loud, badly","word":"gratitude","hook":"three things, out loud, badly","length":"5:30","carry":"thank you anyway","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: enough, and knowing it. Today closes week two, with three things, said badly.","screen":["`ENOUGH.` → `TWO WEEKS.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Family dinner. Somebody says, okay, everyone name one thing you're thankful for. Half the table groans. Somebody says the food. Somebody says Wi-Fi. A kid says the dog. It's awkward, it's a bit forced, and it's over in a minute. That clumsy minute may be worth more than it looks. Today asks for three things. Out loud. Badly. Not eloquent, not the perfect list. Just said.","screen":["`NAME ONE THING.` → `THE FOOD, THE WI-FI, THE DOG.` → `THREE THINGS, OUT LOUD, BADLY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Start with the emperor from day three. Marcus Aurelius's private notebook doesn't open with philosophy at all, at least as it comes down to us. It opens with a list of thank-yous. From his grandfather, good morals and an even temper. From his mother, generosity, and a simple way of living. Teacher after teacher, friend after friend, what each one gave him. A man who ruled an empire starts by counting what he'd been given. Paul, writing to a young church, tells them, in old English wording: \"In every thing give thanks.\" Notice he doesn't say for everything. In everything. Inside whatever is happening, find something to thank. Jewish tradition has a short prayer, the Modeh Ani, said on waking, while still in bed, thanking God for giving back the soul for another day. Thanks comes first, before coffee, before news. And then the lab. In 2003 the psychologists Robert Emmons and Michael McCullough asked some people to write down, each week, a few things they were grateful for, while others wrote about hassles or ordinary events. After ten weeks, the gratitude group rated their lives as a whole more highly and felt more hopeful about the week ahead. Nobody was asked to feel grateful first. They were only asked to write it down. That's why badly is allowed. Gratitude isn't a mood you wait for. It's a thing you say, and on hard days you say it anyway. The Stoic Seneca kept an evening version: once the lamp was out, he went back over his day. So if you keep a journal and wonder what to write, here's a pair. In the morning: what matters today? At night: what went well, and what would I do differently?","screen":["`AN EMPEROR'S LIST OF THANK-YOUS.` · `IN EVERY THING GIVE THANKS.` · `THANKS BEFORE GETTING OUT OF BED.` · `WRITE IT DOWN, NO FEELING REQUIRED.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 15","voice":"Here's the practice, and yes, it's out loud. If you're somewhere you can't speak, whisper it, or just move your lips. Name three things you're thankful for right now, the first three that come. They can be tiny: warm socks, a text from someone, the bus that came. Say them badly. Then, for about forty-five seconds, sit with the third one and let it land.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Gratitude. Noticing what you've been given, and saying so, even before the feeling arrives. Fourteenth word. Thoughts, body, sit, the phone, silence, enough, gratitude: that's week two.","screen":["`GRATITUDE — your 14th word` → `WEEK TWO. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: thank you anyway. It matters most on the days it's hardest to say. Day fourteen, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE RULE THAT KEEPS TURNING UP.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"the rule that keeps turning up","word":"the golden rule","hook":"the rule that keeps turning up","length":"6:00","carry":"be good to people","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: gratitude, and thank you anyway. Week three turns toward other people. Day fifteen.","screen":["`THANK YOU ANYWAY.` → `WEEK THREE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"A playground, right after somebody grabbed something that wasn't theirs. A grown-up crouches down and says it: treat people the way you want to be treated. Plenty of kids hear it before they can read. It's so familiar it sounds like nothing. But here's the strange part. You can find it in ancient China, in Jewish law, in the sayings of the rabbis, in the words of Jesus, in India's great epic, in Greek writers. It turns up on path after path.","screen":["`TREAT PEOPLE THE WAY YOU WANT TO BE TREATED.` → `CHINA. THE BIBLE. INDIA. GREECE.` → `PATH AFTER PATH.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Start in China. A student asks Confucius if there's one word you could live by your whole life. In an old translation, Confucius answers: \"Is not RECIPROCITY such a word? What you do not want done to yourself, do not do to others.\" Notice it's a don't. The Hebrew Bible, in among laws about harvests and fair wages, says to love your neighbor as yourself. Centuries later, a man asks the rabbi Hillel to teach him the whole Torah while he stands on one foot. Hillel says, roughly: what is hateful to you, don't do to your neighbor. That's the whole Torah; the rest is commentary. Go and learn it. Then Jesus puts it as a do: \"all things whatsoever ye would that men should do to you, do ye even so to them.\" India's great epic has a version. So does the Greek orator Isocrates. Did each path arrive at it on its own? Nobody can say for sure who heard what from whom. What's clear is that it keeps turning up, and each tradition keeps it in its own place. For Confucius it's one word for a whole life. For Hillel, a summary that sends you back to study. For Jesus, the law and the prophets in a sentence. And notice where the rule starts: with you. What you don't want. What you would want. It asks you to use your own heart as a map of someone else's.","screen":["`CONFUCIUS: RECIPROCITY.` · `HILLEL: THE WHOLE TORAH ON ONE FOOT.` · `JESUS: DO YE EVEN SO TO THEM.` · `YOUR HEART AS A MAP.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Here's the practice. From today the bell gives you a little longer, about seventy-five seconds. Sit, and let the breath settle. Think of someone you'll see today. Then ask: if I were them, today, what would I want? Maybe to be listened to. Maybe to be left alone for a bit. Maybe just a real hello. Let the answer settle. You don't have to do anything with it yet. Just know it.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"The golden rule. What you'd want, given. What you'd hate, held back. Found on path after path. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`THE GOLDEN RULE — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: be good to people. Your own heart already knows the map. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: START WITH THE PERSON IN THE MIRROR.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"start with the person in the mirror","word":"kindness","hook":"start with the person in the mirror","length":"6:00","carry":"may I be at ease","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the golden rule, your own heart as a map of someone else's. Today, the one person the map tends to leave out.","screen":["`THE GOLDEN RULE.` → `BE GOOD TO PEOPLE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"A friend burns the dinner. Misses a deadline. Says the wrong thing at the wrong moment. What comes out of your mouth? Usually something like: hey, it happens, you're tired, it's fine. Now flip it. You burn the dinner. What does the voice inside say then? Sometimes it's a voice nobody would be allowed to use on a friend. Idiot. Of course you did. Typical. One old tradition insists kindness starts with the person in the mirror.","screen":["`WHAT YOU SAY TO A FRIEND.` → `WHAT YOU SAY TO YOURSELF.` → `START WITH THE MIRROR.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"In the early Buddhist teachings there's a word, metta. It's usually translated loving-kindness, but it comes from the Pali word for friend. Metta is friendliness: wanting good for someone the way a friend does. One short Buddhist discourse describes it with a picture: the way a mother would protect her only child with her own life, a person can grow a heart like that toward every living being. Here's the surprise. The Buddhist teacher Buddhaghosa wrote a long guide to meditation, and when he explains how to practice metta, he says not to start with everyone. Start with yourself. Wish yourself well first, with phrases like may I be happy, may I be free from trouble. Then extend it outward: to someone dear, to someone neutral, and eventually to someone difficult. His reasoning is plain. You know from the inside that you want to be happy. That makes you the example: everyone else wants the same. Remember yesterday's line from the Hebrew Bible: \"love thy neighbour as thyself.\" Those last two words carry a quiet assumption: that there's some care for yourself to measure by. Modern research points somewhere similar. The psychologist Kristin Neff studies self-compassion: treating your own failures the way you'd treat a friend's. A common worry is that being kind to yourself will make you lazy. In one set of studies, people nudged to be gentler about a mistake were more willing to own it, and put more effort into doing better. Kindness isn't a reward for getting it right. It's where you start.","screen":["`METTA: THE FRIEND WORD.` · `START WITH YOURSELF, THEN OUTWARD.` · `AS THYSELF.` · `GENTLE WITH MISTAKES, MORE LIKELY TO TRY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Here's the practice. The bell gives you about seventy-five seconds. Sit, and let the breath settle. Then picture yourself the way a good friend might see you right now: tired, trying, doing your best with the day you've had. On each breath out, say quietly inside: may I be at ease. If it feels strange or even a little silly, that's normal. Say it anyway.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Kindness. Wanting good for someone the way a friend does, starting with the one in the mirror. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`KINDNESS — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: may I be at ease. It's the sentence you'd give a friend, turned around. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: DO SOMETHING FOR SOMEONE WITH NO RECEIPT.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"do something for someone with no receipt","word":"service","hook":"do something for someone with no receipt","length":"6:00","carry":"no receipt","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: kindness, starting with the one in the mirror. Today, kindness with no name on it.","screen":["`KINDNESS.` → `MAY I BE AT EASE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"A good deed can come with a little receipt attached. I drove them to the airport. I remembered her birthday. I did the dishes, again. The receipts tend to print themselves. And sometimes the receipt wants to be seen: a thank-you, a mention, a like. Today is about the other kind of giving: no thanks, no credit, maybe nobody even knowing it was you. Four traditions have a lot to say about it.","screen":["`THE RECEIPT PRINTS ITSELF.` → `A THANK-YOU, A MENTION, A LIKE.` → `NO RECEIPT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Jesus talks about giving to the poor, and says to do it so quietly that, in the King James wording, you \"let not thy left hand know what thy right hand doeth.\" Your own other hand shouldn't find out. The Jewish scholar Maimonides set out eight levels of giving, from grudging to generous. Near the very top: the giver and the receiver don't know each other's names. And at the top itself: helping someone so they won't need help again, with a gift, a loan, a partnership, a job. In Sikh tradition the word is seva, selfless service, and you can see it in the gurdwara, the Sikh house of worship. Gurdwaras keep a langar, a free kitchen where anyone, of any faith or none, sits and eats together. Volunteers cook, serve and wash up. At the Golden Temple in Amritsar, the kitchen feeds a vast number of people every day, served by volunteers whose names the people eating will never learn. And Hindu teaching has a famous line about acting without clinging to the fruits of the action: do the work well, and let go of what it earns you. The reasons differ. For Jesus, the Father sees what's done in secret. For Maimonides, the poor person's dignity matters. In the Hindu line, the doer lets go of the reward. They don't mean the same thing by it. But each of them treats the receipt as the part to throw away.","screen":["`LET NOT THY LEFT HAND KNOW.` · `MAIMONIDES' LADDER OF GIVING.` · `SEVA AND THE FREE KITCHEN.` · `LET GO OF THE FRUITS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and let the breath settle. For about seventy-five seconds, think of one small thing you could do for someone where nobody would know it was you. Putting a shopping cart back. Refilling the coffee. Paying for something quietly. Pick one. Picture yourself doing it and walking away, no thank-you. Notice what that feels like.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Service. Doing good for someone else, without keeping the receipt. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`SERVICE — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: no receipt. A good deed is already finished the moment it's done. Day seventeen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: YOU'RE NOT THE ONLY ONE.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"you're not the only one","word":"loneliness","hook":"you're not the only one; the room at sunset","length":"6:00","carry":"the room is full","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: service, and no receipt. Today, a feeling that's very good at hiding.","screen":["`SERVICE.` → `NO RECEIPT.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"You can feel it in a full room. At a party where everyone seems to know everyone. In a group chat that's buzzing with jokes you're not part of. At 2am, awake, when the whole building seems to be asleep but you. Loneliness is strange that way. It isn't about how many people are near you. It's the feeling that nobody is really with you. And one of its cruelest tricks is convincing you that you're the only one who feels it. You're not. Not even close.","screen":["`A FULL ROOM, AND STILL ALONE.` → `AWAKE AT 2AM.` → `YOU'RE NOT THE ONLY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"In 2023, the Surgeon General of the United States issued a formal public health advisory about loneliness, the kind usually saved for things like smoking. It reported that about half of American adults said they'd experienced it. Half. Which means that in almost any room, a good number of the people are quietly feeling what you feel. The old texts knew this ache. Near the start of the Bible, before anything has gone wrong in the garden, God looks at the first human and says, in the King James wording: \"It is not good that the man should be alone.\" It's the first thing in the whole story called not good. Old prayers are full of people saying so, out loud: turn to me, for I am alone. The night before he dies, Jesus asks his closest friends to stay awake with him while he prays, and they fall asleep. Many traditions have noticed that being alone and being lonely aren't the same thing. The same hour on your own can feel like a punishment or like a rest. Monks and hermits choose solitude. Nobody chooses loneliness. Now, the room at sunset. At this hour, somewhere, a family is lighting candles. Someone is unrolling a prayer mat. Someone in a monastery is singing the evening prayers, someone is sitting on a cushion, someone is just stopping on a balcony to watch the light. You can't see them. They can't see you. But you're all doing the same small, brave thing at the same kind of hour. The room is full.","screen":["`HALF OF ADULTS SAID THEY'D FELT IT.` · `THE FIRST THING CALLED NOT GOOD.` · `ALONE ISN'T THE SAME AS LONELY.` · `THE ROOM AT SUNSET.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and let the breath settle. For about seventy-five seconds, picture the room at sunset. Somewhere, someone lighting a candle. Someone kneeling. Someone sitting just like you, a little tired, stopping anyway. You don't know their names. On each breath out, let yourself be one of them. If loneliness is heavy today, let it be here too. It's allowed in the room.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Loneliness. The feeling that nobody is with you, and one of the most widely shared feelings there is. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`LONELINESS — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: the room is full. And if the loneliness is heavy today, telling one person is a brave and good first step. Day eighteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: LOOK AT SOMETHING BIGGER THAN YOU TODAY.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"look at something bigger than you today","word":"awe","hook":"look at something bigger than you today","length":"6:00","carry":"look up","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: loneliness, and the room is full. Today, a feeling that makes you smaller, in the good way.","screen":["`LONELINESS.` → `THE ROOM IS FULL.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"The ocean, seen for the first time. A storm rolling in over a field. A night sky far from any city, so full of stars it looks fake. A newborn's hand. Moments like these can stop a person in their tracks. For a second, the self goes quiet. The problems don't vanish; they just get smaller, because something else got very big. And it's easy to go weeks without one, eyes down: on screens, on sidewalks, on lists.","screen":["`THE OCEAN, A STORM, THE STARS.` → `FOR A SECOND, THE SELF GOES QUIET.` → `EYES DOWN, FOR WEEKS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"In 1836, the American writer Ralph Waldo Emerson asked his readers to imagine something: \"If the stars should appear one night in a thousand years, how would men believe and adore\". His point is that they do appear, every clear night. The wonder is always on offer, to anyone who looks up. A psalm looked at the same night sky, long before: \"When I consider thy heavens, the work of thy fingers, the moon and the stars, which thou hast ordained; What is man, that thou art mindful of him?\" Notice the move. Look at the vast thing, and the question comes back to you: how small I am, and how strange that I'm here at all. Now the lab. Researchers who study awe, including the psychologist Dacher Keltner at Berkeley, find it has a signature: people describe feeling small, and it isn't a bad feeling. The self shrinks, and the world opens. In one study, people spent one minute looking up at a grove of very tall eucalyptus trees, while others looked up at a tall building. Then a researcher stumbled and dropped a handful of pens. The people who'd been looking at the trees picked up more of them. One minute of awe, and people were a little more helpful. Some astronauts who've seen the whole earth from space describe a version of this too. An essayist, a psalmist, a lab: very different beliefs about what's behind the stars, and the same move. Look up, and come back down a little different.","screen":["`ONE NIGHT IN A THOUSAND YEARS.` · `WHEN I CONSIDER THY HEAVENS.` · `SMALL, AND IT ISN'T BAD.` · `THE TREES AND THE DROPPED PENS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Here's the practice. If you can, look out a window, or step outside. Find the biggest thing you can see: the sky, a tree, a building, the clouds. If you're stuck inside, close your eyes and remember the biggest thing you've ever stood under. For about seventy-five seconds, just look. Let it be big. Let yourself be small next to it. You don't need to name what you feel.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Awe. The feeling of standing next to something vast, and getting a little smaller, and a little more generous. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`AWE — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: look up. The sky is free, and it's there every night. Day nineteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: EVERY TRADITION SAYS IT: YOU CAN'T DO THIS ALONE.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"every tradition says it: you can't do this alone","word":"company","hook":"every tradition says it: you can't do this alone","length":"6:00","carry":"find your people","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: awe, and look up. Today, the people who make a practice stick.","screen":["`AWE.` → `LOOK UP.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"40 sec","voice":"Anyone who's tried to change a habit alone knows how it goes. Day one, fired up. Day four, a little tired. Day nine, you skip once, then twice, and nobody notices, because nobody knew. Then there are the times something actually stuck. Very often, someone else was in it. A running buddy who'd be standing on the corner at seven. A friend who'd ask, did you do it? A group that noticed when you weren't there. Many of the world's traditions, for all their differences, say it in their own words: you can't do this alone.","screen":["`DAY NINE, NOBODY NOTICES.` → `SOMEONE ON THE CORNER AT SEVEN.` → `YOU CAN'T DO THIS ALONE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"There's a conversation in the early Buddhist texts that surprises people. The Buddha's attendant, Ananda, says that good friendship, having admirable companions, must be half of the spiritual life. The Buddha corrects him. Not half, he says. It's the whole of it. The Hebrew Bible says it plainly: \"Two are better than one\". And then the picture: \"For if they fall, the one will lift up his fellow: but woe to him that is alone when he falleth; for he hath not another to help him up.\" In Jewish practice, some prayers, like the mourner's Kaddish, are traditionally said only with a minyan, a group of ten. You need other people just to say them. In Islam, the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, taught that prayer offered together in congregation is worth many times more than prayer offered alone; one well-known report puts it at twenty-seven times. Sikhs speak of the sangat, the gathered community, and Christians of the church, meaning the gathered people, not only the building. And a long-running study at Harvard has followed hundreds of people for more than eighty years. When its directors are asked what best predicts who stays healthy and happy into old age, their answer isn't money or fame. It's the quality of people's relationships. So a sit on your own with an app is a real start. But each of these homes adds something: find your people.","screen":["`NOT HALF. THE WHOLE OF IT.` · `TWO ARE BETTER THAN ONE.` · `A MINYAN, A CONGREGATION, A SANGAT.` · `EIGHTY YEARS OF ONE STUDY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and let the breath settle. For about seventy-five seconds, bring to mind the people who've lifted you up when you fell. One at a time. A face, a name, a moment. Then let one more face come: someone you'd like to walk this path with, even a little. Don't plan anything. Just let them arrive. If you pray, you can hold them there.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Company. The people who lift you up when you fall, and the ones who notice when you're not there. Twentieth word.","screen":["`COMPANY — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: find your people. Someone who notices when you're not there is worth a lot. Tomorrow, camp one closes.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"the mountain ahead","word":"Rumi","hook":"the mountain ahead; Rumi's field beyond our ideas of right and wrong (a paraphrase; the well-known wording is a modern, copyrighted version)","length":"6:15","carry":"meet me there","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: company, and find your people. Today, the last day of camp one.","screen":["`COMPANY.` → `FIND YOUR PEOPLE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"35 sec","voice":"Three weeks ago you took one breath with a bell. Today you've got twenty words on your strand, and you're standing at the edge of the first camp, looking up at the mountain ahead. So for the last word of camp one, a poet. Not a rule, not a technique. A person. A Persian poet whose lines get shared on phones every day, often with his name on them and his world left off.","screen":["`TWENTY WORDS ON YOUR STRAND.` → `THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.` → `A PERSON, NOT A RULE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min","voice":"Jalal al-Din Rumi was born in the eastern Persian world, in what's now Afghanistan or Tajikistan; the old biographies disagree on the town. His family moved west, ahead of the Mongol invasions, and settled in Konya, in today's Turkey. He became a respected Muslim scholar and teacher, steeped in the Qur'an, and his poetry is full of it. Popular versions often leave that out. Then, already a grown man with students of his own, he met a wandering mystic called Shams of Tabriz, and his life turned over. The old biographies say the friendship was so intense that his students grew jealous, and Shams eventually disappeared. Out of that loss came an ocean of poems. His great work, the Masnavi, begins with a reed flute crying. It was cut from the reed bed, and every note it plays is longing to go back. There's a famous saying credited to Rumi about a field, somewhere beyond our ideas of doing wrong and doing right, where he'll meet you. The wording most people share is a modern English version, not his, and scholars aren't sure how closely it follows the Persian. So, paraphrased and honestly labeled: a place past the arguing, where two people can simply meet. That's where camp one has been walking you. Meant to be. Breathe. Memento mori. Namaste. Manifesting. The shadow. Karma. Thoughts. Body. Sit. The phone. Silence. Enough. Gratitude. The golden rule. Kindness. Service. Loneliness. Awe. Company. Rumi. Twenty-one words from many homes, and not one of them asked you to believe anything.","screen":["`A MUSLIM SCHOLAR IN KONYA.` · `SHAMS, AND THE LOSS.` · `THE REED FLUTE CRIES.` · `A FIELD PAST THE ARGUING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"1 min 45","voice":"Here's the practice. Sit, and let the breath settle. For about seventy-five seconds, picture a wide open field, early evening. No arguments there. Nobody keeping score. Then let one person come to mind that you've been at odds with, even a little. Don't fix anything. Just picture them walking into the same field, from the other side, and let there be room for both of you.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Rumi. A Persian Muslim poet and teacher from Konya, and the voice of longing to go home. Twenty-first word, and the end of camp one.","screen":["`RUMI — your 21st word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: meet me there. That's camp one, finished; tomorrow, camp two opens with a Roman emperor who doesn't want to get out of bed.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS. CAMP ONE COMPLETE.` → `TOMORROW: MARCUS DOESN'T WANT TO GET UP.`"]}]}}};
